<script>
window.STUDY_DATA = {"topics":[{"id":"objectives","code":"A1","title":"Objectives of Financial Reporting","subtitle":"Conceptual Framework Ch 1 · users, statements, roles and the ASC","los":{"1":"The role of financial reporting in stakeholders’ decisions","2":"Stakeholders’ roles; general purpose, special purpose, NFP and statutory body FS","3":"The mandate of the ASC"},"accounts":[],"steps":[{"id":"purpose","name":"Why report at all","short":"Why report"},{"id":"users","name":"Users and their decisions","short":"Users"},{"id":"statements","name":"The complete set of statements","short":"Statements"},{"id":"types","name":"Four kinds of financial statements","short":"FS types"},{"id":"roles","name":"Who does what, and the ASC","short":"Roles & ASC"}],"map":{"purpose":{"label":"Objective, primary users, limits","star":"FS are for the people who give the entity money"},"users":{"label":"Match each user to its decision"},"statements":{"label":"Five statements, five questions"},"types":{"label":"General, special, NFP, statutory body","star":"Not every Singapore entity uses SFRS(I)"},"roles":{"label":"IASB writes, ASC adopts, ACRA enforces"}},"preamble":["Before any debits and credits, a quick question: **why** does anyone bother producing financial statements? This short chapter answers that, and it is the “why” behind the whole course.","Here is the route:","1. **Why report at all:** the objective, who the reports are for, and what they can’t tell you. ★ special","2. **Users and their decisions:** match each reader to the decision they make. Creditors decide whether to lend; that’s practice Q3 in one line.","3. **The complete set:** five statements, each answering one question. The cash flow statement is about liquidity and solvency (practice Q45).","4. **Four kinds of financial statements:** general purpose, special purpose, charities and statutory boards, each with its own rule book. ★ special","5. **Who does what:** directors prepare, auditors check, ACRA enforces, and the ASC sets Singapore’s standards.","+ The one idea to hold on to: general purpose FS are written for the people who **give the entity money**: investors, lenders and other creditors. Not management, not regulators."],"intros":{"purpose":"First stop, and the one the rest of the course hangs on. Why prepare financial statements, and for whom? ★ The answer is narrower than you’d think: they are written for the people who give the entity money (Investors, Lenders, other Creditors), not for management or regulators. We finish with what FS *can’t* do, including telling you what a company is worth.","users":"Now we know who the reports are *for*, let’s meet everyone who actually reads them, and what each is trying to decide. This is a matching game: the exam names a user and asks for its decision. Creditors → whether to lend. Easy marks, as long as you don’t hand the supplier an investor’s decision.","statements":"We know who reads the reports. Next: what’s actually *in* them? Five statements, each answering one question. Mostly common sense, with one pairing the exam keeps testing: the statement of cash flows is about **liquidity and solvency**, not profit and not gearing.","types":"So far we’ve talked about one kind of report. Time to widen the lens: not every report is general purpose, and not every Singapore entity follows SFRS(I). ★ This is the Singapore-specific bit: listed companies, other companies, charities and statutory boards each have their own rule book, and a report written to one user’s template is special purpose.","roles":"Last stop: the cast list. Who writes the rules, who follows them, who checks, who polices? Mostly routine, but exam setters love to swap the ASC, ACRA and the IASB. The chart from your notes puts them in order: IASB writes → ASC adopts → directors prepare → auditors check → ACRA enforces → users decide."},"pitfalls":{"mgmt-primary":"Treating management as a primary user (it has internal information).","regulator-primary":"Treating regulators, IRAS or the public as primary users.","wrong-user-decision":"Matching a user with another user’s decision (e.g. a creditor buying shares).","cf-profit":"Saying the statement of cash flows shows profitability or gearing (it shows liquidity and solvency).","fs-value":"Thinking financial statements show what the entity is worth.","gp-simple":"Thinking “general purpose” means simple or small, or calling a report to one user’s template general purpose.","sfrs-everyone":"Answering SFRS(I) for every entity, including charities and statutory boards.","asc-enforce":"Confusing the ASC (sets standards) with ACRA (enforces) or the IASB (writes IFRS).","auditor-prepares":"Thinking the auditors prepare, or are responsible for, the financial statements."},"examTypes":[{"id":"user-decision","title":"Which decision would the company’s creditors make? (practice Q3)","pitfall":"Picking an investor’s or a customer’s decision.","see":"users"},{"id":"statement-role","title":"What is the role of the statement of cash flows? (practice Q45)","pitfall":"Picking profitability or gearing instead of liquidity and solvency.","see":"statements"},{"id":"primary-users","title":"Who are the primary users, and what do they assess?","pitfall":"Including management or regulators; thinking FS show value.","see":"purpose"},{"id":"gp-sp","title":"Which of these is a special purpose report?","pitfall":"Thinking general purpose means simple or small.","see":"types"},{"id":"nfp-sb","title":"Which standards does a charity or statutory board apply?","pitfall":"Answering SFRS(I) for every entity.","see":"types"},{"id":"roles","title":"Who prepares, audits and enforces?","pitfall":"Making the auditor responsible for the FS.","see":"roles"},{"id":"asc-mandate","title":"What is the mandate of the ASC?","pitfall":"Choosing enforcement (ACRA) or writing IFRS (IASB).","see":"roles"}],"pages":[{"type":"map","id":"map-overview","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"intro","lo":1,"title":"Why financial statements exist","body":["This is the “why” chapter. No journal entries, no present values: just the reason the rest of the course exists, and who does what to make it happen.","As always, each idea starts with a question. Think it through, then reveal the answer.","It is small and non-core, but the exam likes to pick an easy mark or two from it, usually by offering a plausible user, statement or body that is *almost* right."],"id":"intro-18r5wi","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"map","id":"map-1-purpose","bucket":"purpose","sect":1,"part":"Why report at all"},{"id":"think-jduhmo","type":"think","bucket":"purpose","lo":1,"scenario":"Kopi Kakis Pte Ltd runs six cafés. The two founders know every number in the business by heart. Now they want a S$2m {l|bank loan} and two new outside shareholders.","prompt":"The founders already know how the business is doing. So why go to the trouble of preparing financial statements at all?","answer":["Because the **bank and the new shareholders** don’t know. They can’t walk into the back office and read the till rolls, but they are being asked to hand over money.","Financial accounting does two jobs: it **measures** the business’s activities, then **communicates** those measurements to the people deciding whether to give it money.","The founders’ heads are full of numbers; the bank needs them on paper, in a standard format, before it parts with S$2m."],"sect":1,"part":"Why report at all"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"purpose","lo":1,"title":"The objective of general purpose financial reporting","body":["Kopi Kakis showed that outsiders can’t see inside the business, so someone has to tell them. That is what financial reporting is for, and the Conceptual Framework says it in one sentence.","Financial accounting has **two functions**:","1. **measure** the business’s activities, then","2. **communicate** those measurements to decision makers.","The **objective** (Conceptual Framework Ch 1): provide financial information about the entity that is useful to **existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors** when they decide about **providing resources** to it.","Those decisions are about:","- **buying, selling or holding** {e|shares} and {l|debt}","- **giving or settling {l|loans}** and other credit","- **voting on or influencing** management’s actions","+ Primary users = the people who **give the entity money**: **I**nvestors, **L**enders, other **C**reditors."],"id":"rule-1thp5z","sect":1,"part":"Why report at all"},{"type":"think","bucket":"purpose","lo":1,"scenario":"Kopi Kakis publishes its annual report. Six readers pick it up: (1) a current shareholder, (2) someone thinking of buying shares, (3) the bank, (4) a coffee-bean supplier who sells on 30-day credit, (5) the CEO, (6) IRAS.","prompt":"Which of these is the report actually *written for*? Who is the odd one out, and why?","answer":["(1)–(4) are **primary users**: existing and potential investors, a lender, and another creditor. Note that **potential** investors count too.","The **CEO** is the odd one out: management can get any information it wants **internally**, so it is not a primary user.","**IRAS** (and other regulators, and the public) may find the report useful, but it is **not written for them**. IRAS gets its own tax computation anyway."],"id":"think-1j1r1g","sect":1,"part":"Why report at all"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"purpose","lo":1,"title":"What users assess, and who is not a primary user","body":["We know who the reports are written for. Next: what are those readers trying to work out when they open them, and who is left off the list?","To make those decisions, users assess two things:","1. the entity’s prospects for **future net {a|cash} inflows**, and","2. how well management has looked after the entity’s {a|resources} (**stewardship**).","- **Management is not a primary user:** it has internal information.","- **Regulators and the public** may find the reports useful, but the reports are not directed at them.","! Exam options love slipping in “management” or “regulators” as primary users. Both are wrong."],"id":"rule-gubhmr","sect":1,"part":"Why report at all"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"purpose","lo":1,"title":"The objective as a chain","intro":"Read it top to bottom: who the reports are for, what they decide, and what they need to know to decide it.","notes":{"gpfr":"Reports for users who **can’t demand** reports tailored to them.","users":"The people who give the entity money, now or possibly later. **Potential** investors and lenders count.","d1":"Investors deciding whether to buy, sell or hold {e|shares} or {l|bonds}.","d2":"Lenders and suppliers deciding whether to lend or give credit, and on what terms.","d3":"Shareholders voting on directors and influencing management.","cash":"Will the entity generate {a|cash} to pay dividends, interest and {l|debts}? Users estimate this from the reports.","stew":"Has management looked after the {a|resources} it was given? This feeds the vote."},"walk":[{"nodes":["gpfr","users"],"text":"The reports are written for **primary users**: investors, lenders and other creditors, existing and potential."},{"nodes":["d1","d2","d3"],"text":"Those users make three kinds of decision about **providing resources**."},{"nodes":["cash","stew"],"text":"To decide, they assess **future net cash inflows** and management’s **stewardship**."}],"caption":"Investors, Lenders, other Creditors: the people who give the entity money.","id":"diagram-1qjoiy","sect":1,"part":"Why report at all","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:374px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 340.00 244.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 233.08)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-233.08 329.33,-233.08 329.33,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u0