=\"-214.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Notes\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node10\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>q5\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M251.81,-236C251.81,-236 180.53,-236 180.53,-236 174.53,-236 168.53,-230 168.53,-224 168.53,-224 168.53,-212 168.53,-212 168.53,-206 174.53,-200 180.53,-200 180.53,-200 251.81,-200 251.81,-200 257.81,-200 263.81,-206 263.81,-212 263.81,-212 263.81,-224 263.81,-224 263.81,-230 257.81,-236 251.81,-236\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"182.42\" y=\"-219.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">How were the\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"177.17\" y=\"-207.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">numbers made?\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge5\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>notes&#45;&gt;q5\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M83.38,-218C104.46,-218 134.65,-218 160.82,-218\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"160.57,-220.1 166.57,-218 160.57,-215.9 160.57,-220.1\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"mcq-16b0vc","type":"mcq","bucket":"statements","lo":1,"q":"Kopi Kakis issued new {e|shares} and paid a {e|dividend} this year. Which statement shows both movements side by side, together with the profit for the year?","options":[{"t":"Statement of profit or loss and OCI","why":"Share issues and dividends are transactions with owners. They never go through profit or loss."},{"t":"Statement of changes in equity","ok":true,"why":"It shows every movement in each {e|equity component}: profit, OCI, share issues and dividends."},{"t":"Statement of financial position","why":"It shows the {e|closing equity balance}, not the movements in it."},{"t":"Notes to the financial statements","why":"The notes explain the numbers, but the movements themselves are presented in the statement of changes in equity."}],"sect":3,"part":"The complete set of statements"},{"type":"map","id":"map-4-types","bucket":"types","sect":4,"part":"Four kinds of financial statements"},{"type":"think","bucket":"types","lo":2,"scenario":"Kopi Kakis sends the bank two documents: its audited annual financial statements, and a 3-page cash forecast filled in on the bank’s own loan application template.","prompt":"One is “general purpose”, one is “special purpose”. Which is which, and what makes the difference?","answer":["The **annual financial statements** are **general purpose**: written for users who **can’t demand** a report tailored to them, such as shareholders and most lenders.","The **cash forecast on the bank’s template** is **special purpose**: one specific user set the terms and the format.","Notice it’s about **who it is for**, not how big or simple it is. A 300-page listed-company annual report is general purpose; a one-page tax computation for IRAS is special purpose."],"id":"think-1wx5of","sect":4,"part":"Four kinds of financial statements"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"types","lo":2,"title":"General purpose vs special purpose","body":["The audited FS and the bank’s template differ in **who** they are made for. That difference is the line between general purpose and special purpose reports.","- **General purpose** FS: for users who **cannot demand** reports tailored to them (shareholders, lenders of a company).","- **Special purpose** FS: for a **specific** user or purpose who can set the terms: a bank loan application, a tax computation for IRAS, a regulatory return, a contract. Prepared on whatever basis the user, contract or law requires.","! “General purpose” does **not** mean simple or small. It means the readers can’t ask for their own."],"id":"rule-1oopdx","sect":4,"part":"Four kinds of financial statements"},{"id":"think-ykzb8k","type":"think","bucket":"types","lo":2,"scenario":"Three Singapore reporting entities: a charity that runs a soup kitchen, the CPF Board, and a small private trading company with five staff.","prompt":"Do all three follow SFRS(I), like a listed company? If not, what is different about what their readers want?","answer":["No. A **charity**’s readers (donors, members, the Commissioner of Charities) care about **accountability for the funds** given, not profit. Charities can use the **Charities Accounting Standard (CAS)**, with things like {e|restricted and unrestricted funds}.","A **statutory board** like the CPF Board reports to Parliament, ministries and the public, using **Statutory Board Financial Reporting Standards (SB-FRS)**, based closely on SFRS(I).","A **non-listed company** can use FRS or SFRS(I), and a small one may use **SFRS for Small Entities**."],"sect":4,"part":"Four kinds of financial statements"},{"id":"visual-1kiuwv","type":"visual","bucket":"types","lo":2,"title":"Four types of financial statements in the syllabus","html":"\u003cp>General purpose vs special purpose is one split. Here are the four types of financial statements the syllabus names, side by side.\u003c/p>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl reveal-rows\">\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Type\u003c/th>\u003cth>For whom and why\u003c/th>\u003cth>Rules in Singapore\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>General purpose\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Users who \u003cstrong>cannot demand\u003c/strong> tailored reports, e.g. shareholders and lenders\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Listed: \u003cstrong>SFRS(I)\u003c/strong> (identical to IFRS). Other companies: FRS or SFRS(I); small entities may use SFRS for Small Entities\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Special purpose\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A specific user who sets the terms: a loan application, IRAS tax computation, a regulatory return, a contract\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Whatever basis the user, contract or law requires\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Not-for-profit\u003c/strong> (charities, societies)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Donors, members, Commissioner of Charities. Focus: \u003cstrong>accountability for funds\u003c/strong>, not profit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Charities Accounting Standard (CAS)\u003c/strong>, issued by the ASC\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Statutory bodies\u003c/strong> (CPF Board, HDB)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Parliament, ministries, the public\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>SB-FRS\u003c/strong> (see the ASSB website), based closely on SFRS(I)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cp class=\"cap\">Rules on which entity uses which standard live on the ASC, ACRA and ASSB websites. Check there if a question turns on the detail.\u003c/p>","sect":4,"part":"Four kinds of financial statements"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"types","lo":2,"title":"Which kind of report, which rule book?","intro":"Two questions sort every report: is it written to one user’s specification, and if not, what kind of entity is reporting?","notes":{"q1":"A bank’s template, an IRAS tax computation, a regulatory return or a contract: one user sets the terms.","sp":"Special purpose: prepared on whatever basis that user, contract or law requires.","q2":"Not tailored to one user, so the entity type decides the rule book.","listed":"**SFRS(I)**, which is identical to IFRS.","other":"Non-listed companies may use FRS or SFRS(I). Small entities may use **SFRS for Small Entities**.","nfp":"The focus is accountability for funds. Charities can use the **Charities Accounting Standard**, issued by the ASC.","sb":"**SB-FRS**, for statutory boards such as the CPF Board and HDB. Based closely on SFRS(I)."},"walk":[{"nodes":["q1","sp"],"text":"First: was it written to **one user’s specification**? Then it is **special purpose**, on that user’s basis."},{"nodes":["q2","listed","other"],"text":"Otherwise it is general purpose. Companies: **SFRS(I)** if listed; FRS, SFRS(I) or SFRS for Small Entities if not."},{"nodes":["nfp"],"text":"**Charities:** the Charities Accounting Standard. Readers care about how the funds were used."},{"nodes":["sb"],"text":"**Statutory boards:** SB-FRS. Not every Singapore entity uses SFRS(I)."}],"caption":"Not every Singapore entity uses SFRS(I): check who is reporting.","id":"diagram-2krlbh","sect":4,"part":"Four kinds of financial statements","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:490px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 445.00 226.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 215.61)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-215.61 433.97,-215.61 433.97,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>q1\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" points=\"116.28,-204.81 0,-172.17 116.28,-139.53 232.56,-172.17 116.28,-204.81\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"116.28\" y=\"-173.72\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Written to one user’s\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"116.28\" y=\"-161.72\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">own terms?\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>sp\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#f3eeff\" stroke=\"#6d28d9\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M139.79,-102.28C139.79,-102.28 52.76,-102.28 52.76,-102.28 46.76,-102.28 40.76,-96.28 40.76,-90.28 40.76,-90.28 40.76,-76.64 40.76,-76.64 40.76,-70.64 46.76,-64.64 52.76,-64.64 52.76,-64.64 139.79,-64.64 139.79,-64.64 145.79,-64.64 151.79,-70.64 151.79,-76.64 151.79,-76.64 151.79,-90.28 151.79,-90.28 151.79,-96.28 145.79,-102.28 139.7