ce=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"524.12\" y=\"-67.62\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">presented clearly\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"524.12\" y=\"-56.38\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">and concisely \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"think","bucket":"enhancing","lo":6,"scenario":"A proposed new standard would require every company, however small, to publish a detailed sustainability-cost breakdown. A small family company estimates it would cost S$200,000 a year to produce. Its only outside reader is its bank.","prompt":"Who pays for financial information, who benefits, and who should decide whether it is worth it?","answer":["**Costs** fall first on preparers (collecting, processing, checking, publishing), but they reach users through **lower returns**. Users also pay to analyse information, or to estimate what isn’t reported.","**Benefits:** better decisions, more efficient capital markets and a **lower cost of capital**.","The judgement is made for **financial reporting in general**, mainly by **standard setters**, not company by company. That’s why lighter rules exist for small entities (SFRS for Small Entities)."],"id":"think-1g14qn","sect":2,"part":"Enhancing characteristics and the cost constraint"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"enhancing","lo":6,"title":"The cost constraint","body":["Even useful information has a price, and someone pays it. The cost constraint asks whether the benefit justifies that cost.","- **Cost is a pervasive constraint:** the benefits of reporting a piece of information must **justify the costs** of providing and using it.","- **Costs:** preparers collect, process, verify and publish, and pass the cost on to users through lower returns. Users also bear analysis costs.","- **Benefits:** better decisions, more efficient capital markets, a lower cost of capital.","- The assessment is made for **reporting in general**, mainly by standard setters, not separately by each company.","! Cost is a **constraint**, not a qualitative characteristic. US GAAP textbooks may also list materiality as a constraint; under the 2018 Framework materiality is part of **relevance**."],"id":"rule-k3aecg","sect":2,"part":"Enhancing characteristics and the cost constraint"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"enhancing","lo":6,"title":"The cost constraint: who pays, who benefits, who decides","intro":"Costs on one side, benefits on the other, and the standard setter holding the scales.","notes":{"prep":"Preparers bear the first costs: collecting, processing, checking and publishing.","ucost":"Users pay too: through lower returns, and by analysing information or estimating what isn’t reported.","ben":"Good information means better decisions, more efficient capital markets and a lower cost of capital.","judge":"The test: do the benefits justify the costs?","ss":"Made for financial reporting **in general**, mainly by standard setters. Not each company deciding for itself.","sme":"An example: lighter requirements for small entities, where costs are high relative to benefits."},"walk":[{"nodes":["prep","ucost"],"text":"**Costs:** preparers pay first, and pass it on to users through lower returns."},{"nodes":["ben"],"text":"**Benefits:** better decisions, efficient markets, lower cost of capital."},{"nodes":["judge","ss"],"text":"The balance is struck by **standard setters**, for reporting in general."},{"nodes":["sme"],"text":"That is why small entities get lighter rules."}],"caption":"Cost is a constraint, not a qualitative characteristic.","id":"diagram-xas659","sect":2,"part":"Enhancing characteristics and the cost constraint","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:289px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 263.00 380.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1