0 = 2,000).","Not income: the owner’s 40,000 (a contribution to {e|equity}), the {l|loan} 60,000 (a {l|liability}), and the 5,000 {l|deposit} (Lumière still has to do the work, so it is an {l|obligation} for now). On the {a|equipment}, only the {i|gain} is {i|income}: the rest of the proceeds just replace the {a|asset}.","{i|Total income} = **S$124,500**."],"id":"calc-1ie3v7","sect":4,"part":"Income and expenses"},{"type":"visual","bucket":"performance","lo":7,"title":"Lumière’s cash, by what it really is","html":"\u003cp>The calculation as a picture. The top bar is every dollar Lumière received, coloured by what it is. Only the \u003cspan class=\"el el-i\">income\u003c/span> pieces make it into the bottom bar: the owner’s money, the \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">loan\u003c/span>, the \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">deposit\u003c/span> and the part of the sale proceeds that just replaces the \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">equipment\u003c/span> all stay out.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"cbars\">\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">All cash received: \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">S$236,500\u003c/span>\u003c/strong>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003cspan class=\"seg i\" style=\"width:50.74%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg e\" style=\"width:16.91%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg l\" style=\"width:25.37%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg i\" style=\"width:1.06%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:2.96%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg i\" style=\"width:0.85%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg l\" style=\"width:2.11%\">\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">Of which \u003cspan class=\"el el-i\">income\u003c/span>: 120,000 + 2,500 + 2,000 = \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-i\">S$124,500\u003c/span>\u003c/strong>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003cspan class=\"seg i\" style=\"width:50.74%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg i\" style=\"width:1.06%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg i\" style=\"width:0.85%\">\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-key\">\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg i\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-i\">income\u003c/span>: sales, interest, the gain on the equipment\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg e\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">owner’s contribution\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg l\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">loan\u003c/span> and \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">customer deposit\u003c/span>: obligations\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg a\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">equipment carrying amount\u003c/span> swapped for cash\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n  \u003c/div>","id":"visual-i544ed","sect":4,"part":"Income and expenses"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"performance","lo":7,"q":"Which of the following transactions gives rise to income?","options":[{"t":"Issuing new ordinary shares for cash","why":"A contribution from owners: {e|equity} rises but it is excluded from {i|income}."},{"t":"Drawing down a new bank loan","why":"{a|Assets} and {l|liabilities} rise equally; {e|equity} doesn’t change."},{"t":"A supplier agreeing to waive an amount the entity owed it","ok":true,"why":"A decrease in a {l|liability} that increases {e|equity}, not from owners: {i|income}."},{"t":"Buying a machine for cash","why":"One {a|asset} swaps for another; {e|equity} doesn’t change."}],"id":"mcq-1khpjc","sect":4,"part":"Income and expenses"},{"type":"examtypes","id":"examtypes","bucket":"wrap","sect":5,"part":"Wrap-up"},{"type":"summary","lo":7,"title":"A2 on one page","rows":[["Fundamental QCs?","Relevance and faithful representation. Need both."],["Parts of relevance?","Predictive value, confirmatory value. Materiality is its entity-specific side; no fixed threshold."],["Parts of faithful representation?","Complete, neutral (supported by prudence), free from error. Substance over form."],["Prudence?","Caution under uncertainty. Supports neutrality; never deliberate understatement."],["Enhancing QCs?","Comparability, understandability, timeliness, verifiability (CUT V)."],["Verifiability means?","Knowledgeable, independent observers could reach consensus that it is faithful."],["Consistency?","Helps achieve comparability; not a QC."],["The constraint?","Cost: benefits must justify costs, judged for reporting in general by standard setters."],["{a|Asset}?","Present economic resource controlled as a result of past events (a right with potential to produce benefits)."],["{l|Liability}?","Present obligation to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events."],["{e|Equity}?","The residual interest in {a|assets} after deducting all {l|liabilities}."],["{i|Income} vs contribution?","{i|Income} = {e|equity} increase **not** from owners; {e|share issues} are contributions, {e|dividends} are distributions."],["Which CF chapters?","Ch 2 qualitative characteristics; Ch 4 elements (2018 Framework). The study guide’s “Ch 3” is out of date."]],"id":"summary-1xccrh","bucket":"wrap","sect":5,"part":"Wrap-up"}],"quiz":{"cases":{"pulau":{"name":"Pulau Paints","text":["Pulau Paints Pte Ltd (Pulau) manufactures industrial paint. Its finance team is preparing the financial statements for the year ended 31 December 20X4.","(a) Pulau is defending a lawsuit from a customer. The directors want to say nothing about it in the financial statements, “because the outcome is uncertain”.","(b) The CFO proposes recognising a S$2 million “{l|provision} for future hard times”, which is not linked to any obligation.","(c) Pulau has switched its {a|inventory} cost formula between FIFO and weighted average in each of the last three years.","(d) Pulau’s auditors re-performed the FIFO calculation from the purchase records and agreed that the {a|inventory} figure is a fair depiction.","(e) Pulau’s financial statements are usually published 11 months after the year end."]}},"questions":[{"id":"q1","case":"pulau","lo":4,"type":"apply-qc","q":"If Pulau says nothing about the lawsuit in (a), which qualitative characteristic is most directly undermined?","options":[{"t":"Faithful representation, because the information would not be complete","ok":true,"why":"Complete means everything a user needs, including descriptions and explanations. Uncertainty is not a reason to omit."},{"t":"Timeliness, because the lawsuit has not been decided yet","why":"Timeliness is about getting information out in time, not about leaving it out."},{"t":"Prudence, because uncertain items should be left out","pit":"prudence-qc","why":"Prudence is not a QC, and it doesn’t justify omitting information."},{"t":"Understandability, because lawsuits are complex","pit":"complex-omit","why":"Complex items are not left out for being hard to understand."}]},{"id":"q2","case":"pulau","lo":4,"type":"apply-qc","q":"How should the CFO’s proposed {l|provision} in (b) be assessed?","options":[{"t":"It is acceptable, because prudence requires a cautious approach","pit":"prudence-understate","why":"Prudence does not allow deliberately overstating {l|liabilities}."},{"t":"It breaks neutrality, and so faithful representation: prudence does not allow deliberately overstating {l|liabilities}","ok":true,"why":"A {l|provision} with no obligation is bias, not caution."},{"t":"It is acceptable if the amount is immaterial","why":"S$2 million is unlikely to be immaterial, and bias is not acceptable anyway."},{"t":"It harms comparability only","why":"The main problem is bias: neutrality, part of faithful representation."}]},{"id":"q3","case":"pulau","lo":5,"type":"comparability","q":"Which qualitative characteristic is most harmed by Pulau’s changes of cost formula in (c)?","options":[{"t":"Consistency","pit":"consistency-qc","why":"Consistency is not itself a QC. It is a means of achieving comparability."},{"t":"Comparability","ok":true,"why":"Users can’t identify similarities and differences over time when the method keeps changing."},{"t":"Verifiability","why":"Each year’s figure can still be verified. The problem is comparing years."},{"t":"Relevance","why":"The figures may still be relevant. Comparing them is the problem."}]},{"id":"q4","case":"pulau","lo":5,"type":"verifiability","q":"What does the auditors’ work in (d) illustrate?","options":[{"t":"Direct verification","why":"Direct verification is observing the item itself, such as counting {a|cash}. Re-performing a calculation from inputs is indirect."},{"t":"Indirect verification","ok":true,"why":"Checking the inputs to a model or formula and recalculating the output, as with FIFO, is indirect verification."},{"t":"Faithful representation, an enhancing characteristic","pit":"fr-enhancing","why":"Faithful representation is fundamental, not enhancing. The auditors’ consensus illustrates verifiability."},{"t":"Timeliness","why":"Nothing about when the information is available."}]},{"id":"q5","case":"pulau","lo":5,"type":"classify-qc","q":"Which statement about Pulau publishing its financial statements 11 months after the year end, in (e), is correct?","options":[{"t":"It harms timeliness, an enhancing characteristic","ok":true,"why":"Information should be available in time to influence decisions. Eleven months is a long wait."},{"t":"It harms relevance, an enhancing characteristic","pit":"enhancing-fundamental","why":"Relevance is fundamental, not enhancing. The characteristic directly at stake is timeliness."},{"t":"It harms timeliness, a fundamental characteristic","pit":"enhancing-fundamental","why":"Timeliness is enhancing."},{"t":"It has no effect, as old information keeps all its usefulness","why":"Older information generally becomes less useful, though some (trends) stays useful."}]},{"id":"q6","lo":5,"type":"verifiability","source":"Official practice paper Q1","q":"The Conceptual Framework, issued by the International Accounting Standards Board and adopted by Institute of Singapore Chartered Accountants, outlines enhancing qualitative characteristics that improve the usefulness of financial information. Which enhancing qualitative characteristic of information implies that different knowledgeable and independent observers would reach consensus regarding whether the information represents the economic phenomena it purports to represent?","options":[{"t":"Understandability","why":"Understandability is about classifying, characterising and presenting information clearly and concisely."},{"t":"Verifiability","ok":true,"why":"The Framework’s definition word for word: knowledgeable and independent observers could reach consensus."},{"t":"Comparability","why":"Comparability is about identifying similarities and differences across entities and over time."},{"t":"Faithful representation","pit":"fr-enhancing","why":"Faithful representation is a fundamental characteristic, not enhancing. Verifiability helps assure it."}]},{"id":"q7","lo":7,"type":"element-def","source":"Official practice paper Q2","q":"The IFRS conceptual framework defines the elements of financial statements. Identify which of the following is the correct definition of equity?","options":[{"t":"The present economic resource controlled by the entity as a result of past events","pit":"wrong-element","why":"That is the definition of an {a|asset}."},{"t":"The present obligation of the entity to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events","pit":"wrong-element","why":"That is the definition of a {l|liability}."},{"t":"Arises from an increase in assets or reduction in liabilities","pit":"wrong-element","why":"That is part of the definition of {i|income}."},{"t":"The residual interest in the assets of the entity after deducting all its liabilities","ok":true,"why":"The definition of {e|equity}: {a|assets} minus {l|liabilities}."}]},{"id":"q8","lo":4,"type":"apply-qc","source":"Official practice paper Q40","q":"The Conceptual Framework for Financial Reporting sets out the fundamental qualitative characteristics of financial information. Identify which of the fundamental qualitative characteristics is most directly supported by the recognition of a provision for an onerous contract in accordance with SFRS(I) 1-37 Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets?","options":[{"t":"Relevance","why":"The {l|provision} is relevant too, but recognising an {l|existing obligation} and its {x|unavoidable loss} is first about showing the entity’s position faithfully: complete and neutral."},{"t":"Faithful representation","ok":true,"why":"Recognising the {l|obligation} that exists depicts the substance of the contract completely and without bias."},{"t":"Materiality","pit":"materiality-qc","why":"Materiality is an entity-specific aspect of relevance, not a fundamental QC."},{"t":"Prudence","pit":"prudence-qc","why":"Prudence supports neutrality; it is not a qualitative characteristic."}]},{"id":"q9","lo":4,"type":"classify-qc","q":"Which of the following are the fundamental qualitative characteristics of useful financial information?","options":[{"t":"Relevance and faithful representation","ok":true,"why":"The two must-haves."},{"t":"Relevance and reliability","why":"“Reliability” is the pre-2010 name. The current Framework says faithful representation."},{"t":"Comparability and verifiability","pit":"enhancing-fundamental","why":"Both are enhancing characteristics."},{"t":"Faithful representation and prudence","pit":"prudence-qc","why":"Prudence supports neutrality; it is not a QC."}]},{"id":"q10","lo":4,"type":"apply-qc","q":"Which statement about materiality is correct?","options":[{"t":"It is a separate fundamental qualitative characteristic","pit":"materiality-qc","why":"Materiality is part of relevance."},{"t":"It is an entity-specific aspect of relevance, with no fixed threshold","ok":true,"why":"Judged on the nature and size of the item in that entity’s context."},{"t":"It is set by the Framework at 5% of profit","why":"The Framework sets no threshold."},{"t":"It is a constraint, like cost","why":"Some US GAAP texts call it a constraint; under the 2018 Framework it is part of relevance."}]},{"id":"q11","lo":6,"type":"cost","q":"Which statement best describes the cost constraint?","options":[{"t":"It is an enhancing qualitative characteristic","pit":"cost-qc","why":"Cost is a constraint, not a QC."},{"t":"Each company decides which standards to apply based on its own costs","pit":"cost-qc","why":"The judgement is made for reporting in general, mainly by standard setters."},{"t":"The benefits of reporting information must justify the costs of providing and using it, a judgement made mainly by standard setters","ok":true,"why":"That is the cost constraint."},{"t":"Information must be produced at the lowest possible cost, even if it is less relevant","why":"Cost limits what is reported; it doesn’t override relevance and faithful representation."}]},{"id":"q12","lo":7,"type":"asset-liability","q":"A software company says its most valuable asset is its team of engineers, who are on standard employment contracts with one month’s notice. Does the team meet the definition of an asset?","options":[{"t":"Yes, because the team is expected to generate future economic benefits","pit":"old-definition","why":"Potential benefits are not enough: the entity must also control the resource."},{"t":"No, because the company does not control the engineers’ skills: they can leave and take them elsewhere","ok":true,"why":"Control is the present ability to direct the use and obtain the benefits. The company doesn’t have it."},{"t":"No, because the benefits are not certain","why":"The 2018 definition doesn’t require benefits to be certain or even likely."},{"t":"Yes, because salaries are paid each month","why":"Paying salaries doesn’t create control over the people’s skills."}]},{"id":"q13","lo":7,"type":"income-or-not","q":"Which of the following is income under the Conceptual Framework?","options":[{"t":"Cash received from issuing new shares","pit":"owner-income","why":"A contribution from holders of equity claims ({e|share capital}) is excluded from {i|income}."},{"t":"Cash received from a new bank loan","pit":"loan-income","why":"{a|Assets} and {l|liabilities} rise together; {e|equity} doesn’t change."},{"t":"Fees earned from services provided to a customer","ok":true,"why":"An increase in {a|assets} that raises {e|equity}, not from owners: {i|income}."},{"t":"Cash received when a customer pays an invoice already recorded as a receivable","why":"One {a|asset} ({a|receivable}) swaps for another ({a|cash}). The {i|income} was recognised when the service was provided."}]},{"id":"q14","lo":7,"type":"asset-liability","q":"Which of the following meets the definition of a liability at 31 December 20X4?","options":[{"t":"A board decision in December to buy a new machine in March 20X5","pit":"plan-liability","why":"A plan is not an obligation: the entity can still avoid the payment."},{"t":"A refund policy published on the website and always honoured, for goods sold in 20X4","ok":true,"why":"A {l|constructive obligation} from past sales that the entity has no practical ability to avoid."},{"t":"Share capital issued in 20X4","why":"{e|Share capital} is {e|equity}, not a {l|liability}."},{"t":"Expected staff salaries for 20X5","pit":"plan-liability","why":"No past event yet: the service hasn’t been received."}]},{"id":"q15","lo":5,"type":"classify-qc","q":"Which statement about the enhancing qualitative characteristics is correct?","options":[{"t":"They can make information useful even if it is not relevant","why":"Enhancing characteristics cannot make irrelevant or unfaithful information useful."},{"t":"They include comparability, understandability, timeliness and verifiability, and may be traded off against each other","ok":true,"why":"CUT V, and trade-offs between them are allowed (e.g. timeliness against verifiability)."},{"t":"They include faithful representation","pit":"fr-enhancing","why":"Faithful representation is fundamental."},{"t":"They include consistency and prudence","pit":"consistency-qc","why":"Neither is a QC. Consistency helps comparability; prudence supports neutrality."}]}]},"exam":{"cases":{"kopi":{"name":"Kopi Start","text":["Kopi Start Pte Ltd (Kopi Start) began trading on 1 January 20X1. Its transactions for the year ended 31 December 20X1 were:","1. The founders paid S$100,000 for ordinary shares.","2. It borrowed S$80,000 from a bank, and repaid S$20,000 of it in November.","3. It bought coffee equipment for S$60,000 cash (ignore depreciation).","4. Cash sales were S$150,000 and cash operating expenses were S$110,000.","5. It paid a dividend of S$15,000 to its shareholders.","6. In December the board approved a plan to buy a S$30,000 coffee roaster in March 20X2. No order has been placed.","All transactions were in cash. There were no other assets or liabilities."]}},"questions":[{"id":"x1","case":"kopi","lo":7,"type":"income-or-not","q":"What is Kopi Start’s profit for the year ended 31 December 20X1?","options":[{"t":"S$25,000","pit":"owner-income","why":"This treats the {e|dividend} as an {x|expense}. A distribution to owners never goes through profit."},{"t":"S$40,000","ok":true,"why":"{i|Income} 150,000 − {x|expenses} 110,000 = 40,000. The {e|share issue}, {l|loan}, {l|repayment}, {a|equipment} and {e|dividend} are not {i|income} or {x|expenses}."},{"t":"S$140,000","pit":"owner-income","why":"This counts the founders’ {e|share capital} as {i|income}. It is a contribution from owners."},{"t":"S$120,000","pit":"loan-income","why":"This counts the {l|bank loan} as {i|income}. A {l|loan} raises {a|assets} and {l|liabilities} equally."}]},{"id":"x2","case":"kopi","lo":7,"type":"element-def","q":"What is Kopi Start’s equity at 31 December 20X1?","options":[{"t":"S$185,000","pit":"wrong-element","why":"That is total {a|assets}. {e|Equity} is the residual after deducting {l|liabilities}."},{"t":"S$140,000","why":"This forgets the {e|dividend}, which reduces {e|equity} directly."},{"t":"S$125,000","ok":true,"why":"{a|Assets} ({a|cash} 125,000 + {a|equipment} 60,000 = 185,000) − {l|loan} 60,000 = {e|125,000}. Check: {e|capital} 100,000 + profit 40,000 − {e|dividend} 15,000."},{"t":"S$100,000","why":"{e|Share capital} alone ignores the profit and the {e|dividend}."}]},{"id":"x3","case":"kopi","lo":7,"type":"income-or-not","q":"Which journal entry records the dividend paid by Kopi Start?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Dividend expense (P&L)","amount":15000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":15000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"owner-income","why":"A {e|dividend} is a distribution to owners, not an {x|expense}. It never goes through profit or loss."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":15000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":15000,"el":"a"}],"ok":true,"why":"{e|Equity} falls directly; {a|cash} falls. A distribution to holders of equity claims."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":15000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":15000,"el":"e"}],"why":"Backwards: paying a {e|dividend} reduces {a|cash} and {e|equity}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Share capital","amount":15000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":15000,"el":"a"}],"why":"Dividends are paid out of {e|retained earnings}, not by reducing {e|share capital}."}]},{"id":"x4","case":"kopi","lo":7,"type":"income-or-not","q":"Which of Kopi Start’s transactions increased its total assets without being either income or a contribution from owners?","options":[{"t":"The share issue (1)","pit":"owner-income","why":"It did increase {a|assets}, but it is a contribution from owners ({e|share capital})."},{"t":"The bank loan drawn down (2)","ok":true,"why":"{a|Assets} and {l|liabilities} rose equally: neither income nor a contribution."},{"t":"The purchase of equipment (3)","why":"One {a|asset} swapped for another: total {a|assets} didn’t change."},{"t":"The cash sales (4)","why":"{i|Sales} increased {a|assets}, but they are {i|income}."}]},{"id":"x5","case":"kopi","lo":7,"type":"asset-liability","q":"How should the planned purchase of the coffee roaster (6) be treated at 31 December 20X1?","options":[{"t":"As a liability of S$30,000, because the board has approved it","pit":"plan-liability","why":"A board plan is not an obligation: Kopi Start can still avoid the payment."},{"t":"As an asset of S$30,000, because it will produce economic benefits","pit":"plan-liability","why":"No past event yet: nothing has been bought or contracted."},{"t":"Neither an asset nor a liability: there is no past event and no present obligation","ok":true,"why":"A decision to buy in future is only an intention."},{"t":"As a reduction of equity, because the cash is committed","why":"Nothing is committed. {e|Equity} is unaffected."}]},{"id":"x6","lo":4,"type":"apply-qc","q":"In what order does the Conceptual Framework apply the fundamental qualitative characteristics?","options":[{"t":"Find the information that can be faithfully represented, then decide if it is relevant","why":"Backwards. Relevance comes first; faithful representation is the test."},{"t":"Identify the useful economic phenomenon, find the most relevant information about it, then check whether it can be faithfully represented","ok":true,"why":"If it can’t, use the next most relevant type of information."},{"t":"Apply the enhancing characteristics first, then the fundamental ones","why":"Enhancing characteristics only improve information that already meets the fundamentals."},{"t":"Apply materiality first, then prudence","pit":"prudence-qc","why":"Neither is a fundamental QC."}]},{"id":"x7","lo":4,"type":"apply-qc","q":"A company recognises a {l|provision} for a lawsuit using its best estimate and explains the key assumptions in the notes. The final settlement is 15% higher. Which statement is correct?","options":[{"t":"The {l|provision} was not free from error, because the amount turned out wrong","pit":"error-free-exact","why":"Free from error doesn’t mean perfectly accurate. A clearly described estimate can be faithful."},{"t":"The {l|provision} should have been omitted because of measurement uncertainty","why":"Measurement uncertainty doesn’t stop information being useful. Omitting it would make the FS incomplete."},{"t":"The {l|provision} could still be a faithful representation, as it was a clearly described estimate properly made","ok":true,"why":"Free from error means no errors in the description or the process."},{"t":"The company should have provided double the estimate to be prudent","pit":"prudence-understate","why":"Prudence does not allow deliberately overstating {l|liabilities}."}]},{"id":"x8","lo":5,"type":"classify-qc","q":"Which of the following lists only enhancing qualitative characteristics?","options":[{"t":"Comparability, consistency, timeliness, verifiability","pit":"consistency-qc","why":"Consistency helps achieve comparability, but is not a QC."},{"t":"Comparability, understandability, timeliness, verifiability","ok":true,"why":"CUT V."},{"t":"Relevance, understandability, timeliness, verifiability","pit":"enhancing-fundamental","why":"Relevance is fundamental."},{"t":"Comparability, understandability, faithful representation, verifiability","pit":"fr-enhancing","why":"Faithful representation is fundamental."}]},{"id":"x9","lo":7,"type":"element-def","q":"Which statement about the elements under the 2018 Conceptual Framework is correct?","options":[{"t":"A liability exists only if an outflow of resources is probable","pit":"old-definition","why":"Pre-2018 wording. Probability is no longer in the definitions; uncertainty is dealt with at recognition and measurement."},{"t":"Income includes all increases in equity, including contributions from shareholders","pit":"owner-income","why":"Contributions from holders of equity claims are excluded from {i|income}."},{"t":"An obligation is a duty or responsibility that the entity has no practical ability to avoid, and may be legal or constructive","ok":true,"why":"The Framework’s notion of an obligation."},{"t":"An asset is a future economic benefit expected to flow to the entity","pit":"old-definition","why":"Pre-2018 wording. An {a|asset} is now a present economic resource: a right with the potential to produce benefits."}]},{"id":"x10","lo":5,"type":"verifiability","q":"A listed company can publish its results three weeks after year end with lighter checks, or ten weeks after with every figure fully verified. Which statement is correct?","options":[{"t":"This is a trade-off between two enhancing characteristics: timeliness and verifiability","ok":true,"why":"The Framework allows enhancing characteristics to be balanced against each other."},{"t":"This is a trade-off between relevance and faithful representation, which the Framework allows freely","why":"The fundamentals are both required. The trade-off here is between enhancing characteristics."},{"t":"Verifiability is fundamental, so the company must always wait for full verification","pit":"enhancing-fundamental","why":"Verifiability is enhancing."},{"t":"Timeliness is a constraint, like cost","pit":"cost-qc","why":"Timeliness is an enhancing characteristic. Cost is the only constraint."}]}]},"prepared":true},{"id":"measurement-bases","code":"B1.0","title":"Measurement Bases","subtitle":"Conceptual Framework Ch 6 · SFRS(I) 13 fair value","los":{"8":"Measurement bases and methods for major classes of assets"},"accounts":[{"name":"Plant and equipment","el":"a"},{"name":"Accumulated depreciation","el":"a"},{"name":"Inventory","el":"a"},{"name":"Investment property","el":"a"},{"name":"Cash","el":"a"},{"name":"Depreciation expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Impairment loss","el":"x"},{"name":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","el":"e"}],"steps":[{"id":"menu","name":"The menu: cost or current value","short":"The menu"},{"id":"hc","name":"Historical cost","short":"Hist. cost"},{"id":"fv","name":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)","short":"Fair value"},{"id":"viu","name":"Value in use and current cost","short":"VIU & CC"},{"id":"choose","name":"Which basis for which asset","short":"Which basis"}],"map":{"menu":{"label":"Historical cost vs three current values"},"hc":{"label":"Price + transaction costs, then depreciation"},"fv":{"label":"Exit price: market, costs, HBU, hierarchy","star":"Transaction costs choose the market but are never deducted"},"viu":{"label":"Entity’s own discounted cash flows; current cost","star":"Value in use is entity-specific, fair value is the market’s view"},"choose":{"label":"Each asset class picks its basis"}},"preamble":["Welcome to the gateway of Section B. Before we meet {a|inventory}, {a|PPE}, {a|investment property} and {a|financial assets} one by one, we need the **menu** they all order from: the ways an asset can be measured.","Here is the route:","1. First, **the menu**: historical cost, or one of three current values. One espresso machine, four honest numbers.","2. Then **historical cost**: what you paid, plus transaction costs, less wear and tear. Comfortable and familiar.","3. Next, **fair value** under SFRS(I) 13: an exit price, which market to use, which costs to knock off, highest and best use and the three-level hierarchy. ★ special","4. Then **value in use** (the entity’s own discounted cash flows) and **current cost**. ★ special: this is where practice Q7 lives.","5. Last stop: **which basis for which asset**. It doubles as the road map for B1.1 to B1.4 and B3.","+ The big idea: cost looks **back**, fair value looks **out** to the market, value in use looks **in** at the entity’s own plans."],"intros":{"menu":"First stop: the menu. Every asset needs a number, and the Conceptual Framework offers four ways to get one. We’ll put all four on a single espresso machine, so you can see they really are different questions with different answers.","hc":"Now that the menu is on the table, start with the dish you know: **historical cost**. What was paid, transaction costs included, then worn down by {x|depreciation}. Pretty vanilla, and that is exactly its appeal (and its flaw: it goes stale).","fv":"Time for the star of the show. ★ **Fair value** under SFRS(I) 13 is where most of the exam traps live: it is a **selling** price, it is the **market’s** view, you value it in the **principal** market, and transaction costs never come off it. Then two extras: highest and best use for things like land, and the Level 1–3 hierarchy.","viu":"We’ve seen the market’s view. Now the entity’s own. ★ **Value in use** is the present value of what *this* entity expects to earn from using the asset and disposing of it. Mix it up with fair value and practice Q7 bites you. We finish with **current cost**, the entry-price cousin of historical cost.","choose":"We have the whole menu. Last stop: who orders what? Each asset class picks the basis that tells users the most, and this chart is your table of contents for the asset chapters that follow (B1.1 to B1.4, then B3 impairment, where value in use makes a comeback)."},"pitfalls":{"viu-vs-fv":"Confusing value in use (the entity’s own discounted cash flows) with fair value (the market’s exit price).","entry-price":"Using the price paid to buy (an entry price) as fair value.","deduct-tc":"Deducting transaction costs in measuring fair value.","forget-transport":"Forgetting to deduct transport costs when location is a characteristic of the asset.","wrong-market":"Using the most advantageous market when a principal market exists.","current-use":"Valuing a non-financial asset at its current use instead of its highest and best use.","level1-similar":"Putting inputs in the wrong hierarchy level (e.g. calling prices of similar assets Level 1).","inv-at-fv":"Saying {a|inventory} is measured at fair value (it is the lower of cost and NRV, and NRV is entity-specific).","hc-no-tc":"Leaving transaction costs (delivery, installation, legal fees) out of historical cost or current cost.","undiscounted":"Adding up future cash flows without discounting them for value in use.","ra-lower":"Taking the lower of FVLCOD and value in use as recoverable amount (it is the higher).","fvlcod-no-costs":"Using fair value instead of fair value less costs of disposal for recoverable amount."},"examTypes":[{"id":"define-basis","title":"Which measurement basis does this definition describe? (practice Q7)","pitfall":"Calling value in use \"fair value\": fair value is a market selling price, not the entity’s forecast.","see":"viu"},{"id":"hc-amount","title":"What is the asset’s historical cost carrying amount?","pitfall":"Leaving delivery, installation or legal fees out of {a|cost}.","see":"hc"},{"id":"fv-markets","title":"An asset trades in two markets: what is its fair value?","pitfall":"Deducting transaction costs, or skipping the principal market.","see":"fv"},{"id":"hbu","title":"Land is used one way but zoned for another: fair value?","pitfall":"Valuing at current use only.","see":"fv"},{"id":"hierarchy","title":"Which level of the fair value hierarchy?","pitfall":"Calling prices of similar assets Level 1 (Level 1 needs identical assets).","see":"fv"},{"id":"viu-calc","title":"What is the asset’s value in use?","pitfall":"Forgetting to discount, or leaving out the disposal proceeds.","see":"viu"},{"id":"current-cost","title":"What is the asset’s current cost?","pitfall":"Leaving out today’s transaction costs, or giving the selling price.","see":"viu"},{"id":"which-basis","title":"Which basis does inventory / PPE / IP / a financial asset use?","pitfall":"Saying {a|inventory} is at fair value, or {a|IP} {i|gains} go to OCI.","see":"choose"},{"id":"recoverable","title":"What is the recoverable amount?","pitfall":"Taking the lower of FVLCOD and value in use, or forgetting costs of disposal.","see":"choose"}],"pages":[{"type":"map","id":"map-overview","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"intro","lo":8,"title":"Measurement bases, the Socratic way","body":["Every asset on a balance sheet needs a number. This chapter is about **which** number: what it cost, what the market would pay, or what it is worth to the entity.","Each idea starts with a question. Think it through, then reveal the answer. The rule comes after, once you have half-worked it out.","Account names are coloured by element. Tap **Legend** at the top any time. At the end: a chapter quiz in exam style, then a harder exam."],"id":"intro-11yhr9","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"map","id":"map-1-menu","bucket":"menu","sect":1,"part":"The menu: cost or current value"},{"type":"think","bucket":"menu","lo":8,"scenario":"Tiong Bahru Kopi bought an espresso machine on 1 Jan 20X1 for $18,000, plus $2,000 delivery and installation. At 31 Dec 20X3 the owner jots down some facts: a second-hand dealer would pay $7,000 for it; the cash the shop expects to make from using it for two more years, discounted to today, is $9,500; and a three-year-old machine of the same model would cost $8,500 to buy today, plus $600 delivery.","prompt":"How many different \"values\" could you put on this one machine? What question does each one answer?","answer":["At least four. **What did we pay?** $20,000, less three years of wear: that is **historical cost**.","**What would the market pay us for it?** $7,000: that is **fair value**.","**What is it worth to us, the way we use it?** $9,500: that is **value in use**.","**What would it cost to get an equivalent one today?** $8,500 + $600 = $9,100: that is **current cost**.","Same machine, four honest answers. Accounting has to pick one, and that is what this chapter is about."],"id":"think-s093mp","sect":1,"part":"The menu: cost or current value"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"menu","lo":8,"title":"The menu: four measurement bases","body":["The espresso machine had four honest values. The Conceptual Framework turns them into a menu of measurement bases, and each standard picks from it.","The Conceptual Framework (Ch 6) offers two families of measurement basis:","1. **Historical cost**: the cost when the asset was acquired, including transaction costs. It looks **back**.","2. **Current value**: updated to conditions at the measurement date. It comes in three flavours:","- **Fair value**: the price to **sell** to market participants (a market view).","- **Value in use**: the present value of the cash flows **this entity** expects from using the asset and disposing of it. For {l|liabilities} the twin is **fulfilment value**.","- **Current cost**: what it would cost **today** to get an equivalent asset, plus transaction costs.","+ Historical cost is simple and verifiable but goes stale. Current values are more relevant but need more estimates (measurement uncertainty)."],"id":"rule-uu3onz","sect":1,"part":"The menu: cost or current value"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"menu","lo":8,"title":"Historical cost vs the three current values","intro":"This is the chart from your notes. One root, two families, and the current-value family has three children.","notes":{"root":"The Conceptual Framework’s menu. Each standard picks from it the basis that gives the most useful information for that kind of asset.","hc":"What was paid, plus transaction costs. Afterwards only {x|depreciation} and {x|impairment} change it. An **entry** value: the price to get in.","cv":"Updated at every measurement date, so it reflects conditions **now**.","fv":"The price to **sell** in an orderly transaction between market participants. Market view, not the entity’s own.","viu":"Present value of **this entity’s** expected cash flows from use and ultimate disposal. Entity-specific.","cc":"The cost of an **equivalent** asset today, plus transaction costs. Like historical cost, but at today’s prices: an entry value."},"walk":[{"nodes":["root"],"text":"Every asset needs a number. The Framework gives a menu."},{"nodes":["hc"],"text":"**Historical cost** looks back: what we paid to get it, transaction costs included."},{"nodes":["cv"],"text":"**Current value** looks at today. Three ways to do that:"},{"nodes":["fv"],"text":"**Fair value**: what the **market** would pay us to take it off our hands (exit price)."},{"nodes":["viu"],"text":"**Value in use**: what it is worth to **us**, the way we will use it. Its {l|liability} twin is fulfilment value."},{"nodes":["cc"],"text":"**Current cost**: what an equivalent would cost to buy **today** (entry price)."}],"caption":"Cost looks back; fair value looks out to the market; value in use looks at the entity’s own plans.","id":"diagram-8w80b6","sect":1,"part":"The menu: cost or current value","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:531px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 483.00 183.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 172.08)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-172.08 471.7,-172.08 471.7,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>root\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M201.03,-161.28C201.03,-161.28 84.75,-161.28 84.75,-161.28 78.75,-161.28 72.75,-155.28 72.75,-149.28 72.75,-149.28 72.75,-137.28 72.75,-137.28 72.75,-131.28 78.75,-125.28 84.75,-125.28 84.75,-125.28 201.03,-125.28 201.03,-125.28 207.03,-125.28 213.03,-131.28 213.03,-137.28 213.03,-137.28 213.03,-149.28 213.03,-149.28 213.03,-155.28 207.03,-161.28 201.03,-161.28\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"90.76\" y=\"-143.83\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Measurement bases\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"81.39\" y=\"-134.73\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">(Conceptual Framework Ch 6)\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>hc\u003c/ti