.72 127.19,-43.03 128.86,-39.18\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node5\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>viu\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M281.15,-37.64C281.15,-37.64 156.62,-37.64 156.62,-37.64 150.62,-37.64 144.62,-31.64 144.62,-25.64 144.62,-25.64 144.62,-12 144.62,-12 144.62,-6 150.62,0 156.62,0 156.62,0 281.15,0 281.15,0 287.15,0 293.15,-6 293.15,-12 293.15,-12 293.15,-25.64 293.15,-25.64 293.15,-31.64 287.15,-37.64 281.15,-37.64\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"187.39\" y=\"-23.87\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Value in use\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"153.26\" y=\"-14.77\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">PV of the entity&#39;s own cash flows\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"164.89\" y=\"-5.77\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">(liabilities: fulfilment value)\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge4\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>cv&#45;&gt;viu\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M218.89,-62.27C218.89,-56.99 218.89,-51.13 218.89,-45.48\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"220.99,-45.51 218.89,-39.51 216.79,-45.51 220.99,-45.51\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node6\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>cc\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M448.9,-37.64C448.9,-37.64 322.88,-37.64 322.88,-37.64 316.88,-37.64 310.88,-31.64 310.88,-25.64 310.88,-25.64 310.88,-12 310.88,-12 310.88,-6 316.88,0 322.88,0 322.88,0 448.9,0 448.9,0 454.9,0 460.9,-6 460.9,-12 460.9,-12 460.9,-25.64 460.9,-25.64 460.9,-31.64 454.9,-37.64 448.9,-37.64\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"352.89\" y=\"-23.87\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Current cost\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"319.51\" y=\"-14.77\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">cost of an equivalent asset today\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"322.14\" y=\"-5.77\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">+ transaction costs · entry value\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge5\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>cv&#45;&gt;cc\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M269.45,-62.1C288,-55.36 309.14,-47.69 328.27,-40.74\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"328.87,-42.76 333.79,-38.74 327.44,-38.81 328.87,-42.76\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"menu","lo":8,"q":"Which pair of measurement bases are both **entry** values (the price to acquire, not the price to sell)?","options":[{"t":"Fair value and value in use","why":"Both are exit-type values: fair value is a selling price, value in use is the benefit of using then disposing."},{"t":"Historical cost and current cost","ok":true,"why":"Both measure what it costs to **get** the asset, then (historical cost) or now (current cost), transaction costs included."},{"t":"Historical cost and fair value","why":"Fair value is an exit price: the price to **sell**."},{"t":"Current cost and fair value","why":"Current cost is an entry value, but fair value is the price to sell."}],"id":"mcq-351uyo","sect":1,"part":"The menu: cost or current value"},{"type":"map","id":"map-2-hc","bucket":"hc","sect":2,"part":"Historical cost"},{"id":"think-1u13yx","type":"think","bucket":"hc","lo":8,"scenario":"Tiong Bahru Kopi’s espresso machine, bought on 1 Jan 20X1: price $18,000, delivery and installation $2,000, 5-year life, no residual value, straight-line.","prompt":"What is its historical cost on day 1? And three years later, does \"historical cost\" still mean the same figure?","answer":["Day 1: price + the costs of getting it ready = 18,000 + 2,000 = **$20,000**. Transaction costs are part of cost.","Three years later the cost is still 20,000, but the asset is carried at cost **less what has been used up**: {x|depreciation} (and any {x|impairment}). The market price of espresso machines can do what it likes; historical cost does not care."],"sect":2,"part":"Historical cost"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"hc","lo":8,"title":"Historical cost","body":["First item on the menu, and the one you will meet most: what the asset cost. It is fixed on day 1 and only moves for a few reasons.","> Historical cost = consideration paid + transaction costs","After day 1 it is updated only for:","- **{x|depreciation}** (or {x|amortisation}) of the part used up","- **{x|impairment}**, if the asset can no longer earn back its {a|carrying amount}","- for {a|financial assets} at **amortised cost**: {i|interest} accrued and {a|cash} received","+ Strengths: simple, cheap and verifiable (there is an invoice). Weakness: it goes **out of date**. A building bought in 1995 still shows its 1995 price.","! Transaction costs are **in** historical cost (and current cost). Forgetting delivery, installation or legal fees understates {a|cost}."],"id":"rule-18j3lv","sect":2,"part":"Historical cost"},{"id":"calc-besy37","type":"calc","bucket":"hc","lo":8,"q":"Tiong Bahru Kopi bought an espresso machine on 1 Jan 20X1 for $18,000, plus $2,000 delivery and installation. It is depreciated straight-line over 5 years with no residual value. At 31 Dec 20X3 a second-hand dealer would pay $7,000 for it. What is its {a|carrying amount} under historical cost at 31 Dec 20X3?","answer":8000,"unit":"$","working":["{a|Cost} = 18,000 + 2,000 = 20,000.","{x|Depreciation} = 20,000 ÷ 5 = 4,000 a year.","{a|Carrying amount} = {a|cost} 20,000 − {a|accumulated depreciation} 3 × 4,000 = **$8,000**. The dealer’s $7,000 offer doesn’t change it."],"sect":2,"part":"Historical cost"},{"type":"visual","bucket":"hc","lo":8,"title":"Historical cost, year by year","html":"\u003cp>The espresso machine’s \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">carrying amount\u003c/span> rolled forward from 1 Jan 20X1, twice: once done right, once with the installation forgotten. Step through the years and watch what the market price does to it (nothing).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wg\" data-wg=\"roll\" data-key=\"hc-b10\" data-cfg=\"{&quot;target&quot;:{&quot;v&quot;:7000,&quot;label&quot;:&quot;dealer offer 7,000&quot;},&quot;panels&quot;:[{&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Installation capitalised&quot;,&quot;sub&quot;:&quot;cost 18,000 + 2,000&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;a&quot;,&quot;bal&quot;:&quot;Carrying amount&quot;,&quot;start&quot;:20000,&quot;startCap&quot;:&quot;1 Jan 20X1: {a|cost} = price 18,000 + installation 2,000 = {a|20,000}.&quot;,&quot;years&quot;:[{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;20X1&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;depreciation&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:-4000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;20X1: {x|depreciation} = 20,000 ÷ 5 = {x|4,000}, so {a|carrying amount} = {a|16,000}.&quot;}]},{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;20X2&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;depreciation&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:-4000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;20X2: {x|depreciation} = 20,000 ÷ 5 = {x|4,000}, so {a|carrying amount} = {a|12,000}.&quot;}]},{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;20X3&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;depreciation&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:-4000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;20X3: {x|depreciation} = 20,000 ÷ 5 = {x|4,000}, so {a|carrying amount} = {a|8,000}.&quot;}]}]},{&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Installation forgotten (trap)&quot;,&quot;sub&quot;:&quot;cost 18,000 only&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;a&quot;,&quot;bal&quot;:&quot;Carrying amount&quot;,&quot;start&quot;:18000,&quot;startCap&quot;:&quot;1 Jan 20X1: installation left out, so {a|cost} = {a|18,000}, already 2,000 too low.&quot;,&quot;years&quot;:[{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;20X1&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;depreciation&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:-3600,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;20X1: {x|depreciation} = 18,000 ÷ 5 = {x|3,600}, so {a|carrying amount} = {a|14,400}.&quot;}]},{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;20X2&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;depreciation&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:-3600,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;20X2: {x|depreciation} = 18,000 ÷ 5 = {x|3,600}, so {a|carrying amount} = {a|10,800}.&quot;}]},{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;20X3&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;depreciation&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:-3600,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;20X3: {x|depreciation} = 18,000 ÷ 5 = {x|3,600}, so {a|carrying amount} = {a|7,200}.&quot;}]}]}],&quot;notes&quot;:{&quot;start&quot;:&quot;Same machine, 5-year life, straight-line, no residual value. The only difference: was the 2,000 installation put into {a|cost}? Press Play or Next.&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[&quot;Each year {x|depreciation} uses up one fifth of {a|cost}. The dashed line is what a dealer would pay: historical cost ignores it.&quot;],&quot;end&quot;:&quot;**The error never washes out on its own.** At 31 Dec 20X3 the right answer is {a|8,000}, the trap gives {a|7,200}, and neither moves towards the dealer’s 7,000: historical cost looks back, not at the market.&quot;}}\">\u003c/div>","id":"visual-3alnzc","sect":2,"part":"Historical cost"},{"type":"map","id":"map-3-fv","bucket":"fv","sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"id":"think-9efvbi","type":"think","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"scenario":"A company holds a parcel of {a|listed shares}. Its CFO says: \"We paid $5.00 a share, we think they are really worth $6.50 because we know the company well, and right now the market will pay $5.60 for them. To buy more today we’d pay $5.62.\"","prompt":"Which of these is the shares’ fair value? Whose view counts, and is it a buying price or a selling price?","answer":["**$5.60.** Fair value is an **exit price**: what you would **receive to sell**, not what you would pay to buy ($5.62) or what you paid ($5.00).","And it is the **market’s** view, not the CFO’s. Fair value uses the assumptions of **market participants**, so the private hunch of $6.50 doesn’t count. Sorry, CFO."],"sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"title":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)","body":["Next, the market’s view. The CFO’s four prices showed how easy it is to grab the wrong one, so SFRS(I) 13 defines fair value carefully.","**Fair value** = the price that would be **received to sell** an asset (or **paid to transfer** a {l|liability}) in an **orderly transaction** between **market participants** at the **measurement date**.","Four ideas packed in there:","1. **Exit price**: the price to sell, not to buy.","2. **Orderly transaction**: normal marketing, not a forced or fire sale.","3. **Market participants**: independent, knowledgeable, able and willing buyers and sellers. Use **their** assumptions, not the entity’s.","4. **The asset’s characteristics** count if market participants would consider them: condition, location, restrictions on sale or use.","! Fair value ignores what the entity itself plans to do with the asset. That is value in use’s job."],"id":"rule-1vlaj1","sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"type":"think","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"scenario":"The notes’ example. An asset can be sold in two markets. Market A: price $26, transaction costs $3, transport $2. Market B: price $25, transaction costs $1, transport $2.","prompt":"Which market do you value it in? And when you take its price, which of the costs do you knock off?","answer":["First, the **principal market** (the one with the most volume and activity for this asset). If A is the principal market, fair value = 26 − 2 transport = **$24**, even though B would net more.","Only if there is **no** principal market do you go hunting for the **most advantageous** market, comparing what you would net after **both** costs: A 26 − 3 − 2 = 21; B 25 − 1 − 2 = 22. B wins.","Then fair value in B = 25 − 2 = **$23**. Transaction costs help **choose** the market, but are **not deducted** from fair value. Transport is."],"id":"think-tsim4g","sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"title":"Which market, and which costs","body":["Fair value is a price in a market, but an asset may trade in more than one, and selling costs money. This rule settles which market and which costs.","1. Use the **principal market**: the market with the greatest volume and level of activity for the asset.","2. If there is no principal market, use the **most advantageous market**: the one that gives the best amount after transaction **and** transport costs.","3. Take the price in that market and deduct **transport costs** (if location is a characteristic of the asset).","> Fair value = price in the chosen market − transport costs   (not transaction costs)","- **Transaction costs are not deducted**: they belong to the transaction, not the asset. They are only used to pick the most advantageous market.","! The classic trap: deducting transaction costs from fair value. They choose the market; they never reduce the price."],"id":"rule-1sl071","sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"title":"Fair value in two moves: pick the market, then the costs","intro":"The rule as a chart. Use it with the notes’ A/B example: tap the walkthrough.","notes":{"start":"Any asset or liability measured at fair value: {a|shares}, an {a|investment property}, a {a|commodity}.","q":"The principal market always wins if there is one, even if another market would pay more.","pm":"Notes example: if A is principal, fair value = 26 − 2 = 24.","mam":"Compare the **net** amounts: A 21, B 22. B is most advantageous.","price":"The quoted or observed price in the chosen market.","tr":"Getting the asset to the market is a real cost of its **location**, so it comes off.","tc":"Transaction costs belong to the **deal**, not the asset. They help choose a market but never reduce fair value.","fv":"No principal market: 25 − 2 = 23."},"walk":[{"nodes":["start","q"],"text":"First question: is there a **principal market** for this asset?"},{"nodes":["pm","price"],"text":"Yes → use it, full stop. If A is principal: price 26."},{"nodes":["mam"],"text":"No → find the **most advantageous** market by comparing what you’d net after both costs: A 21, B 22."},{"nodes":["tr","fv"],"text":"Take that market’s price and deduct **transport** only: 25 − 2 = 23."},{"nodes":["tc"],"text":"Transaction costs never come off fair value. They only helped choose the market."}],"caption":"Transaction costs choose; transport costs reduce.","id":"diagram-1mi5i3","sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:385px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 350.00 396.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 385.44)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-385.44 339.45,-385.44 339.45,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>start\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M181.9,-374.64C181.9,-374.64 97.12,-374.64 97.12,-374.64 91.12,-374.64 85.12,-368.64 85.12,-362.64 85.12,-362.64 85.12,-350.64 85.12,-350.64 85.12,-344.64 91.12,-338.64 97.12,-338.64 97.12,-338.64 181.9,-338.64 181.9,-338.64 187.9,-338.64 193.9,-344.64 193.9,-350.64 193.9,-350.64 193.9,-362.64 193.9,-362.64 193.9,-368.64 187.9,-374.64 181.9,-374.64\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"93.76\" y=\"-357.19\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Asset to measure\u003