,-43.32\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node7\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>tc\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#ffe4e6\" stroke=\"#be123c\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M316.65,-110.75C316.65,-110.75 230.38,-110.75 230.38,-110.75 224.38,-110.75 218.38,-104.75 218.38,-98.75 218.38,-98.75 218.38,-86.75 218.38,-86.75 218.38,-80.75 224.38,-74.75 230.38,-74.75 230.38,-74.75 316.65,-74.75 316.65,-74.75 322.65,-74.75 328.65,-80.75 328.65,-86.75 328.65,-86.75 328.65,-98.75 328.65,-98.75 328.65,-104.75 322.65,-110.75 316.65,-110.75\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"227.01\" y=\"-93.3\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Transaction costs\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"243.89\" y=\"-84.2\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">NOT deducted\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge8\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>tc&#45;&gt;fv\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,2\" d=\"M257.64,-74.51C248.61,-64.71 237.19,-52.31 227.44,-41.72\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"229.15,-40.47 223.54,-37.48 226.06,-43.32 229.15,-40.47\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"253.03\" y=\"-52.25\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> ✗ \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"visual","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"title":"The two markets as bars","html":"\u003cp>The notes’ A/B example drawn to scale, per unit. Each price splits into three layers: what you would \u003cstrong>net\u003c/strong> (solid), the \u003cstrong>transaction costs\u003c/strong> (they help choose the market but stay inside fair value) and \u003cstrong>transport\u003c/strong> (hatched, it comes off). With no principal market, B wins on net (22 vs 21), and its fair value is 23.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"cbars\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">Market A: price 26 = net 21 + transaction 3 + transport 2. Fair value there = 26 − 2 = \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">24\u003c/span>\u003c/strong>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003cspan class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:80.77%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg n\" style=\"width:11.54%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg out-a\" style=\"width:7.69%\">\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">Market B: price 25 = net 22 + transaction 1 + transport 2. Fair value there = 25 − 2 = \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">23\u003c/span>\u003c/strong>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003cspan class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:84.62%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg n\" style=\"width:3.85%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg out-a\" style=\"width:7.69%\">\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-key\">\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg a\">\u003c/i>what you would net: \u003cstrong>picks\u003c/strong> the market\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg n\">\u003c/i>transaction costs: used to pick, \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> deducted\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg out-a\">\u003c/i>transport: \u003cstrong>comes off\u003c/strong> fair value\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>","id":"visual-lhhfb2","sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"id":"calc-1zzudm","type":"calc","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"q":"Changi Fresh holds 400 tonnes of frozen prawns. There is no principal market. Market P: $9,200 a tonne, transaction costs $600, transport $300. Market Q: $9,000 a tonne, transaction costs $200, transport $350. What is the fair value of the whole {a|stock}?","answer":3460000,"unit":"$","working":["Net per tonne: P = 9,200 − 600 − 300 = 8,300; Q = 9,000 − 200 − 350 = 8,450. Q is most advantageous, despite its lower price.","Fair value per tonne in Q = 9,000 − 350 transport = 8,650.","× 400 tonnes = **$3,460,000**."],"sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"id":"think-erl85l","type":"think","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"scenario":"Kallang Motors uses a plot of {a|land} as its staff car park. As a car park it would sell for about $1,800,000. The land is zoned for residential use, and developers would happily pay $2,600,000 to build flats on it. Kallang has no plans to stop using it as a car park.","prompt":"What is the land’s fair value? Does it matter that Kallang will keep parking cars on it?","answer":["**$2,600,000.** For a non-financial asset, fair value assumes its **highest and best use** by market participants, even if the entity uses it differently.","Kallang’s own plans don’t matter, because fair value is a market view. A developer bidding for the land would bid for flats, not for parking bays."],"sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"title":"Non-financial assets: highest and best use","body":["Kallang’s land showed that the entity’s own use can understate what the market would pay. For non-financial assets, fair value looks at the **best** use instead.","A **non-financial asset** ({a|land}, {a|buildings}, {a|equipment}) is valued at its **highest and best use** by market participants. That use must be:","1. **physically possible**,","2. **legally permissible** (zoning, permits), and","3. **financially feasible**.","- It applies even if the entity uses the asset differently.","! The trap is valuing at **current use** only. If the zoning allows flats and flats pay more, the car park is worth flat money."],"id":"rule-1i5r6v","sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"q":"Which of these is **not** a condition for a use to count as the highest and best use of a non-financial asset?","options":[{"t":"It is physically possible","why":"That is one of the three conditions."},{"t":"It is legally permissible","why":"That is one of the three conditions: zoning and permits matter."},{"t":"It is the use the entity intends for the asset","ok":true,"why":"The entity’s intentions are irrelevant. Highest and best use is what **market participants** would do with it."},{"t":"It is financially feasible","why":"That is one of the three conditions."}],"id":"mcq-1ghuae","sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"id":"visual-k3kw94","type":"visual","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"title":"The fair value hierarchy","html":"\u003cp>Fair value is only as good as its inputs. SFRS(I) 13 ranks those inputs in three levels, from a quoted price to the entity’s own model.\u003c/p>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl reveal-rows\">\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\u003cth>Inputs\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Level 1\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Quoted prices in \u003cstrong>active markets\u003c/strong> for \u003cstrong>identical\u003c/strong> assets\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Listed shares\u003c/span> on SGX\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Level 2\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Other \u003cstrong>observable\u003c/strong> inputs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Recent sale prices of similar buildings nearby; market interest rates\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Level 3\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Unobservable\u003c/strong> inputs (the entity’s own assumptions)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Discounted cash flow model for an unlisted company\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cp class=\"cap\">The hierarchy ranks the \u003cstrong>inputs\u003c/strong>, not the asset. The more you can see in a market, the higher the level, and the more users can trust the number.\u003c/p>","sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"fv","lo":8,"title":"Which level? Ask about the input","intro":"Two questions sort any fair value input into its level.","notes":{"inp":"The hierarchy classifies the **input**, not the asset itself.","q1":"All three words matter: **quoted**, **active** market, **identical** asset.","q2":"Anything else you can observe in a market: prices of **similar** assets, interest rates, yield curves.","l1":"Best evidence. Use it without adjustment whenever it exists.","l2":"Observable but not identical: a valuer adjusts for differences.","l3":"Unobservable: the entity’s own assumptions. Most judgement, most disclosure."},"walk":[{"nodes":["inp","q1"],"text":"Is there a **quoted** price in an **active** market for the **identical** asset?"},{"nodes":["l1"],"text":"Yes → **Level 1**. Think SGX share prices."},{"nodes":["q2","l2"],"text":"No, but something observable (a similar building’s sale price) → **Level 2**."},{"nodes":["l3"],"text":"Nothing observable, so the entity builds its own model → **Level 3**."}],"caption":"Similar is Level 2. Only identical, quoted and active is Level 1.","id":"diagram-1rk8ow","sect":3,"part":"Fair value (SFRS(I) 13)","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:359px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 326.00 269.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 257.94)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-257.94 315.33,-257.94 315.33,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>inp\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M241.78,-247.14C241.78,-247.14 152.5,-247.14 152.5,-247.14 146.5,-247.14 140.5,-241.14 140.5,-235.14 140.5,-235.14 140.5,-223.14 140.5,-223.14 140.5,-217.14 146.5,-211.14 152.5,-211.14 152.5,-211.14 241.78,-211.14 241.78,-211.14 247.78,-211.14 253.78,-217.14 253.78,-223.14 253.78,-223.14 253.78,-235.14 253.78,-235.14 253.78,-241.14 247.78,-247.14 241.78,-247.14\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"162.64\"