oss}). More in B3."},{"t":"$500,000","why":"That is the {a|carrying amount}. It is above recoverable amount, so the asset is impaired."},{"t":"$435,000","why":"There is no averaging: take the higher of the two."}],"sect":5,"part":"Which basis for which asset"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"choose","lo":8,"q":"Which statement about measurement after initial recognition is correct?","options":[{"t":"{a|Inventory} is written down to fair value when fair value falls below cost","why":"Inventory uses **net realisable value**, which is entity-specific, not fair value."},{"t":"{a|Investment property} under the fair value model is also depreciated","why":"No {x|depreciation} under the fair value model."},{"t":"An {a|intangible asset} can be revalued only if there is an active market for it","ok":true,"why":"SFRS(I) 1-38 allows the revaluation model only with an active market."},{"t":"{a|Financial assets} are always measured at fair value","why":"Many are at **amortised cost** (held to collect, SPPI)."}],"id":"mcq-1oadqh","sect":5,"part":"Which basis for which asset"},{"type":"examtypes","id":"examtypes","bucket":"wrap","sect":6,"part":"Wrap-up"},{"type":"summary","lo":8,"title":"Measurement bases on one page","rows":[["The four bases?","Historical cost; current value = fair value, value in use (fulfilment value for {l|liabilities}), current cost."],["Historical cost?","Price paid + transaction costs; later only {x|depreciation} and {x|impairment} (and {i|interest} for amortised cost)."],["Fair value?","Price to **sell** in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date. Exit price, market view."],["Which market?","Principal market; if none, the most advantageous (best net of transaction and transport costs)."],["Costs in fair value?","Transport deducted (if location matters). Transaction costs **not** deducted."],["Non-financial assets?","Highest and best use: physically possible, legally permissible, financially feasible."],["Hierarchy?","Level 1 quoted identical, active · Level 2 other observable · Level 3 unobservable."],["Value in use?","PV of the cash flows the entity expects from use and ultimate disposal. Entity-specific."],["Current cost?","Cost of an equivalent asset today + transaction costs. Entry value."],["Which basis where?","{a|Inventory}: lower of cost and NRV · {a|PPE}: cost or revaluation · {a|IP}: fair value or cost · {a|Financial assets}: AC, FVOCI, FVTPL · {a|Intangibles}: cost, or revaluation with an active market."],["Recoverable amount?","Higher of fair value less costs of disposal and value in use."]],"id":"summary-1b9qxb","bucket":"wrap","sect":6,"part":"Wrap-up"}],"quiz":{"cases":{"bakery":{"name":"Bukit Timah Bakery","text":["Bukit Timah Bakery Pte Ltd bought an {a|industrial oven} on 1 January 20X1 for $120,000 and paid $8,000 for delivery and installation. The oven has a useful life of 10 years and no residual value; the bakery uses the straight-line method. Its financial year ends on 31 December.","At 31 December 20X3 the finance manager collects the following information:","(a) Used ovens of this kind are bought and sold mainly through dealers, which is the principal market. A dealer would pay $95,000 for the oven. The dealer charges a selling commission of $4,000, and moving the oven to the dealer would cost $1,000.","(b) The bakery expects the oven to generate net cash inflows of $15,000 at the end of each of the next 7 years, and to sell it for scrap for $5,000 at the end of that period. An appropriate discount rate is 8%.","(c) A three-year-old oven of the same model would cost $100,000 to buy today, plus $6,000 for delivery and installation."]},"marina":{"name":"Marina Traders","text":["Marina Traders Pte Ltd holds 10,000 units of a commodity at 31 December 20X5. The commodity can be sold in two markets:","Market X: price $48 a unit, transaction costs $4 a unit, transport costs $3 a unit.","Market Y: price $46 a unit, transaction costs $1 a unit, transport costs $3 a unit.","Marina also holds {a|shares} in a company listed on SGX, and owns a plot of {a|land} used as its staff car park."]}},"questions":[{"id":"q1","case":"bakery","lo":8,"type":"hc-amount","q":"Under historical cost, at what amount is the oven carried at 31 December 20X3?","options":[{"t":"$84,000","pit":"hc-no-tc","why":"This leaves delivery and installation out of {a|cost}. They are part of historical cost."},{"t":"$89,600","ok":true,"why":"{a|Cost} 120,000 + 8,000 = 128,000; {x|depreciation} 12,800 a year × 3 = {a|accumulated depreciation} 38,400; {a|carrying amount} 128,000 − 38,400 = 89,600."},{"t":"$128,000","why":"That is {a|cost} before {x|depreciation}. Historical cost is updated for {x|depreciation}."},{"t":"$102,400","why":"Only two years of {x|depreciation}. 1 Jan 20X1 to 31 Dec 20X3 is 3 years."}]},{"id":"q2","case":"bakery","lo":8,"type":"fv-markets","q":"What is the fair value of the oven at 31 December 20X3?","options":[{"t":"$90,000","pit":"deduct-tc","why":"The selling commission is a transaction cost. It is not deducted in measuring fair value."},{"t":"$95,000","pit":"forget-transport","why":"Transport to the market is deducted, because location is a characteristic of the asset."},{"t":"$94,000","ok":true,"why":"Price in the principal market 95,000 − transport 1,000 = 94,000. The commission is not deducted."},{"t":"$89,600","why":"That is the historical cost {a|carrying amount}, not fair value."}]},{"id":"q3","case":"bakery","lo":8,"type":"viu-calc","q":"What is the oven’s value in use at 31 December 20X3?","options":[{"t":"$110,000","pit":"undiscounted","why":"Undiscounted. Value in use is a **present** value."},{"t":"$78,096","why":"Leaves out the scrap proceeds. Value in use includes the cash from **ultimate disposal**."},{"t":"$81,013","ok":true,"why":"15,000 × 5.2064 = 78,096, plus 5,000 × 0.5835 = 2,917. Total 81,013."},{"t":"$94,000","pit":"viu-vs-fv","why":"That is fair value, the market’s exit price. Value in use is the entity’s own discounted cash flows."}]},{"id":"q4","case":"bakery","lo":8,"type":"current-cost","q":"What is the oven’s current cost at 31 December 20X3?","options":[{"t":"$106,000","ok":true,"why":"Cost of an equivalent (same-age) oven today 100,000 + transaction costs 6,000 = 106,000."},{"t":"$100,000","pit":"hc-no-tc","why":"Current cost includes the transaction costs that would be incurred today."},{"t":"$94,000","why":"That is fair value, an exit price. Current cost is an entry price: what it would cost to acquire an equivalent oven today."},{"t":"$81,013","why":"That is value in use."}]},{"id":"q5","case":"bakery","lo":8,"type":"define-basis","q":"Which statement about the four values the finance manager has collected is correct?","options":[{"t":"Fair value would rise if the bakery planned to use the oven for longer","pit":"viu-vs-fv","why":"Fair value ignores the entity’s own plans. A longer planned use would change value in use, not fair value."},{"t":"Value in use reflects the bakery’s own expectations, while fair value reflects market participants’ assumptions","ok":true,"why":"Value in use is entity-specific; fair value is a market-based exit price."},{"t":"Historical cost and fair value are both entry values","why":"Fair value is an exit value: the price to sell."},{"t":"Current cost excludes delivery and installation because they are transaction costs","why":"Current cost **includes** the transaction costs that would be incurred today."}]},{"id":"q6","case":"marina","lo":8,"type":"fv-markets","q":"Market X is the principal market for the commodity. What is the fair value of Marina’s holding?","options":[{"t":"$410,000","pit":"deduct-tc","why":"Deducts transaction costs. Only transport is deducted from fair value."},{"t":"$450,000","ok":true,"why":"Principal market X: (48 − 3) × 10,000 = 450,000."},{"t":"$430,000","pit":"wrong-market","why":"Y may net more, but when there is a principal market you must use it."},{"t":"$480,000","pit":"forget-transport","why":"Transport costs are deducted."}]},{"id":"q7","case":"marina","lo":8,"type":"fv-markets","q":"Suppose neither Market X nor Market Y is the principal market for the commodity. What is the fair value of Marina’s holding?","options":[{"t":"$450,000","why":"X has the higher price, but after both costs it nets only 41 a unit against Y’s 42. Y is the most advantageous market."},{"t":"$420,000","pit":"deduct-tc","why":"Right market, but transaction costs are not deducted from fair value. They only help choose the market."},{"t":"$430,000","ok":true,"why":"Net: X 41, Y 42, so Y is most advantageous. Fair value = (46 − 3) × 10,000 = 430,000."},{"t":"$460,000","pit":"forget-transport","why":"Transport costs are deducted."}]},{"id":"q8","case":"marina","lo":8,"type":"hierarchy","q":"Marina measures its {a|SGX-listed shares} using their closing price on SGX at the year end. Which level of the fair value hierarchy is this?","options":[{"t":"Level 1","ok":true,"why":"A quoted price in an active market for identical assets."},{"t":"Level 2","why":"Level 2 is for other observable inputs, such as prices of similar assets. This is the identical share."},{"t":"Level 3","why":"Level 3 is unobservable inputs. An SGX price is about as observable as it gets."},{"t":"None: listed shares are measured at historical cost","why":"{a|Shares} are {a|financial assets} measured at fair value (FVTPL or FVOCI)."}]},{"id":"q9","case":"marina","lo":8,"type":"hbu","q":"Marina’s car park {a|land} would sell for $2,400,000 as a car park. It is zoned for residential use, and developers would pay $3,600,000 for it as a residential site. Marina intends to keep using it as a car park. What is the land’s fair value?","options":[{"t":"$2,400,000","pit":"current-use","why":"That is the value in its current use. Fair value assumes the highest and best use by market participants."},{"t":"$3,600,000","ok":true,"why":"Residential use is physically possible, legally permissible (zoned) and financially feasible, so it is the highest and best use."},{"t":"$3,000,000","why":"Fair value is not an average of uses."},{"t":"It cannot be measured at fair value while Marina uses it as a car park","why":"The entity’s current use doesn’t stop fair value measurement; highest and best use applies anyway."}]},{"id":"q10","case":"marina","lo":8,"type":"define-basis","q":"Marina’s directors believe commodity prices will rise sharply next year and want to use their own price forecast as the fair value. Which statement is correct?","options":[{"t":"They may, because fair value reflects the entity’s best estimate of the asset’s worth","pit":"viu-vs-fv","why":"Fair value uses **market participants’** assumptions, not the entity’s."},{"t":"They may not: fair value is the price to sell at the measurement date between market participants","ok":true,"why":"An exit price at the measurement date, on market participants’ assumptions."},{"t":"They may, provided they classify the measurement as Level 1","why":"Their own forecast would be an unobservable input, and anyway a forecast is not today’s exit price."},{"t":"They must use the price they paid for the commodity","pit":"entry-price","why":"That is historical cost, an entry price."}]},{"id":"q11","lo":8,"type":"define-basis","source":"Official practice paper Q7","q":"Elements recognised in financial statements are quantified in monetary terms. This requires the selection of a measurement basis. With reference to the Conceptual Framework, identify which measurement base does the following definition refer to? ‘The present value of the cash flows, or other economic benefits, that an entity expects to derive from the use of an asset and from its ultimate disposal.’","options":[{"t":"Historical cost","why":"Historical cost is the price paid plus transaction costs, not a present value of future cash flows."},{"t":"Fair value","pit":"viu-vs-fv","why":"Fair value is a selling price between market participants, not the entity’s own forecast of cash flows from use."},{"t":"Value in use","ok":true,"why":"\"Present value… that **an entity** expects… from the **use** of an asset and from its ultimate disposal\" is the Framework’s definition of value in use."},{"t":"Fulfilment value","why":"Fulfilment value is the liability twin: the PV of what the entity expects to transfer to **fulfil a {l|liability}**. The definition is about an asset."}]},{"id":"q12","lo":8,"type":"which-basis","q":"Which of the following correctly matches an asset class with how it is measured after initial recognition?","options":[{"t":"{a|Inventory}: fair value less costs to sell","pit":"inv-at-fv","why":"Inventory is at the lower of cost and **net realisable value**, which is entity-specific."},{"t":"{a|Investment property} under the fair value model: fair value, with changes in OCI","why":"Investment property fair value changes go to **P&L**."},{"t":"{a|Property, plant and equipment}: cost model or revaluation model, chosen per class","ok":true,"why":"SFRS(I) 1-16 allows either, applied to a whole class."},{"t":"{a|Intangible assets}: always at fair value","why":"Cost model, or revaluation only if there is an active market."}]},{"id":"q13","lo":8,"type":"recoverable","q":"An item of {a|equipment} has a {a|carrying amount} of $860,000. Its fair value less costs of disposal is $700,000 and its value in use is $745,000. What is its recoverable amount?","options":[{"t":"$700,000","pit":"ra-lower","why":"Recoverable amount is the **higher** of the two."},{"t":"$860,000","why":"That is the {a|carrying amount}; recoverable amount is below it, so the equipment is impaired."},{"t":"$745,000","ok":true,"why":"Higher of 700,000 and 745,000 = 745,000. {x|Impairment loss} 115,000."},{"t":"$115,000","why":"That is the {x|impairment loss}, not the recoverable amount."}]},{"id":"q14","lo":8,"type":"hierarchy","q":"An entity holds {a|shares} in an unlisted family company. It measures them using a discounted cash flow model based on its own forecasts of the company’s profits. Which level of the fair value hierarchy is this measurement?","options":[{"t":"Level 1","why":"There is no quoted price in an active market."},{"t":"Level 2","pit":"level1-similar","why":"The key inputs are the entity’s own forecasts, which are unobservable."},{"t":"Level 3","ok":true,"why":"A model driven by unobservable inputs (the entity’s own assumptions) is Level 3."},{"t":"It is value in use, not fair value","pit":"viu-vs-fv","why":"A DCF can be used to estimate fair value if it reflects what market participants would assume. It is still fair value, at Level 3."}]},{"id":"q15","lo":8,"type":"define-basis","q":"How are transaction costs treated under each measurement basis?","options":[{"t":"Included in historical cost and current cost; not deducted in measuring fair value","ok":true,"why":"Exactly: entry values include them; fair value is the price itself, before transaction costs."},{"t":"Included in historical cost; deducted in measuring fair value","pit":"deduct-tc","why":"Fair value does not deduct transaction costs."},{"t":"Excluded from historical cost and current cost; deducted in measuring fair value","pit":"hc-no-tc","why":"Transaction costs are part of historical cost and current cost."},{"t":"Included in all bases, including fair value","why":"Fair value neither adds nor deducts them."}]}]},"exam":{"cases":{"jurong":{"name":"Jurong Precision","text":["Jurong Precision Pte Ltd bought a {a|CNC machine} on 1 January 20X1 for $600,000. Useful life 8 years, no residual value, straight-line. Financial year ends 31 December.","At 31 December 20X4 a fall in demand for the machine’s output is an indicator of impairment. The finance team gathers:","(a) Used machines like this one trade mainly through specialist dealers, which is the principal market. Dealers are currently buying similar used machines for $240,000. A dealer’s commission of $12,000 would be payable on a sale, and transporting the machine to the dealer would cost $8,000.","(b) Jurong expects the machine to generate net cash inflows of $100,000, $80,000, $60,000, $40,000 at the end of 20X5, 20X6, 20X7 and 20X8 respectively, with nothing on disposal. A suitable discount rate is 10%."]}},"questions":[{"id":"x1","case":"jurong","lo":8,"type":"fv-markets","q":"What is the fair value of the machine at 31 December 20X4?","options":[{"t":"$220,000","pit":"deduct-tc","why":"Deducts the commission, a transaction cost. That gives fair value **less costs of disposal**, not fair value."},{"t":"$232,000","ok":true,"why":"Principal market price 240,000 − transport 8,000 = 232,000. Commission is not deducted from fair value."},{"t":"$240,000","pit":"forget-transport","why":"Transport to the market is deducted, since location is a characteristic of the asset."},{"t":"$300,000","why":"That is the {a|carrying amount}: {a|cost} 600,000 − {a|accumulated depreciation} 4 × 75,000."}]},{"id":"x2","case":"jurong","lo":8,"type":"recoverable","q":"What is the machine’s fair value less costs of disposal?","options":[{"t":"$232,000","pit":"fvlcod-no-costs","why":"That is fair value. For FVLCOD the costs of disposal (the commission) are deducted."},{"t":"$228,000","pit":"forget-transport","why":"Starts from the gross price: transport is already deducted in arriving at fair value."},{"t":"$240,000","why":"Neither transport nor the commission has been deducted."},{"t":"$220,000","ok":true,"why":"Fair value 232,000 − commission 12,000 = 220,000. Here the transaction cost **is** deducted, because the measure is fair value **less costs of disposal**."}]},{"id":"x3","case":"jurong","lo":8,"type":"viu-calc","q":"What is the machine’s value in use at 31 December 20X4?","options":[{"t":"$280,000","pit":"undiscounted","why":"Undiscounted. Value in use is a present value."},{"t":"$229,425","ok":true,"why":"Discount each year separately: 100,000 → 90,909; 80,000 → 66,116; 60,000 → 45,079; 40,000 → 27,321. Total 229,425."},{"t":"$191,244","why":"Discounts the whole 280,000 as if it all arrived at the end of year 4. Each year’s cash flow is discounted for its own timing."},{"t":"$220,000","pit":"viu-vs-fv","why":"That is the market-based measure (FVLCOD). Value in use is the entity’s own discounted cash flows."}]},{"id":"x4","case":"jurong","lo":8,"type":"recoverable","q":"What is the machine’s recoverable amount at 31 December 20X4?","options":[{"t":"$220,000","pit":"ra-lower","why":"That is the lower of the two measures. Recoverable amount is the **higher**."},{"t":"$232,000","pit":"fvlcod-no-costs","why":"Recoverable amount uses fair value **less costs of disposal**, not fair value."},{"t":"$229,425","ok":true,"why":"Higher of FVLCOD 220,000 and value in use 229,425 = 229,425. Against a {a|carrying amount} of 300,000, the {x|impairment loss} is 70,575 (B3)."},{"t":"$300,000","why":"That is the {a|carrying amount}, which is above recoverable amount."}]},{"id":"x5","case":"jurong","lo":8,"type":"hierarchy","q":"Which level of the fair value hierarchy is the dealers’ price for similar used machines?","options":[{"t":"Level 1","pit":"level1-similar","why":"Level 1 needs quoted prices in an active market for **identical** assets. These are similar machines."},{"t":"Level 2","ok":true,"why":"An observable market price for **similar** assets: Level 2."},{"t":"Level 3","why":"The dealers’ price is observable in the market, not an internal assumption."},{"t":"No level applies, because Jurong does not intend to sell the machine","pit":"viu-vs-fv","why":"Fair value does not depend on the entity’s intentions."}]},{"id":"x6","lo":8,"type":"hbu","q":"An entity uses an old warehouse for storage. In its current use the site would sell for $3,200,000. The site is zoned for residential development; market participants would pay $5,000,000 for a cleared residential site, but would first have to spend $400,000 demolishing the warehouse. What is the fair value of the site?","options":[{"t":"$3,200,000","pit":"current-use","why":"That is the current-use value. Fair value assumes the highest and best use, which here is redevelopment."},{"t":"$5,000,000","why":"A market participant buying it for flats would have to pay for the demolition, so it would pay less than a cleared site."},{"t":"$4,600,000","ok":true,"why":"Highest and best use is residential: 5,000,000 − 400,000 demolition = 4,600,000, which beats 3,200,000 in current use."},{"t":"$2,800,000","why":"Deducts demolition from the current-use value, which mixes two different uses."}]},{"id":"x7","lo":8,"type":"fv-markets","q":"An entity holds 20,000 units of a product. It normally sells in Market P, which has by far the greatest volume and activity for the product (price $70, transaction costs $2, transport $3 a unit). Market Q pays $75 a unit, with transaction costs of $5 and transport of $2. What is the fair value of the holding?","options":[{"t":"$1,460,000","pit":"wrong-market","why":"Q nets more (68 vs 65), but P is the principal market, and the principal market wins."},{"t":"$1,340,000","ok":true,"why":"Principal market P: (70 − 3) × 20,000 = 1,340,000."},{"t":"$1,300,000","pit":"deduct-tc","why":"Right market, but transaction costs are not deducted from fair value."},{"t":"$1,360,000","pit":"wrong-market","why":"Wrong market and transaction costs deducted: two errors."}]},{"id":"x8","lo":8,"type":"define-basis","q":"An entity bought {a|equipment} from a retail dealer for $50,000. If it sold the same equipment at the measurement date in its principal market, it would receive $42,000. Under SFRS(I) 13, what is the equipment’s fair value at that date?","options":[{"t":"$50,000","pit":"entry-price","why":"That is the price paid to buy: an entry price. Fair value is an exit price."},{"t":"$42,000","ok":true,"why":"Fair value is the price that would be **received to sell** the asset in the principal market."},{"t":"$46,000","why":"Fair value is not a mid-point of buying and selling prices."},{"t":"The entity’s estimate of what the equipment is worth to it in use","pit":"viu-vs-fv","why":"That is value in use, which is entity-specific."}]},{"id":"x9","lo":8,"type":"define-basis","q":"With reference to the Conceptual Framework, which measurement basis is described as the present value of the cash, or other economic resources, that an entity expects to be obliged to transfer as it fulfils a {l|liability}?","options":[{"t":"Fair value","why":"Fair value of a liability is the price to **transfer** it to a market participant, not the entity’s own cost of fulfilling it."},{"t":"Fulfilment value","ok":true,"why":"Fulfilment value is the liability twin of value in use: entity-specific, a present value."},{"t":"Current cost","why":"Current cost of a {l|liability} is the consideration that would be received today to take on an equivalent {l|liability}."},{"t":"Value in use","pit":"viu-vs-fv","why":"Value in use is for **assets**. Its equivalent for {l|liabilities} is fulfilment value."}]},{"id":"x10","lo":8,"type":"which-basis","q":"Which of the following statements about measurement after initial recognition is correct?","options":[{"t":"{a|Inventory} is written down to fair value less costs to sell if that is lower than cost","pit":"inv-at-fv","why":"Inventory is written down to **net realisable value**, which is entity-specific, not fair value."},{"t":"Under the revaluation model, management may revalue only the {a|PPE} items whose value has risen","why":"Revaluation applies to an entire **class** of PPE, not hand-picked items."},{"t":"An entity may use the fair value model for some {a|investment properties} and the cost model for others","why":"One model applies to **all** investment property."},{"t":"A {a|debt instrument} held to collect contractual cash flows that are solely principal and interest is measured at amortised cost","ok":true,"why":"Held-to-collect business model + SPPI cash flows → amortised cost (unless designated at FVTPL to remove a mismatch)."}]}]},"prepared":true},{"id":"inventory","code":"B1.1","title":"Inventory","subtitle":"SFRS(I) 1-2 · cost, cost formulas and lower of cost and NRV","los":{"9":"Lower of cost and net realisable value","10":"Write-down of inventory","11":"Periodic and perpetual systems: journal entries","12":"Cost formulas: FIFO, weighted average, specific identification","13":"Inventory disclosures"},"accounts":[{"name":"Inventory","el":"a"},{"name":"Accounts payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Accounts receivable","el":"a"},{"name":"Cash","el":"a"},{"name":"GST receivable","el":"a"},{"name":"Sales revenue","el":"i"},{"name":"Cost of goods sold","el":"x"},{"name":"Purchases","el":"x"},{"name":"Freight-in","el":"x"},{"name":"Purchase returns","el":"x"},{"name":"Purchase discounts","el":"x"},{"name":"Freight-out expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Storage expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","el":"e"},{"name":"Retained earnings","el":"e"}],"map":{"classify":{"label":"Held for sale, in production, or materials"},"recognise":{"label":"When control of the goods passes","off":true},"initial":{"label":"Cost to get it ready to sell; selling costs out"},"subsequent":{"label":"Lower of cost and NRV, item by item","star":"Never above NRV; write-downs reverse, but only back to cost"},"derecognise":{"label":"Sold → {x|COGS} (perpetual or periodic)","star":"A cost formula (FIFO, weighted average, specific ID) decides which cost leaves"},"present":{"label":"{a|Current asset}; cost formula and write-downs disclosed"}},"preamble":["Welcome to the shopkeeper’s chapter. **{a|Inventory}** is the stuff a business means to get rid of: goods to sell, goods half-made, and materials to be used up making them. Unlike {a|PPE}, which clocks in for years, inventory is supposed to leave the building quickly, ideally in a customer’s shopping bag.","Here is the route, with ★ on the bits that are special to inventory:","1. First we **classify**: is it inventory at all? Held for sale, in production, or materials to be consumed.","2. A quick stop at **recognise**: whose goods are they when they are on a ship at year end?","3. Then **initial measurement**: cost is everything to get the goods ready to sell, and nothing after. Plus the perpetual vs periodic bookkeeping.","4. Next, every year end: the **lower of cost and NRV**, item by item, with write-downs that can be reversed only back to cost. ★ special","5. Then the exit: goods are sold and their cost leaves as **{x|COGS}**. A **cost formula** (FIFO, weighted average, specific ID) decides which cost leaves. ★ special","6. Last stop: **presentation and disclosure**, an eight-item checklist.","+ The big idea: record inventory at **cost**, expense it as **{x|COGS}** when sold, and never carry it above what you can get for it (**NRV**)."],"intros":{"classify":"First stop: is it inventory at all? One question does most of the work: will it be sold, or used up making something to sell, in the normal course of business? Then it’s {a|inventory}. Used for years to run the place? {a|PPE}. Pretty vanilla, but it sets up everything after.","recognise":"A short stop, barely used in the exam. Inventory goes on the books when the entity **controls** it, which matters mostly at the year end, when goods are on a ship or sitting in someone else’s warehouse. Shipping terms tell you whose they are.","initial":"Now that we know what inventory is, time to put a number on it. Cost is everything it took to get the goods **onto the shelf, ready to sell**: price less trade discounts, duty, freight-in, and conversion costs for a manufacturer. {x|Storage}, {x|freight-out} and {x|selling costs} stay out. We also meet the two bookkeeping systems, perpetual and periodic, which record the same costs in different places.","subsequent":"We have a cost, so what happens at the year end? ★ This is the special part: inventory is held at the **lower of cost and NRV**, tested item by item. If the goods are worth less than they cost, write them down now. If the value comes back, reverse the write-down, but never above cost. No fair value model, no {e|revaluation surplus}, no OCI.","derecognise":"Inventory is built to leave, so this is where the action is. When goods are sold their cost becomes **{x|COGS}**, either at every sale (perpetual) or as a balancing figure after the count (periodic). ★ Special: when identical units were bought at different prices, a **cost formula** (FIFO or weighted average; specific ID for unique items; never LIFO) decides which cost leaves and which stays on the shelf. We finish with counting errors, which fix themselves after two years.","present":"Last stop: showing it off. {a|Inventory} is a current {a|asset}, and the notes follow an eight-item checklist: policy (cost formula), amounts by class, the expense, write-downs and reversals, and anything pledged to the bank. Learn the list and these questions are free marks."},"pitfalls":{"total-not-item":"Comparing total cost with total NRV instead of testing item by item.","nrv-miss-costs":"Forgetting the costs to complete or the costs to sell when working out NRV.","cost-minus-sell":"Deducting selling costs from cost instead of from the selling price.","write-up":"Carrying inventory above original cost (writing up to NRV, or reversing more than the write-down).","no-reversal":"Refusing to reverse a write-down when NRV recovers (the US GAAP rule).","ignore-writedown":"Ignoring an NRV write-down ({a|closing inventory} left at cost, {x|COGS} too low).","writedown-oci":"Taking an inventory write-down to OCI or a {e|revaluation surplus} instead of profit or loss.","recoverable-tax":"Including recoverable GST in the cost of inventory.","storage-freight-out":"Capitalising {x|storage}, {x|freight-out}, {x|admin}, {x|selling costs} or {x|abnormal waste} (or expensing freight-in).","system-mixup":"Using periodic accounts ({x|Purchases}, {x|Freight-in}) under the perpetual system, or the reverse.","periodic-cogs-entry":"Recording a {x|COGS} entry at each sale under the periodic system.","wrong-average":"Using the periodic weighted average when the question says perpetual (moving average), or the reverse.","lifo":"Using LIFO, which SFRS(I) does not permit.","rm-writedown":"Writing down raw materials when the finished goods still sell above cost (or not writing them down when they don’t).","error-no-reverse":"Forgetting that an inventory error reverses in the following year.","transit-terms":"Putting goods in transit or on consignment in the wrong party’s inventory.","tb-opening":"Under the periodic method, treating the trial-balance {a|inventory} (opening) or {x|purchases} as the year-end figure.","deferred-rev":"Deducting {l|deferred revenue} released in the year from {i|sales} instead of adding it."},"examTypes":[{"id":"what-is-inventory","title":"Which of these assets is inventory?","pitfall":"Calling equipment used in the business inventory, or missing supplies consumed in services.","see":"classify"},{"id":"goods-transit","title":"Whose inventory are goods in transit or on consignment?","pitfall":"Ignoring the shipping terms; counting consigned goods held for others.","see":"recognise"},{"id":"cost-components","title":"What is the cost of inventory? (price, discounts, duty, GST, freight, storage)","pitfall":"Including {a|recoverable GST}, {x|storage} or {x|freight-out}.","see":"initial"},{"id":"system-entry","title":"Which entry records it? (periodic or perpetual)","pitfall":"Adding a {x|COGS} entry at each sale under periodic.","see":"initial"},{"id":"lcnrv-amount","title":"At what amount should inventory be reported? (practice Q5)","pitfall":"Comparing total cost with total NRV, or carrying above cost.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"nrv-writedown","title":"What write-down is required? (WIP with costs to complete and to sell)","pitfall":"Forgetting the costs to complete or the costs to sell.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"reversal","title":"What is the carrying amount after NRV recovers?","pitfall":"Reversing above original cost, or not reversing at all.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"raw-materials","title":"Raw materials’ replacement cost has fallen…","pitfall":"Writing down materials when the finished goods still sell above cost.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"cost-formula","title":"Closing inventory or COGS under FIFO and weighted average?","pitfall":"Using the periodic average when the question says perpetual (moving average).","see":"derecognise"},{"id":"gp-cogs","title":"Gross profit or COGS from a trial balance (practice Q21)","pitfall":"Leaving the write-down out of {x|COGS}.","see":"derecognise"},{"id":"inv-error","title":"Closing inventory was misstated. What is the effect?","pitfall":"Forgetting it reverses the next year.","see":"derecognise"},{"id":"disclosure","title":"Which of these is (not) a required disclosure?","pitfall":"Picking a real disclosure item; learn the eight.","see":"present"}],"pages":[{"type":"map","id":"map-overview","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"intro","lo":9,"title":"Inventory, the Socratic way","body":["{a|Inventory} looks like the easiest asset on the balance sheet: you buy stuff, you sell stuff. The exam disagrees. It loves three things: **what goes into cost**, **which cost leaves when you sell** (FIFO or weighted average), and the **lower of cost and NRV** test.","Each idea starts with a question. Think it through, then reveal the answer. The rule comes after, once you have half-worked it out.","Account names are coloured by element. Tap **Legend** at the top any time. At the end: a chapter quiz in exam style, then a harder exam."],"id":"intro-1lo3cg","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"map","id":"map-1-classify","bucket":"classify","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"think","bucket":"classify","lo":11,"scenario":"Merlion Motors sells and services cars. It owns: (1) 40 new cars on the showroom floor, (2) the showroom building, (3) a stock of tyres it fits during paid servicing, and (4) the workshop’s hydraulic lifts.","prompt":"Which of these are inventory? What do they have in common that the others don’t?","answer":["The **cars** are held **for sale** in the ordinary course of business: {a|inventory}.","The **tyres** are supplies **used up** in rendering a service (the servicing job): also {a|inventory}.","The **showroom** and the **lifts** are used to run the business over many years: {a|PPE}. The lifts are never going to be sold to a customer, however nicely you polish them.","Common thread: {a|inventory} is **turned into {i|revenue} within the normal operating cycle**, either by selling it or by using it up making something to sell."],"id":"think-d34v2y","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"classify","lo":11,"title":"What is inventory?","body":["The cars and the tyres had one thing in common: they are turned into {i|revenue} within the operating cycle. Here is the standard’s version of that idea, so you can sort any item before deciding how to measure it.","{a|Inventory} (SFRS(I) 1-2) is an asset that is:","1. held **for sale** in the ordinary course of business (finished goods, or merchandise for a retailer),","2. **in the process of production** for such sale (work in progress), or","3. **materials or supplies** to be consumed in the production process or in rendering services (raw materials).","- A retailer usually has one kind: **{a|merchandise}**. A manufacturer has three: **{a|raw materials} → {a|work in progress} → {a|finished goods}**.","- A property developer’s unsold flats are {a|inventory} too: held for sale in the ordinary course of its business.","+ The test is **purpose** again: sell it or use it up making something to sell → {a|inventory}. Use it over several periods → {a|PPE}."],"id":"rule-1v5pti","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"classify","lo":11,"title":"A manufacturer’s three kinds of inventory","intro":"Follow a factory’s costs from the delivery dock to the customer. Every box above the line is {a|inventory}; costs only reach profit or loss when the goods are sold (or wasted).","notes":{"rm":"Materials waiting to be used in production. {a|Inventory}, at cost.","lab":"Wages of the people who make the product. A **conversion cost**: added to {a|WIP}.","oh":"Factory costs of production (not head-office admin, not selling). Also a conversion cost.","wip":"Part-made goods: materials plus the conversion costs so far.","fg":"Completed goods waiting for a buyer. Still an {a|asset}, still at cost (or NRV if lower).","cogs":"Only when the goods are **sold** does their cost become an {x|expense}, matched with the {i|revenue}.","abn":"{x|Abnormal amounts of wasted material, labour or overheads} are never part of cost: expense them in the period."},"walk":[{"nodes":["rm"],"text":"It starts as **{a|raw materials}**: {a|inventory} sitting on a shelf."},{"nodes":["rm","lab","oh","wip"],"text":"Issue the materials to production and add **conversion costs** (labour, production overheads): now it is **{a|work in progress}**."},{"nodes":["fg"],"text":"Finished: **{a|finished goods}**, still an {a|asset}."},{"nodes":["cogs"],"text":"Sold: the cost moves to **{x|cost of goods sold}** in P&L, in the same period as the {i|revenue}."},{"nodes":["abn"],"text":"{x|Abnormal waste} skips the queue and goes straight to P&L. It is never {a|inventory}."}],"caption":"Three kinds of inventory, one direction of travel: cost waits on the balance sheet until the goods are sold.","id":"diagram-1to0no","sect":1,"part":"Classify","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:452px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 411.00 277.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 266.55)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-266.55 400.58,-266.55 400.58,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>rm\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M97.53,-255.75C97.53,-255.75 12,-255.75 12,-255.75 6,-255.75 0,-249.75 0,-243.75 0,-243.75 0,-231.75 0,-231.75 0,-225.75 6,-219.75 12,-219.75 12,-219.75 97.53,-219.75 97.53,-219.75 103.53,-219.75 109.53,-225.75 109.53,-231.75 109.53,-231.75 109.53,-243.75 109.53,-243.75 109.53,-249.75 103.53,-255.75 97.53,-255.75\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"18.02\" y=\"-238.3\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Raw materials\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"8.64\" y=\"-229.2\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">bought in, not yet used\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node4\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>wip\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M219.78,-182.5C219.78,-182.5 135.75,-182.5 135.75,-182.5 129.75,-182.5 123.75,-176.5 123.75,-170.5 123.75,-170.5 123.75,-158.5 123.75,-158.5 123.75,-152.5 129.75,-146.5 135.75,-146.5 135.75,-146.5 219.78,-146.5 219.78,-146.5 225.78,-146.5 231.78,-152.5 231.78,-158.5 231.78,-158.5 231.78,-170.5 231.78,-170.5 231.78,-176.5 225.78,-182.5 219.78,-182.5\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"132.39\" y=\"-165.05\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Work in progress\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"156.77\" y=\"-155.95\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">part&#45;made\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge1\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>rm&#45;&gt;wip\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M84.85,-219.32C101.83,-209.49 123.17,-197.13 141.12,-186.73\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"142.11,-188.58 146.25,-183.76 140.01,-184.95 142.11,-188.58\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"140.88\"