.91,-270 106.91,-270 112.91,-276 112.91,-282 112.91,-282 112.91,-294 112.91,-294 112.91,-300 106.91,-306 100.91,-306\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"45.77\" y=\"-289.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Freight&#45;out\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"66.02\" y=\"-278.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">700\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge7\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>fout&#45;&gt;pl\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M113.23,-294.4C135.83,-298.29 164.82,-303.27 188.62,-307.36\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"188.07,-309.4 194.33,-308.34 188.78,-305.26 188.07,-309.4\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"visual","bucket":"initial","lo":11,"title":"Changi Gadgets: paid vs cost","html":"\u003cp>Two bars on the same scale. The top one is \u003cstrong>everything Changi Gadgets paid\u003c/strong> for the speakers; the bottom one is what is left as the \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cost of inventory\u003c/span>. The difference is the GST it gets back (a \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">receivable\u003c/span>) and the costs that come after the goods are ready to sell (\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">expenses\u003c/span>).\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"cbars\">\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-key\">\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg a\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cost of inventory\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg n\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">GST receivable\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg x\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">expensed\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">Paid: 36,000 + 1,800 + 1,200 + 300 + GST 3,402 + storage 900 + freight-out 700 = \u003cstrong>44,302\u003c/strong>\u003c/div>\n      \u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003ci class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:88.71%\" title=\"Cost 39,300\">\u003c/i>\u003ci class=\"seg n\" style=\"width:7.68%\" title=\"GST 3,402\">\u003c/i>\u003ci class=\"seg x\" style=\"width:3.61%\" title=\"Storage + freight-out 1,600\">\u003c/i>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cost\u003c/span>: 36,000 + 1,800 + 1,200 + 300 = \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">39,300\u003c/span>\u003c/strong> (78.6 a speaker)\u003c/div>\n      \u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003ci class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:81.26%\" title=\"Price after discount 36,000\">\u003c/i>\u003ci class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:4.06%\" title=\"Duty 1,800\">\u003c/i>\u003ci class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:2.71%\" title=\"Freight-in 1,200\">\u003c/i>\u003ci class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:0.68%\" title=\"Insurance 300\">\u003c/i>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n  \u003c/div>\n  \u003cp class=\"cap\">Out of cost: \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">GST\u003c/span> 3,402 comes back from IRAS; \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">storage\u003c/span> 900 and \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">freight-out\u003c/span> 700 happen after the speakers are ready to sell.\u003c/p>","id":"visual-5iy09n","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"calc-10094b","type":"calc","bucket":"initial","lo":11,"q":"Changi Gadgets imported 500 speakers whose {a|cost} came to $39,300 in total (price after trade discount, import duty, freight-in and insurance in transit). It later sells 300 of them. Using the cost per unit, what is the {x|cost of goods sold}?","answer":23580,"unit":"$","working":["{a|Cost} per speaker = 39,300 ÷ 500 = 78.6.","{x|COGS} = 300 × 78.6 = **$23,580**.","Had you loaded {a|GST}, {x|storage} and {x|freight-out} into {a|cost} (44,302 in total), every speaker would look 10 more expensive, and the {x|expenses} would hide inside {x|COGS}."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"initial","lo":11,"q":"A furniture maker incurs the following this month. Which is part of the cost of its finished tables?","options":[{"t":"Salaries of the head-office accounts team","why":"{x|Admin overheads} not related to production are expensed."},{"t":"Wood ruined when a storeroom flooded","why":"{x|Abnormal waste} is expensed, never part of cost."},{"t":"Depreciation of the factory’s cutting machines","ok":true,"why":"A production overhead: part of conversion costs, so it is included in the cost of the tables."},{"t":"Showroom rent where finished tables are displayed for sale","why":"A {x|selling cost}: expensed."}],"id":"mcq-kb1fz3","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"think-1xppyo","type":"think","bucket":"initial","lo":11,"scenario":"Two shops sell the same goods. Shop A’s system updates the {a|stock balance} every time a sale is scanned at the till. Shop B just records purchases during the year and counts the shelves on 31 December.","prompt":"When does each shop know its {x|cost of goods sold}? What accounts would each one need during the year?","answer":["**Shop A (perpetual)** knows {x|COGS} **at every sale**: each purchase goes straight into {a|Inventory}, and each sale moves its cost out to {x|Cost of goods sold}.","**Shop B (periodic)** only knows {x|COGS} **at the year end**, after the count. During the year purchases collect in temporary accounts: {x|Purchases}, {x|Freight-in}, {x|Purchase returns}, {x|Purchase discounts}.","Same goods, same final answer for {x|COGS}; different bookkeeping on the way."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"visual-155jk1","type":"visual","bucket":"initial","lo":11,"title":"Perpetual vs periodic: the entries side by side","html":"\u003cp>Shop A and Shop B, entry by entry. The only real difference: where the cost of stock waits during the year. Perpetual keeps \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Inventory\u003c/span> up to date; periodic parks it in temporary accounts until the count.\u003c/p>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl reveal-rows\">\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Transaction\u003c/th>\u003cth>Perpetual\u003c/th>\u003cth>Periodic\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Buy on credit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Inventory\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Accounts payable\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Purchases\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Accounts payable\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Freight-in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Inventory\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Freight-in\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Return to supplier\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Accounts payable\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Inventory\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Accounts payable\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Purchase returns\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Discount taken\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Accounts payable\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Inventory\u003c/span>, Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Accounts payable\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Purchase discounts\u003c/span>, Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sale\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Accounts receivable\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-i\">Sales revenue\u003c/span> \u003cstrong>+\u003c/strong> Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Cost of goods sold\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Inventory\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dr \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Accounts receivable\u003c/span> / Cr \u003cspan class=\"el el-i\">Sales revenue\u003c/span> (no COGS entry)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Period end\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No adjustment (except shrinkage)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Close the temporary accounts, set up \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">closing Inventory\u003c/span> from the count, record \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Cost of goods sold\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cp class=\"cap\">Perpetual: everything that changes the cost of stock hits \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Inventory\u003c/span> directly. Periodic: it waits in temporary accounts until the year-end count.\u003c/p>","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"worked-5grs4t","type":"worked","bucket":"initial","lo":11,"title":"Worked example: a purchase cycle, perpetual system","q":"No {a|opening stock}. Buy 100 units at $20 on credit, return 20 units, pay $160 freight-in, pay the supplier within the 2% discount period, then sell 50 units at $40 on credit.","intro":["A typical exam question: a run of purchase-cycle events (buy, return, freight, pay early, sell) and you give the entries or the unit cost. Remember two things:","- Under **perpetual**, anything that changes what the goods cost (returns, {a|freight-in}, discounts taken) goes **straight to {a|Inventory}**.","- A sale is **two entries**: the {i|revenue}, and the cost moving out of {a|Inventory} to {x|Cost of goods sold}.","Then follow these steps: buy → return → freight-in → pay early → unit cost → sell."],"steps":[{"h":"Record the purchase on credit.","t":"100 × 20 = {a|2,000} goes straight into {a|inventory}.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Inventory","amount":2000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accounts payable","amount":2000,"el":"l"}]},{"h":"Record the return of 20 units.","t":"20 × 20 = {a|400} comes back out of {a|inventory}, and the {l|payable} falls.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accounts payable","amount":400,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Inventory","amount":400,"el":"a"}]},{"h":"Add the freight-in to cost.","t":"Getting the goods here is part of their {a|cost}: {a|160}.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Inventory","amount":160,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":160,"el":"a"}]},{"h":"Pay the supplier within the discount period.","t":["{l|Owed} = 2,000 − 400 = **{l|1,600}**","Discount = 1,600 × 2% = **32**, which **reduces the cost** of the goods, so it comes off {a|inventory}. {a|Cash} paid = 1,600 − 32 = {a|1,568}."],"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accounts payable","amount":1600,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Inventory","amount":32,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":1568,"el":"a"}]},{"h":"Work out the cost per unit.","t":"{a|Inventory} = 2,000 − 400 + 160 − 32 = **{a|1,728}** for 80 units = **21.6** each."},{"h":"Record the sale of 50 units: two entries.","t":["{i|Revenue} = 50 × 40 = **{i|2,000}**","{x|Cost of goods sold} = 50 × 21.6 = **{x|1,080}**, moved out of {a|inventory}"],"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accounts receivable","amount":2000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Sales revenue","amount":2000,"el":"i"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Cost of goods sold","amount":1080,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Inventory","amount":1080,"el":"a"}],"after":"{a|Inventory left} = 30 units × 21.6 = **{a|648}**. Every cost flowed through {a|Inventory}, and every sale moved cost out straight away. No year-end drama."}],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"je","bucket":"initial","lo":11,"q":"Your turn, with one line filled in. Bukit Batok Bikes (perpetual) owes a supplier $10,000 for 40 bikes at $250. It pays within the 2% discount period. Complete the entry.","accounts":["Inventory","Accounts payable","Accounts receivable","Cash","GST receivable","Sales revenue","Cost of goods sold","Purchases","Freight-in","Purchase returns","Purchase discounts","Freight-out expense","Storage expense","Revaluation surplus (OCI)","Retained earnings"],"given":[0],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accounts payable","amount":10000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Inventory","amount":200},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":9800}],"why":"Discount 10,000 × 2% = 200, which reduces the cost of the bikes, so it comes off {a|Inventory}. {a|Cash} paid 10,000 − 200 = 9,800.","id":"je-1pno0v","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"je","bucket":"initial","lo":11,"q":"Now a blank one. Woodlands Wines (perpetual) bought 60 cases at $120 on credit and returned 10 damaged cases (already recorded). It now pays the supplier in full within the 3% discount period. Record the payment.","accounts":["Inventory","Accounts payable","Accounts receivable","Cash","GST receivable","Sales revenue","Cost of goods sold","Purchases","Freight-in","Purchase returns","Purchase discounts","Freight-out expense","Storage expense","Revaluation surplus (OCI)","Retained earnings"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accounts payable","amount":6000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Inventory","amount":180},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":5820}],"why":"Owed after the return: (60 − 10) × 120 = 6,000. Discount 3% = 180 off {a|Inventory}; {a|cash} 5,820.","id":"je-1flis0","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"mcq-ugmof0","type":"mcq","bucket":"initial","lo":11,"q":"A business uses the **periodic** system. It owes a supplier $1,600 for goods bought on credit (already recorded) and pays within the 2% discount period. Which entry records the payment?","options":[{"t":"Dr {l|Accounts payable} 1,600 / Cr {a|Inventory} 32, Cr {a|Cash} 1,568","why":"That is the perpetual entry. Under periodic, {a|Inventory} is not touched during the year."},{"t":"Dr {l|Accounts payable} 1,600 / Cr {x|Purchase discounts} 32, Cr {a|Cash} 1,568","ok":true,"why":"Periodic: the discount collects in a temporary {x|Purchase discounts} account, closed at the year end."},{"t":"Dr {l|Accounts payable} 1,600 / Cr {a|Cash} 1,600","why":"Ignores the discount actually taken: only the net amount is paid."},{"t":"Dr {x|Purchases} 1,568 / Cr {a|Cash} 1,568","why":"The purchase was already recorded when the goods arrived; this is settling the {l|payable}."}],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"initial","lo":11,"special":"{a|Inventory} cost stops at the shop door: everything to get it **ready to sell** is in, and anything after that ({x|storage}, {x|freight-out}, {x|selling}) is out.","id":"compare-1kumyt-2","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"map","id":"map-4-subsequent","bucket":"subsequent","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"think","bucket":"subsequent","lo":9,"scenario":"A phone shop has 200 last-year models in its stockroom. They cost $600 each. The new model launched last week, and the old ones will now only sell for about $450, after paying a $20 commission per sale.","prompt":"Should the balance sheet still say these phones are worth $600 each? What would a careful accountant show?","answer":["No. Carrying them at $600 would promise users more than the shop will ever get back: an overstated {a|asset}, and a {x|loss} pushed into next year.","What the shop will actually get is $450 − $20 = **$430** a phone: the **net realisable value**. Write them down to $430 now, and take the $170 a phone {x|loss} **this year**, when the value was lost.","{a|Inventory} never sits on the balance sheet at a price nobody will pay."],"id":"think-1v62sd","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"subsequent","lo":9,"title":"Lower of cost and net realisable value","body":["So far {a|inventory} has sat at cost. The phone shop showed the problem: cost can be more than the goods will ever bring in. This rule caps the {a|carrying amount} at what the entity expects to get back.","{a|Inventory} is measured at the **lower of cost and net realisable value** (NRV).","> NRV = estimated selling price − costs to complete − costs to sell","NRV can fall below cost because of:","- damage or obsolescence","- falling selling prices","- rising costs to complete or to sell","- NRV is **entity-specific** (what *this* entity expects to get). Fair value is a market measure. They can differ.","! Your FA textbook is US GAAP. Under SFRS(I) the test is always cost vs **NRV**: ignore any US “market” or replacement-cost ceiling and floor rules."],"id":"rule-1ltkh1","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"subsequent","lo":9,"title":"Lower of cost and NRV, item by item","intro":"This is the chart from your notes. Run every item through it at each reporting date.","notes":{"item":"Work out NRV **for each item** (or group of similar related items), never for a whole category.","q":"The only question: is NRV below cost?","cost":"NRV at or above cost: no adjustment. {a|Inventory} is never **written up** above cost.","wd":"NRV below cost: write down to NRV. The {x|loss} goes to profit or loss this period, usually inside {x|Cost of goods sold}.","rev":"If NRV recovers later and the goods are still on hand, reverse the write-down, but **only back up to original cost**.","rm":"Raw materials are only written down if the finished goods they go into will sell **below** cost."},"walk":[{"nodes":["item"],"text":"For each item, NRV = selling price − costs to complete − costs to sell."},{"nodes":["q","cost"],"text":"NRV not below cost? Keep it at **cost**. No write-up, ever."},{"nodes":["q","wd"],"text":"NRV below cost? **Write down** to NRV: Dr {x|COGS} / Cr {a|Inventory}."},{"nodes":["wd","rev"],"text":"Next year NRV recovers? Reverse, capped at the original write-down."},{"nodes":["rm"],"text":"Special case: raw materials stay at cost if the finished product still sells at or above cost."}],"caption":"“Down any amount, back up only to cost.”","id":"diagram-az384h","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:564px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 513.00 247.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" trans