5 82.14,-70.47 73.56,-55.37 67.18,-42.95\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"69.12,-42.13 64.55,-37.71 65.37,-44.01 69.12,-42.13\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"130.26\" y=\"-73.25\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> Yes: road, fence,\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"130.26\" y=\"-62\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">car park,\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"130.26\" y=\"-50.75\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">sprinklers \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node7\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>bldg\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M240.03,-36C240.03,-36 141.75,-36 141.75,-36 135.75,-36 129.75,-30 129.75,-24 129.75,-24 129.75,-12 129.75,-12 129.75,-6 135.75,0 141.75,0 141.75,0 240.03,0 240.03,0 246.03,0 252.03,-6 252.03,-12 252.03,-12 252.03,-24 252.03,-24 252.03,-30 246.03,-36 240.03,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"190.89\" y=\"-13.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Building (depreciated)\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge6\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>c3&#45;&gt;bldg\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M155.84,-95.21C160.68,-91.57 165.25,-87.41 168.89,-82.75 177.74,-71.41 183.16,-56.26 186.41,-43.58\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"188.41,-44.24 187.73,-37.92 184.32,-43.29 188.41,-44.24\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"214.6\" y=\"-67.62\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> No, it is the\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"214.6\" y=\"-56.38\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">building itself \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"classify","lo":14,"q":"A company buys a site with an old warehouse for $4,200,000, pays $180,000 to demolish the warehouse and sells the scrap for $40,000. How are the demolition and scrap treated?","options":[{"t":"Demolition $180,000 expensed; scrap $40,000 to {i|other income}","why":"Demolition is needed to get the land ready for use, so it is capitalised, and the scrap reduces that cost."},{"t":"Net $140,000 added to the cost of {a|land}","ok":true,"why":"Clearing the site is part of preparing the land; salvage proceeds are deducted."},{"t":"Demolition $180,000 added to {a|land}; scrap $40,000 to {i|other income}","why":"The scrap proceeds are deducted from the cost, not taken to {i|income}."},{"t":"Net $140,000 added to the new {a|building}","why":"Clearing an old structure prepares the {a|land}, not the new {a|building} (in the approach used by your texts and the exam)."}],"id":"mcq-4uadi1","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"map","id":"map-2-recognise","bucket":"recognise","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"think","bucket":"recognise","lo":14,"scenario":"An airline spends money on one of its planes this year: (1) cleaning and minor repairs after every flight, and (2) replacing both engines, which had come to the end of their lives.","prompt":"Which of these is a new asset, and which is just the cost of keeping the plane flying?","answer":["**Cleaning and minor repairs** are day-to-day servicing. They keep the plane as it was: expense them.","**New engines** bring future benefits for years: capitalise them, and **derecognise** the {a|carrying amount} of the old engines they replace.","This only works neatly if the engines were tracked as a separate **component** from the start. That idea comes back in depreciation."],"id":"think-d8e2zc","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"recognise","lo":14,"title":"Recognition: two tests, then components","body":["Classified as PPE? Next: when does a cost become an {a|asset} at all? The airline’s cleaning vs engines shows the line. The same two tests apply on the purchase day and to every cost afterwards.","Recognise an item of PPE (and any later cost) as an {a|asset} when:","1. it is **probable** that future economic benefits will flow to the entity, and","2. its **cost can be measured reliably**.","Costs after acquisition:","- **day-to-day servicing** (labour, consumables, small parts) → {x|expense} as incurred","- **replacing a significant part** → add to the {a|asset} and derecognise the part replaced","+ Significant parts with different useful lives are recognised and depreciated as separate **components**."],"id":"rule-wdi762","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"recognise","lo":14,"q":"A factory replaces the roof of its building, which had worn out, and repaints the office walls. Which is correct?","options":[{"t":"Both are expensed as repairs","why":"The new roof is a significant part that brings benefits for many years: capitalise it."},{"t":"Capitalise the roof (derecognising the old roof); expense the repainting","ok":true,"why":"Replacing a significant part is capitalised; repainting is day-to-day upkeep."},{"t":"Capitalise both, as they improve the building","why":"Repainting keeps the building as it was; it is servicing, not a new benefit."},{"t":"Capitalise the roof and keep the old roof in the cost too","why":"The replaced part is derecognised, otherwise the building holds two roofs."}],"id":"mcq-dj5vzj","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"map","id":"map-3-initial","bucket":"initial","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"think","bucket":"initial","lo":14,"scenario":"A company buys a machine. List price $100,000, less a 5% trade discount. It also pays import duty $3,000, GST at 9% (recoverable from IRAS), delivery $2,000, installation and testing $5,500, and $2,500 to train staff to run it. It must remove the machine and restore the site at the end of its life; the present value of that cost is $5,000.","prompt":"Which of these belong in the machine’s cost? Test each one against: “did we need this to get the machine ready to work?”","answer":["**In:** the price after discount (95,000), duty, delivery, installation and testing, and the {l|**dismantling** obligation}.","**Out:** the **{a|recoverable GST}** (it comes back from IRAS, so it is not a cost to the company) and the **{x|training}** (it improves the staff, not the machine).","The surprise is dismantling: a cost you will pay in 10 years goes into the {a|asset} on day one."],"id":"think-18qxtq","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","lo":14,"title":"Cost: everything to get it ready for use","body":["Once an item passes recognition, it needs a number. The machine showed the test: if the asset needed it to start working, it is in the cost. Here is the full list.","> {a|Cost} = purchase price − trade discounts + duties and non-refundable taxes + directly attributable costs + PV of dismantling and restoration","Directly attributable costs include:","- delivery, handling and **site preparation**","- installation, assembly and **testing** that it works properly","- professional fees (architects, engineers, legal)","- the **estimated cost of dismantling** and restoring the site, at present value, with a {l|Provision for dismantling}","+ Measure PPE at **cost** when first recognised. The cost model or revaluation model only takes over afterwards."],"id":"rule-18hiz9","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","lo":14,"title":"What never goes into cost","body":["The flip side of the last page. These costs are real and often paid at the same time as the purchase, but none of them gets the asset ready to work, so they stay out.","- **Recoverable** GST: claim it back as a {a|GST receivable}","- staff **training**","- opening a new facility, advertising a new product","- **admin and general overheads**","- costs **after** the asset is ready for use, including **relocating** it","- **abnormal waste** and initial operating losses","! Training, relocation, admin overheads and recoverable GST are the favourite exam distractors. None of them is ever part of cost."],"id":"rule-9jx1fa","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"calc-1btkiy","type":"calc","bucket":"initial","lo":14,"q":"A company buys a machine: list price $100,000 less a 5% trade discount, import duty $3,000, GST at 9% (recoverable from IRAS), delivery $2,000, installation and testing $5,500, and $2,500 to train staff to run it. The present value of the cost to dismantle it and restore the site at the end of its life is $5,000. What is the cost of the machine?","answer":110500,"unit":"$","working":["Price 100,000 − 5% discount = 95,000.","95,000 + duty 3,000 + delivery 2,000 + installation and testing 5,500 + PV of dismantling 5,000 = **{a|$110,500}**.","{a|GST} 8,550 (recoverable) and {x|training} 2,500 are left out."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"initial","lo":14,"title":"The machine’s cost, built up","intro":"The same answer as a picture. Five amounts build the cost; GST and training head elsewhere.","notes":{"price":"List price 100,000 less the 5% trade discount (5,000). Trade discounts and rebates always come off.","duty":"Import duty is a non-refundable tax: part of cost.","deliv":"Getting it to site: part of cost.","inst":"Installing it and testing that it works: part of cost.","dism":"The obligation to remove it later. Its present value goes into cost, and the same amount is a {l|Provision for dismantling}.","gst":"9% × 95,000 = 8,550. Recoverable, so it is not a cost to the company.","train":"Training improves the people, not the machine. {x|Expense}.","cost":"95,000 + 3,000 + 2,000 + 5,500 + 5,000 = 110,500.","rec":"An {a|asset}: the company claims it back.","pl":"{x|Training} hits profit or loss when incurred."},"walk":[{"nodes":["price","cost"],"text":"Start from the price **after** trade discount."},{"nodes":["duty","deliv","inst","cost"],"text":"Add every cost of getting it to site and working: duty, delivery, installation and testing."},{"nodes":["dism","cost"],"text":"★ Add the **present value of dismantling**. This is the special bit of PPE: a future cost capitalised today."},{"nodes":["gst","rec"],"text":"Recoverable GST is a {a|receivable}, not cost."},{"nodes":["train","pl"],"text":"Training is an {x|expense}."},{"nodes":["cost"],"text":"{a|Cost} = **110,500**."}],"caption":"Ready-for-use test: if the machine needed it to work, it is in.","id":"diagram-1k5mef","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:384px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 349.00 358.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 346.8)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-346.8 338.61,-346.8 338.61,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>price\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M129.78,-236C129.78,-236 12,-236 12,-236 6,-236 0,-230 0,-224 0,-224 0,-212 0,-212 0,-206 6,-200 12,-200 12,-200 129.78,-200 129.78,-200 135.78,-200 141.78,-206 141.78,-212 141.78,-212 141.78,-224 141.78,-224 141.78,-230 135.78,-236 129.78,-236\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"19.51\" y=\"-219.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Price after discount\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"8.64\" y=\"-208.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">100,000 − 5,000 = 95,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node8\" class=\"node\">\n\u