=\"#6b7280\" points=\"390.39,-71.79 396.74,-71.44 391.55,-67.76 390.39,-71.79\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"338.68\" y=\"-66.13\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> ✗ never \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node6\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>re\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#166534\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M485.59,-36C485.59,-36 397.06,-36 397.06,-36 391.06,-36 385.06,-30 385.06,-24 385.06,-24 385.06,-12 385.06,-12 385.06,-6 391.06,0 397.06,0 397.06,0 485.59,0 485.59,0 491.59,0 497.59,-6 497.59,-12 497.59,-12 497.59,-24 497.59,-24 497.59,-30 491.59,-36 485.59,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"393.7\" y=\"-18.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Retained earnings\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"402.32\" y=\"-9.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">direct, within equity\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge4\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>rs&#45;&gt;re\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#166534\" d=\"M292.81,-17.7C298.05,-17.32 303.27,-16.99 308.31,-16.75 335.28,-15.47 342.06,-16.51 369.06,-16.75 371.76,-16.77 374.51,-16.8 377.29,-16.83\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#166534\" stroke=\"#166534\" points=\"377.15,-18.93 383.18,-16.91 377.21,-14.73 377.15,-18.93\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"338.68\" y=\"-18.5\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> may transfer \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"mcq-1cbb85","type":"mcq","bucket":"derecognise","lo":15,"q":"A revalued building with a {a|carrying amount} of $380,000 and a {e|revaluation surplus} of $60,000 is sold for $400,000. What is recognised?","options":[{"t":"{i|Gain} of $80,000 in P&L","why":"Recycles the {e|surplus} through P&L. The surplus is never reclassified."},{"t":"{i|Gain} of $20,000 in P&L; {e|surplus} of $60,000 may be transferred directly to {e|retained earnings}","ok":true,"why":"400,000 − 380,000 = 20,000 in P&L; the {e|surplus} moves within equity."},{"t":"{i|Gain} of $20,000 in OCI","why":"A {i|disposal gain} goes to profit or loss."},{"t":"{i|Revenue} of $400,000","why":"Selling PPE is not revenue; only the {i|gain} is recognised."}],"sect":7,"part":"Derecognise / transfer"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"derecognise","lo":14,"special":"PPE leaves at **{a|carrying amount}**: {a|proceeds} minus {a|carrying amount} to P&L, and any **{e|revaluation surplus}** goes straight to {e|retained earnings}, never recycled.","id":"compare-1kumyt-3","sect":7,"part":"Derecognise / transfer"},{"type":"map","id":"map-8-present","bucket":"present","sect":8,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"type":"think","bucket":"present","lo":16,"scenario":"Two airlines report fleets of similar size. One carries its planes at cost; the other revalues them every year. One depreciates over 15 years; the other over 25.","prompt":"What would an analyst need from the notes to compare them fairly?","answer":["For each class: the **measurement basis**, **depreciation methods and useful lives**, and a **reconciliation** of what moved the {a|carrying amount}.","For the revaluer: **when** the valuation was done, **whether an independent valuer** was involved, the {a|carrying amount} **had the cost model been used**, and the **{e|revaluation surplus}** and its movement."],"id":"think-8oujv9","sect":8,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"present","lo":16,"title":"Disclosure checklist","body":["Last stop. Two companies with the same assets can report very different numbers (cost vs revaluation, 15 vs 25 years), so the notes must give a reader enough to compare them.","**For each class of PPE:**","1. measurement basis (cost or revaluation)","2. depreciation methods","3. useful lives or depreciation rates","4. {a|gross carrying amount} and {a|accumulated depreciation}, at the start and end of the period","5. a **reconciliation** of the {a|carrying amount}: additions, disposals, revaluations, impairment, depreciation, other movements","**Also:** restrictions on title and PPE **pledged** as security; spending on assets under construction; **contractual commitments** to buy PPE; compensation for impaired or lost items; nature and effect of **changes in estimates**.","**If revalued:** effective date; whether an **independent valuer** was involved; for each class, the {a|carrying amount} **had the cost model been used**; the **{e|revaluation surplus}**, its movement and any restrictions on distributing it.","- **Encouraged, not required:** idle PPE, fully depreciated items still in use, items retired from use, fair value of PPE carried at cost if materially different.","! A {e|revaluation surplus} is never “revenue”, so no disclosure of a surplus recognised as revenue can be required."],"id":"rule-rmzbio","sect":8,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"present","lo":16,"q":"Which of these PPE disclosures is **encouraged** but not required?","options":[{"t":"A reconciliation of the {a|carrying amount} for each class","why":"Required for every class."},{"t":"The {a|gross carrying amount} of fully depreciated PPE still in use","ok":true,"why":"Encouraged only, along with idle PPE and items retired from use."},{"t":"For revalued classes, the {a|carrying amount} had the cost model been used","why":"Required when PPE is revalued."},{"t":"Contractual commitments to buy PPE","why":"Required."}],"id":"mcq-h0ucut","sect":8,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"present","lo":16,"special":"Revalued PPE must also show the **cost-model carrying amount**, the valuation date, whether the valuer was independent, and the **{e|revaluation surplus}** movement.","id":"compare-1kumyt-4","sect":8,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"type":"examtypes","id":"examtypes","bucket":"wrap","sect":9,"part":"Wrap-up"},{"type":"summary","lo":16,"title":"PPE on one page","rows":[["What is PPE?","Tangible items held for use (production, supply, rental, admin) for more than one period."],["Land vs land improvements?","{a|Land} (price, duty, legal, clearing − salvage): not depreciated. Roads, fences, sprinklers: {a|land improvements}, depreciated."],["What goes into cost?","Price less discounts + duties + all costs to get it ready + PV of dismantling."],["What never goes into cost?","Training, admin, relocation, advertising, recoverable GST, abnormal waste. {i|Test-output sales} go to P&L."],["Depreciable amount?","{a|Cost} − residual value, over useful life, from when available for use, by component."],["Change in life or residual value?","Change in estimate: {a|carrying amount} over the remaining life, prospectively."],["Revaluation increase?","{e|Revaluation surplus (OCI)}; P&L only to reverse an earlier {x|P&L loss} on that asset."],["Revaluation decrease?","Use that asset’s {e|surplus} first (OCI), then P&L."],["Surplus later?","Transfer to {e|Retained earnings} (excess depreciation or on disposal), never through P&L."],["Disposal?","{a|Net proceeds} − {a|carrying amount}, to P&L. Not revenue."],["Disclosures?","Per class: basis, methods, lives, {a|gross amount} and {a|accumulated depreciation}, reconciliation. Revalued: date, valuer, cost-model {a|carrying amount}, {e|surplus}."]],"id":"summary-wcfgkg","bucket":"wrap","sect":9,"part":"Wrap-up"}],"quiz":{"cases":{"sigma":{"name":"Sigma Intelligence","source":"Official practice paper (Q26–30 case)","text":["Sigma Intelligence Pte Ltd (Sigma) is a fast-growing Singapore based AI software company that uses machine learning and natural language processing to develop software solutions that help logistics companies to optimise their supply chain operations.","During 20X3, Sigma decided to move out of their rented office buildings and invest in a bespoke long-term headquarters.","Sigma purchased a piece of land in the Punggol Digital District (PDD) sustainable business park and began work on constructing a sustainable and eco-friendly office building to house their 300 employees and an underground vault for its AI servers.","The existing old building on site was demolished and materials salvaged were sold for $0.25 million.","The following costs were incurred during 20X3:","• Land purchase price: $7,500k","• Stamp duty: $375k","• Legal fees: $150k","• Clearing of land and demolition of old building: $425k","• Construction of new HQ: office building: $15,500k","• Construction of new HQ: underground server room: $4,500k","• Cost of private road: $650k","• Installation of fencing: $95k","• Installation of sprinkler system: $135k","Sigma completed the construction of the new headquarters, and it was fully operational by the financial year ending 31 December 20X3. Sigma uses the revaluation model to value Property Plant and Equipment and depreciation is applied on a straight-line basis.","The expected useful life of the new headquarters is 50 years with a residual value of $0.750 million for the office building and $0.5m for the server room.","Note: Sigma applies a full year’s depreciation in the year of construction and fully complies with SFRS(I) 1-16, Property, Plant and Equipment."]},"tembusu":{"name":"Tembusu Manufacturing","text":["Tembusu Manufacturing Pte Ltd (Tembusu) has a financial year ending 31 December and uses the cost model for plant and machinery, depreciated straight-line.","On 1 April 20X5 Tembusu bought a CNC machine with a list price of $240,000, less a 10% trade discount. GST of 9% was paid and is recoverable from IRAS.","It also paid import duty of $6,000, delivery of $3,500, installation and testing of $8,000, and $4,000 to train operators. Parts produced during testing were sold for $1,500. Head office allocated $2,000 of admin overheads to the project.","Tembusu must dismantle the machine and restore the floor at the end of its life. The present value of that obligation on 1 April 20X5 is $12,900.","The machine was ready for use on 1 April 20X5. Useful life 8 years, residual value $22,400."]}},"questions":[{"id":"q1","case":"sigma","lo":14,"type":"land-cost","source":"Official practice paper Q26","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-16, Property, Plant and Equipment, identify which of the following is the amount Sigma should capitalise as the cost of land during 20X3? All figures are in $’000.","options":[{"t":"$8,050","why":"Leaves out the legal fees of 150. Legal fees on buying the {a|land} are part of its cost."},{"t":"$8,200","ok":true,"why":"7,500 + 375 + 150 + 425 − salvage 250 = 8,200."},{"t":"$8,450","pit":"no-salvage","why":"Forgets to deduct the 250 salvage proceeds from demolition."},{"t":"$9,080","pit":"li-as-land","why":"Adds the road, fencing and sprinklers (880) to {a|land}. They are {a|land improvements}, depreciated separately."}]},{"id":"q2","case":"sigma","lo":14,"type":"land-improvements","source":"Official practice paper Q27","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-16, identify how Sigma should treat the construction of the private road and the installation of fencing and sprinkler system costs incurred during the year ending 31 December 20X3?","options":[{"t":"$880,000 should be capitalised as land improvements and depreciated over the individual useful lives","ok":true,"why":"Road 650 + fencing 95 + sprinklers 135 = 880k. They have limited lives, so they are {a|land improvements}, capitalised and depreciated."},{"t":"$880,000 should be capitalised as cost of land and not depreciated as land has an indefinite useful life","pit":"li-as-land","why":"{a|Land} is never depreciated, but roads, fences and sprinklers wear out. They are {a|land improvements}."},{"t":"The landscaping costs of $135,000 should be expensed to the Statement of profit or loss and the costs of the private road and fencing should be capitalised and depreciated as land improvements","why":"A sprinkler system is a fire-safety installation with a limited life, not landscaping to expense. All three are {a|land improvements}."},{"t":"All of the $880,000 should be expensed to the Statement of profit or loss during the year","pit":"expense-li","why":"These costs bring benefits for many years, so they are capitalised, not expensed."}]},{"id":"q3","case":"sigma","lo":15,"type":"reval-surplus","source":"Official practice paper Q28","q":"There was no significant change in the fair values of the headquarters until end of 20X6. On 31 December 20X6, Sigma had the headquarters revalued at a fair value of $24 million. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-16, identify which of the following amounts should be recognised in the revaluation surplus on 31 December 20X6? All figures are in $’000.","options":[{"t":"$5,125","pit":"wrong-years","why":"Only 3 years of depreciation. With a full year in 20X3, 20X3–20X6 is 4 years: 1,500."},{"t":"$5,200","pit":"ignore-residual","why":"Ignores the residual values and charges only 3 years."},{"t":"$5,500","ok":true,"why":"Cost 15,500 + 4,500 = 20,000; residual 750 + 500 = 1,250. {x|Depreciation} (20,000 − 1,250) ÷ 50 = 375 a year × 4 = 1,500. {a|Carrying amount} 18,500; 24,000 − 18,500 = {e|5,500}."},{"t":"$6,375","why":"No correct working gives this. The {e|surplus} is fair value minus {a|carrying amount}: 24,000 − 18,500."}]},{"id":"q4","case":"sigma","lo":15,"type":"reval-surplus","source":"Official practice paper Q29","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-16, identify how should Sigma account for the revaluation surplus arising from the revaluation of the headquarters on 31 December 20X6?","options":[{"t":"The revaluation surplus should be recognised as income in the Statement of profit or loss","pit":"surplus-to-pl","why":"A revaluation gain on PPE is not income in P&L (unless it reverses an earlier {x|P&L loss}, which there wasn’t)."},{"t":"The revaluation surplus should be credited to retained earnings","pit":"surplus-to-re","why":"It goes through OCI into the {e|revaluation surplus}. Only later may it be transferred to {e|retained earnings}."},{"t":"The revaluation surplus should be treated as a deferred income until the asset is sold","why":"There is no “{l|deferred income}” for revaluations; it is recognised in OCI now."},{"t":"The revaluation surplus should be credited to other comprehensive income and accumulated in equity","ok":true,"why":"An increase goes to other comprehensive income and accumulates in equity as the {e|revaluation surplus}."}]},{"id":"q5","case":"sigma","lo":16,"type":"disclosure","source":"Official practice paper Q30","q":"With reference to SFRS (I) 1-16, identify which of the following is NOT required to be disclosed in the financial statements in relation to the revalued Property, Plant and Equipment?","options":[{"t":"The effective date of the revaluation","why":"Required for revalued PPE."},{"t":"Whether an independent valuer was involved in the revaluation","why":"Required for revalued PPE."},{"t":"For each revalued class of property, plant and equipment, the carrying amount that would have been recognised had the assets been carried under the cost model","why":"Required: users can compare with cost-model companies."},{"t":"The amount of the revaluation surplus recognised as revenue in the Statement of profit or loss during the period","ok":true,"why":"Not required, because a {e|revaluation surplus} is never recognised as revenue in P&L. This is the odd one out."}]},{"id":"q6","case":"tembusu","lo":14,"type":"asset-cost","q":"At what amount should Tembusu initially recognise the machine?","options":[{"t":"$244,900","pit":"test-proceeds-cost","why":"Deducts the {i|test-output sales}. Those go to profit or loss, not against cost."},{"t":"$246,400","ok":true,"why":"216,000 + 6,000 + 3,500 + 8,000 + dismantling 12,900 = 246,400."},{"t":"$233,500","pit":"no-dismantle","why":"Leaves out the present value of the {l|dismantling obligation}."},{"t":"$252,400","pit":"capitalise-training","why":"Includes {x|training} and {x|admin overheads}, which are expensed."}]},{"id":"q7","case":"tembusu","lo":14,"type":"asset-cost","q":"How should Tembusu treat the $1,500 from selling parts produced during testing?","options":[{"t":"Deduct it from the cost of the machine","pit":"test-proceeds-cost","why":"That was the old rule. Now {i|test-output sales} go to profit or loss."},{"t":"Recognise it in profit or loss","ok":true,"why":"{i|Proceeds from items produced while testing}, and their cost, go to profit or loss."},{"t":"Credit it to a {l|deferred income liability}","why":"There is no obligation to defer; it is recognised in profit or loss."},{"t":"Credit it to the {l|provision for dismantling}","why":"Unrelated to the {l|dismantling obligation}."}]},{"id":"q8","case":"tembusu","lo":14,"type":"depreciation","q":"What depreciation should Tembusu charge for 20X5?","options":[{"t":"$28,000","pit":"wrong-years","why":"A full year. The machine was available for use from 1 April, so 9 months."},{"t":"$21,000","ok":true,"why":"(246,400 − 22,400) ÷ 8 = 28,000 × 9/12 = 21,000."},{"t":"$23,100","pit":"ignore-residual","why":"Ignores the residual value."},{"t":"$0, because depreciation starts in the first full year","why":"Depreciation starts when the asset is available for use."}]},{"id":"q9","case":"tembusu","lo":14,"type":"estimate-change","q":"On 1 January 20X7 Tembusu decides the machine has only 4 more years of use; the residual value is unchanged. What is the depreciation for 20X7?","options":[{"t":"$28,000","why":"Keeps the old estimate. The revised life must be used from the date of change."},{"t":"$43,750","ok":true,"why":"{a|Carrying amount} at 31 Dec 20X6: 246,400 − 21,000 − 28,000 = 197,400. (197,400 − 22,400) ÷ 4 = 43,750."},{"t":"$49,350","pit":"ignore-residual","why":"Spreads the whole {a|carrying amount} over the remaining life, forgetting the residual value."},{"t":"$43,750, plus a catch-up charge for 20X5–20X6 in {e|retained earnings}","pit":"retro-estimate","why":"No catch-up: a change in estimate is applied prospectively only."}]},{"id":"q10","case":"tembusu","lo":14,"type":"disposal","q":"On 1 January 20X7 Tembusu revised the machine’s remaining useful life to 4 years (residual value unchanged). It sells the machine on 31 December 20X7 for $150,000. What is the gain or loss?","options":[{"t":"Loss of $3,650","ok":true,"why":"{a|Carrying amount} 197,400 − 43,750 = 153,650. 150,000 − 153,650 = (3,650)."},{"t":"Loss of $96,400","pit":"cost-not-ca","why":"Compares {a|proceeds} with {a|cost}. Use the {a|carrying amount}."},{"t":"Loss of $19,400","why":"Uses the old {x|depreciation charge} for 20X7, ignoring the revised life."},{"t":"Revenue of $150,000","why":"Selling PPE is not revenue; only the {i|gain} or {x|loss} is recognised."}]},{"id":"q11","lo":15,"type":"reval-entry","source":"Official practice paper Q10","q":"Scout Construction Pte Ltd (Scout) has its financial year end on 31 December. On 1 January 20X4, Scout purchased a piece of machinery for $250,000. The machinery has an expected useful life of 10 years and a residual value of $25,000. On 31 December 20X5, the machinery had a fair value of $220,000. Scout uses the straight-line method to calculate depreciation and the revaluation model to account for PPE after initial recognition. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-16, Property, Plant and Equipment, identify which of the following journal entries correctly accounts for the revaluation of the machinery at 31 December 20X5?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Property, Plant and Equipment","amount":30000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Gain on disposal","amount":20000,"el":"i"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Property, Plant and Equipment – accumulated depreciation","amount":50000,"el":"a"}],"why":"A revaluation is not a disposal, and an increase never creates a debit to an {x|expense} or {i|gain} account."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation deficit","amount":5000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Property, Plant and Equipment","amount":5000,"el":"a"}],"why":"Fair value is above the {a|carrying amount}, so there is an increase, not a deficit."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Property, Plant and Equipment – accumulated depreciation","amount":50000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Property, Plant and Equipment","amount":30000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus – other comprehensive income","amount":20000,"el":"e"}],"pit":"ignore-residual","why":"Forgets the residual value: {x|depreciation} is (250,000 − 25,000) ÷ 10 = 22,500, not 25,000."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Property, Plant and Equipment – accumulated depreciation","amount":45000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Property, Plant and Equipment","amount":30000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus – other comprehensive income","amount":15000,"el":"e"}],"ok":true,"why":"{x|Depreciation} 22,500 × 2 = {a|accumulated depreciation} 45,000; {a|carrying amount} 205,000; fair value 220,000 → {e|surplus} 15,000 to OCI. {a|PPE gross amount} falls 250,000 → 220,000."}]},{"id":"q12","lo":15,"type":"reval-decrease","q":"A revalued building has a {a|carrying amount} of $900,000 and a {e|revaluation surplus} of $50,000. Another building in the same class has a {e|surplus} of $200,000. The first building is revalued to $820,000. How much goes to P&L?","options":[{"t":"$0: the decrease is absorbed by the class’s total {e|surplus}","why":"A decrease can only use the {e|surplus} on the same asset."},{"t":"$30,000","ok":true,"why":"Fall 900,000 − 820,000 = 80,000. 50,000 uses up this building’s {e|surplus} (OCI); 30,000 to P&L."},{"t":"$80,000","pit":"all-to-pl-down","why":"Ignores this building’s own {e|surplus}, which absorbs the first part."},{"t":"$50,000","why":"That is the part that goes to OCI, not P&L."}]},{"id":"q13","lo":15,"type":"reval-surplus","q":"A company revalues one of its five office buildings to fair value. Which statement is correct?","options":[{"t":"It may revalue just that one building if it has risen most in value","why":"Cherry-picking is not allowed: the whole class is revalued."},{"t":"It must revalue all the office buildings in that class","ok":true,"why":"If one item is revalued, the entire class to which it belongs must be."},{"t":"It must revalue all its PPE, including vehicles","why":"The choice is per class; other classes can stay at cost."},{"t":"The gain goes to P&L because the building is held for use","pit":"surplus-to-pl","why":"Revaluation gains go to OCI."}]},{"id":"q14","lo":15,"type":"reval-surplus","q":"After revaluing a building, a company charges depreciation of $15,000 a year; depreciation on original cost would have been $10,000. It chooses to transfer the excess each year. Which entry is correct?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation surplus","amount":5000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Depreciation expense","amount":5000,"el":"x"}],"pit":"recycle-surplus","why":"Reduces {x|depreciation} in P&L. The {e|surplus} is never recycled through P&L."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation surplus","amount":5000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":5000,"el":"e"}],"ok":true,"why":"15,000 − 10,000 = 5,000, moved directly from the {e|surplus} to {e|retained earnings}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation surplus","amount":5000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Other income (P&L)","amount":5000,"el":"i"}],"pit":"recycle-surplus","why":"The transfer is equity to equity, not income."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":5000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus","amount":5000,"el":"e"}],"why":"Reversed: the {e|surplus} is reduced as it is realised through use."}]},{"id":"q15","lo":14,"type":"depreciation","q":"Which depreciation method is NOT permitted for PPE?","options":[{"t":"Straight-line","why":"Permitted."},{"t":"Diminishing balance","why":"Permitted."},{"t":"Units of production","why":"Permitted."},{"t":"A method based on the revenue the asset generates","ok":true,"why":"Revenue-based methods are not allowed: {i|revenue} reflects price and volume, not consumption of the asset."}]},{"id":"q16","lo":14,"type":"subsequent-cost","q":"An airline replaces the engines of a plane after 4 years. The engines were recognised as a separate component costing $15,000,000, depreciated over 10 years. What happens to the old engines?","options":[{"t":"Their {a|carrying amount} of $9,000,000 is derecognised","ok":true,"why":"15,000,000 − 4 × 1,500,000 = 9,000,000 is derecognised; the new engines are capitalised."},{"t":"They stay in the cost of the plane until the plane is sold","why":"A replaced part is derecognised, or the asset would include two sets of engines."},{"t":"Their original cost of $15,000,000 is expensed","pit":"cost-not-ca","why":"Derecognise the {a|carrying amount}, not the {a|cost}; part of it has already been depreciated."},{"t":"The new engines are expensed as a repair","why":"Replacing a significant part meets the recognition tests: capitalise."}]},{"id":"q17","lo":14,"type":"asset-cost","q":"A company swaps one of its delivery trucks for a similar truck from another company, and the exchange lacks commercial substance. How is the new truck measured?","options":[{"t":"At the fair value of the truck received","why":"Fair value applies only when the exchange has commercial substance."},{"t":"At the {a|carrying amount} of the truck given up","ok":true,"why":"Without commercial substance, the new asset is measured at the {a|carrying amount} of the asset given up. No gain."},{"t":"At the original cost of the truck given up","why":"Its {a|carrying amount} (net of depreciation), not its {a|original cost}."},{"t":"At nil, as no cash changed hands","why":"The new truck is an {a|asset} and must be measured."}]},{"id":"q18","lo":14,"type":"subsequent-cost","q":"Which of these costs should be expensed rather than capitalised?","options":[{"t":"Replacing the worn-out lining of a furnace, recognised as a separate component","why":"Replacing a significant component is capitalised."},{"t":"Routine monthly servicing of factory machines","ok":true,"why":"Day-to-day servicing is expensed as incurred."},{"t":"Installing a new lift in an office building","why":"A new significant part with future benefits: capitalise."},{"t":"Architect’s fees for designing a new warehouse","why":"Professional fees directly attributable to construction are part of cost."}]},{"id":"q19","lo":14,"type":"land-cost","q":"A company buys land for a car park for $4,200,000, paying stamp duty of $126,000 and legal fees of $60,000. It then lays the car park surface and lighting for $140,000. What is the cost of {a|land}?","options":[{"t":"$4,200,000","why":"Stamp duty and legal fees are part of the cost of acquiring the {a|land}."},{"t":"$4,386,000","ok":true,"why":"4,200,000 + 126,000 + 60,000. The car park surface and lighting are {a|land improvements}."},{"t":"$4,526,000","pit":"li-as-land","why":"The car park and lighting wear out: {a|land improvements}, depreciated."},{"t":"$4,260,000","why":"Stamp duty is also part of the cost."}]}]},"exam":{"cases":{"lcv":{"name":"Lion City Venture","source":"Official practice paper (Q21–25 case, PPE lines only)","text":["The following balances have been extracted from the trial balance of Lion City Venture (LCV) on 31 December 20X5, the date of the financial year end, before adjustments. All figures are in $’000.","• {a|Building}: 63,500 (debit). {a|Accumulated depreciation} to 31 Dec 20X4 – building: 20,000 (credit).","• {a|Machinery}: 13,750 (debit). {a|Accumulated depreciation} to 31 Dec 20X4 – machinery: 2,500 (credit).","LCV applies the cost model on building and machinery held for business. The building was depreciated over 50 years on a straight-line basis with an expected residual value of $13,500. Machinery was depreciated over 5 years on a straight-line basis with an expected residual value of $1,250. LCV charges all depreciation to other operating expenses."]},"marina":{"name":"Marina Logistics","text":["Marina Logistics Pte Ltd (Marina) has a 31 December year end and uses the revaluation model for its buildings, eliminating {a|accumulated depreciation} on revaluation. It transfers excess depreciation from the {e|revaluation surplus} to {e|retained earnings} each year.","Marina bought its warehouse building on 1 January 20X1 for $2,400,000 (ignore land). Useful life 40 years, no residual value, straight-line.","Fair value was $2,700,000 at 31 December 20X4 and $1,800,000 at 31 December 20X8. There were no other revaluations; the useful life is unchanged.","On 31 December 20X9 Marina sold the warehouse for $1,800,000."]}},"questions":[{"id":"x1","case":"lcv","lo":14,"type":"depreciation","source":"Official practice paper Q22","q":"Identify which of the following is the correct depreciation amount to be shown in the Statement of profit or loss for LCV on 31 December 20X5? All figures are in $'000.","options":[{"t":"$3,120","why":"Divides the {a|carrying amounts} by the full lives and ignores residual values: 870 + 2,250."},{"t":"$3,500","ok":true,"why":"Building (63,500 − 13,500) ÷ 50 = 1,000; machinery (13,750 − 1,250) ÷ 5 = 2,500. Total {x|depreciation} 3,500."},{"t":"$3,750","pit":"ignore-residual","why":"Ignores the machinery’s residual value: 13,750 ÷ 5 = 2,750."},{"t":"$4,020","pit":"ignore-residual","why":"Ignores both residual values."}]},{"id":"x2","case":"lcv","lo":14,"type":"depreciation","q":"What is the {a|carrying amount} of the building at 31 December 20X5? All figures are in $’000.","options":[{"t":"$43,500","why":"Forgets the 20X5 {x|depreciation}: the {a|accumulated depreciation} in the trial balance is only to 31 Dec 20X4."},{"t":"$42,500","ok":true,"why":"63,500 − (20,000 + 1,000) = 42,500."},{"t":"$42,230","pit":"ignore-residual","why":"Charges 20X5 {x|depreciation} without deducting the residual value."},{"t":"$62,500","why":"Deducts only this year’s {x|depreciation} and ignores the {a|accumulated depreciation brought forward}."}]},{"id":"x3","case":"lcv","lo":14,"type":"depreciation","q":"What is the {a|carrying amount} of the machinery at 31 December 20X5? All figures are in $’000.","options":[{"t":"$8,750","ok":true,"why":"13,750 − (2,500 + 2,500) = 8,750."},{"t":"$11,250","why":"Forgets the 20X5 {x|depreciation}."},{"t":"$8,500","pit":"ignore-residual","why":"Ignores the residual value in the 20X5 {x|depreciation charge}."},{"t":"$12,500","why":"That is the depreciable amount, not the {a|carrying amount}."}]},{"id":"x4","case":"lcv","lo":15,"type":"reval-entry","q":"Suppose instead that LCV used the revaluation model for buildings, and the building’s fair value at 31 December 20X5 (after the year’s depreciation) was $50,000k. Which entry records the revaluation? All figures are in $’000.","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accumulated depreciation – building","amount":21000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Building","amount":13500,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":7500,"el":"e"}],"ok":true,"why":"{a|Carrying amount} 42,500; fair value 50,000 → {e|surplus} 7,500. Eliminate {a|accumulated depreciation} 21,000; {a|building gross amount} 63,500 → 50,000."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accumulated depreciation – building","amount":20000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Building","amount":13500,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":6500,"el":"e"}],"why":"Uses the {a|accumulated depreciation brought forward} and forgets the 20X5 {x|depreciation}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accumulated depreciation – building","amount":21000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Building","amount":13500,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Gain on revaluation (P&L)","amount":7500,"el":"i"}],"pit":"surplus-to-pl","why":"A revaluation increase goes to OCI, not P&L."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Building","amount":7500,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":7500,"el":"e"}],"pit":"surplus-to-re","why":"The {e|surplus} is credited to OCI, not {e|retained earnings}, and {a|accumulated depreciation} must be dealt with."}]},{"id":"x5","case":"lcv","lo":14,"type":"disposal","q":"On 1 January 20X6 LCV sells all its machinery for $8,000k. What is recognised in profit or loss? All figures are in $’000.","options":[{"t":"Loss of $5,750","pit":"cost-not-ca","why":"Compares {a|proceeds} with {a|cost} instead of {a|carrying amount}."},{"t":"Loss of $3,250","why":"Uses the {a|carrying amount} before the 20X5 {x|depreciation}."},{"t":"Loss of $750","ok":true,"why":"8,000 − {a|carrying amount} 8,750 = (750)."},{"t":"Revenue of $8,000","why":"Selling PPE is not revenue."}]},{"id":"x6","case":"marina","lo":15,"type":"reval-surplus","q":"What {e|revaluation surplus} does Marina recognise on 31 December 20X4?","options":[{"t":"$300,000","pit":"cost-not-ca","why":"Compares fair value with {a|cost}. Use the {a|carrying amount}."},{"t":"$540,000","ok":true,"why":"2,400,000 − 4 × 60,000 = 2,160,000. 2,700,000 − 2,160,000 = 540,000."},{"t":"$480,000","pit":"wrong-years","why":"Only 3 years of depreciation; 20X1–20X4 is 4."},{"t":"$540,000, recognised in profit or loss","pit":"surplus-to-pl","why":"Right amount, wrong home: OCI."}]},{"id":"x7","case":"marina","lo":15,"type":"reval-surplus","q":"Which entries does Marina record for depreciation and the reserve transfer in 20X5?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Depreciation expense","amount":60000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":60000,"el":"a"}],"why":"{x|Depreciation} is now on the revalued amount, and the transfer is missing."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Depreciation expense","amount":75000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":75000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation surplus","amount":15000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":15000,"el":"e"}],"ok":true,"why":"2,700,000 ÷ 36 = 75,000. Excess over 60,000 = 15,000, {e|surplus} → {e|retained earnings}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Depreciation expense","amount":60000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation surplus","amount":15000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":75000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"recycle-surplus","why":"Charges part of the {x|depreciation} to the {e|surplus}, so P&L only bears the old amount. P&L takes the full charge on the revalued amount."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Depreciation expense","amount":75000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":75000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation surplus","amount":15000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Other income (P&L)","amount":15000,"el":"i"}],"pit":"recycle-surplus","why":"The transfer goes to {e|retained earnings} directly, not through P&L."}]},{"id":"x8","case":"marina","lo":15,"type":"reval-decrease","q":"Which entry records the revaluation on 31 December 20X8?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":300000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation loss (P&L)","amount":600000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Building","amount":900000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"all-to-pl-down","why":"Ignores the {e|surplus} still left on this building, which absorbs the first part of the fall."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":300000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":600000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Building","amount":900000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"all-to-oci-down","why":"OCI can only absorb the {e|surplus} left (480,000); the rest goes to P&L."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":300000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":480000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation loss (P&L)","amount":120000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Building","amount":900000,"el":"a"}],"ok":true,"why":"{a|Carrying amount} 2,700,000 − 4 × 75,000 = 2,400,000; fall 600,000. {e|Surplus} left 540,000 − 4 × 15,000 = 480,000 → OCI; 120,000 → P&L."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":300000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":540000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Building","amount":900000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":-60000,"el":"e"}],"why":"Uses the original {e|surplus}, forgetting four years of transfers, and credits {e|retained earnings} with a balancing figure."}]},{"id":"x9","case":"marina","lo":15,"type":"reval-decrease","q":"If Marina had NOT made the yearly excess-depreciation transfers, how much of the 20X8 fall would go to profit or loss?","options":[{"t":"$120,000","why":"That is the answer with transfers. Without them, the {e|surplus} is larger."},{"t":"$60,000","ok":true,"why":"The {e|surplus} would still be 540,000, so OCI absorbs 540,000 of the 600,000 fall; 60,000 to P&L."},{"t":"$600,000","pit":"all-to-pl-down","why":"Ignores the {e|surplus} entirely."},{"t":"$900,000","pit":"cost-not-ca","why":"Compares the two fair values and ignores {x|depreciation} since 20X4."}]},{"id":"x10","case":"marina","lo":14,"type":"disposal","q":"What is recognised in profit or loss when Marina sells the warehouse on 31 December 20X9?","options":[{"t":"$0: it is sold at its fair value","why":"It is carried at 1,800,000 less a year’s {x|depreciation}, so a {i|gain} arises."},{"t":"Gain of $56,250","ok":true,"why":"{x|Depreciation} 20X9 1,800,000 ÷ 32 = 56,250; {a|carrying amount} 1,743,750. 1,800,000 − 1,743,750 = {i|56,250}. No {e|surplus} is left to transfer."},{"t":"Loss of $60,000","why":"Uses the {a|carrying amount} as if the building had never been revalued."},{"t":"Gain of $536,250","pit":"recycle-surplus","why":"Adds back the {e|surplus} used up in 20X8. Revaluation movements in OCI are never recycled to P&L."}]},{"id":"x11","lo":14,"type":"asset-cost","q":"A company builds its own warehouse over 18 months. Costs: materials $2,400,000, labour $900,000, abnormal waste from a flood $120,000, architect’s fees $150,000, interest on a loan taken to fund construction $80,000, opening ceremony $25,000, general admin overheads $60,000. During testing, goods stored for a client earned $30,000. What is the cost of the warehouse?","options":[{"t":"$3,500,000","pit":"test-proceeds-cost","why":"Deducts the {i|testing income}, which goes to P&L."},{"t":"$3,530,000","ok":true,"why":"2,400,000 + 900,000 + 150,000 + borrowing costs 80,000 (qualifying asset) = 3,530,000."},{"t":"$3,650,000","pit":"capitalise-training","why":"{x|Abnormal waste} is expensed."},{"t":"$3,450,000","why":"Borrowing costs on a qualifying asset are capitalised."}]},{"id":"x12","lo":14,"type":"subsequent-cost","q":"An aircraft is depreciated by components: airframe $42,000,000 over 25 years, engines $15,000,000 over 10 years, cabin $3,000,000 over 5 years (no residual values). At the end of year 4 the engines are replaced with new ones costing $18,000,000, with a 10-year life. What is the depreciation for year 5?","options":[{"t":"$3,780,000","why":"Uses the old engines’ charge. The new engines are depreciated on their own cost."},{"t":"$4,080,000","ok":true,"why":"Airframe 1,680,000 + new engines 18,000,000 ÷ 10 = 1,800,000 + cabin 600,000 = 4,080,000. The old engines ({a|carrying amount} 9,000,000) are derecognised."},{"t":"$5,580,000","why":"Keeps depreciating the old engines too. They are derecognised on replacement."},{"t":"$3,120,000","why":"Ignores components and spreads everything over the airframe’s life."}]},{"id":"x13","lo":15,"type":"reval-entry","q":"A machine under the revaluation model had a $25,000 {x|revaluation loss} charged to P&L last year (it had no {e|surplus}). This year its {a|carrying amount} is $410,000 and fair value is $480,000. {a|Accumulated depreciation} has been eliminated already, so the net increase is posted to the asset. Which entry is correct?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Machine","amount":70000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":70000,"el":"e"}],"why":"Part of the increase reverses last year’s {x|P&L loss} on this same asset, so that part goes to P&L."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Machine","amount":70000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation gain (P&L)","amount":25000,"el":"i"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":45000,"el":"e"}],"ok":true,"why":"480,000 − 410,000 = 70,000: 25,000 reverses the earlier {x|loss} in P&L, 45,000 to OCI."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Machine","amount":70000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation gain (P&L)","amount":70000,"el":"i"}],"pit":"surplus-to-pl","why":"Only the reversal of the earlier loss goes to P&L."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Machine","amount":70000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":25000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":45000,"el":"e"}],"pit":"surplus-to-re","why":"The reversal goes through P&L, not straight to {e|retained earnings}."}]},{"id":"x14","lo":14,"type":"asset-cost","q":"A company exchanges a truck ({a|carrying amount} $30,000, fair value $34,000) for a similar truck. The exchange lacks commercial substance. Which entry is correct?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Truck (new)","amount":34000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Truck (old, net)","amount":30000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Gain on exchange","amount":4000,"el":"i"}],"why":"Fair value and a {i|gain} apply only when the exchange has commercial substance."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Truck (new)","amount":30000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Truck (old, net)","amount":30000,"el":"a"}],"ok":true,"why":"No commercial substance: the new truck takes the {a|carrying amount} of the old one. No {i|gain}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Truck (new)","amount":34000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Truck (old, net)","amount":30000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":4000,"el":"e"}],"pit":"surplus-to-re","why":"An exchange is not a revaluation; and here it is at {a|carrying amount}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Loss on exchange","amount":30000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Truck (old, net)","amount":30000,"el":"a"}],"why":"The new truck is an {a|asset}; nothing is lost."}]},{"id":"x15","lo":14,"type":"asset-cost","q":"On 1 January a company completes an offshore platform for $5,000,000. It must remove it in 20 years at an estimated $1,000,000; the discount rate is 6%. Straight-line, no residual value. What is the total charge to P&L for year 1 relating to the platform and its {l|removal obligation} (nearest dollar)?","options":[{"t":"$250,000","pit":"no-dismantle","why":"Leaves the {l|dismantling obligation} out of cost and ignores the {x|unwinding}."},{"t":"$265,590","why":"Right {x|depreciation}, but forgets the {x|unwinding} of the discount on the {l|provision} (a {x|finance cost})."},{"t":"$284,298","ok":true,"why":"PV 1,000,000 ÷ 1.06^20 = 311,805; {a|cost} 5,311,805; {x|depreciation} 265,590 + {x|unwinding} 311,805 × 6% = 18,708 → 284,298."},{"t":"$300,000","why":"Adds the undiscounted removal cost. The obligation is measured at present value."}]}]},"prepared":true},{"id":"investment-property","code":"B1.3","title":"Investment Property","subtitle":"SFRS(I) 1-40 · fair value model and transfers","los":{"17":"Investment property measurement methods","18":"Transfers to and from investment property","19":"Investment property disclosures"},"accounts":[{"name":"Investment property","el":"a"},{"name":"Building (PPE)","el":"a"},{"name":"Accumulated depreciation: building","el":"a"},{"name":"Inventory","el":"a"},{"name":"Cash","el":"a"},{"name":"Fair value gain on investment property","el":"i"},{"name":"Fair value loss on investment property","el":"x"},{"name":"Gain on transfer","el":"i"},{"name":"Loss on transfer","el":"x"},{"name":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","el":"e"},{"name":"Retained earnings","el":"e"},{"name":"Depreciation expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Rental income","el":"i"},{"name":"Legal fees expense","el":"x"}],"map":{"classify":{"label":"IP vs PPE vs inventory vs lease receivable","star":"Why the property is held decides the standard"},"recognise":{"label":"Probable benefits + reliable cost"},"initial":{"label":"Cost incl. transaction costs"},"subsequent":{"label":"FV model → P&L, or cost model","star":"Fair value changes go to P&L, with no depreciation"},"derecognise":{"label":"Transfers in and out; disposal","star":"Change in use drives transfers: PPE → IP gain to OCI, inventory → IP to P&L"},"present":{"label":"FV disclosed even under cost model"}},"preamble":["Welcome to the landlord’s chapter. **Investment property** is land or buildings held to earn rent or to grow in value, not to use or to sell. Think of it as the landlord who doesn’t do the cleaning: PPE goes to work every day, while investment property sits there collecting rent and (hopefully) getting more expensive.","You already know PPE, so a lot of this trip is familiar road. Here is the route, with ★ on the bits that are new:","1. First we **classify**: why is the property held? That one question decides the standard. ★ special","2. Then we **recognise** it and measure it at **cost**, transaction costs included. Pretty vanilla: same as PPE.","3. Next, every year end: the **fair value model** or the **cost model**, one for the whole portfolio. ★ special","4. Then it changes jobs: **transfers** in and out on a change in use, and finally **disposal**. ★ special","5. Last stop: **presentation and disclosure**, where fair value turns up even under the cost model.","+ The big idea: under the fair value model there is **no {x|depreciation}**, and every change in fair value goes to **profit or loss**. Not OCI, as for a PPE revaluation. Hold on to that and half the exam questions answer themselves."],"intros":{"classify":"First stop, and the most important one. Before any numbers we ask one question: **why** is this property held? Sell it → {a|inventory}; use it → {a|PPE}; finance-lease it out → {a|lease receivable}; rent it out or sit on it while it grows → {a|investment property}. ★ This is the special part: we classify by purpose, not by what the bricks look like.","recognise":"Now that we know *which* properties are investment property, when does one get onto the balance sheet? Pretty vanilla: the same two tests as PPE (probable benefits, reliable cost), and the same logic for money spent later. Fresh paint is an {x|expense}; a new lift is an {a|asset}. Nothing special here, so enjoy the breather.","initial":"It’s on the books, so time to put a first number on it. This is PPE all over again: purchase price plus directly attributable costs such as stamp duty and legal fees. The one thing to watch: even if the fair value model follows, day 1 is still **{a|cost}**, so transaction costs are capitalised. The launch party and the empty-floor blues stay out.","subsequent":"We have our starting cost, so what happens at every year end after that? ★ This is where investment property goes its own way: pick **one model** for the whole portfolio. Under the fair value model there is **no {x|depreciation}**, and every swing in value, up or down, lands in **P&L**, never OCI. The cost model is just PPE in a different outfit, with fair value disclosed.","derecognise":"We can measure it while it sits still, so what happens when it changes jobs or leaves? ★ Special again: property only moves in or out on an actual **change in use**, not a change of heart in the boardroom. Where the gain goes depends on where the property came from (PPE → IP to OCI, inventory → IP to P&L), and on the way out fair value becomes {a|deemed cost}. Selling it at the end is vanilla: {a|net proceeds} − {a|carrying amount}, to P&L.","present":"Last stop: showing it off in the accounts. Mostly routine: its own line on the balance sheet, {i|rental income} in P&L, and a pile of notes on valuations. The one to remember: cost-model companies must still **disclose fair value**, so nobody gets to hide a goldmine at its 1990s price."},"pitfalls":{"fv-to-oci":"Sending investment property {i|fair value gains} or {x|losses} to OCI (they go to P&L).","fv-to-re":"Taking an investment property {i|fair value gain} straight to {e|retained earnings}.","depreciate-fv":"Depreciating {a|investment property} under the fair value model.","stale-ca":"Leaving investment property at last year’s fair value (or the trial balance amount) instead of fair value at the reporting date.","misclassify-ppe":"Calling owner-occupied property (offices, staff housing, hotels) investment property.","misclassify-inv":"Calling a developer’s property held for sale investment property.","expense-tc":"Leaving legal fees and stamp duty out of the {a|cost} of investment property.","ppe-ip-to-pl":"PPE → investment property: taking the increase to P&L instead of the {e|revaluation surplus} (OCI).","inv-ip-to-oci":"Inventory → investment property: taking the difference to OCI instead of P&L.","intention-transfer":"Transferring property on a change of intention alone, without a change in use.","deemed-cost-old":"After a transfer out of investment property, using an old value instead of fair value at the date of change as {a|deemed cost}.","recycle-surplus":"Recycling the {e|revaluation surplus} from a PPE → IP transfer through P&L on disposal.","cost-model-no-fv":"Forgetting that fair value is still disclosed under the cost model."},"examTypes":[{"id":"classify-ip","title":"Which of these properties is investment property?","pitfall":"Calling owner-occupied offices, staff housing, a hotel or a developer’s stock investment property.","see":"classify"},{"id":"initial-cost","title":"What is the initial cost of the investment property?","pitfall":"Leaving out legal fees and stamp duty, or capitalising start-up costs.","see":"initial"},{"id":"fv-entry","title":"Which entry records the change in fair value? (practice Q20)","pitfall":"Crediting OCI or {e|retained earnings} instead of P&L.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"model-effect","title":"What is the effect on profit of the fair value vs the cost model?","pitfall":"Depreciating investment property under the fair value model.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"sfp-amount","title":"At what amount is investment property shown in the SFP?","pitfall":"Leaving it at the trial balance amount.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"transfer-ppe","title":"An owner-occupied building is leased out. How is the transfer recorded?","pitfall":"Taking the increase to P&L instead of OCI.","see":"derecognise"},{"id":"transfer-other","title":"Inventory → IP, IP → PPE, or no transfer at all?","pitfall":"Inventory → IP difference to OCI; transferring on intention alone.","see":"derecognise"},{"id":"disposal","title":"What gain arises on selling investment property?","pitfall":"Ignoring selling costs, or recycling an old {e|revaluation surplus} through P&L.","see":"derecognise"},{"id":"disclosure","title":"Which disclosure is required (cost or fair value model)?","pitfall":"Forgetting that fair value is still disclosed under the cost model.","see":"present"}],"pages":[{"type":"map","id":"map-overview","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"intro","lo":17,"title":"Investment property, the Socratic way","body":["Most of this chapter turns on one question: **why does the entity hold this property?** Get that right and the rest follows.","Each idea starts with a question. Think it through, then reveal the answer. The rule comes after, once you have half-worked it out.","Account names are coloured by element. Tap **Legend** at the top any time. At the end: a chapter quiz in exam style, then a harder exam."],"id":"intro-1v0dxe","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"map","id":"map-1-classify","bucket":"classify","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"think","bucket":"classify","lo":17,"scenario":"Merlion Holdings owns three properties: the office tower its own staff work in, a warehouse it rents out to a logistics company, and a plot of land in Jurong it bought because it expects land prices to rise.","prompt":"Which of these is working *inside* the business, like a machine, and which is really an investment that just happens to be made of bricks?","answer":["The **office tower** is used to run the business. It wears out as it is used, like any other {a|PPE}.","The **warehouse** earns rent and the **land** is held for growth in value. Neither is used in operations. Their benefit comes from the property itself: cash rent, or a rising price.","Those two are **{a|investment property}**. The standard gives them their own rules because what matters to a user is their **value**, not how fast they wear out.","Short version: {a|PPE} clocks in for work every day; {a|investment property} just sits there earning rent and looking expensive."],"id":"think-9fjidv","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"classify","lo":17,"title":"What is investment property?","body":["You have just sorted three properties by why they are held. Here is the definition that does the sorting, so you can apply it to any building the exam throws at you.","{a|Investment property} = land or a building (or part of one) held **to earn rentals, for capital appreciation, or both**, rather than for **use** in the business or for **sale** in the ordinary course of business.","It can be owned, or held by a lessee as a {a|right-of-use asset}.","Examples of investment property:","- land held for **long-term capital appreciation**","- land held for a **currently undetermined** future use","- a building leased out under an **operating lease**","- a **vacant** building held to be leased out","- property being **built or developed** for future use as investment property","+ The test is the **purpose** of holding it, not what the property looks like."],"id":"rule-1h8hh9","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"think","bucket":"classify","lo":17,"scenario":"Two more cases. (1) A property developer has 40 finished condo units that it is marketing to buyers. (2) A manufacturer owns a block of flats that it lets to its own factory workers at a below-market rent.","prompt":"Both earn money from property. Are either of them investment property?","answer":["Neither. The developer’s units are held **for sale in the ordinary course of business**: they are its stock, so they are {a|Inventory} (SFRS(I) 1-2).","The staff flats support the manufacturer’s own operations. Property **occupied by employees** is owner-occupied, so it is {a|PPE} (SFRS(I) 1-16), whether or not the staff pay market rent."],"id":"think-1sckcc","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"classify","lo":17,"title":"What is not investment property","body":["The definition has edges. The developer’s condos and the staff flats both earn money from property, yet neither qualifies: these are the three look-alikes and where each one goes instead.","- Property held **for sale** in the ordinary course of business → **{a|inventory}** (SFRS(I) 1-2).","- **Owner-occupied** property (offices, factories, staff housing) → **{a|PPE}** (SFRS(I) 1-16).","- Property leased to another party under a **finance lease** → the lessor derecognises it and holds a {a|Lease receivable} instead.","! Staff quarters are the classic trap. Employees living there makes it owner-occupied, even if they pay a market rent. Your staff are not tenants in disguise."],"id":"rule-1xedqy","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"classify","lo":17,"title":"One question decides the standard","intro":"This is the chart from your notes. Every property starts at the same question, and the answer sends it to one of four standards.","notes":{"q":"The only test is **purpose**: why the entity holds it. What the property looks like doesn’t matter.","inv":"Held **for sale** in the ordinary course of business: a developer’s stock, so {a|inventory}. Lower of cost and NRV.","ppe":"{a|PPE}: used by the owner: factory, office, admin, and **staff housing** too. Depreciated.","rec":"A **finance lease** is in substance a sale on credit. The lessor removes the property and holds a {a|Lease receivable}.","ip":"{a|Investment property}: held **to earn rent, for growth in value, or both**. Includes vacant buildings held to let and land with an undecided future use."},"walk":[{"nodes":["q"],"text":"Start with one question: **why** is the land or building held?"},{"nodes":["inv"],"text":"To **sell** in the normal course of business → {a|inventory}. Think of a developer’s unsold condo units."},{"nodes":["ppe"],"text":"To **use** itself → {a|PPE}. Offices, factories and staff quarters all count as use."},{"nodes":["rec"],"text":"Leased out on a **finance lease** → the lessor holds a {a|receivable}, not the building."},{"nodes":["ip"],"text":"For **rent or growth** → {a|investment property}. This is the only branch that gets SFRS(I) 1-40."}],"caption":"Same building, four possible answers: the purpose decides.","id":"diagram-x8lmqt","sect":1,"part":"Classify","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:475px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 432.00 225.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 214.44)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-214.44 421.11,-214.44 421.11,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>q\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M99.78,-118.82C99.78,-118.82 12,-118.82 12,-118.82 6,-118.82 0,-112.82 0,-106.82 0,-106.82 0,-94.82 0,-94.82 0,-88.82 6,-82.82 12,-82.82 12,-82.82 99.78,-82.82 99.78,-82.82 105.78,-82.82 111.78,-88.82 111.78,-94.82 111.78,-94.82 111.78,-106.82 111.78,-106.82 111.78,-112.82 105.78,-118.82 99