as in a hotel \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"classify","lo":17,"q":"Which property should be classified as investment property by its owner?","options":[{"t":"A hotel the company owns and runs, with housekeeping, a restaurant and a front desk","why":"Significant services to guests: owner-occupied {a|PPE}."},{"t":"An office block leased to tenants, with security and cleaning of common areas provided by the owner","ok":true,"why":"Those services are insignificant to the arrangement as a whole, so the block is {a|investment property}."},{"t":"A block of flats occupied by the company’s staff, who pay market rent","why":"Property occupied by employees is owner-occupied, whatever rent they pay."},{"t":"A building with 70% of its floor area used as the owner’s factory, which cannot be split","why":"Inseparable, and the owner-occupied part is far from insignificant: the whole building is {a|PPE}."}],"id":"mcq-1io283","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"classify","preamble":"Investment property sits between its two cousins: the same building is PPE if you use it, inventory if you build it to sell, and investment property if you hold it for rent or growth.","lo":17,"special":"Investment property is classified by **why** it is held, not what it is: the same building can be {a|inventory}, {a|PPE}, {a|investment property} or a {a|lease receivable}.","id":"compare-1kumyt","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"map","id":"map-2-recognise","bucket":"recognise","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"id":"think-13iidw","type":"think","bucket":"recognise","lo":17,"scenario":"Bayfront Pte Ltd owns an office block it leases to tenants. This year it spends $25,000 repainting corridors, and $180,000 replacing a worn-out lift (the old lift’s {a|carrying amount} is $40,000).","prompt":"Which of these, if any, should be added to the property?","answer":["The **repainting** is day-to-day servicing. It keeps the building as it was, so it is a {x|repairs expense}: $25,000.","The **new lift** replaces a part and brings future benefits. Add the $180,000 to the {a|carrying amount} of the property and derecognise the old lift’s {a|carrying amount} of $40,000.","Exactly the same logic as for PPE."],"sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"recognise","lo":17,"title":"Recognition: the usual two tests","body":["Once we know it is {a|investment property}, when does it go on the balance sheet, and what about money spent on it later? Nothing new here: the same two tests as for {a|PPE}, which is exactly why the repaint and the new lift split the way they did.","Recognise {a|investment property} as an asset when:","1. it is **probable** that future economic benefits will flow to the entity, and","2. its **cost can be measured reliably**.","The same tests apply to costs incurred later:","- **day-to-day servicing** (repairs, cleaning, small parts) → {x|expense} as incurred","- **replacing a part** → add to the {a|carrying amount} and derecognise the part replaced"],"id":"rule-11qjbg","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"map","id":"map-3-initial","bucket":"initial","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"think","bucket":"initial","lo":17,"scenario":"Clementi Lofts Pte Ltd buys a building to lease out for $3,200,000. It also pays stamp duty of $96,000, legal fees of $18,000, and $12,000 for a launch party to attract tenants. The building is half empty for its first three months, causing an operating loss of $25,000.","prompt":"Which of these belong in the cost of the property? Think about what you would do if this were a machine.","answer":["Capitalise what it took to **acquire** the asset: the price, **stamp duty** and **legal fees** (transaction costs).","The launch party is a **start-up cost**, and the early vacancy loss is an **operating loss before the planned level of occupancy**. Neither is part of the property’s cost: they are {x|expenses}. The party may have been great, but it didn’t buy the building."],"id":"think-qhhb11","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","lo":17,"title":"Initial measurement: cost","body":["Time to put a number on it. Whichever model is chosen later, every {a|investment property} starts life at **cost**, built up exactly like a machine’s.","> {a|Cost} = purchase price + **directly attributable** costs (legal fees, stamp duty and other transaction costs)","Not part of cost:","- **start-up costs** (unless needed to bring the property to working condition)","- **operating losses** incurred before the property reaches its planned occupancy","- **abnormal** amounts of wasted material, labour or other resources in building it","! Even under the fair value model, the property starts at **cost**. Transaction costs are capitalised in {a|investment property}, not expensed on day 1."],"id":"rule-msatg8","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"calc-sgdy12","type":"calc","bucket":"initial","lo":17,"q":"Clementi Lofts buys a building to lease out for $3,200,000. It pays stamp duty of $96,000, legal fees of $18,000 and $12,000 for a launch party to attract tenants, and makes an operating loss of $25,000 while the building is half empty for its first three months. What is the initial cost of its investment property?","answer":3314000,"unit":"$","working":["Price 3,200,000 + stamp duty 96,000 + legal fees 18,000 = {a|**$3,314,000**}.","The launch party (12,000) and the vacancy loss (25,000) are {x|expenses}."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"initial","lo":17,"title":"Clementi Lofts: what goes into cost","intro":"The same answer as a picture: three amounts build the cost of the {a|investment property}; two are {x|expenses} in profit or loss.","notes":{"price":"The price paid for the building.","stamp":"**Directly attributable** transaction cost: capitalised, even under the fair value model.","legal":"**Directly attributable** transaction cost: capitalised.","cost":"3,200,000 + 96,000 + 18,000 = {a|3,314,000}. This is where the {a|investment property} starts, whichever model is chosen later.","launch":"A **start-up cost**. It attracts tenants but does not acquire the asset, so it is an {x|expense}.","vacancy":"An **operating loss** before the property reaches planned occupancy. An {x|expense}.","pl":"Both items are {x|expenses} in profit or loss in the year they arise."},"walk":[{"nodes":["price","cost"],"text":"Start with the purchase price."},{"nodes":["stamp","legal","cost"],"text":"Add the **transaction costs**: stamp duty and legal fees. They are part of acquiring the asset."},{"nodes":["cost"],"text":"{a|Cost} = **3,314,000**. The fair value model only takes over from the first reporting date."},{"nodes":["launch","vacancy","pl"],"text":"Start-up costs and early operating losses are **not** cost. They are {x|expenses}."}],"caption":"Same build-up as PPE: price + directly attributable costs.","id":"diagram-7h3k1o","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:420px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 382.00 281.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 270.62)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-270.62 371.61,-270.62 371.61,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>price\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M99.03,-144C99.03,-144 27,-144 27,-144 21,-144 15,-138 15,-132 15,-132 15,-120 15,-120 15,-114 21,-108 27,-108 27,-108 99.03,-108 99.03,-108 105.03,-108 111.03,-114 111.03,-120 111.03,-120 111.03,-132 111.03,-132 111.03,-138 105.03,-144 99.03,-144\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"23.64\" y=\"-127.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.0