,"prompt":"Would depreciating these buildings over 40 years give its shareholders useful information? What would you report instead?","answer":["Not much. {x|Depreciation} tells you how an asset is being **used up**; these buildings are not being used up in operations, they are held for their value.","Users want to see **fair value**, and how it changed. So the standard lets an entity carry {a|investment property} at fair value, with **no {x|depreciation}**.","But fair values need valuers and can be volatile, so a **cost model** is allowed too."],"id":"think-vfyw9h","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"subsequent","lo":17,"title":"Two models: pick one for all investment property","body":["Now the special part. After day 1 the standard offers a choice, because depreciation tells investors little about property held for its value (the question you just answered).","Choose **one model** and apply it to **all** of the entity’s investment property.","1. **Fair value model:** carry at **fair value** at each reporting date. **No {x|depreciation}.** Every change in fair value goes to **profit or loss** in the period it arises: a {i|gain} or a {x|loss}.","2. **Cost model:** as the PPE cost model: {a|cost} − {a|accumulated depreciation} − {a|accumulated impairment}. **Fair value is still disclosed** in the notes.","- Changing model is a change in accounting policy. A move from fair value to cost is very unlikely to give more relevant information."],"id":"rule-c7w6t0","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"subsequent","lo":17,"title":"Two models: where each change goes","intro":"Follow each model to see where the year’s movement lands: profit or loss, OCI, or only the notes.","notes":{"start":"Starts at {a|cost}. Then pick **one model for all** investment property.","fvm":"Carry at fair value at each reporting date.","cm":"As the PPE cost model: {a|cost} − {a|accumulated depreciation} − {a|accumulated impairment}.","fvchg":"The whole change since the last valuation: a {i|fair value gain} or a {x|fair value loss}.","nodep":"Under the fair value model the building is **not depreciated**: any fall in value is already caught by the fair value change.","dep":"{x|Depreciation} charged every year, as for PPE.","fvnote":"Under the cost model the fair value is not booked…","pl":"{i|Fair value gains} and {x|losses} (fair value model) and {x|depreciation} (cost model) all land here.","oci":"{a|Investment property} gains **never** go to OCI. A {e|revaluation surplus} is the PPE revaluation model, a different standard.","notes":"…but it is still **disclosed**, so users can compare with fair value companies."},"walk":[{"nodes":["start","fvm","cm"],"text":"After initial cost, the entity picks **one model** for all its investment property."},{"nodes":["fvm","fvchg","pl"],"text":"**Fair value model:** every change in fair value, up or down, goes to **profit or loss**."},{"nodes":["nodep","oci"],"text":"Two things that do **not** happen: no {x|depreciation}, and nothing goes to OCI."},{"nodes":["cm","dep","pl"],"text":"**Cost model:** {x|depreciation} (and any {x|impairment loss}) goes to profit or loss, exactly as for PPE."},{"nodes":["fvnote","notes"],"text":"Under the cost model, fair value is **still disclosed** in the notes."}],"caption":"Bayfront in 20X1: +60,000 {i|fair value gain} under one model, −51,000 {x|depreciation} under the other.","id":"diagram-7tqily","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:540px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 491.00 269.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 257.8)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-257.8 479.95,-257.8 479.95,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>start