amily=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">OCI, \u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"396.01\" y=\"-14.77\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">never\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"420.76\" y=\"-14.77\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\"> recycled;\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"382.51\" y=\"-5.77\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">dividends → P&amp;L\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge10\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>el&#45;&gt;fvoce\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M403.79,-139.75C406.57,-115.87 411.6,-72.76 414.8,-45.26\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"416.87,-45.65 415.48,-39.45 412.7,-45.16 416.87,-45.65\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"422.49\" y=\"-85.02\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> yes \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"think","bucket":"classify","lo":20,"scenario":"Two 3-year notes, each costing $100,000. Note A pays 5% interest a year and $100,000 back at the end. Note B pays nothing until maturity, then returns $100,000 × the change in the Straits Times Index.","prompt":"Which note behaves like a plain loan? Would it make sense to spread a fixed interest return over the life of Note B?","answer":["**Note A** is a basic lending arrangement: the cash is just repayment of principal plus interest for the time value of money and credit risk.","Note B’s cash depends on share prices. There is no fixed “interest” to spread, so the effective interest method would tell you nothing. Only **fair value through profit or loss** shows what it is worth."],"id":"think-u1thgo","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"classify","lo":20,"title":"Test 1: solely payments of principal and interest (SPPI)","body":["The contractual cash flows must be **solely payments of principal and interest** on the principal outstanding, on specified dates.","1. **Principal:** the fair value of the asset at initial recognition.","2. **Interest:** reward for the time value of money and credit risk (plus basic lending costs and a profit margin).","- Returns linked to share prices, commodity prices or the issuer’s profits **fail** SPPI → **FVTPL**.","! Shares can never pass SPPI: they have no principal and no interest. So shares are **never** at amortised cost."],"id":"rule-1kpfz5","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"think","bucket":"classify","lo":20,"scenario":"Three treasury teams hold identical 5-year corporate bonds. Team A keeps them to maturity and collects the coupons. Team B collects the coupons but also sells bonds whenever it needs cash or prices are good. Team C buys and sells daily to profit from price moves.","prompt":"Same bond, same cash flows. Should all three measure it the same way? What number would each team’s managers care about?","answer":["**Team A** never realises price changes, so the useful number is the cost plus interest earned: **amortised cost**.","**Team B** earns both coupons and sale prices, so the balance sheet should show **fair value**, but day-to-day price swings it may never realise stay out of profit: **FVOCI**.","**Team C** lives on price moves, so every move belongs in profit: **FVTPL**."],"id":"think-ikz620","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"classify","lo":20,"title":"Test 2: the business model (debt that passes SPPI)","body":["1. **Hold to collect** contractual cash flows → **amortised cost**.","2. **Hold to collect and sell** → **fair value through OCI (FVOCI)**.","3. **Any other model** (trading, managed on a fair value basis) → **FVTPL**.","The business model is set by key management for a **portfolio**, based on how assets are actually managed. It is not an intention for one asset. Occasional sales (for example when credit risk rises) do not break “hold to collect”.","+ Collect → amortised cost. Collect + sell → FVOCI. Anything else → FVTPL.","! “Historical cost” is not an SFRS(I) 9 category. If it appears as an option, it is wrong."],"id":"rule-1drpot","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"classify","lo":20,"q":"A company buys corporate bonds that pay fixed interest. It plans to keep them until they mature and collect the coupons and principal; sales are rare and only when a bond’s credit rating falls sharply. How are the bonds measured?","options":[{"t":"Fair value through other comprehensive income (FVOCI)","why":"FVOCI needs a model of collecting **and selling**. Rare sales after a credit downgrade are consistent with hold to collect."},{"t":"Amortised cost","ok":true,"why":"SPPI is met (fixed interest and principal) and the model is hold to collect. Occasional sales on credit deterioration do not change that."},{"t":"Fair value through profit or loss (FVTPL)","why":"FVTPL is for debt that fails SPPI or sits in a trading or other model. Neither applies."},{"t":"Historical cost","why":"Not an SFRS(I) 9 category for financial assets."}],"id":"mcq-6zxqzf","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"id":"think-15kpe8","type":"think","bucket":"classify","lo":20,"scenario":"A company buys a 5% stake in a key supplier as a long-term strategic holding. It has no plan to sell. Its CFO worries that the supplier’s share price is volatile.","prompt":"Shares fail SPPI, so they cannot be at amortised cost. By default they go to FVTPL. Why might the CFO dislike that, and what option would you give her?","answer":["Under FVTPL every swing in the supplier’s share price hits **profit**, even though the company has no intention of selling. Profit becomes noisy for reasons unrelated to performance.","SFRS(I) 9 lets her make an **irrevocable election** on day one to present fair value changes in **OCI** instead. The price for that choice: those gains **never** reach profit, even when the shares are sold. Think of it as a prenup with the share price: no drama in profit, but no share of the winnings either."],"sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"id":"rule-b0titi","type":"rule","bucket":"classify","lo":20,"title":"The two day-one elections","body":["+ Both elections **override the default classification**. Both are made **at initial recognition** and both are **irrevocable**.","- **Default for shares: FVTPL.**","- **FVOCI election (equity):** made at initial recognition, share by share, **irrevocable**, and only for shares **not held for trading**. Fair value changes go to OCI and are **never recycled** to profit or loss. {i|Dividends} still go to profit or loss.","- **Fair value option (debt):** at initial recognition an entity may irrevocably **designate** a debt asset at FVTPL if that removes or significantly reduces an **accounting mismatch**.","! The FVOCI election is not available for shares held for trading. A trading portfolio is always FVTPL."],"sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"id":"visual-fslpfc","type":"visual","bucket":"classify","lo":20,"title":"The two elections side by side","html":"\u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl\">\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Election\u003c/th>\u003cth>Applies to\u003c/th>\u003cth>Moves it from → to\u003c/th>\u003cth>Condition\u003c/th>\u003cth>Rationale\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>FVOCI election\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Shares\u003c/td>\u003ctd>FVTPL → \u003cstrong>FVOCI\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Not held for trading\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A strategic, long-term stake shouldn’t make profit swing with the share price. In return, gains are \u003cstrong>never recycled\u003c/strong>, so the entity can’t pick when to book them by choosing when to sell\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Fair value option\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Debt assets\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Amortised cost / FVOCI → \u003cstrong>FVTPL\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Removes or significantly reduces an \u003cstrong>accounting mismatch\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>If a related liability or derivative is already at FVTPL, measuring the asset at cost would show one side’s gains in profit and hide the other’s. Fair value on both puts gains and losses in \u003cstrong>profit together\u003c/strong>, so they offset\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cp class=\"cap\">Mirror images: the equity election takes P&amp;L volatility \u003cstrong>out\u003c/strong>; the fair value option brings a debt asset \u003cstrong>into\u003c/strong> P&amp;L so it matches what is already there.\u003c/p>","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"tree","bucket":"classify","lo":20,"title":"Classifying a financial asset","start":"n1","nodes":{"n1":{"q":"What kind of instrument is it?","why":"Debt and equity go down different paths: only debt can pass the SPPI test.","opts":[{"t":"Debt: a contractual right to cash (bond, loan, receivable)","to":"sppi"},{"t":"Equity: shares in another entity","to":"trade"}]},"sppi":{"q":"Are the cash flows solely payments of principal and interest (SPPI)?","why":"Amortised cost spreads interest over the life. That only means something if the cash flows are a basic loan.","opts":[{"t":"Yes: fixed or floating interest plus principal","to":"bm"},{"t":"No: returns linked to shares, commodities or profits","to":"fvtpl"}]},"bm":{"q":"What is the business model for this portfolio?","why":"How the entity realises cash (collecting it, or selling) decides whether fair value matters.","opts":[{"t":"Hold to collect contractual cash flows","to":"fvoA"},{"t":"Hold to collect and sell","to":"fvoB"},{"t":"Other: trading, or managed on a fair value basis","to":"fvtpl"}]},"fvoA":{"q":"Did the entity designate it at FVTPL on day one to remove an accounting mismatch?","why":"The fair value option is the only way to override the business model result, and only at initial recognition.","opts":[{"t":"No (the usual case)","to":"ac"},{"t":"Yes","to":"fvo"}]},"fvoB":{"q":"Did the entity designate it at FVTPL on day one to remove an accounting mismatch?","why":"The fair value option is the only way to override the business model result, and only at initial recognition.","opts":[{"t":"No (the usual case)","to":"fvoci"},{"t":"Yes","to":"fvo"}]},"trade":{"q":"Is it held for trading?","why":"The FVOCI election is not available for a trading portfolio.","opts":[{"t":"Yes: bought to sell in the near term for profit","to":"fvtpl"},{"t":"No: a longer-term or strategic holding","to":"elect"}]},"elect":{"q":"Did the entity irrevocably elect FVOCI when it first recognised the shares?","why":"Without the election, shares default to FVTPL.","opts":[{"t":"Yes","to":"fvoce"},{"t":"No (the default)","to":"fvtpl"}]},"ac":{"result":"Amortised cost","body":["Initial: fair value + transaction costs.","- {i|Interest income} = {a|opening carrying amount} × effective rate, to P&L.","- No fair value changes recognised. Expected credit losses apply."]},"fvoci":{"result":"FVOCI (debt)","body":["Initial: fair value + transaction costs.","- {i|Interest income} to P&L exactly as under amortised cost.","- Carried at fair value; the difference goes to {e|Fair value reserve (OCI)}.","- On sale the {e|OCI balance} is **recycled** to P&L."]},"fvoce":{"result":"FVOCI (equity, elected)","body":["Initial: fair value + transaction costs.","- Fair value changes to {e|Fair value reserve (OCI)}, **never recycled**. On sale they may be transferred within equity to {e|Retained earnings}.","- {i|Dividend income} to P&L. No impairment."]},"fvtpl":{"result":"FVTPL","body":["Initial: fair value; {x|transaction costs} **expensed**.","- All fair value changes, {i|interest} and {i|dividends} to P&L.","- No impairment test: fair value already captures it."]},"fvo":{"result":"FVTPL (designated under the fair value option)","body":["Same accounting as any FVTPL asset. The designation is irrevocable and allowed only to remove or reduce an accounting mismatch, for example when a related liability is already at FVTPL."]}},"id":"tree-1jvej2","sect":1,"part":"Classify","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:640px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 778.00 479.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 468.08)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-468.08 767.58,-468.08 767.58,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>n1\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M537.78,-457.28C537.78,-457.28 420.75,-457.28 420.75,-457.28 414.75,-457.28 408.75,-451.28 408.75,-445.28 408.75,-445.28 408.75,-433.28 408.75,-433.28 408.75,-427.28 414.75,-421.28 420.75,-421.28 420.75,-421.28 537.78,-421.28 537.78,-421.28 543.78,-421.28 549.78,-427.28 549.78,-433.28 549.78,-433.28 549.78,-445.28 549.78,-445.28 549.78,-451.28 543.78,-457.28 537.78,-457.28\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"479.26\" y=\"-440.83\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">What kind of instrument is\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"479.26\" y=\"-428.83\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">it?\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>sppi\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M465.03,-350.28C465.03,-350.28 349.5,-350.28 349.5,-350.28 343.5,-350.28 337.5,-344.28 337.5,-338.28 337.5,-338.28 337.5,-317.64 337.5,-317.64 337.5,-311.64 343.5,-305.64 349.5,-305.64 349.5,-305.64 465.03,-305.64 465.03,-305.64 471.03,-305.64 477.03,-311