.76\" y=\"-50.75\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">asked \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge6\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>elect&#45;&gt;fvtpl\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M131.02,-84.06C137.6,-80.3 144.21,-76.07 150.01,-71.5 156.09,-66.72 167.61,-53.88 177.73,-42.09\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"179.28,-43.51 181.57,-37.58 176.08,-40.78 179.28,-43.51\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"179.99\" y=\"-56.38\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> No \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"classify","preamble":"Financial assets differ from PPE and investment property because they are **contracts for cash**, not physical things you use or rent out. Their mirror image is the financial liability, under the same standard.","special":"Financial assets are classified by two tests, business model and SPPI. The category, not the asset, then decides whether gains land in P&L, OCI or nowhere.","id":"compare-1kumyt","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"map","id":"map-2-recognise","bucket":"recognise","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"think","bucket":"recognise","lo":20,"scenario":"In November the board approves a plan to buy $1 million of bonds “early next year”. On 5 January the company signs the purchase contract with a broker.","prompt":"When does the bond first appear on the balance sheet: November, 5 January, or when cash changes hands?","answer":["Not in November. A board plan is a wish list, not a contract, and it gives no right to anything. No contract, no asset.","The company recognises the asset when it **becomes a party to the contract**. For a standard market purchase (a “regular way” trade) it can use either the **trade date** or the **settlement date**, applied consistently."],"id":"think-1qimx5","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"recognise","lo":20,"title":"Recognition","body":["Recognise a financial asset when the entity **becomes a party to the contractual provisions** of the instrument.","- Planned or forecast purchases are not recognised: no contract, no asset.","- {a|Trade receivables} arise when the sale is made and the entity has an unconditional right to the cash.","- Regular way purchases of securities: trade date or settlement date accounting, as an accounting policy applied consistently."],"id":"rule-puryko","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"map","id":"map-3-initial","bucket":"initial","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"think","bucket":"initial","lo":20,"scenario":"A company buys 10,000 shares at $2.00 each and pays the broker $300. The shares are at FVTPL, so at every reporting date they will be remeasured to the market price.","prompt":"Should the $300 brokerage be added to the shares? Think about what happens at the very next remeasurement if it is.","answer":["If you capitalise it, the shares start at $20,300. Their market value is only $20,000, so the first remeasurement knocks the $300 straight out again as a “{x|fair value loss}”.","That’s a pointless round trip: capitalised in January, knocked out in December. It is cleaner to **expense** it on day one, and that is the rule for FVTPL. For every other category the brokerage is **added** to the {a|asset}, because those assets are not remeasured through profit."],"more":{"title":"Additional notes: what is brokerage?","body":["**Brokerage** is the commission a stockbroker charges to carry out your buy or sell order. You can’t trade on the exchange directly, so a licensed broker places the order for a fee: usually a % of the trade value, often with a minimum.","**Real-world example:** buy 1,000 DBS shares on SGX at S$35 through DBS Vickers, POEMS or Tiger Brokers. The contract note shows S$35,000 for the shares, plus brokerage (roughly 0.1–0.3%, or a flat fee on app brokers), SGX clearing and access fees, and GST on the fees. Rates vary by broker.","Brokerage is the classic **transaction cost**: incremental, and paid only because you bought this asset. SGX fees count too; recoverable GST doesn’t.","- **FVTPL:** expense it.","- **Amortised cost / FVOCI:** add it to the {a|asset}. A S$100,000 bond with S$500 brokerage starts at S$100,500; at amortised cost the S$500 is spread over the bond’s life through the effective interest rate."]},"id":"think-1ncm5u","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","lo":20,"title":"Initial measurement: fair value plus transaction costs","body":["> {a|Initial amount} = Fair value + transaction costs   (FVTPL: {x|transaction costs} → P&L)","- **Amortised cost, FVOCI debt, FVOCI equity:** transaction costs are **added** to the {a|asset}.","- **FVTPL:** {x|transaction costs} are **expensed** immediately.","Fair value at initial recognition is normally the price paid. The exception is {a|Trade receivables} without a significant financing component, which start at the **transaction price**.","**Why the split?** Brokerage is part of what it cost you to get the asset, so the default is to capitalise it, just like stamp duty on PPE. The question is where it ends up.","- **Amortised cost:** the asset is never remeasured. The cost sits in the {a|carrying amount} and is spread over the life through the effective rate as slightly lower {i|interest income}, so it is matched against the return it bought.","- **FVOCI debt:** same as amortised cost. {i|Interest} is still calculated on cost, so the cost reaches P&L gradually through the effective rate.","- **FVOCI equity:** the next remeasurement takes the cost out of the {a|carrying amount}, because fair value excludes transaction costs. The loss lands in {e|Fair value reserve (OCI)} and never reaches P&L.","- **FVTPL:** the next remeasurement would write the cost off to P&L anyway, since fair value excludes it. Capitalising it would only delay the hit to the next measurement date, so it is expensed straight away.","+ One line: capitalise unless the asset is about to be marked to market through P&L, where capitalising would be pointless.","! Brokerage on **FVTPL** shares is {x|expensed}. If the shares had been **elected FVOCI**, the same brokerage would be capitalised."],"id":"rule-1pkl0i","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"initial","lo":20,"title":"Transaction costs: capitalise or expense?","intro":"The same purchase: 10,000 shares at $2.00 plus $300 brokerage. Only the category changes.","notes":{"buy":"Fair value of the shares (10,000 × $2.00 = 20,000) plus the broker’s fee. The {a|cash} out is the same whatever the category.","cat":"Classify first (the classify step), then measure. The category alone decides where the brokerage goes.","cap":"Every category **except FVTPL**: amortised cost, FVOCI debt and elected FVOCI equity.","tpl":"Shares with no election, trading assets, debt that fails SPPI or is designated.","capA":"{a|Initial amount} = fair value + transaction costs = 20,000 + 300 = **20,300**. For a bond, these costs are then spread over its life through the effective interest rate.","tplA":"Only the fair value, 20,000. Capitalising 300 would just be knocked out by the first remeasurement to market price.","tplX":"Dr {x|Transaction costs expense} 300. Practice paper Q31 tests exactly this."},"walk":[{"nodes":["buy"],"text":"The company pays $20,300 in total: $20,000 for the shares and $300 to the broker."},{"nodes":["cat","cap","capA"],"text":"Amortised cost or either FVOCI: the brokerage is **added**. The {a|asset} starts at $20,300."},{"nodes":["cat","tpl","tplA","tplX"],"text":"FVTPL: the {a|asset} starts at fair value $20,000 and the {x|$300} is **expensed** to P&L at once."},{"nodes":["capA","tplX"],"text":"Memory hook: if fair value changes go to P&L anyway, the costs go to P&L too. Otherwise they ride with the asset."}],"id":"diagram-1j1kfd","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"