235.58,-48.2 249.95,-40.16\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"250.88,-42.05 255.09,-37.29 248.83,-38.38 250.88,-42.05\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"je","bucket":"initial","lo":20,"q":"Record the purchase: 10,000 shares at $2.00, brokerage $300, all paid in cash. The shares are at FVTPL.","accounts":["Investment in shares","Cash","Transaction costs expense","Fair value gain (P&L)","Fair value loss (P&L)","Fair value reserve (OCI)","Dividend income","Retained earnings","Accruals"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Investment in shares","amount":20000},{"side":"Dr","account":"Transaction costs expense","amount":300},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":20300}],"why":"FVTPL: the {a|shares} start at fair value 20,000 (10,000 × 2.00); the {x|300 brokerage} goes to P&L.","id":"je-17n0t0","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"mcq-ra5z9b","type":"mcq","bucket":"initial","lo":20,"q":"A company buys 10,000 shares at $2.00 each and pays $300 brokerage, all in cash. It holds them as a long-term strategic stake and elects FVOCI on day one. At what amount are the shares first recognised?","options":[{"t":"$20,000","why":"That is the FVTPL answer. Under FVOCI the brokerage is not expensed."},{"t":"$20,300","ok":true,"why":"Fair value 20,000 + transaction costs 300. Every category except FVTPL capitalises them."},{"t":"$19,700","why":"Transaction costs are never deducted from the asset."},{"t":"$20,000, with the $300 taken to OCI","why":"Transaction costs are part of the asset’s initial amount, not an OCI item."}],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"think","bucket":"initial","lo":20,"scenario":"A 3-year bond has a face value of $100,000 and pays a 5% coupon ($5,000) at the end of each year. Similar bonds now yield 6%.","prompt":"Would you pay the full $100,000 for it? More, or less?","answer":["**Less.** Its 5% coupon is below the 6% you could earn elsewhere, so you only pay a price that gives you 6% on what you invest.","That price is the **present value** of the coupons and the face value at 6%. The shortfall below face is a **discount**. A coupon above the market rate gives a **premium**."],"id":"think-t99r53","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","lo":20,"title":"Refresher: coupons, discount factors and annuity factors","body":["- **Face value**: what the issuer repays at the end ($100,000).","- **Coupon**: the {a|interest cash} paid each year = face × **coupon rate** ($100,000 × 5% = $5,000). The coupon rate is printed on the bond and only sets the cash.","- **Market rate** (yield): the return investors demand today for similar bonds (6%). This is the rate you **discount** at.","> Discount factor (year n) = 1 ÷ (1 + r)ⁿ  →  today’s value of $1 received once, in year n","> Annuity factor (n years) = DF year 1 + DF year 2 + … + DF year n  →  today’s value of $1 received every year for n years","> Price = coupon × annuity factor + face value × discount factor (final year)","+ The exam gives you both factors. Annuity factor for the coupons, which repeat every year; discount factor for the face value, which arrives once at the end.","! Discount at the **market** rate, not the coupon rate. At the coupon rate you would always get exactly face value."],"id":"rule-1gjfbb","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"initial","lo":20,"title":"Pricing the bond on a timeline","intro":"Face $100,000, 5% coupon paid at the end of each year, 3 years, market rate 6%. Each cash flow is shrunk back to today by its discount factor.","notes":{"c1":"Coupon = 100,000 × 5% = 5,000, received at the end of year 1.","p1":"Year 1 discount factor = 1 ÷ 1.06 = 0.94340. 5,000 × 0.94340 = 4,717.","p2":"Year 2 discount factor = 1 ÷ 1.06² = 0.89000. Further away, so worth less today.","p3":"Year 3 discount factor = 1 ÷ 1.06³ = 0.83962.","f3":"The face value comes back once, at the end of year 3, alongside the last coupon.","pf":"100,000 × 0.83962 = 83,962. One cash flow, so the single discount factor.","pc":"The three coupons are worth 13,365 today.","af":"Adding the three yearly discount factors gives the annuity factor, so 5,000 × 2.67301 = 13,365 does all three coupons in one step.","price":"13,365 + 83,962 = 97,327: a discount of 2,673 below face, because the coupon (5%) is below the market rate (6%)."},"walk":[{"nodes":["c1","c2","c3","f3"],"text":"Lay out the cash: a $5,000 coupon at the end of each year, plus the $100,000 face value at the end of year 3."},{"nodes":["p1","p2","p3"],"text":"Discount each coupon: 5,000 × 0.94340 = 4,717; 5,000 × 0.89000 = 4,450; 5,000 × 0.83962 = 4,198. Total 13,365."},{"nodes":["af","pc"],"text":"Shortcut: the three discount factors add up to the **annuity factor** (2.67301). 5,000 × 2.67301 = 13,365 in one step."},{"nodes":["f3","pf"],"text":"The face value arrives once: 100,000 × 0.83962 = 83,962."},{"nodes":["price"],"text":"Price = 13,365 + 83,962 = **$97,327**."}],"id":"diagram-1x1uai","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:574px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 522.00 305.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 290.4)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-290.4 507.71,-290.4 507.71,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>c1\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M114.03,-276C114.03,-276 12,-276 12,-276 6,-276 0,-270 0,-264 0,-264 0,-252 0,-252 0,-246 6,-240 12,-240 12,-240 114.03,-240 114.03,-240 120.03,-240 126.03,-246 126.03,-252 126.03,-252 126.03,-264 126.03,-264 126.03,-270 120.03,-276 114.03,-276\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"8.64\" y=\"-254.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weig