-44.64 151,-44.64 145,-38.64 145,-32.64 145,-32.64 145,-12 145,-12 145,-6 151,0 157,0 157,0 271.78,0 271.78,0 277.78,0 283.78,-6 283.78,-12 283.78,-12 283.78,-32.64 283.78,-32.64 283.78,-38.64 277.78,-44.64 271.78,-44.64\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"214.39\" y=\"-29.87\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">On the balance sheet:\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"214.39\" y=\"-17.87\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">right&#45;of&#45;use asset + lease\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"214.39\" y=\"-5.87\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">liability\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge10\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>n5&#45;&gt;bs\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M173.5,-115.39C178.66,-111.72 183.53,-107.47 187.39,-102.64 198.97,-88.16 205.72,-68.43 209.57,-52.27\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"211.57,-52.92 210.81,-46.61 207.47,-52.02 211.57,-52.92\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"217.48\" y=\"-76.27\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> No \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"classify","blabel":"is it a lease?","lo":22,"q":"Which of these contracts contains a lease for the customer?","options":[{"t":"A courier firm hires \"a van\" daily from a pool. The rental company picks which van each morning.","why":"No identified asset: the supplier chooses and can substitute freely."},{"t":"A retailer rents a specified shop unit for 5 years and decides its opening hours and what it sells there.","ok":true,"why":"Identified asset, substantially all the benefits, and the retailer directs the use. All three tests pass."},{"t":"A company pays for cloud storage capacity on whatever servers the provider allocates.","why":"Capacity on unspecified servers is a service; there is no identified asset."},{"t":"An event organiser books a hotel ballroom for one evening, with the hotel setting the layout and catering.","why":"The hotel directs how the room is used. The customer buys a service."}],"id":"mcq-i50pkx","sect":1,"part":"Classify · is it a lease?"},{"type":"map","id":"map-2-recognise","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"lessee","sect":2,"part":"Recognise · lessee"},{"type":"think","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"lessee","lo":22,"scenario":"Kopi Co also rents a forklift for 10 months to move stock during a renovation, and leases 20 tablets for staff (each about $400 new) for 3 years.","prompt":"Would it be worth the effort to put these on the balance sheet? What would you want the rule to say?","answer":["Probably not. Both are small or short, and tracking them as {a|assets} and {l|liabilities} costs more than the information is worth.","SFRS(I) 16 agrees: a lessee **may elect** to recognise **short-term** and **low-value** leases as a {x|lease expense} straight-line instead."],"id":"think-q6ubkb","sect":2,"part":"Recognise · lessee"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"lessee","lo":22,"title":"Two optional exemptions (lessee only)","body":["We now know it is a lease. The default is the balance sheet, but small and short leases would bury preparers in paperwork for little information, so the lessee gets two opt-outs.","1. **Short-term:** lease term of **12 months or less** with **no purchase option**.","2. **Low-value asset:** judged on the asset’s value **when new**, regardless of the lessee’s size. Laptops, phones, tablets, small furniture.","Exempt leases: {x|Lease expense} recognised **straight-line** over the lease term. Nothing goes on the balance sheet.","! A 12-month lease **with a purchase option** is *not* short-term. And a car is not low-value just because the company is huge."],"id":"rule-1lcdxc","sect":2,"part":"Recognise · lessee"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"lessee","lo":22,"q":"Which lease can Kopi Co keep off the balance sheet using an exemption?","options":[{"t":"A 3-year lease of a delivery van","why":"Over 12 months and a van is not low-value when new."},{"t":"A 10-month lease of a forklift, no purchase option","ok":true,"why":"Short-term: 12 months or less and no purchase option."},{"t":"A 12-month lease of a forklift with an option to buy it at the end","why":"A purchase option disqualifies the short-term exemption."},{"t":"A 5-year lease of a building floor, because Kopi Co is a large group","why":"Low-value is judged on the asset when new, not on the lessee’s size."}],"id":"mcq-1ys9x6","sect":2,"part":"Recognise · lessee"},{"type":"map","id":"map-3-initial","bucket":"initial","blabel":"lessee","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement · lessee"},{"type":"think","bucket":"initial","blabel":"lessee","lo":23,"scenario":"Kopi Co signs the RoastPro lease on 1 Jan 20X1: 4 years, $10,000 a year paid in **arrears** (each 31 Dec). The rate implicit in the lease is 8%. Kopi Co cannot cancel.","prompt":"On day 1, how much does Kopi Co owe? Is it $40,000?","answer":["No. $40,000 is paid over four years, and money paid later is worth less today. Kopi Co owes the **present value**: {l|lease liability} = $10,000 × 3.3121 = **$33,121**.","The difference ($6,879) is {x|interest} Kopi Co will pay over the lease, exactly like a loan."],"id":"think-1msvo8","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement · lessee"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","blabel":"lessee","lo":23,"title":"The lease liability","body":["The lease is on the balance sheet, so now we put a number on it. Start with what is owed: like a loan, it is measured at present value, not at the sum of the payments.","> {l|Lease liability} = PV of lease payments not yet paid","- **Discount rate:** the **rate implicit in the lease** if readily determinable; otherwise the lessee’s **incremental borrowing rate**.","- **Arrears** (end of each period) = ordinary annuity. **Advance** (start of each period) = annuity due. More on that shortly.","! Lessees no longer split leases into finance and operating. Every lease (bar the exemptions) goes on the balance sheet."],"id":"rule-niodw9","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement · lessee"},{"id":"calc-122zgt","type":"calc","bucket":"initial","blabel":"lessee","lo":23,"q":"Work it out yourself. Kopi Co leases a coffee roaster from RoastPro from 1 Jan 20X1 for 4 years: 4 annual payments of $10,000 in arrears (each 31 Dec), rate implicit in the lease 8%. The 4-year annuity factor at 8% is 3.3121. What is the initial {l|lease liability}?","answer":33121,"unit":"$","working":["$10,000 × 3.3121 = $33,121","Round to the nearest dollar."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement · lessee"},{"id":"think-dyls00","type":"think","bucket":"initial","blabel":"lessee","lo":23,"scenario":"Kopi Co leases a coffee roaster from RoastPro for 4 years at $10,000 a year. Suppose the contract also said Kopi Co pays an extra 2% of the café’s coffee sales to RoastPro each year, and rent rises each year with the consumer price index (CPI).","prompt":"Which of those extra payments can Kopi Co measure reliably on day 1, and so include in the {l|liability}?","answer":["The **CPI-linked** increase depends on an **index**: include it, measured using the index at commencement.","The **2% of sales** depends on future performance. Leave it out, and recognise it as an {x|expense} when the sales happen."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement · lessee"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","blabel":"lessee","lo":23,"title":"What counts as a lease payment","body":["The {l|liability} discounts “the lease payments”, so which payments count? The test is whether the amount is fixed or measurable on day 1.","Included in the {l|liability}:","- fixed payments, less lease incentives receivable","- variable payments that depend on an **index or rate**","- amounts expected under a **residual value guarantee**","- a **purchase option** price, if **reasonably certain** to be exercised","- **termination penalties**, if the lease term assumes termination","! Variable payments linked to **sales or usage** are *not* lease payments. They are an {x|expense} as incurred."],"id":"rule-4ans74","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement · lessee"},{"id":"mcq-vw3bro","type":"mcq","bucket":"initial","blabel":"lessee","lo":23,"q":"Which of these is excluded from the {l|lease liability}?","options":[{"t":"Rent that rises each year in line with CPI","why":"Index-linked: included, using the index at commencement."},{"t":"A purchase option the lessee is reasonably certain to exercise","why":"Included at its exercise price."},{"t":"1% of the lessee’s annual revenue from the store","ok":true,"why":"Linked to sales, so it is an {x|expense} when incurred, not included in the {l|liability}."},{"t":"A residual value guarantee the lessee expects to pay","why":"Amounts expected to be payable under a residual value guarantee are included."}],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement · lessee"},{"id":"think-1s9nc3","type":"think","bucket":"initial","blabel":"lessee","lo":23,"scenario":"Kopi Co’s roaster lease has a {l|lease liability} of $33,121 on day 1. Kopi Co also paid its lawyers $1,000 in {a|cash} to negotiate the lease. Without the lease, it would not have spent this.","prompt":"Expense it, or add it to the {a|asset}? Think about what you would do if you had bought the machine instead.","answer":["Add it to the {a|asset}. When you buy PPE, costs directly needed to get it are capitalised. The same logic applies here.","These are **initial direct costs**. The {a|Right-of-use asset} becomes $33,121 + $1,000 = **$34,121**."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement · lessee"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","blabel":"lessee","lo":23,"title":"The right-of-use asset","body":["The {l|liability} is done; the {a|asset} is built on top of it. Same logic as buying PPE: everything spent to get the asset in use goes into its cost.","> {a|Right-of-use asset} = {l|Lease liability} + payments at or before commencement + initial direct costs + {l|restoration costs} − incentives received","- Payments made on or before day 1 are not in the {l|liability} (they are already paid) but they *are* part of the {a|asset}’s cost.","- **Restoration costs:** if the lessee must restore the site at the end, it recognises a {l|provision} and adds the same amount to the {a|asset}.","- Incentives received from the lessor reduce the {a|asset}."],"id":"rule-37e3oq","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement · lessee"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"initial","blabel":"lessee","lo":23,"title":"Building the ROU asset","intro":"Five pieces build the asset’s cost on day 1. Colours match the account legend.","notes":{"ll":"The starting point: the {l|lease liability}, the PV of the payments not yet paid. Kopi Co: $33,121.","pre":"Already paid, so not in the {l|liability}, but still part of the {a|asset}’s cost. In an advance lease this is the first payment.","idc":"Costs Kopi Co would not have had without the lease. Kopi Co’s legal fees: $1,000.","rest":"If the lessee must restore the site, it recognises a {l|Provision for restoration} and adds the same amount here.","inc":"{a|Cash} or benefits received from the lessor to sign the lease reduce the {a|asset}.","rou":"Kopi Co (arrears): {a|ROU asset} = 33,121 + 1,000 = $34,121."},"walk":[{"nodes":["ll","rou"],"text":"Start with the {l|lease liability}: the {a|asset} is at least what you owe for it."},{"nodes":["pre","idc"],"text":"Add what you already paid: payments on or before day 1 and initial direct costs."},{"nodes":["rest"],"text":"Add restoration costs, booked at the same time as a {l|provision}."},{"nodes":["inc"],"text":"Deduct any incentives the lessor gave you."},{"nodes":["rou"],"text":"The total is the {a|ROU asset}’s cost. Kopi Co: $34,121."}],"caption":"Same idea as PPE: everything needed to get the asset in use is capitalised.","id":"diagram-1dyumj","s