mount with tax base.","id":"compare-1kumyt","sect":3,"part":"Classify · tax base"},{"type":"map","id":"map-4-initial","bucket":"initial","blabel":"current tax","sect":4,"part":"Initial measurement · current tax"},{"id":"think-16m9nu","type":"think","bucket":"initial","blabel":"current tax","lo":28,"scenario":"Lim Trading’s year 1, the year it bought a $100,000 machine. Profit $200,000 after {x|depreciation} of $25,000 and $6,000 of non-deductible {x|entertainment}. Capital allowances: $50,000.","prompt":"Starting from accounting profit, what would you add back and take off to reach taxable profit?","answer":["Add back what tax doesn’t allow: **{x|depreciation}** (tax gives allowances instead) and **{x|entertainment}**.","Take off what tax does allow that the accounts didn’t charge: the **capital allowances**."],"sect":4,"part":"Initial measurement · current tax"},{"id":"visual-p2nmlv","type":"visual","bucket":"initial","blabel":"current tax","lo":28,"title":"Current tax computation","html":"\u003cp>Here are the add-backs and deductions you just listed, laid out the way a tax computation is: start from accounting profit, end at the taxable profit that IRAS taxes at 17%.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl num\">\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Accounting profit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>200,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Add: \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">depreciation\u003c/span> (not deductible)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>25,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Less: capital allowances\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(50,000)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Add: \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">entertainment\u003c/span> (permanent)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Taxable profit\u003c/th>\u003ctd>\u003cb>181,000\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>","sect":4,"part":"Initial measurement · current tax"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"initial","blabel":"current tax","lo":28,"title":"From accounting profit to taxable profit","intro":"The same computation as a bridge. Every adjustment is either permanent or temporary, and that decides whether deferred tax follows.","notes":{"profit":"Lim’s accounting profit under SFRS(I): $200,000.","perm":"Non-deductible {x|entertainment} of $6,000 is added back. It will never be deductible, so it is permanent.","temp":"{x|Depreciation} ($25,000) is added back and capital allowances ($50,000) are taken off. Over the machine’s life both total $100,000, so this is only timing.","taxable":"Taxable profit = 200,000 + 6,000 + 25,000 − 50,000 = $181,000.","cur":"{x|Current tax} = 181,000 × 17% = $30,770: what Lim owes IRAS for this year.","etr":"A permanent item gets no deferred tax. It only pushes the effective tax rate away from 17%.","dt":"A timing item reverses later, so it creates deferred tax: here a {l|DTL} of 25,000 × 17% = $4,250."},"walk":[{"nodes":["profit"],"text":"Start from accounting profit, the number in the income statement."},{"nodes":["perm","etr"],"text":"Permanent adjustments ({x|fines}, {x|entertainment}) never reverse: no deferred tax."},{"nodes":["temp","dt"],"text":"Temporary adjustments ({x|depreciation} vs allowances) reverse later: deferred tax follows."},{"nodes":["taxable","cur"],"text":"Both kinds reach taxable profit, $181,000. × 17% gives {x|current tax} of $30,770."}],"caption":"Current tax uses every adjustment. Deferred tax picks up only the temporary ones.","id":"diagram-19a91c","sect":4,"part":"Initial measurement · current tax","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:387px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 352.00 251.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 236.48)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-236.48 337.62,-236.48 337.62,14.4 -14