j","type":"tree","bucket":"subsequent","blabel":"movement","lo":29,"title":"Deferred tax, one item at a time","scenario":"At year end Lim revalues a piece of plant from $400,000 to $480,000, with the gain in OCI. Before the revaluation the plant’s {a|carrying amount} and tax base were both $400,000, and the tax base doesn’t change. Assume the gain would be taxed when the plant’s value is recovered. Lim is profitable.","expect":"dtlOci","start":"n1","nodes":{"n1":{"q":"Will this item **ever** be taxable or deductible?","why":"Deferred tax only exists for differences that reverse. An item that never enters the tax computation can’t create a future tax effect.","opts":[{"t":"Never (a {x|fine}, non-deductible {x|entertainment}, {i|exempt dividends})","to":"perm"},{"t":"Yes, now or in a later year","to":"n2"}]},"perm":{"result":"Permanent difference: no deferred tax","body":["It is in accounting profit but never in taxable profit (or the other way round), so nothing reverses later.","- It only appears in the **tax reconciliation**, as a reason the effective rate isn’t 17%."]},"n2":{"q":"Step 1: what kind of item is it?","why":"The tax base rule differs for assets and liabilities. Unused tax losses have no carrying amount at all, but they still cut future tax.","opts":[{"t":"An {a|asset} ({a|plant}, a {a|receivable}, a {a|revalued building})","to":"nA"},{"t":"A {l|liability} (a {l|provision}, {l|revenue received in advance})","to":"nL"},{"t":"Unused tax losses carried forward","to":"dd"}]},"nA":{"q":"Step 2: compare the asset’s **{a|carrying amount}** with its **tax base** (what is still deductible). Which is bigger?","why":"When you recover an asset carried above its tax base, you earn more than you can deduct, so tax is payable later.","opts":[{"t":"{a|Carrying amount} > tax base","to":"tx"},{"t":"{a|Carrying amount} \u003c tax base","to":"dd"},{"t":"They are equal","to":"none"}]},"nL":{"q":"Step 2: compare the liability’s **{l|carrying amount}** with its **tax base** ({l|carrying amount} less future deductions). Which is bigger?","why":"Liabilities flip. A liability carried above its tax base gives a deduction when it is settled, so tax is saved later.","opts":[{"t":"{l|Carrying amount} > tax base","to":"dd"},{"t":"{l|Carrying amount} \u003c tax base","to":"tx"},{"t":"They are equal","to":"none"}]},"none":{"result":"No temporary difference: no deferred tax","body":["Carrying amount and tax base agree, so recovering or settling the item has no extra tax effect."]},"tx":{"q":"Step 3: a **taxable** difference → {l|Deferred tax liability} = difference × enacted rate. Step 5: where did the item that created the difference go?","why":"A {l|DTL} has no probable-profit test, so step 4 is skipped. The only question left is where its movement goes: deferred tax follows the item.","opts":[{"t":"Profit or loss (e.g. capital allowances faster than {x|depreciation})","to":"dtlPl"},{"t":"OCI or equity (e.g. a {e|revaluation surplus})","to":"dtlOci"}]},"dd":{"q":"Step 3–4: a **deductible** difference or unused loss → a possible {a|Deferred tax asset}. Is future taxable profit probable to use it against?","why":"A future deduction is only worth something if there will be taxable profit to set it against. This test applies to {a|DTAs} only.","opts":[{"t":"Yes, enough for all of it","to":"dtaWhere"},{"t":"Only for part of it","to":"dtaPart"},{"t":"No convincing evidence (e.g. a run of recent losses)","to":"dtaNone"}]},"dtaWhere":{"q":"Step 5: where did the item that created the difference go?","why":"Deferred tax goes to the same place as the item: P&L, OCI or equity.","opts":[{"t":"Profit or loss (e.g. a {l|warranty provision})","to":"dtaPl"},{"t":"OCI or equity (e.g. a loss recognised in OCI)","to":"dtaOci"}]},"dtlPl":{"result":"{l|DTL}, movement through P&L","body":["> {l|DTL} = taxable difference × enacted (or substantively enacted) rate","1. Work out the **{l|closing balance}** first. The {x|Deferred tax expense} (or {i|Deferred tax income}) is {l|closing} − {l|opening}.","2. If the rate changed, the {l|opening balance} is remeasured too, and that effect also goes to P&L."]},"dtlOci":{"result":"{l|DTL}, movement through OCI","body":["> In the scenario: {l|DTL} = 80,000 × 17% = $13,600","- Dr {e|Revaluation surplus (OCI)} 13,600, Cr {l|Deferred tax liability} 13,600. The {e|surplus} left in equity is $66,400.","- Nothing goes to P&L: the gain went to OCI, so its tax goes there too.","- The {l|DTL} itself is an ordinary non-current liability in the statement of financial position."]},"dtaPl":{"result":"{a|DTA}, movement through P&L","body":["> {a|DTA} = deductible difference × enacted rate","- Dr {a|Deferred tax asset}, Cr {i|Deferred tax income} for the increase in the year (the reverse for a decrease).","- Reassess recoverability at every reporting date."]},"dtaOci":{"result":"{a|DTA}, movement through OCI or equity","body":["> {a|DTA} = deductible difference × enacted rate","- The movement goes to OCI (or equity) alongside the item, not to P&L.","- The probable-profit test still applies."]},"dtaPart":{"result":"{a|DTA} only for the part that is recoverable","body":["1. Recognise a {a|DTA} on the part covered by probable taxable profit, counting taxable differences that will reverse in the same periods.","2. Disclose the rest (for example, unused tax losses with no {a|DTA}).","3. Movement to P&L, or to OCI/equity if the item went there."]},"dtaNone":{"result":"No {a|DTA}: disclose instead","body":["- Recent losses are strong evidence against recognition unless there is convincing other evidence.","- Disclose the unrecognised deductible differences and unused tax losses.","- Reassess every year. When profit becomes probable, recognise the {a|DTA} then."]}},"sect":8,"part":"Subsequent measurement · movement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:640px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 874.00 659.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg 