111827\">retraining, relocation,\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"138.8\" y=\"-6.49\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">marketing, future losses\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge7\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>prov&#45;&gt;out\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,2\" d=\"M138.84,-65.66C145.61,-59.33 153.41,-52.06 160.73,-45.23\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"162.18,-47.22 165.63,-40.66 158.84,-43.64 162.18,-47.22\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"calc-1qjjca","type":"calc","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"special cases","lo":33,"q":"Bishan Biscuits’ board approved a detailed formal plan to close its Tuas factory on 1 December and announced it to the staff and union on 20 December (year end 31 December). The closure is expected to cost: redundancy pay $300,000; penalty for ending the factory’s maintenance contract $70,000; retraining staff who move to other sites $80,000; relocating them $50,000; marketing the new brand $40,000; operating losses until closure $60,000. What is the {l|restructuring provision}?","answer":370000,"unit":"$","working":["Direct costs only: redundancy 300,000 + contract penalty 70,000 = $370,000.","Retraining, relocation and marketing relate to the continuing business. Future operating losses are never provided for."],"sect":5,"part":"Recognise · special cases"},{"type":"map","id":"map-6-recognise","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"onerous contracts","sect":6,"part":"Recognise · onerous contracts"},{"type":"think","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"onerous contracts","lo":33,"scenario":"Jurong Steel agreed last year to supply steel beams at a fixed price. Steel prices have since soared. Delivering what is left will cost $300,000 and bring in only $220,000. Jurong could instead walk away by paying the customer a penalty of $50,000.","prompt":"Jurong is locked into losing money. How much should it provide for now?","answer":["Delivering loses 300,000 − {i|220,000} = {x|$80,000}. Walking away costs $50,000. A sensible Jurong picks the cheaper exit.","So the **unavoidable** cost, the least net cost of getting out, is **$50,000**. That is the {l|provision}, booked now, not when the contract ends."],"id":"think-y5fy10","sect":6,"part":"Recognise · onerous contracts"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"onerous contracts","lo":33,"title":"Onerous contracts","body":["Jurong showed the one kind of future loss that **is** provided for: a contract you can’t escape. Here is the rule and the formula.","- A contract is **onerous** when the **unavoidable costs** of meeting it exceed the economic benefits expected from it.","> {l|Provision} = least net cost of exiting = lower of ({x|cost to fulfil} − {i|expected benefits}) and the penalty for not fulfilling","1. First, **impair** any {a|assets} dedicated to the contract.","2. Then recognise the {l|provision} for the rest of the {x|loss}: Dr {x|Onerous contract expense}, Cr {l|Provision for onerous contract}.","! Provide for the **net** loss, not the full cost to fulfil. The remaining {i|revenue} still comes in.","+ You are already locked into losing this money, so the {x|loss} belongs in this year."],"id":"rule-13msqf","sect":6,"part":"Recognise · onerous contracts"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"onerous contracts","lo":33,"title":"Onerous contract: the least net cost","intro":"Jurong’s two ways out, and the cheaper one becomes the {l|provision}.","notes":{"fulfil":"What it will cost to finish the contract: $300,000.","rev":"What the customer will still pay ({i|revenue} still to come): $220,000.","net":"Option 1, deliver: lose 300,000 − 220,000 = $80,000.","pen":"Option 2, walk away: pay the penalty of $50,000 (and do no work, receive no {i|revenue}).","low":"The unavoidable cost is the cheaper exit: lower of 80,000 and 50,000 = $50,000.","imp":"If {a|assets} are used only for this contract (e.g. special tooling), impair them first. The {l|provision} covers what is left.","prov":"Dr {x|Onerous contract expense}, Cr {l|Provision for onerous contract} 50,000."},"walk":[{"nodes":["fulfil","rev","net"],"text":"Option 1: finish the job and lose $80,000."},{"nodes":["pen"],"text":"Option 2: pay the $50,000 penalty and walk away."},{"nodes":["low"],"text":"The unavoidable cost is the cheaper of the two."},{"nodes":["imp","prov"],"text":"Impair dedicated {a|assets} first, then provide $50,000."}],"caption":"Net loss, not gross cost. And if walking away is cheaper, the penalty caps it.","id":"diagram-n6ey8x","sect":6,"part":"Recognise · onerous contracts","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:466px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 424.00 248.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 233.4)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-233.4 409.5,-233.4 409.5,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>fulfil\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M74.1,-219C74.1,-219 12,-219 12,-219 6,-219 0,-213 0,-207 0,-207 0,-195 0,-195 0,-189 6,-183 12,-183 12,-183 74.1,-183 74.1,-183 80.1,-183 86.1,-189 86.1,-195 86.1,-195 86.1,-207 86.1,-207 86.1,-213 80.1,-219 74.1,-219\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"10.8\" y=\"-201.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Cost to fulfil\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"26.17\" y=\"-192.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">300,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node3\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>net\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\