.98 136.93,-37.98 134.48,-44.98 139.38,-44.98\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"je-box8bk","type":"je","bucket":"initial","blabel":"best estimate","lo":32,"q":"Kallang Kitchens sold 1,000 ovens this year with a one-year warranty. Its {l|warranty provision} at year end, measured at expected value, is $40,000. Record it.","accounts":["Warranty expense","Provision for warranties","Legal claims expense","Provision for legal claims","Onerous contract expense","Provision for onerous contract","Restructuring expense","Restructuring provision","Decommissioning provision","Property, plant and equipment","Finance cost","Reimbursement receivable","Cash","Trade payables","Accruals","Retained earnings","Other income"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Warranty expense","amount":40000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for warranties","amount":40000}],"why":"The {x|expense} belongs to the year of the sales that carry the warranty. Repairs paid next year are charged against the {l|provision}, not to {x|expense}.","sect":7,"part":"Initial measurement · best estimate"},{"id":"worked-1jllsn","type":"worked","bucket":"initial","blabel":"best estimate","lo":32,"title":"Worked example: a warranty provision","q":"Sengkang Fans sold 2,000 ceiling fans this year with a one-year warranty. Past data: 80% need no repair, 15% need a minor repair at $60, and 5% need a replacement motor at $250. What is the {l|provision}, and the entry?","intro":["A typical exam question: you get the units sold and a table of outcomes with probabilities and costs, and must give the {l|provision} or the entry. Remember two things:","- Many similar items → **expected value** (probability × cost, summed). One big item → most likely outcome.","- “No repair” contributes nothing, but it still counts in the probabilities. Don’t drop it and rescale the others.","Then follow these steps: weight each outcome → cost per unit → × units → entry."],"steps":[{"h":"Decide: population or single item?","t":"Thousands of similar fans: a large population, so use the **expected value**."},{"h":"Weight each outcome by its probability.","t":["Minor: 15% × 60 = **9**","Replacement motor: 5% × 250 = **12.5**","No repair: 80% × 0 = 0"]},{"h":"Add them up: the expected cost per fan.","t":"9 + 12.5 = **$21.5** per fan."},{"h":"Scale up to all the fans sold.","t":"{l|Provision} = 2,000 × 21.5 = **{l|$43,000}**. Within a year, so discounting isn’t material."},{"h":"Record the entry.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Warranty expense","amount":43000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for warranties","amount":43000}],"after":"Next year’s repairs are charged against the {l|provision}, not to {x|expense} again."}],"sect":7,"part":"Initial measurement · best estimate"},{"id":"calc-1g58m2","type":"calc","bucket":"initial","blabel":"best estimate","lo":32,"given":2,"q":"Bedok Kettles sold 5,000 kettles with a one-year warranty: 90% no fault, 8% a minor fault at $20, 2% a replacement at $90. The first steps are done. Finish the {l|provision}.","answer":17000,"unit":"$","working":["Large population → expected value.","Minor 8% × 20 = 1.6; replacement 2% × 90 = 1.8.","Per kettle = 3.4; × 5,000 = $17,000."],"sect":7,"part":"Initial measurement · best estimate"},{"id":"think-lgiwwx","type":"think","bucket":"initial","blabel":"best estimate","lo":32,"scenario":"Marina Ferries faces one lawsuit. Its lawyers estimate a 60% chance of losing and paying $500,000, and a 40% chance of winning and paying nothing.","prompt":"The expected value is 60% × 500,000 = $300,000. Is that the {l|provision}?","answer":["**No.** Marina will pay either $500,000 or nothing; it will never pay $300,000. For a **single obligation**, start from the **most likely outcome**: **$500,000**.","Adjust only if the other outcomes are mostly higher or mostly lower than the most likely one. Here the only other outcome is nil and the loss is probable, so the {l|provision} stays at the most likely amount."],"sect":7,"part":"Initial measurement · best estimate"},{"id":"calc-18ucu2","type":"calc","bucket":"initial","blabel":"best estimate","lo":32,"q":"Toa Payoh Toasters sold 1,500 toasters with a one-year warranty. 70% will have no fault, 25% a minor fault costing $40, and 5% a major fault costing $300. What {l|warranty provision} should it recognise?","answer":37500,"unit":"$","working":["A large population of similar items: expected value.","Per toaster: 25% × 40 + 5% × 300 = 10 + 15 = 25.","1,500 × 25 = $37,500."],"sect":7,"part":"Initial measurement · best estimate"},{"type":"map","id":"map-8-initial","bucket":"initial","blabel":"discounting","sect":8,"part":"Initial measurement · discounting"},{"id":"think-oy9cgx","type":"think","bucket":"initial","blabel":"discounting","lo":32,"scenario":"Pasir Power builds a plant for $900,000 on its leased site. Its licence says it must remove the plant and clean up the site in 3 years, at an expected cost of $100,000. The discount rate is 10%.","prompt":"Two questions: is the {l|provision} $100,000? And should it be expensed now?","answer":["Not $100,000: the cash is paid in 3 years, so the time value of money is material. Discount it: 100,000 ÷ 1.10³ = **$75,131**.","And not expensed: the obligation comes from building the plant, so the cost is part of getting the plant ready. **Add it to the {a|asset}** (SFRS(I) 1-16): {a|plant cost} 900,000 + 75,131 = $975,131, depreciated over its life."],"sect":8,"part":"Initial measurement · discounting"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","blabel":"discounting","lo":32,"title":"Discounting and decommissioning","body":["The best estimate so far ignored timing. When the cash goes out years from now, as in a clean-up, two extra rules kick in: discount it, and put it into the {a|asset}.","- **Discount** when the time value of money is **material**. Use a pre-tax rate that reflects current market assessments of the time value of money and the risks specific to the {l|liability}.","- **Decommissioning or restoration**: the obligation arises when the damage is done (building the plant). Recognise the present value as a {l|provision} and **add it to the cost of the {a|asset}**.","> Dr {a|Property, plant and equipment}, Cr {l|Decommissioning provision} (present value)","- After that, the {l|provision} **unwinds** each year as a **{x|finance cost}**, growing to the full cash amount by the time it is paid.","! Expensing the decommissioning cost on day 1, or providing the undiscounted amount, are both wrong."],"id":"rule-51s8p9","sect":8,"part":"Initial measurement · discounting"},{"id":"je-bm9ho0","type":"je","bucket":"initial","blabel":"discounting","lo":32,"q":"Pasir Power has just built a plant for $900,000. Its licence requires it to remove the plant and clean up the site in 3 years, at an expected cost of $100,000; discounted at 10%, that is $75,131 today. Record the {l|decommissioning provision} on day 1.","accounts":["Warranty expense","Provision for warranties","Legal claims expense","Provision for legal claims","Onerous contract expense","Provision for onerous contract","Restructuring expense","Restructuring provision","Decommissioning provision","Property, plant and equipment","Finance cost","Reimbursement receivable","Cash","Trade payables","Accruals","Retained earnings","Other income"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Property, plant and equipment","amount":75131},{"side":"Cr","account":"Decommissioning provision","amount":75131}],"why":"100,000 ÷ 1.10³ = 75,131, added to the {a|plant’s cost}. It reaches P&L through {x|depreciation} over 3 years and the {x|unwinding of the discount}.","sect":8,"part":"Initial measurement · discounting"},{"type":"map","id":"map-9-initial","bucket":"initial","blabel":"reimbursements","sect":9,"part":"Initial measurement · reimbursements"},{"id":"think-1ibt7v","type":"think","bucket":"initial","blabel":"reimbursements","lo":32,"scenario":"Marina Ferries is being sued by passengers injured when one of its gangways collapsed, and has recognised a $200,000 {l|provision} for the lawsuit. Its insurer has confirmed it will reimburse $150,000 of any damages; recovery is virtually certain.","prompt":"Should Marina show a {l|net liability} of $50,000?","answer":["No. The {l|provision} stays at the full $200,000, because Marina is still liable to the passengers. The reimbursement is a **{a|separate asset}** of $150,000.","In P&L, the {x|expense} may be shown net of the reimbursement ($50,000)."],"sect":9,"part":"Initial measurement · reimbursements"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","blabel":"reimbursements","lo":32,"title":"Reimbursements","body":["Sometimes someone else will pay part of the bill: an insurer, a supplier. This rule stops the {l|provision} from shrinking just because help is on the way.","1. Recognise the {a|reimbursement} (insurance, an indemnity, a supplier’s guarantee) **only when virtually certain** to be received.","2. Show it as a **{a|separate asset}** ({a|Reimbursement receivable}), never netted against the {l|provision} in the statement of financial position.","3. The {a|asset} can’t be **more than the {l|provision}**.","- In P&L, the {x|expense} may be presented net of the reimbursement.","! A reimbursement that is only probable is not recognised. It is the contingent-asset threshold again."],"id":"rule-1w2pxg","sect":9,"part":"Initial measurement · reimbursements"},{"type":"visual","bucket":"initial","blabel":"reimbursements","lo":32,"title":"Gross on the balance sheet, net in P&L","html":"\u003cp>Marina’s lawsuit in three layers. The balance sheet shows \u003cstrong>two\u003c/strong> items: the full \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">provision\u003c/span> (Marina still owes the passengers) and, separately, the \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">insurance receivable\u003c/span>. Only in P&amp;L may the two be netted, leaving a \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">net expense\u003c/span> of 50,000.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"cbars\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">Liabilities: \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Provision for legal claims\u003c/span> \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">200,000\u003c/span>\u003c/strong> (gross)\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003cspan class=\"seg l\" style=\"width:100.00%\">\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">Assets: \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Reimbursement receivable\u003c/span> \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">150,000\u003c/span>\u003c/strong> (shown separately)\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003cspan class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:75.00%\">\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">P&amp;L: \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">expense\u003c/span> 200,000 − reimbursement 150,000 = \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">50,000\u003c/span>\u003c/strong> net\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003cspan class=\"seg x\" style=\"width:25.00%\">\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"seg out\" style=\"width:75.00%\">\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-key\">\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg l\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">liability\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg a\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">separate asset\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg x\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">net expense\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg out\">\u003c/i>cancelled by the reimbursement\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>","id":"visual-yovwv9","sect":9,"part":"Initial measurement · reimbursements"},{"id":"je-nz62nd","type":"je","bucket":"initial","blabel":"reimbursements","lo":32,"q":"Marina Ferries is being sued by passengers injured when one of its gangways collapsed. Record its {l|lawsuit provision} of $200,000 and the virtually certain {a|insurance reimbursement} of $150,000.","accounts":["Warranty expense","Provision for warranties","Legal claims expense","Provision for legal claims","Onerous contract expense","Provision for onerous contract","Restructuring expense","Restructuring provision","Decommissioning provision","Property, plant and equipment","Finance cost","Reimbursement receivable","Cash","Trade payables","Accruals","Retained earnings","Other income"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Legal claims expense","amount":200000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for legal claims","amount":200000},{"side":"Dr","account":"Reimbursement receivable","amount":150000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Legal claims expense","amount":150000}],"why":"The {l|provision} stays gross at 200,000; the reimbursement is a {a|separate asset} of 150,000. {x|Net expense} in P&L: 50,000.","alt":"Crediting a separate {i|income line} instead of the {x|expense line} is also acceptable; the net presentation in P&L is a choice.","sect":9,"part":"Initial measurement · reimbursements"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"initial","blabel":"reimbursements","lo":32,"special":"**Best estimate**: expected value for a population, most likely outcome for a single obligation; discounted if material. Decommissioning PV is added to the {a|asset’s cost}.","id":"compare-1kumyt-3","sect":9,"part":"Initial measurement · reimbursements"},{"type":"map","id":"map-10-subsequent","bucket":"subsequent","blabel":"unwinding","sect":10,"part":"Subsequent measurement · unwinding"},{"id":"think-oj41to","type":"think","bucket":"subsequent","blabel":"unwinding","lo":32,"scenario":"Pasir Power must pay $100,000 to clean up its plant site in 3 years. On day 1 it recognised a {l|decommissioning provision} at the present value, $75,131 (discounted at 10%). A year on, the cash is one year closer. Nothing else has changed.","prompt":"Should the {l|provision} still be $75,131? If it grows, where does the increase go?","answer":["It grows: the cash is now only 2 years away, so its present value is higher. 75,131 × 10% = **$7,513**.","That growth is the **unwinding of the discount**. It is {x|interest}, so it goes to **{x|finance cost}**, not to {x|operating expenses} and not to the {a|plant}."],"sect":10,"part":"Subsequent measurement · unwinding"},{"id":"je-17ev38","type":"je","bucket":"subsequent","blabel":"unwinding","lo":32,"q":"Pasir Power’s {l|decommissioning provision} was recognised on day 1 at $75,131, the present value of $100,000 payable in 3 years, discounted at 10%. Record the year-1 unwinding.","accounts":["Warranty expense","Provision for warranties","Legal claims expense","Provision for legal claims","Onerous contract expense","Provision for onerous contract","Restructuring expense","Restructuring provision","Decommissioning provision","Property, plant and equipment","Finance cost","Reimbursement receivable","Cash","Trade payables","Accruals","Retained earnings","Other income"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Finance cost","amount":7513},{"side":"Cr","account":"Decommissioning provision","amount":7513}],"why":"{l|Opening provision} 75,131 × 10% = 7,513 {x|finance cost}. The {l|provision} becomes {l|82,644}.","sect":10,"part":"Subsequent measurement · unwinding"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"subsequent","blabel":"unwinding","lo":32,"title":"Decommissioning: from present value to cash","intro":"Pasir Power’s {l|provision} over its three years. Each step’s growth is a {x|finance cost}.","notes":{"y0":"Day 1: 100,000 ÷ 1.10³ = $75,131. Dr {a|Property, plant and equipment}, Cr {l|Decommissioning provision}.","y1":"End of year 1: {l|opening} 75,131 + {x|unwinding} 7,513 = {l|closing} $82,644.","y2":"End of year 2: {l|opening} 82,644 + {x|unwinding} 8,264 = {l|closing} $90,908.","y3":"End of year 3: {l|opening} 90,908 + {x|unwinding} 9,092 = {l|closing} $100,000 (last year’s figure rounded so it lands exactly on the cash).","ppe":"The day-1 PV joins the {a|plant’s cost} (900,000 + 75,131 = 975,131) and reaches P&L through {x|depreciation}.","fc":"Each year’s unwinding: Dr {x|Finance cost}, Cr {l|Decommissioning provision}.","pay":"At the end, the full $100,000 is paid out of the provision: Dr {l|Decommissioning provision}, Cr {a|Cash}."},"walk":[{"nodes":["y0","ppe"],"text":"Day 1: the present value goes on the balance sheet, on both sides."},{"nodes":["y1","fc"],"text":"Year 1: {x|unwinding} 7,513 to {x|finance cost}."},{"nodes":["y2","y3"],"text":"Each year the {l|provision} grows by the rate × its {l|opening balance}."},{"nodes":["pay"],"text":"By the end of year 3 it equals the $100,000 cash cost, and is used to pay it."}],"caption":"Two charges reach P&L: {x|depreciation} of the extra {a|asset cost}, and the unwinding as {x|finance cost}.","id":"diagram-183bzk","sect":10,"part":"Subsequent measurement · unwinding","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:640px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 733.00 204.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 189.4)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-189.4 718.9,-189.4 718.9,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>y0\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M52.35,-55C52.35,-55 12,-55 12,-55 6,-55 0,-49 0,-43 0,-43 0,-31 0,-31 0,-25 6,-19 12,-19 12,-19 52.35,-19 52.35,-19 58.35,-19 64.35,-25 64.35,-31 64.35,-31 64.35,-43 64.35,-43 64.35,-49 58.35,-55 52.35,-55\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"17.55\" y=\"-37.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Day 1\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"10.8\" y=\"-28.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">PV 75,131\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>y1\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M225.07,-94C225.07,-94 158.47,-94 158.47,-94 152.47,-94 146.47,-88