158 159.38,-158 159.38,-158 153.38,-158 147.38,-152 147.38,-146 147.38,-146 147.38,-134 147.38,-134 147.38,-128 153.38,-122 159.38,-122 159.38,-122 212.48,-122 212.48,-122 218.48,-122 224.48,-128 224.48,-134 224.48,-134 224.48,-146 224.48,-146 224.48,-152 218.48,-158 212.48,-158\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"158.18\" y=\"-136.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">P&amp;L credit\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge8\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>rev&#45;&gt;cr\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,2\" d=\"M126.35,-140C130.4,-140 134.45,-140 138.51,-140\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"138.31,-142.45 145.31,-140 138.31,-137.55 138.31,-142.45\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node6\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>close\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M81.97,-36C81.97,-36 43.88,-36 43.88,-36 37.88,-36 31.87,-30 31.87,-24 31.87,-24 31.87,-12 31.87,-12 31.87,-6 37.88,0 43.88,0 43.88,0 81.97,0 81.97,0 87.97,0 93.97,-6 93.97,-12 93.97,-12 93.97,-24 93.97,-24 93.97,-30 87.97,-36 81.97,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"42.67\" y=\"-18.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Closing\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"46.05\" y=\"-9.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">121,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge5\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>unw&#45;&gt;close\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M62.92,-60.62C62.92,-55.62 62.92,-50.06 62.92,-44.68\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"65.38,-44.98 62.93,-37.98 60.48,-44.98 65.38,-44.98\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node10\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>fc\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#ffe4e6\" stroke=\"#be123c\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M205.1,-97C205.1,-97 140.75,-97 140.75,-97 134.75,-97 128.75,-91 128.75,-85 128.75,-85 128.75,-73 128.75,-73 128.75,-67 134.75,-61 140.75,-61 140.75,-61 205.1,-61 205.1,-61 211.1,-61 217.1,-67 217.1,-73 217.1,-73 217.1,-85 217.1,-85 217.1,-91 211.1,-97 205.1,-97\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"139.55\" y=\"-75.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Finance cost\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge9\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>unw&#45;&gt;fc\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,2\" d=\"M107.18,-79C111.46,-79 115.74,-79 120.02,-79\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"119.73,-81.45 126.73,-79 119.73,-76.55 119.73,-81.45\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"present","lo":34,"q":"Which of these must be disclosed?","options":[{"t":"A lawsuit against the entity where the chance of losing is remote","why":"Remote contingent liabilities need no disclosure."},{"t":"A claim the entity is making against a supplier, which it will possibly (but not probably) win","why":"Contingent assets are disclosed only when the inflow is probable."},{"t":"A lawsuit against the entity that it will possibly (but not probably) lose","ok":true,"why":"A contingent liability that isn’t remote: disclose its nature and, where practicable, its financial effect."},{"t":"Comparatives for the provisions reconciliation","why":"The reconciliation of movements doesn’t need comparative figures."}],"id":"mcq-mgkrok","sect":13,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"present","lo":34,"special":"A **reconciliation** of each class of {l|provision} (no comparatives needed); contingent liabilities disclosed unless remote; contingent assets disclosed only if probable.","id":"compare-1kumyt-4","sect":13,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"type":"examtypes","id":"examtypes","bucket":"wrap","sect":14,"part":"Wrap-up"},{"type":"summary","lo":34,"title":"Provisions on one page","rows":[["{l|Provision}?","A {l|liability} of uncertain timing or amount."],["Recognise when?","Present obligation (legal or constructive) + past event + probable outflow + reliable estimate."],["Measure at?","Best estimate: expected value (many items) or most likely outcome (one item); discount if material."],["Contingent liability?","Possible obligation, or not probable / not measurable: disclose unless remote."],["Contingent asset?","Disclose if probable; recognise only if virtually certain."],["Onerous contract?","Provide the least net cost: lower of (cost to fulfil − benefits) and the exit penalty."],["Restructuring?","Only with a detailed formal plan and a valid expectation (started or announced). Direct costs only."],["Future operating losses?","Never provided for."],["Decommissioning?","PV added to the {a|asset}; unwinding to {x|finance cost}."],["Key disclosure?","Reconciliation of movements in each class of {l|provision}."]],"id":"summary-backlp","bucket":"wrap","sect":14,"part":"Wrap-up"}],"quiz":{"cases":{"tanjong":{"name":"Tanjong Appliances","text":["Tanjong Appliances Pte Ltd (Tanjong) makes washing machines and has a 31 December 20X5 year end.","In 20X5 Tanjong sold 4,000 machines with a one-year warranty. Past experience shows that 85% need no repair, 12% need a minor repair costing $50, and 3% need a major repair costing $400. No claims had been paid by year end.","A customer is suing Tanjong for $250,000 after a machine flooded her shop in September 20X5. Tanjong’s lawyers think it is about 70% likely that Tanjong will lose and have to pay the full amount.","Tanjong is itself suing a supplier for $120,000 for faulty motors. Its lawyers think Tanjong will probably win, but the case won’t be heard until 20X6.","On 15 December 20X5 the board decided to close a loss-making assembly line. A detailed plan was drawn up, but it will not be announced to staff until February 20X6."]},"worksafe":{"name":"WorkSafe Hub","source":"Official practice paper (Q36–40 case; Q39 used here)","text":["WorkSafe Hub Pte Ltd (WorkSafe) is an education technology company that sells accredited health and safety training packages to a range of manufacturing clients.","In January 20X2, WorkSafe entered into a contract for S$325,000 with Iron Edge Fabrication (Iron Edge) to provide the following services:","• A 12-month subscription to its online accredited health and safety training database, custom onboarding, and 3 in-person training sessions.","The contract was signed by both parties on 1 January 20X2."]}},"questions":[{"id":"q1","case":"tanjong","lo":32,"type":"measure","q":"What {l|warranty provision} should Tanjong recognise at 31 December 20X5?","options":[{"t":"$0","why":"The most likely outcome for one machine is no repair, but with thousands of machines some will need repairs. Use expected value.","pit":"ev-single"},{"t":"$24,000","why":"Only the minor repairs. The major repairs must be weighted in too."},{"t":"$72,000","ok":true,"why":"4,000 × (12% × 50 + 3% × 400) = 4,000 × 18 = 72,000."},{"t":"$240,000","why":"Treats every repair as a major one. Weight each outcome by its own cost."}]},{"id":"q2","case":"tanjong","lo":33,"type":"prov-vs-cl","q":"How should Tanjong account for the customer’s lawsuit?","options":[{"t":"Disclose a contingent liability of $250,000","why":"The outflow is probable (70%), so a {l|provision} is recognised, not just disclosed.","pit":"probable-disclosed"},{"t":"Recognise a {l|provision} of $250,000","ok":true,"why":"Present obligation from a past event (the flood), probable outflow, reliable estimate. One lawsuit: use the most likely outcome, the full amount."},{"t":"Recognise a {l|provision} of $175,000","why":"Expected value (70% × 250,000). For a single obligation, start from the most likely outcome.","pit":"ev-single"},{"t":"No entry until the court decides","why":"A {l|provision} doesn’t wait for certainty. Probable is enough.","pit":"probable-disclosed"}]},{"id":"q3","case":"tanjong","lo":33,"type":"contingent-asset","q":"How should Tanjong treat its claim against the supplier?","options":[{"t":"Recognise a {a|receivable} of $120,000","why":"A contingent asset is recognised only when the inflow is virtually certain. Probable is not enough.","pit":"ca-recognised"},{"t":"Offset the $120,000 against the {l|warranty provision}","why":"It isn’t virtually certain, and even a recognised reimbursement is a {a|separate asset}, never netted against the {l|provision}.","pit":"reimb-net"},{"t":"Disclose it as a contingent asset","ok":true,"why":"A probable inflow is a contingent asset: disclosed, not recognised."},{"t":"Ignore it completely","why":"A probable contingent asset should be disclosed."}]},{"id":"q4","case":"tanjong","lo":33,"type":"special-cases","q":"Should Tanjong recognise a {l|restructuring provision} for closing the assembly line at 31 December 20X5?","options":[{"t":"Yes, because the board has approved a detailed plan","why":"A plan is only half the test. Without a start or an announcement, there is no valid expectation in those affected.","pit":"intent-obligation"},{"t":"Yes, but only for the redundancy costs","why":"Redundancy costs would qualify, but only once there is a constructive obligation. There isn’t one yet.","pit":"intent-obligation"},{"t":"No, because there is no valid expectation in those affected at the year end","ok":true,"why":"A detailed formal plan and a valid expectation (started or announced) are both needed. The announcement is in February 20X6."},{"t":"No, because restructurings are never provided for","why":"They are, once there is a constructive obligation."}]},{"id":"q5","case":"tanjong","lo":32,"type":"use-reverse","q":"In 20X6 Tanjong pays warranty claims of $57,600 on the 20X5 sales, and the warranty period ends. What happens to the rest of the 20X5 {l|warranty provision}?","options":[{"t":"It stays as a general {l|provision} for future claims","why":"The warranties have expired, so there is no obligation left. And a {l|provision} can’t be kept for other purposes.","pit":"wrong-use"},{"t":"The remaining $14,400 is reversed, a credit to 20X6 profit or loss","ok":true,"why":"{l|Provision} 72,000 − 57,600 used = {l|14,400} no longer needed: reverse it through P&L."},{"t":"The 20X5 accounts are restated to the actual claims","why":"The 20X5 {l|provision} was the best estimate then. Differences are a change in estimate, dealt with in 20X6.","pit":"wrong-use"},{"t":"It is transferred to the {l|restructuring provision}","why":"A {l|provision} may only be used for the expenditure it was set up for.","pit":"wrong-use"}]},{"id":"q6","case":"worksafe","lo":33,"type":"onerous","source":"Official practice paper Q39","q":"In July 20X2, WorkSafe suffered a cybersecurity breach which compromised the online health and safety training database. WorkSafe needed to rebuild the database, fix the vulnerabilities that allowed the breach and implement additional security controls. The estimated cost of fulfilling the remaining contract totalled $250,000 and the estimated contract revenue remaining was $110,000. With reference to SFRS(I) 37, Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets, identify how WorkSafe should account for the impact of the cybersecurity attack on its contract with Iron Edge?","options":[{"t":"No adjustment is required until the completion of the 12 month contract","why":"The loss is unavoidable now, so it is recognised now, not when the contract ends.","pit":"onerous-wait"},{"t":"An onerous contract provision of $140,000 should be immediately recognised in the Statement of financial position","ok":true,"why":"The contract is onerous: unavoidable cost 250,000 − remaining {i|revenue} 110,000 = 140,000. No exit penalty is given, so the {l|provision} is the net loss, recognised immediately."},{"t":"An expense of $250,000 should be recognised in the Statement of profit or loss","why":"The full cost to fulfil ignores the $110,000 of {i|revenue} still to come. Only the net loss is provided.","pit":"onerous-gross"},{"t":"An onerous contract provision of $250,000 should be immediately recognised in the Statement of financial position","why":"Right idea, wrong amount: the {l|provision} is the net loss, not the gross cost.","pit":"onerous-gross"}]},{"id":"q7","case":"worksafe","lo":33,"type":"onerous","q":"In July 20X2 a cybersecurity breach made WorkSafe’s contract with Iron Edge onerous: fulfilling the rest of it will cost $250,000 and bring in only $110,000 of {i|revenue}. Which journal entry records WorkSafe’s {l|onerous contract provision}?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Onerous contract expense","amount":140000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for onerous contract","amount":140000}],"ok":true,"why":"The net unavoidable loss of 140,000 is expensed now, with a {l|provision} in {l|liabilities}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Onerous contract expense","amount":250000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for onerous contract","amount":250000}],"why":"The gross cost to fulfil. The remaining {i|revenue} reduces the {x|loss}.","pit":"onerous-gross"},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":140000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for onerous contract","amount":140000}],"why":"The loss arises this year, so it goes through this year’s P&L, not straight to {e|equity}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Onerous contract expense","amount":140000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accruals","amount":140000}],"why":"The amount and timing are uncertain: it is a {l|provision}, not an {l|accrual}."}]},{"id":"q8","case":"worksafe","lo":33,"type":"onerous","q":"In July 20X2 a cybersecurity breach made WorkSafe’s contract with Iron Edge onerous: fulfilling the rest of it will cost $250,000 and bring in only $110,000 of {i|revenue}. Suppose instead that WorkSafe could terminate the contract by paying Iron Edge a penalty of $90,000. What {l|provision} would be needed?","options":[{"t":"$140,000","why":"Fulfilling costs a net 140,000, but walking away costs only 90,000. The unavoidable cost is the cheaper exit.","pit":"onerous-gross"},{"t":"$90,000","ok":true,"why":"Least net cost of exiting = lower of net cost to fulfil (140,000) and the penalty (90,000) = 90,000."},{"t":"$160,000","why":"Cost to fulfil minus the penalty mixes the two options. Compare the net cost of each option instead."},{"t":"$0, because WorkSafe can exit","why":"Exiting still costs the penalty, which is unavoidable either way."}]},{"id":"q9","case":"worksafe","lo":33,"type":"onerous","q":"In July 20X2 a cybersecurity breach made WorkSafe’s contract with Iron Edge onerous: fulfilling the rest of it will cost $250,000 and bring in only $110,000 of {i|revenue}. WorkSafe owns {a|servers} used only for the Iron Edge contract. What should it do with them before recognising the {l|onerous contract provision}?","options":[{"t":"Nothing: the {l|provision} covers all the losses","why":"{a|Assets} dedicated to the contract are tested for impairment first."},{"t":"Test them for impairment and recognise any {x|impairment loss} first","ok":true,"why":"Impair {a|assets} dedicated to the contract first; the {l|provision} covers the remaining loss."},{"t":"Revalue them to fair value through OCI","why":"Nothing here supports a revaluation. The issue is impairment."},{"t":"Add their {a|carrying amount} to the {l|provision}","why":"The servers are an {a|asset}; any shortfall is an {x|impairment} of that asset, not part of the {l|provision}."}]},{"id":"q10","case":"worksafe","lo":33,"type":"special-cases","q":"After a cybersecurity breach in July 20X2, WorkSafe expects its whole training business to make an operating loss of about $200,000 next year. How should it account for this?","options":[{"t":"Recognise a {l|provision} of $200,000 for future operating losses","why":"Future operating losses are never provided for: there is no present obligation.","pit":"future-losses"},{"t":"Add $200,000 to the {l|onerous contract provision}","why":"The onerous {l|provision} covers only the Iron Edge contract’s unavoidable loss, not the business’s expected trading losses.","pit":"future-losses"},{"t":"Disclose a contingent liability of $200,000","why":"Future losses are not a possible obligation either. They come from future trading."},{"t":"No {l|provision}; consider whether the business’s {a|assets} are impaired","ok":true,"why":"Future losses are not provided for, but they are a sign of possible impairment."}]},{"id":"q11","lo":32,"type":"prov-vs-cl","source":"Official practice paper Q6","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-37, Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets, identify which of the following best distinguishes a contingent liability from a provision?","options":[{"t":"(On the official paper this option only repeated the question text.)","why":"Not a real answer: it just repeats the question."},{"t":"A contingent liability involves an existing uncertainty of an obligation arising from a past event, of the outflow of resources or its amounts whilst a provision has a present obligation (legal or constructive) arising from a past event.","ok":true,"why":"The difference is about the obligation: a {l|provision} is a present obligation (legal or constructive) from a past event; a contingent liability involves uncertainty about whether there is an obligation, or about the outflow or its amount."},{"t":"The outflow from a contingent liability can be reliably estimated whereas the outflow from a provision cannot be reliably determined.","why":"Backwards and not the key difference. A {l|provision} needs a reliable estimate; both can be uncertain in amount."},{"t":"A contingent liability needs to be remeasured at the end of each reporting period to reflect the current best estimate whereas a provision does not.","why":"Backwards: a {l|provision} is reviewed and adjusted to the current best estimate at each reporting date."}]},{"id":"q12","lo":32,"type":"obligation","q":"Which of the following creates a **constructive** obligation?","options":[{"t":"A signed contract to repair goods sold","why":"That is a legal obligation (from a contract)."},{"t":"A widely published environmental policy to clean up any contamination it causes, which the company has always honoured, after it contaminates land in a country with no clean-up law","ok":true,"why":"Its published policy and past practice create a valid expectation that it will clean up: a constructive obligation."},{"t":"A board minute stating the company intends to donate to charity next year","why":"An intention that can be changed. No one has a valid expectation yet.","pit":"intent-obligation"},{"t":"A new law requiring equipment upgrades by next year","why":"A legal requirement for the future: the company can still avoid it by its own future actions, so no obligation exists yet.","pit":"intent-obligation"}]},{"id":"q13","lo":33,"type":"special-cases","q":"An airline must overhaul each aircraft every three years by law. At year end an aircraft is 18 months from its next overhaul. Is a {l|provision} recognised for the overhaul?","options":[{"t":"Yes: half the expected overhaul cost","why":"There is no present obligation: the airline could sell the aircraft and never do the overhaul.","pit":"future-losses"},{"t":"Yes: the full expected overhaul cost","why":"Future repairs are avoidable, so they aren’t provided for.","pit":"future-losses"},{"t":"No: the cost is reflected through {x|depreciation} (e.g. a separate overhaul component)","ok":true,"why":"Future repairs and refurbishment are not provided for. The cost is spread through {x|depreciation} instead."},{"t":"No, but disclose a contingent liability","why":"There is no possible obligation either, just a future cost that can be avoided."}]},{"id":"q14","lo":34,"type":"disclose","q":"A lawsuit against a company is assessed as **remote**. What is required?","options":[{"t":"Recognise a {l|provision} for the most likely outcome","why":"A remote outflow is nowhere near probable.","pit":"cl-recognised"},{"t":"Disclose it as a contingent liability","why":"Contingent liabilities are disclosed unless remote.","pit":"remote-disclosed"},{"t":"Nothing","ok":true,"why":"Remote contingent liabilities need neither recognition nor disclosure."},{"t":"Disclose it only if the claim is above materiality","why":"Remote items need no disclosure, whatever their size.","pit":"remote-disclosed"}]},{"id":"q15","lo":32,"type":"reimburse","q":"A company recognises a {l|provision} of $80,000 for a customer’s claim. Its insurer has agreed in writing to cover claims up to $100,000, and payment is virtually certain. How is the reimbursement shown?","options":[{"t":"A {a|separate asset} of $100,000","why":"The {a|asset} can’t exceed the {l|provision} it relates to."},{"t":"A {a|separate asset} of $80,000","ok":true,"why":"Virtually certain, so recognised; separate from the {l|provision}; capped at the provision of 80,000."},{"t":"Netted against the {l|provision}, leaving nil","why":"Reimbursements are never netted against the {l|provision} in the statement of financial position.","pit":"reimb-net"},{"t":"Disclosed only, as a contingent asset","why":"Virtually certain means it is no longer contingent: recognise it."}]},{"id":"q16","lo":32,"type":"discount","q":"A company must pay a settlement of $242,000 in 2 years. The discount rate is 10% and the effect is material. What is the {x|finance cost} in the first year?","options":[{"t":"$0: a settlement isn’t a loan, so there is no interest","why":"A discounted {l|provision} unwinds, and the unwinding is a {x|finance cost}."},{"t":"$24,200","why":"Applies the rate to the undiscounted amount. The unwinding is the rate × the opening present value.","pit":"no-discount"},{"t":"$20,000","ok":true,"why":"{l|PV} = 242,000 ÷ 1.10² = 200,000; × 10% = 20,000, Dr {x|finance cost}, Cr {l|provision}."},{"t":"$42,000","why":"That is the total discount over both years, not the first year’s unwinding."}]},{"id":"q17","lo":32,"type":"discount","q":"A mining company has just started digging, which immediately damages the land it must restore at the end of the licence. How is the {l|restoration provision} recognised on day 1?","options":[{"t":"Dr {x|Restoration expense}, Cr {l|Provision}, at the undiscounted cost","why":"Neither expensed nor undiscounted: the PV is added to the {a|asset}.","pit":"decom-expense"},{"t":"Dr {a|Mine asset (PPE)}, Cr {l|Provision}, at the present value of the cost","ok":true,"why":"The restoration cost is part of the {a|asset’s cost} under SFRS(I) 1-16, at present value."},{"t":"No entry until the licence ends","why":"The damage has been done, so the obligation exists now."},{"t":"Dr {a|Mine asset (PPE)}, Cr {l|Provision}, at the undiscounted cost","why":"The time value of money is material over a licence period, so the {l|provision} is discounted.","pit":"no-discount"}]},{"id":"q18","lo":34,"type":"disclose","q":"Which of these is part of the required disclosure for each class of {l|provision}?","options":[{"t":"A reconciliation of the opening and closing balance, with comparatives","why":"The reconciliation is required, but comparatives aren’t."},{"t":"A reconciliation of the opening and closing balance, showing additions, amounts used, unused amounts reversed and the unwinding of the discount","ok":true,"why":"That is the movement table required for each class."},{"t":"Only the closing balance; the movements are confidential","why":"The reconciliation of movements is a core disclosure."},{"t":"The name of every claimant in each lawsuit","why":"Not required, and the seriously-prejudicial exemption may even allow less disclosure about disputes."}]}]},"exam":{"cases":{"pulau":{"name":"Pulau Energy","text":["Pulau Energy Pte Ltd (Pulau) has a 31 December year end. On 1 January 20X1 it finished building a gas terminal on an offshore site, at a cost of $2,000,000.","Pulau’s licence requires it to remove the terminal and restore the seabed at the end of 5 years, which is also the terminal’s useful life. The removal is expected to cost $500,000 at that time. The appropriate pre-tax discount rate is 8%.","Pulau depreciates the terminal straight-line with no residual value.","In November 20X1 a pipe burst and leaked fuel into the sea. Local law requires Pulau to clean it up; the clean-up, not yet started, will cost an estimated $80,000 and will be done in early 20X2. Pulau has claimed $60,000 from its insurer, which has said the claim is “under review”."]},"bukit":{"name":"Bukit Retail","text":["Bukit Retail Ltd (Bukit) sells household electronics and has a 31 December 20X3 year end.","On 10 November 20X3 the board approved a detailed plan to close all its suburban stores, and on 1 December 20X3 the plan was announced to staff and landlords. Expected costs: redundancy pay $450,000; penalties for ending store contracts early $120,000; retraining staff moving to city stores $90,000; moving stock and fixtures to the city stores $60,000; a rebranding campaign $75,000; and operating losses of the suburban stores until they close $200,000.","Bukit also has a non-cancellable contract to supply TVs to a hotel chain at a fixed price. Completing it will cost $600,000 and bring in $450,000. Bukit could end the contract by paying a penalty of $200,000.","Bukit’s {l|warranty provision} was $90,000 at 1 January 20X3. During 20X3 it paid claims of $70,000. At 31 December 20X3, 6,000 products are under warranty: 10% are expected to need a repair costing $80 and 2% a replacement costing $500; the rest no claim."]}},"questions":[{"id":"x1","case":"pulau","lo":32,"type":"discount","q":"At what amount should the {a|terminal} be recognised on 1 January 20X1?","options":[{"t":"$2,000,000","why":"Leaves out the removal obligation, which is part of the {a|asset’s cost}.","pit":"decom-expense"},{"t":"$2,500,000","why":"Adds the undiscounted removal cost. It is five years away, so it is discounted.","pit":"no-discount"},{"t":"$2,340,292","ok":true,"why":"2,000,000 + PV of removal (500,000 ÷ 1.08⁵ = 340,292) = 2,340,292."},{"t":"$1,659,708","why":"The {l|provision} is added to the {a|asset}, not deducted from it."}]},{"id":"x2","case":"pulau","lo":32,"type":"discount","q":"Which journal entry records the unwinding of the {l|decommissioning provision} for 20X1?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Finance cost","amount":27223},{"side":"Cr","account":"Decommissioning provision","amount":27223}],"ok":true,"why":"{l|Opening PV} 340,292 × 8% = 27,223, a {x|finance cost}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Property, plant and equipment","amount":27223},{"side":"Cr","account":"Decommissioning provision","amount":27223}],"why":"Only the day-1 PV goes into the {a|asset}. The unwinding is a {x|finance cost}.","pit":"unwind-opex"},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Finance cost","amount":40000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Decommissioning provision","amount":40000}],"why":"Applies 8% to the undiscounted cash. Unwinding is 8% × the {l|opening PV}.","pit":"no-discount"},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Finance cost","amount":31942},{"side":"Cr","account":"Decommissioning provision","amount":31942}],"why":"Spreads the discount straight-line. Unwinding uses the effective rate on the {l|opening balance}."}]},{"id":"x3","case":"pulau","lo":32,"type":"discount","q":"What is the {l|decommissioning provision} at 31 December 20X2?","options":[{"t":"$367,515","why":"That is the {l|31 December 20X1 balance}. Unwind a second year."},{"t":"$396,916","ok":true,"why":"340,292 → 367,515 (+27,223) → 396,916 (+29,401). Check: 500,000 ÷ 1.08³ ≈ 396,916."},{"t":"$394,738","why":"Adds the year-1 {x|unwinding} twice. The second year’s {x|unwinding} is on the {l|higher opening balance}."},{"t":"$500,000","why":"The {l|provision} only reaches the full cash amount at the end of year 5.","pit":"no-discount"}]},{"id":"x4","case":"pulau","lo":32,"type":"discount","q":"What is the total charge to profit or loss for 20X1 for the terminal and its decommissioning (ignore the fuel leak)?","options":[{"t":"$495,281","ok":true,"why":"{x|Depreciation} 2,340,292 ÷ 5 = 468,058 + {x|unwinding} 27,223 = 495,281."},{"t":"$468,058","why":"{x|Depreciation} only. The unwinding is a {x|finance cost} in P&L too."},{"t":"$740,292","why":"Expenses the decommissioning PV on day 1. It is capitalised and depreciated.","pit":"decom-expense"},{"t":"$427,223","why":"Depreciates only the construction cost. The decommissioning PV is part of the {a|depreciable cost}."}]},{"id":"x5","case":"pulau","lo":33,"type":"reimburse","q":"How should Pulau account for the fuel leak at 31 December 20X1?","options":[{"t":"{l|Provision} of $80,000 and a {a|receivable} of $60,000","why":"The insurance claim is only “under review”: not virtually certain, so no {a|asset}.","pit":"reimb-net"},{"t":"{l|Provision} of $20,000, net of the insurance claim","why":"Never net a reimbursement against the {l|provision}, and this one isn’t virtually certain anyway.","pit":"reimb-net"},{"t":"{l|Provision} of $80,000; disclose the insurance claim as a contingent asset if an inflow is probable","ok":true,"why":"The leak and the law create a present obligation, so provide the full amount. The claim is at most a contingent asset."},{"t":"Contingent liability of $80,000, disclosed only","why":"The law requires the clean-up and the cost is probable: recognise a {l|provision}.","pit":"probable-disclosed"}]},{"id":"x6","case":"bukit","lo":33,"type":"special-cases","q":"What {l|restructuring provision} should Bukit recognise at 31 December 20X3?","options":[{"t":"$0, because the stores have not closed yet","why":"A detailed plan plus an announcement before year end creates a constructive obligation.","pit":"intent-obligation"},{"t":"$570,000","ok":true,"why":"Direct costs only: redundancy 450,000 + contract penalties 120,000 = 570,000."},{"t":"$720,000","why":"Retraining and moving to continuing stores relate to the ongoing business.","pit":"restructure-costs"},{"t":"$770,000","why":"Future operating losses are never provided for, even in a restructuring.","pit":"future-losses"}]},{"id":"x7","case":"bukit","lo":33,"type":"onerous","q":"What {l|provision}, if any, is needed for the hotel TV contract?","options":[{"t":"$200,000","why":"The penalty, but delivering is cheaper: a {x|net loss} of 150,000.","pit":"onerous-gross"},{"t":"$150,000","ok":true,"why":"Least net cost: lower of 600,000 − 450,000 = 150,000 and the 200,000 penalty = 150,000."},{"t":"$600,000","why":"The gross cost ignores the {i|revenue} still to come.","pit":"onerous-gross"},{"t":"$0: losses are recognised as the TVs are delivered","why":"An onerous contract’s unavoidable loss is recognised as soon as the contract becomes onerous.","pit":"onerous-wait"}]},{"id":"x8","case":"bukit","lo":32,"type":"measure","q":"What is Bukit’s {x|warranty expense} for 20X3?","options":[{"t":"$108,000","why":"That is the {l|closing provision}. Part of it was already there at the start of the year."},{"t":"$88,000","ok":true,"why":"{l|Closing provision} 6,000 × (10% × 80 + 2% × 500) = 108,000. {l|Opening} 90,000 − used 70,000 = {l|20,000 left}, so {x|top up} 88,000."},{"t":"$70,000","why":"The claims paid were charged against the {l|opening provision}, not to {x|expense}.","pit":"wrong-use"},{"t":"$18,000","why":"Ignores the claims paid out of the {l|provision} in the year."}]},{"id":"x9","case":"bukit","lo":34,"type":"disclose","q":"In Bukit’s provisions note, which line shows the $70,000 of warranty claims paid?","options":[{"t":"Additional provisions made in the year","why":"Additions are new charges to P&L. Claims paid reduce the {l|provision}."},{"t":"Unused amounts reversed","why":"Reversals are amounts no longer needed, credited back to P&L. These claims were needed and paid."},{"t":"Amounts used (incurred and charged against the {l|provision})","ok":true,"why":"Spending charged against the provision is shown as amounts used."},{"t":"Not shown: cash payments aren’t part of the provisions note","why":"The reconciliation shows every movement in the {l|provision}, including amounts used."}]},{"id":"x10","case":"bukit","lo":33,"type":"use-reverse","q":"In 20X4 the store closures cost $30,000 less than provided. Bukit’s finance director suggests keeping the $30,000 in the {l|restructuring provision} to cover a possible fine next year. What is correct?","options":[{"t":"Keep it: provisions can be used for any probable future cost","why":"A {l|provision} may be used only for the expenditure it was originally set up for.","pit":"wrong-use"},{"t":"Reverse the unused $30,000 to profit or loss in 20X4","ok":true,"why":"Unused amounts are reversed. A possible fine needs its own assessment under the four tests."},{"t":"Restate the 20X3 {l|provision} downwards by $30,000","why":"The 20X3 figure was the best estimate then. The difference is a change in estimate in 20X4.","pit":"wrong-use"},{"t":"Transfer the $30,000 to {e|retained earnings}","why":"Provisions are released through P&L, not moved within {e|equity}."}]},{"id":"x11","lo":32,"type":"measure","q":"Changi Cargo is being sued by one customer. Its lawyers say there is a 65% chance of losing and paying $900,000 and a 35% chance of paying nothing. What {l|provision} should it recognise?","options":[{"t":"$585,000","why":"Expected value suits a large population. A single obligation starts from the most likely outcome.","pit":"ev-single"},{"t":"$900,000","ok":true,"why":"Probable outflow, single obligation: the most likely outcome is paying 900,000."},{"t":"$0, but disclose a contingent liability","why":"A 65% chance is probable, so a {l|provision} is recognised.","pit":"probable-disclosed"},{"t":"$315,000","why":"Not a meaningful amount: it is the damages less the expected value."}]},{"id":"x12","lo":32,"type":"discount","q":"A legal settlement of $363,000 is payable in 2 years. The provision was recognised at its present value at a 10% discount rate. What is the {l|provision} after **two** years’ unwinding, and the year-2 {x|finance cost}?","options":[{"t":"$363,000; finance cost $33,000","ok":true,"why":"{l|PV} 300,000; year 1 +30,000; year 2 +33,000 = {l|363,000}, the full cash amount when it is due."},{"t":"$363,000; finance cost $30,000","why":"The year-2 {x|unwinding} is on the {l|higher opening balance}, so it is bigger than year 1."},{"t":"$330,000; finance cost $30,000","why":"That is only after one year."},{"t":"$300,000; finance cost $0","why":"A discounted provision unwinds every year.","pit":"unwind-opex"}]},{"id":"x13","lo":33,"type":"contingent-asset","q":"At year end a court has ruled in the entity’s favour and ordered a solvent defendant to pay $150,000 within 30 days; no appeal is possible. How is this treated?","options":[{"t":"Disclose a contingent asset of $150,000","why":"The inflow is now virtually certain, so it is no longer contingent."},{"t":"Recognise a {a|receivable} of $150,000","ok":true,"why":"Virtually certain: recognise the {a|asset}."},{"t":"Nothing until the cash is received","why":"Recognition doesn’t wait for cash."},{"t":"Recognise it in OCI","why":"It is {i|income} in P&L, like any other settlement."}]},{"id":"x14","lo":34,"type":"disclose","q":"A company is in a dispute with a competitor. Disclosing its estimate of the likely damages would seriously harm its negotiating position. What may it do?","options":[{"t":"Omit any mention of the dispute","why":"The exemption still requires the general nature of the dispute to be disclosed."},{"t":"Disclose the general nature of the dispute, and the fact and reason that the information hasn’t been disclosed","ok":true,"why":"That is the seriously-prejudicial exemption, for extremely rare cases."},{"t":"Recognise a {l|provision} instead, so no disclosure is needed","why":"Recognition depends on the four tests, not on what is convenient. Provisions need disclosure too."},{"t":"Disclose everything anyway: there are no exemptions","why":"There is an exemption for extremely rare seriously-prejudicial cases."}]},{"id":"x15","lo":33,"type":"prov-vs-cl","q":"Which situation requires a **{l|provision}** at the year end?","options":[{"t":"A guarantee of a supplier’s bank loan, where the supplier is financially sound and default is unlikely","why":"An outflow is not probable. At most a contingent liability, disclosed unless remote.","pit":"cl-recognised"},{"t":"Staff have been told about a new bonus scheme that will start next year","why":"The bonuses relate to future service. There is no obligation for past events.","pit":"intent-obligation"},{"t":"A product recall announced publicly in December for a defect found in goods sold in November","ok":true,"why":"The sale of defective goods and the public announcement create a (constructive) present obligation; the outflow is probable."},{"t":"A lawsuit the lawyers think the company will probably win","why":"An outflow is not probable: a contingent liability, disclosed unless remote.","pit":"cl-recognised"}]}]},"prepared":true},{"id":"financial-liabilities","code":"B2.4","title":"Basic Financial Liabilities","subtitle":"SFRS(I) 9 · bonds, loans and the effective interest method","los":{"35":"Recognition and measurement of different financial liabilities","36":"Financial statement effects of the measurement methods"},"accounts":[{"name":"Cash","el":"a"},{"name":"Prepaid issue costs","el":"a"},{"name":"Bonds payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Bank loan","el":"l"},{"name":"Premium on bonds payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Discount on bonds payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Financial liability at FVTPL","el":"l"},{"name":"Interest payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Share capital","el":"e"},{"name":"Own credit reserve (OCI)","el":"e"},{"name":"Retained earnings","el":"e"},{"name":"Interest expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Transaction costs expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Fair value loss (P&L)","el":"x"},{"name":"Loss on derecognition (P&L)","el":"x"},{"name":"Fair value gain (P&L)","el":"i"},{"name":"Gain on derecognition (P&L)","el":"i"},{"name":"Interest income","el":"i"}],"map":{"classify":{"label":"Liability vs equity · AC (default) or FVTPL","star":"Obligation to pay cash = liability; AC unless trading or designated"},"recognise":{"label":"When party to the contract"},"initial":{"label":"FV − transaction costs (FVTPL: expensed)","star":"Costs deducted, not added: the mirror of an asset"},"subsequent":{"label":"Effective interest; premium/discount unwinds to face"},"derecognise":{"label":"Paid, cancelled or expired; gain/loss to P&L"},"present":{"label":"Current vs non-current; own credit in OCI"}},"preamble":["Flip the table round. In financial assets the company was the investor; here it is the **borrower**: {l|bonds}, {l|bank loans} and {l|payables}, all under SFRS(I) 9. The good news: if you survived the effective interest method on the asset side, you already know 80% of this. Same maths, signs reversed.","Here’s the route:","1. **Classify** it: is it a {l|liability} at all (or {e|equity}), and if so, amortised cost or FVTPL? ★ Special: no SPPI, no business model. Amortised cost is the default and FVTPL is the rare exception.","2. **Recognise** it when you become a party to the contract. A quick pit stop.","3. **Measure it on day one** at **fair value minus transaction costs**, and price a bond at a premium or a discount. ★ Special: costs are **deducted** here, where an asset adds them.","4. **Measure it afterwards** with the effective interest roll-forward. The main event in the exam: {l|premium} cuts the {x|expense}, {l|discount} adds to it, and every bond walks to face value.","5. **Derecognise** it when it is paid, cancelled or expires; early buy-backs give a {i|gain} or {x|loss} against the {l|carrying amount}.","6. **Present** it: current or non-current, {x|interest} in {x|finance costs}, and own credit in OCI for designated liabilities.","+ Pack two things: *{l|opening} + {x|interest} − {a|cash} = {l|closing}*, and the mantra *coupon above market → premium; below → discount*."],"intros":{"classify":"First stop: what are we even holding? Before measuring anything we check it is a real **obligation to pay cash** (not {e|equity}, not a {l|provision}), then pick its category. ★ Special: unlike assets there is no SPPI or business model test. Amortised cost is the default; FVTPL is only for trading liabilities like {l|derivatives}, or a day-one designation.","recognise":"Classified. So when does it land on the balance sheet? Quick, vanilla pit stop: when the company becomes a party to the contract. Board plans and wish lists don’t count.","initial":"Now we put a number on it. ★ The special bit: transaction costs are **deducted** from the {l|liability} (an {a|asset} adds them; same logic, opposite sign). Then we price a bond from its cash flows and meet the premium and the discount, which the next stop will unwind.","subsequent":"The liability is on the books at its day-one number; now we follow it through the years. This is where the exam spends its marks: {x|interest expense} is {l|carrying amount} × effective rate, never the coupon. A {l|premium} shrinks the {x|expense} and the {l|liability} slides down to face; a {l|discount} (or issue costs) does the opposite. We finish with the rare FVTPL liability and its own-credit twist.","derecognise":"We’ve carried it, paid interest on it, and now it goes. At maturity nothing happens beyond paying face. The only trap is an early buy-back: compare the {a|cash} with the **{l|carrying amount}**, not face value, and put the difference in P&L.","present":"Last stop: step back and look at the statements. Nothing new to learn, just where each number sits: the {l|liability} moves from non-current to current as maturity approaches, {x|interest} lands in {x|finance costs}, and OCI only appears for a designated liability’s own credit."},"pitfalls":{"coupon-as-expense":"Using the coupon (face × coupon rate) as {x|interest expense} instead of {l|carrying amount} × effective rate.","face-not-carrying":"Using face value instead of the {l|carrying amount} (for interest, or as the balance before maturity).","coupon-rate-pricing":"Discounting a bond at the coupon rate instead of the market rate.","annual-periods":"Using annual rates and periods when coupons are paid half-yearly.","wrong-period":"Picking the figures for the wrong period (e.g. the first half-year when the second is asked).","costs-expensed-ac":"Expensing (or ignoring) transaction costs on an amortised cost liability instead of deducting them.","costs-added":"Adding transaction costs to a {l|liability}, as for a {a|financial asset}.","capitalise-equipment":"Capitalising bond issue costs into {a|equipment} that is not a qualifying asset.","premium-income":"Treating a {l|bond premium} as {i|income} on issue.","premium-at-maturity":"Leaving the premium untouched until maturity instead of amortising it.","premium-direction":"Mixing up premium and discount: which way the {x|expense} and the {l|liability} move.","premium-left":"Giving the {l|unamortised premium} at a later date instead of the premium on issue.","face-only":"Pricing a bond from the face value alone and forgetting the coupons.","repay-vs-face":"Measuring an early repayment {i|gain} or {x|loss} against face value instead of the {l|carrying amount}.","own-credit-pl":"Getting the own-credit rule wrong: it applies only to designated FVTPL liabilities, and goes to OCI (never recycled).","redeemable-equity":"Calling {l|mandatorily redeemable preference shares} {e|equity}, or confusing liability and equity."},"examTypes":[{"id":"bond-price","title":"What premium (or discount) arises on the bond issue? (practice Q46)","pitfall":"Discounting at the coupon rate; forgetting half-yearly periods","see":"initial"},{"id":"interest-je","title":"Which entry records interest expense for the period? (practice Q47)","pitfall":"Coupon as the expense; first half-year instead of the second; premium Dr/Cr mixed up","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"premium-treatment","title":"How should the premium be accounted for? (practice Q48)","pitfall":"Treating it as income, or leaving it until maturity","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"issue-costs","title":"Initial carrying amount with issue costs or arrangement fees","pitfall":"Expensing the costs, or adding them as for an asset","see":"initial"},{"id":"carrying-amount","title":"Interest expense or carrying amount in year 1, 2 or at a half-year","pitfall":"Using face value or the coupon instead of the carrying amount × effective rate","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"early-repay","title":"Gain or loss on repaying a bond early","pitfall":"Comparing cash with face value instead of the carrying amount","see":"derecognise"},{"id":"fvtpl-own-credit","title":"Designated FVTPL liability: where does each part of the change go?","pitfall":"Putting own credit in P&L, or applying the split to trading liabilities","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"classify-liab","title":"Liability or equity? Amortised cost or FVTPL?","pitfall":"Calling redeemable preference shares equity; forgetting AC is the default","see":"classify"}],"pages":[{"type":"map","id":"map-overview","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"intro","lo":35,"title":"Financial liabilities, the Socratic way","body":["{l|Bonds}, {l|bank loans} and {l|trade payables} are **{l|financial liabilities}**: promises to pay cash. This chapter is the mirror image of financial assets, seen from the borrower’s side of the table.","You will work bucket by bucket: classify, recognise, initial measurement, subsequent measurement (the effective interest grind), derecognition, then presentation.","Each idea starts with a question. Think it through, then reveal the answer. Account names are coloured by element; tap **Legend** any time.","One practice-paper case (a bond issued at a premium, paid half-yearly) is built on this chapter. Its questions are waiting in the quiz at the end."],"id":"intro-1xryvu","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"map","id":"map-1-classify","bucket":"classify","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"think","bucket":"classify","lo":35,"scenario":"Kopi Co’s balance sheet has: (1) a 5-year bank loan, (2) amounts owed to suppliers, (3) ordinary shares it issued, (4) preference shares it must buy back for $1 each in 20X9, (5) a provision for a lawsuit, and (6) cash received in advance for coffee it will deliver next month.","prompt":"Which of these are financial liabilities: promises to hand over cash under a contract? Which are something else?","answer":["1. **{l|Bank loan}** and 2. **{l|supplier payables}:** contracts to pay cash. {l|Financial liabilities}.","3. **{e|Ordinary shares}:** no obligation to pay anything. Dividends are at the board’s discretion. That is **{e|equity}**.","4. **{l|Redeemable preference shares}:** called “shares”, but Kopi *must* pay cash in 20X9. A contractual obligation to deliver cash, so a **{l|financial liability}**. Substance over the label.","5. **{l|Lawsuit provision}:** an obligation, but not from a contract to pay cash (it is SFRS(I) 1-37). 6. **{l|Cash in advance}:** Kopi owes **coffee**, not cash. Both are {l|liabilities}, but **not financial** ones."],"id":"think-1vx07c","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"classify","lo":35,"title":"What is a financial liability? Liability vs equity","body":["Kopi Co’s list showed that the name on the label doesn’t decide anything. Before measuring a single dollar, we need one test that sorts every credit balance into {l|financial liability}, {e|equity} or something else.","A **{l|financial liability}** is a **contractual obligation to deliver cash** or another financial asset (or to exchange financial instruments on unfavourable terms).","1. **{l|Financial liabilities}:** {l|Bank loan}, {l|Bonds payable}, {l|trade payables}, {l|mandatorily redeemable preference shares}, {l|derivatives with a negative fair value}.","2. **{e|Equity}:** {e|Share capital} (ordinary shares). There is no obligation to pay; holders get a residual interest.","3. **{l|Liabilities} that are not financial:** {l|provisions}, {l|income tax payable}, {l|contract liabilities} (goods or services owed). These follow their own standards.","+ The test is the **obligation**, not the name. If the issuer cannot avoid paying cash, it is a {l|liability}.","! The liability-vs-equity test sits in SFRS(I) 1-32, next to SFRS(I) 9. Your notes focus on measurement; this page is background so the rest makes sense."],"id":"rule-p5qwqa","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"classify","lo":35,"title":"Liability, equity, or not financial at all?","intro":"Run Kopi Co’s six items through this chart.","notes":{"item":"Anything financed by someone else: loans, payables, shares, provisions, deposits. The question is what kind of claim it is.","ob":"The heart of the test: a **contractual** obligation the entity **cannot avoid**, to pay **cash** or hand over another financial asset.","fl":"{l|Bank loan}, {l|bonds}, {l|supplier payables}, and {l|preference shares that must be redeemed}: the cash outflow is unavoidable.","why":"No contract to pay cash. Is there still something owed (goods, services, an obligation imposed by law)?","nf":"{l|Lawsuit provision} (SFRS(I) 1-37), {l|tax payable}, {l|cash received in advance} for goods (SFRS(I) 15). Real {l|liabilities}, but not SFRS(I) 9.","eq":"{e|Share capital}: holders get whatever is left after the liabilities. Dividends are discretionary.","s9":"Only financial liabilities go on to the SFRS(I) 9 measurement tree on the next pages."},"walk":[{"nodes":["item","ob"],"text":"Ask one question first: under a contract, **must** the entity hand over cash?"},{"nodes":["ob","fl","s9"],"text":"**Yes** → {l|financial liability}, measured under SFRS(I) 9. The {l|bank loan}, the {l|payables} and the {l|redeemable prefs} land here."},{"nodes":["why","nf"],"text":"**No, but something else is owed** (coffee, a court award) → a {l|liability}, just not a financial one."},{"nodes":["why","eq"],"text":"**Nothing owed at all**, only a residual share → {e|equity}. The {e|ordinary shares}."}],"caption":"Obligation to pay cash → financial liability. Residual interest → equity.","id":"diagram-1tghx2","sect":1,"part":"Classify","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:459px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 417.00 283.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 268.82)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-268.82 402.8,-268.82 402.8,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>item\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M307.28,-254.42C307.28,-254.42 179.75,-254.42 179.75,-254.42 173.75,-254.42 167.75,-248.42 167.75,-242.42 167.75,-242.42 167.75,-230.42 167.75,-230.42 167.75,-224.42 173.75,-218.42 179.75,-218.42 179.75,-218.42 307.28,-218.42 307.28,-218.42 313.28,-218.42 319.28,-224.42 319.28,-230.42 319.28,-230.42 319.28,-242.42 319.28,-242.42 319.28,-248.42 313.28,-254.42 307.28,-254.42\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"176.39\" y=\"-232.97\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">An item on the credit side\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>ob\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M308.03,-191.42C308.03,-191.42 179,-191.42 179,-191.42 173,-191.42 167,-185.42 167,-179.42 167,-179.42 167,-161.78 167,-161.78 167,-155.78 173,-149.78 179,-149.78 179,-149.78 308.03,-149.78 308.03,-149.78 314.03,-149.78 320.03,-155.78 320.03,-161.78 320.03,-161.78 320.03,-179.42 320.03,-179.42 320.03,-185.42 314.03,-191.42 308.03,-191.42\"/>\n\u003ct