"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"196.08,-43.1 192.32,-37.97 192.07,-44.33 196.08,-43.1\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"254.39\" y=\"-73.25\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> No, but the CFO\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"254.39\" y=\"-62\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">wants to designate\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"254.39\" y=\"-50.75\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">it now \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node7\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>ac\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M349.9,-36C349.9,-36 285.38,-36 285.38,-36 279.38,-36 273.38,-30 273.38,-24 273.38,-24 273.38,-12 273.38,-12 273.38,-6 279.38,0 285.38,0 285.38,0 349.9,0 349.9,0 355.9,0 361.9,-6 361.9,-12 361.9,-12 361.9,-24 361.9,-24 361.9,-30 355.9,-36 349.9,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"317.64\" y=\"-13.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Amortised cost\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge6\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>des&#45;&gt;ac\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M283,-95.28C287.69,-91.6 292.13,-87.4 295.64,-82.75 304.31,-71.27 309.73,-56.12 313.02,-43.47\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"315.01,-44.16 314.36,-37.83 310.93,-43.18 315.01,-44.16\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"320.56\" y=\"-62\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> No \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"classify","preamble":"Financial liabilities mirror financial assets under the same standard, but with no business model or SPPI test: almost all sit at amortised cost.","special":"No SPPI or business model test: {l|liabilities} sit at amortised cost by default, FVTPL only if held for trading or designated. And first, the obligation to pay cash separates a {l|liability} from {e|equity}.","id":"compare-1kumyt","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"map","id":"map-2-recognise","bucket":"recognise","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"think","bucket":"recognise","lo":35,"scenario":"In October the board approves a plan to issue $1 million of bonds. The bonds are issued, and the cash received, on 2 January.","prompt":"Is there a liability in October? When does the bond appear on the balance sheet?","answer":["Not in October. A board plan creates no obligation to anyone: no contract, no {l|liability}.","The {l|bond} is recognised on **2 January**, when the company **becomes a party to the contract** and owes the bondholders."],"id":"think-19y8nj","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"recognise","lo":35,"title":"Recognition","body":["We know what a {l|financial liability} is and how it will be measured. Now: on which day does it first appear? The board-plan example says it is not when someone decides to borrow.","Recognise a {l|financial liability} when the entity **becomes a party to the contractual provisions** of the instrument.","- {l|Bonds}: when issued. {l|Loans}: when drawn. {l|Trade payables}: when the goods or services are received.","- Planned borrowings are not recognised: no contract, no {l|liability}.","+ Same trigger as a {a|financial asset}, seen from the other side of the same contract."],"id":"rule-puryko","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"id":"mcq-1j43km","type":"mcq","bucket":"recognise","lo":35,"q":"A supplier delivers goods on 28 December. The invoice arrives on 5 January and is paid on 31 January. The year ends on 31 December. When should the {l|trade payable} be recognised?","options":[{"t":"28 December, when the goods are received","ok":true,"why":"Receiving the goods under the purchase contract creates the obligation to pay. The invoice is just paperwork."},{"t":"5 January, when the invoice arrives","why":"The obligation exists once the goods are received; the invoice only confirms the amount."},{"t":"31 January, when it is paid","why":"That is when it is derecognised. Waiting for payment is cash accounting."},{"t":"When the purchase order was placed","why":"Before delivery the contract is still unperformed by the supplier; the {l|payable} arises on delivery."}],"sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"map","id":"map-3-initial","bucket":"initial","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"think","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"scenario":"A company borrows $500,000 for 2 years at 5% interest. The bank charges an arrangement fee of $10,000, so only $490,000 actually lands in the company’s account.","prompt":"On day one, does the company owe $500,000 or $490,000? Should the $10,000 fee hit profit now?","answer":["The company received only $490,000 of {a|cash}, but it will pay back $500,000 plus interest. The fee is really **{x|extra interest} paid up front**: part of the cost of borrowing.","So the {l|loan} starts at **$490,000** (fair value minus transaction costs). The $10,000 is not expensed on day one; it is spread over the 2 years through a higher effective interest rate.","Mirror image alert: for a financial **{a|asset}** at amortised cost, costs are **added**. For a **{l|liability}**, they are **deducted**. Either way, they make the deal worse for the party paying them."],"id":"think-1k7cp2","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"title":"Initial measurement: fair value minus transaction costs","body":["The loan with a fee raised the day-one question: what number does the {l|liability} start at? This rule answers it, and it is the borrower’s mirror of the financial assets rule.","> {l|Initial carrying amount} = Fair value (usually the {a|cash received}) − transaction costs   (FVTPL: {x|costs} → P&L)","1. **Amortised cost:** transaction costs (arrangement fees, bond issue costs, legal fees) are **deducted** from the {l|liability}.","2. **FVTPL:** the {l|liability} starts at fair value; {x|transaction costs} are **expensed** immediately.","- The deducted costs are not lost: they raise the **effective interest rate**, so they reach P&L as {x|interest expense} over the term.","! Issue costs are **not** expensed up front for an amortised cost {l|liability}, and they are **not** capitalised into the {a|asset} the money buys (that would need a qualifying asset under SFRS(I) 1-23)."],"id":"rule-151812","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"title":"Transaction costs: the mirror image","intro":"The loan again: $500,000 borrowed, $10,000 fee. Compare with an investor paying costs on a financial asset.","notes":{"cash":"The bank lends 500,000 and keeps 10,000 as its fee, so 490,000 arrives. The fee is a transaction cost: it would not have been paid without the loan.","cat":"Classify first, then measure. The category decides where the costs go.","ac":"The default for loans, bonds and payables.","acL":"Dr {a|Cash} 490,000, Cr {l|Bank loan} 490,000. The {l|liability} starts at what the company really received in {a|cash}.","eir":"Because 490,000 grows back to 500,000, the effective rate (6.0923%) is above the 5% coupon. The 10,000 fee reaches P&L as extra {x|Interest expense} over 2 years.","tpl":"Only for liabilities held for trading, or designated at FVTPL on day one.","tplL":"{l|Liability} at fair value 500,000. Capitalising the cost would just be undone at the first remeasurement.","tplX":"Dr {x|Transaction costs expense} 10,000 on day one.","asset":"For an **{a|asset}** at amortised cost, costs are **added** (the investor paid more). For a **{l|liability}**, they are **deducted** (the borrower got less). Same logic, opposite sign."},"walk":[{"nodes":["cash"],"text":"500,000 borrowed, 10,000 fee, 490,000 received."},{"nodes":["cat","ac","acL"],"text":"Amortised cost: the {l|liability} starts at **490,000**, net of the fee."},{"nodes":["acL","eir"],"text":"The fee is not lost: it raises the effective rate to about 6.09%, so it is expensed as {x|interest} over the term."},{"nodes":["cat","tpl","tplL","tplX"],"text":"FVTPL: the {l|liability} starts at fair value, 500,000, and the 10,000 is expensed at once."},{"nodes":["acL","asset"],"text":"Mirror image: {a|asset} costs are added, {l|liability} costs are deducted. FVTPL expenses them on both sides."}],"caption":"Amortised cost: fair value − costs. FVTPL: fair value, costs expensed.","id":"diagram-1beubg","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:509px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 463.00 337.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 322.9)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-322.9 448.55,-322.9 448.55,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>cash\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M263.4,-308.5C263.4,-308.5 154.62,-308.5 154.62,-308.5 148.62,-308.5 142.62,-302.5 142.62,-296.5 142.62,-296.5 142.62,-284.5 142.62,-284.5 142.62,-278.5 148.62,-272.5 154.62,-272.5 154.62,-272.5 263.4,-272.5 263.4,-272.5 269.4,-272.5 275.4,-278.5 275.4,-284.5 275.4,-284.5 275.4,-296.5 275.4,-296.5 275.4,-302.5 269.4,-308.5 263.4,-308.5\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"174.89\" y=\"-291.05\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Loan 500,000\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"151.26\" y=\"-281.95\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">fee 10,000 · cash in 490,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u