over 2 years\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge5\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>acL&#45;&gt;eir\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M121.41,-73C110.62,-63.52 97.12,-51.64 85.52,-41.44\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"87.03,-39.97 81.14,-37.58 84.26,-43.12 87.03,-39.97\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node9\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>asset\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#f3eeff\" stroke=\"#6d28d9\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M292.4,-36C292.4,-36 153.62,-36 153.62,-36 147.62,-36 141.62,-30 141.62,-24 141.62,-24 141.62,-12 141.62,-12 141.62,-6 147.62,0 153.62,0 153.62,0 292.4,0 292.4,0 298.4,0 304.4,-6 304.4,-12 304.4,-12 304.4,-24 304.4,-24 304.4,-30 298.4,-36 292.4,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"150.26\" y=\"-18.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Mirror: financial asset at AC\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"170.89\" y=\"-9.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">costs ADDED to the asset\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge8\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>acL&#45;&gt;asset\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-dasharray=\"1,5\" d=\"M160.86,-73C173.57,-61.96 190,-47.69 202.78,-36.58\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"208.67\" y=\"-50.75\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> compare \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"je-14uw2x","type":"je","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"q":"On 1 January 20X1 a company draws down a $500,000 bank loan for 2 years at 5% interest. The bank deducts its $10,000 arrangement fee and pays the rest into the company’s account. The loan is at amortised cost. Record the drawdown.","accounts":["Bank loan","Cash","Interest expense","Transaction costs expense","Prepaid issue costs","Interest payable","Share capital"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":490000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Bank loan","amount":490000}],"alt":"Recording it gross is fine too, as long as it nets: Dr {a|Cash} 500,000, Cr {l|Bank loan} 500,000; then Dr {l|Bank loan} 10,000, Cr {a|Cash} 10,000.","why":"Fair value received 500,000 − transaction costs 10,000 = {l|490,000}. No {x|expense} on day one; the fee is spread through the effective rate.","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"think","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"scenario":"A company issues a 3-year bond: face $1,000,000, coupon 8% paid at the end of each year. Investors can earn 6% on similar bonds.","prompt":"Will investors pay exactly $1,000,000 for it? More, or less?","answer":["**More.** The bond pays 8% when 6% is the going rate, so investors compete for it and bid the price up until their return on what they pay is just 6%.","The price is the **present value** of the coupons and the face value at the **market rate**. The excess over face is a **{l|premium}**. A coupon **below** market gives a **{l|discount}**.","+ Coupon above market → premium. Coupon below market → discount. The price moves so the buyer earns the market rate."],"id":"think-1my7x9","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"title":"Pricing a bond: premium or discount","body":["Bonds add a twist the loan didn’t have: the cash received is set by investors, not by the face value. The think page showed why a generous coupon pushes the price up; here is the formula and the three cases.","> Price = Coupon × annuity factor + Face × discount factor   (both at the **market** rate)","1. **Coupon below market** → issued at a **{l|discount}** (price \u003c face). {x|Interest expense} is **higher** than the coupon; the {l|liability} grows to face.","2. **Coupon equal to market** → issued at **face value**. {x|Interest expense} = coupon.","3. **Coupon above market** → issued at a **{l|premium}** (price > face). {x|Interest expense} is **lower** than the coupon; the {l|liability} shrinks to face.","! Discount at the **market** rate, never the coupon rate. Discounting at the coupon rate always gives face value, which is the giveaway."],"id":"rule-5x524a","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"title":"Build-up: what the bond is worth on day one","intro":"The notes bond: face $1,000,000, coupon 8% ($80,000 a year), 3 years, market rate 6%.","notes":{"cpn":"{a|Cash interest} each year = face × coupon rate = 1,000,000 × 8% = 80,000.","face":"Repaid once, at maturity: 1,000,000.","pvc":"80,000 × 2.673012 (the 3-year annuity factor at 6%) = 213,841.","pvf":"1,000,000 × 0.839619 (the 3-year discount factor at 6%) = 839,619.","price":"213,841 + 839,619 = **1,053,460**. This is the {a|cash} the issuer receives and the {l|starting carrying amount} (no issue costs here).","prem":"1,053,460 − 1,000,000 = 53,460. Not income: it is amortised over the 3 years, reducing {x|interest expense}."},"walk":[{"nodes":["cpn","pvc"],"text":"Discount the 3 coupons of 80,000 at the **market** rate: 213,841."},{"nodes":["face","pvf"],"text":"Discount the face value at the same rate: 839,619."},{"nodes":["pvc","pvf","price"],"text":"Add them: the bond is worth 1,053,460."},{"nodes":["price","prem"],"text":"That is 53,460 above face: a **{l|premium}**, because the 8% coupon beats the 6% market."}],"caption":"Price = PV of coupons + PV of face, at the market rate.","id":"diagram-hyr14b","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:327px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 297.00 248.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 233.4)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-233.4 282.43,-233.4 282.43,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>