ze=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Premium 53,460\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"108.76\" y=\"-9.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">price − face\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge5\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>price&#45;&gt;prem\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M132.39,-60.62C132.39,-55.38 132.39,-49.53 132.39,-43.91\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"134.49,-43.98 132.39,-37.98 130.29,-43.98 134.49,-43.98\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"calc-as9xss","type":"calc","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"q":"A company issues a 3-year bond: face $1,000,000, coupon 8% paid at the end of each year, market rate for similar bonds 6%. At 6%, the 3-year annuity factor is 2.673012 and the 3-year discount factor is 0.839619. How much cash does the issuer receive?","answer":1053460,"unit":"$","working":["PV of coupons = 80,000 × 2.673012 = 213,841","PV of face value = 1,000,000 × 0.839619 = 839,619","Price ({a|cash received}) = 213,841 + 839,619 = **$1,053,460**, a {l|premium} of 53,460."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"think","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"scenario":"A 2-year bond, face $2,000,000, pays an 8% coupon **half-yearly**. The market rate is 6% a year.","prompt":"You have one discount rate and a number of periods to choose. Which ones?","answer":["Cash moves every six months, so count in six-month periods: **halve both rates and double the periods**. Coupon 4% of face = $80,000 per period, discounted at 3% for **4 periods**.","Using annual figures (8%, 6%, 2 periods) gives $2,073,336 instead of the right $2,074,342. Close-ish, which is exactly why the examiner puts it in the options."],"id":"think-uvat0o","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"calc-1vjwrp","type":"calc","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"q":"Price a 2-year bond, face $2,000,000, that pays an 8% coupon **half-yearly** when the market rate is 6% a year. At 3% for 4 periods, the annuity factor is 3.717098 and the discount factor is 0.888487.","answer":2074342,"unit":"$","working":["Coupon per period = 2,000,000 × 4% = 80,000","PV of coupons = 80,000 × 3.717098 = 297,368","PV of face value = 2,000,000 × 0.888487 = 1,776,974","Price = **$2,074,342**, a {l|premium} of 74,342."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"initial","lo":35,"q":"A company issues a bond whose coupon rate is below the market rate for similar bonds. Which statement is correct?","options":[{"t":"It is issued at a premium, and interest expense will be lower than the coupon","why":"That is the coupon-above-market case."},{"t":"It is issued at a discount, and interest expense will be higher than the coupon","ok":true,"why":"Investors pay less than face to earn the market rate. The {l|discount} unwinds as extra {x|interest expense}, taking the {l|liability} up to face."},{"t":"It is issued at face value, because the coupon is fixed in the contract","why":"The coupon is fixed, but the price adjusts to the market rate."},{"t":"It is issued at a discount, and the discount is expensed on day one","why":"The discount is amortised over the term through the effective interest method."}],"id":"mcq-fw5oyz","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"initial","preamble":"The mirror image of a financial asset: transaction costs are **deducted**, not added.","special":"Transaction costs are deducted from the {l|liability} (the mirror of an {a|asset}, where they are added), then spread through the effective rate; FVTPL expenses them.","id":"compare-1kumyt-2","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"map","id":"map-4-subsequent","bucket":"subsequent","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"id":"think-fiqhxs","type":"think","bucket":"subsequent","lo":36,"scenario":"A company issued a 3-year bond (face $1,000,000, coupon 8%) when the market rate was 6%, and received $1,053,460. It pays $80,000 cash each year and $1,000,000 at the end.","prompt":"Is the yearly {x|interest expense} just the $80,000 {a|cash}? What happens to the $53,460 it received but never pays back?","answer":["The real cost of borrowing is the 6% market rate on what was actually borrowed: 1,053,460 × 6% = **$63,208**, not $80,000.","The extra $16,792 of {a|cash} is really **repaying part of the {l|premium}**, so the {l|liability} falls. Year by year it shrinks to $1,000,000. Over the life, the {l|premium} **reduces** {x|total interest expense}."],"sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"subsequent","lo":36,"title":"Amortised cost: the effective interest method","body":["The bond is on the books at its issue price. Now it has to travel to face value by maturity, and the think page showed the coupon alone can’t be the {x|interest expense}. The effective interest method is the engine that gets it there.","> {x|Interest expense} = {l|opening carrying amount} × effective interest rate","> {l|Closing} = {l|opening} + {x|interest expense} − {a|cash paid}","- The **effective rate** discounts the future {a|cash payments} exactly to the {l|initial carrying amount} (after transaction costs). With no costs, it is the market rate at issue.","- **{l|Premium}:** {x|expense} below the {a|coupon}; the {l|liability} falls to face. **{l|Discount}:** {x|expense} above the {a|coupon}; the {l|liability} rises to face.","- Fair value changes are **ignored** under amortised cost.","! {x|Interest expense} is **{l|carrying amount} × effective rate**, never face × coupon. The {a|coupon} is just the cheque you write."],"id":"rule-17mqg0","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"subsequent","lo":36,"title":"The effective interest roll-forward (premium bond)","intro":"The notes bond in 20X1: issued for $1,053,460, face $1,000,000, coupon 8%, effective rate 6%.","notes":{"open":"What the issuer actually borrowed: {l|1,053,460}, a {l|premium} of 53,460 above face.","int":"{x|Interest expense} = {l|opening carrying amount} × **effective** rate = 1,053,460 × 6% = 63,208. It increases the {l|liability} ({l|interest owed}).","cpn":"{a|Cash paid} = face × coupon rate = 80,000. It reduces the {l|liability}.","close":"1,053,460 + 63,208 − 80,000 = 1,036,668. {a|Cash} exceeds the {x|expense} by 16,792: that much of the {l|premium} is repaid.","pl":"Dr {x|Interest expense} 63,208, below the 80,000 {a|coupon}. The {l|premium} **reduces** the {x|expense}.","cash":"Cr {a|Cash} 80,000.","next":"{l|Closing} becomes next year’s {l|opening}, so the {x|expense} falls: 62,200 in 20X2. The final year is rounded so the bond ends at exactly 1,000,000."},"walk":[{"nodes":["open"],"text":"Start from the {l|opening carrying amount}, $1,053,460."},{"nodes":["int","pl"],"text":"Add {x|interest} at the **effective** rate: 1,053,460 × 6% = $63,208, charged to P&L."},{"nodes":["cpn","cash"],"text":"Take off the {a|coupon paid}: $80,000."},{"nodes":["close"],"text":"{l|Closing} $1,036,668. A premium bond slides down towards face value."},{"nodes":["close","next"],"text":"Roll forward: 20X2 {x|interest} is $62,200, a little lower again."}],"caption":"{l|Opening} + {x|interest} (effective rate) − {a|cash} (coupon) = {l|closing}.","id":"diagram-bqb266","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:323px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 294.00 327.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 312.65)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-312.65 279.3,-312.65 279.3,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>open\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M139.53,-298.25C139.53,-298.25 31.5,-298.25 31.5,-298.25 25.5,-298.25 19.5,-292.25 19.5,-286.2