ints=\"119.13,-37.27 112.78,-37.11 117.65,-41.2 119.13,-37.27\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node6\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>loss\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#ffe4e6\" stroke=\"#be123c\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M278.28,-36C278.28,-36 163.5,-36 163.5,-36 157.5,-36 151.5,-30 151.5,-24 151.5,-24 151.5,-12 151.5,-12 151.5,-6 157.5,0 163.5,0 163.5,0 278.28,0 278.28,0 284.28,0 290.28,-6 290.28,-12 290.28,-12 290.28,-24 290.28,-24 290.28,-30 284.28,-36 278.28,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"193.14\" y=\"-18.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Loss 8,332\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"160.14\" y=\"-9.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">1,045,000 − 1,036,668 to P&amp;L\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge5\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>bb&#45;&gt;loss\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,2\" d=\"M220.89,-60.62C220.89,-55.38 220.89,-49.53 220.89,-43.91\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"222.99,-43.98 220.89,-37.98 218.79,-43.98 222.99,-43.98\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"je-he62yz","type":"je","bucket":"derecognise","lo":35,"q":"Record an early buy-back on 31 Dec 20X1, after that year’s interest: the bond (face $1,000,000) is carried at $1,036,668 (net) and the company pays $1,045,000 cash.","accounts":["Bonds payable","Cash","Loss on derecognition (P&L)","Gain on derecognition (P&L)","Interest expense","Retained earnings"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Bonds payable","amount":1036668},{"side":"Dr","account":"Loss on derecognition (P&L)","amount":8332},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":1045000}],"why":"{l|Carrying amount} 1,036,668 out; {a|cash} 1,045,000; the 8,332 difference is a {x|loss} in P&L.","sect":5,"part":"Derecognise / transfer"},{"id":"mcq-wwppha","type":"mcq","bucket":"derecognise","lo":35,"q":"At 31 Dec 20X2, after that year’s interest, a bond issued at a premium is carried at amortised cost of $1,018,868 (face $1,000,000). The company buys it back for $1,012,000. What is recognised?","options":[{"t":"A gain of $6,868 in profit or loss","ok":true,"why":"{l|Carrying amount} 1,018,868 − {a|cash} 1,012,000 = 6,868: the company settled for less than it carried."},{"t":"A loss of $12,000","why":"Compares the cash with face value. The {l|carrying amount} includes the {l|unamortised premium}."},{"t":"A gain of $6,868 in OCI","why":"Gains and losses on derecognition of amortised cost liabilities go to profit or loss."},{"t":"Nothing: the difference is spread over the remaining term","why":"Once the bond is extinguished there is nothing left to spread. The difference is recognised immediately."}],"sect":5,"part":"Derecognise / transfer"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"derecognise","special":"A {l|liability} goes when the obligation is discharged, cancelled or expires; early repayment {i|gain} or {x|loss} = {l|carrying amount} − {a|cash paid}, to P&L.","id":"compare-1kumyt-4","sect":5,"part":"Derecognise / transfer"},{"type":"map","id":"map-6-present","bucket":"present","sect":6,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"id":"think-t5h5kk","type":"think","bucket":"present","lo":36,"scenario":"At 31 Dec 20X1 a bond is carried at $1,036,668 and matures on 31 Dec 20X3. At 31 Dec 20X2 it is carried at $1,018,868.","prompt":"Where does it sit in the statement of financial position at each date: current or non-current?","answer":["At 31 Dec 20X1 it is due in two years: **{l|non-current}**, $1,036,668.","At 31 Dec 20X2 it is due within 12 months: **{l|current}**, $1,018,868. Same bond, it just walked across the balance sheet as maturity approached."],"sect":6,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"present","lo":36,"title":"Where it all shows up","body":["Every bucket is done. This page gathers where each number lands in the statements, so you can read a set of accounts and spot the {l|liability} at each stage.","1. **Statement of financial position:** {l|amortised cost liabilities} at amortised cost (face ± {l|unamortised premium} or {l|discount}, less unamortised issue costs); {l|FVTPL liabilities} at fair value. Split **current / non-current** by when they are due.","2. **Profit or loss:** {x|Interest expense} (effective interest) in {x|finance costs}; FVTPL fair value {i|gains} and {x|losses}; {i|gains} or {x|losses} on derecognition.","3. **OCI:** only the own-credit part of a **designated** {l|FVTPL liability}’s fair value change.","+ The measurement method moves {x|expense} between periods. {x|Total interest} over the life = all the {a|cash paid} − what was {a|received}."],"id":"rule-10oyq2","sect":6,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"id":"visual-qythe0","type":"visual","bucket":"present","lo":36,"title":"The notes bond in the financial statements","html":"\u003cp>The premium bond (face 1,000,000, issued for 1,053,460, due 31 Dec 20X3) one statement line at a time: what hits P&amp;L, what leaves in \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash\u003c/span>, and where the \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">liability\u003c/span> sits each year end.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl num reveal-rows\">\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>20X1\u003c/th>\u003cth>20X2\u003c/th>\u003cth>20X3\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Interest expense\u003c/span> (P&amp;L)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>63,208\u003c/td>\u003ctd>62,200\u003c/td>\u003ctd>61,132\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash interest paid\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>80,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>80,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>80,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Bonds payable\u003c/span> at 31 Dec\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,036,668\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1,018,868\u003c/td>\u003ctd>–\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Current or non-current\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Non-current\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Current\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Repaid\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cp class=\"cap\">\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Total expense\u003c/span> 186,540 = \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash paid\u003c/span> 1,240,000 − \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash received\u003c/span> 1,053,460. The \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">premium\u003c/span> lowers the \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">expense\u003c/span> every year.\u003c/p>\n    \u003cp>The same total as two bars of equal length: everything paid out over the life equals what came in plus the \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">interest expense\u003c/span>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"cbars\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash paid out\u003c/span>: 3 coupons 240,000 + face 1,000,000 = 1,240,000\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003ci class=\"seg out-a\" style=\"width:19.35%\">\u003c/i>\u003ci class=\"seg out-a\" style=\"width:80.65%\">\u003c/i>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash received\u003c/span> 1,053,460 + \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">total interest expense\u003c/span> 186,540\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003ci class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:84.96%\">\u003c/i>\u003ci class=\"seg x\" style=\"width:15.04%\">\u003c/i>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-key\">\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg out-a\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash paid\u003c/span> (coupons, then face)\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg a\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash received\u003c/span> at issue\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg x\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">interest expense\u003c/span> over 3 years\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>","sect":6,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"present","lo":36,"q":"Compared with expensing a loan’s arrangement fee on day one, what does deducting it from the loan (amortised cost) do?","options":[{"t":"Lowers total interest expense over the loan’s life","why":"The total is the same: {a|cash interest} plus the fee. Only the timing changes."},{"t":"Spreads the fee over the term as interest expense; the liability starts lower and builds up to face","ok":true,"why":"The effective rate is higher than the coupon, so the fee is expensed gradually as {x|interest} and the {l|carrying amount} climbs to face by maturity."},{"t":"Puts the fee in OCI","why":"OCI is only for the own-credit part of designated FVTPL liabilities."},{"t":"Capitalises the fee into the asset bought with the loan","why":"That needs a qualifying asset under SFRS(I) 1-23. Under SFRS(I) 9 the fee goes into the {l|liability}’s effective rate."}],"id":"mcq-12s160","sect":6,"part":"Present & disclose"},{"type":"examtypes","id":"examtypes","bucket":"wrap","sect":7,"part":"Wrap-up"},{"type":"summary","lo":36,"title":"Basic financial liabilities on one page","rows":[["{l|Financial liability} or {e|equity}?","Contractual obligation to deliver {a|cash} → {l|liability} (even “shares” that must be redeemed). Residual interest → {e|equity}."],["Default category?","Amortised cost. FVTPL only if held for trading or designated on day one."],["When recognised?","When the entity becomes a party to the contract."],["Initial amount?","Fair value ({a|cash received}) − transaction costs. FVTPL: costs expensed."],["Bond price?","PV of coupons + PV of face, at the market rate."],["Half-yearly coupons?","Halve the rates, double the periods."],["{x|Interest expense}?","{l|Opening carrying amount} × effective rate, not the {a|coupon}."],["{l|Premium}?","Coupon > market. Amortised; reduces {x|interest expense}. {l|Liability} falls to face."],["{l|Discount}?","Coupon \u003c market. Amortised; increases {x|interest expense}. {l|Liability} rises to face."],["Designated FVTPL?","Fair value {i|gains} / {x|losses} to P&L, except own credit → OCI."],["Early repayment?","{i|Gain} / {x|loss} = {l|carrying amount} − {a|cash paid}, to P&L."]],"id":"summary-ax6rvd","bucket":"wrap","sect":7,"part":"Wrap-up"}],"quiz":{"cases":{"helix":{"name":"Helix Bio","source":"Official practice paper (Q46–50 case)","text":["Helix Bio Pte Ltd (Helix) is a biotechnology company that specialises in developing gene therapies for genetic blood disorders such as sickle cell anaemia and haemophilia. Helix has developed a gene therapy which is due to enter the final stage of clinical trials. The final stage is required before the therapy is approved for public use. To fund the purchase of specialised, ready-to-use lab equipment needed for the final stage of the trial, Helix issued a bond for S$6 million. The details of the bond issue are:","• $6,000,000 of 12% bonds","• Issued on 1 January 20X5","• Interest is payable twice a year on 30 June and 31 December","• Maturity date is 31 December 20X8","• Market yield for bonds of similar risk and maturity is 10%"]},"merlion":{"name":"Merlion Logistics","text":["Merlion Logistics Pte Ltd (Merlion) has a financial year ending 31 December. On 1 January 20X1 it borrowed $800,000 from a bank for 3 years to buy delivery vans. Interest of 6% a year is payable each 31 December, and the principal is repaid on 31 December 20X3.","The bank charged an arrangement fee of $21,000, deducted from the loan proceeds. The loan is not held for trading and was not designated at fair value through profit or loss.","Taking the fee into account, the effective interest rate on the loan is 7.0003%."]}},"questions":[{"id":"q1","case":"helix","lo":35,"type":"bond-price","source":"Official practice paper Q46","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 9, Financial Instruments, identify how much Helix should record in its financial statements as the premium on the bond issue? Choose the most correct answer.","options":[{"t":"$212,757","pit":"premium-left","why":"That is the {l|premium} still **unamortised** at 31 December 20X6, after four of the eight half-years. The question asks for the premium on issue."},{"t":"$388,780","ok":true,"why":"Half-yearly: coupon 6% × 6,000,000 = 360,000, discounted at 5% for 8 periods. Price ({a|cash received}) ≈ 6,387,793, {l|premium} ≈ **387,793**. The paper’s $388,780 is the nearest option (a small rounding difference); “most correct” is the clue."},{"t":"$1,938,964","pit":"face-only","why":"That is face 6,000,000 minus the PV of the face value alone (4,061,036). It ignores the PV of the coupons."},{"t":"$2,714,549","why":"Far too big to be a {l|premium}: the 12% coupon is only 2 percentage points above the 10% market, so the premium is a few percent of face."}]},{"id":"q2","case":"helix","lo":36,"type":"interest-je","source":"Official practice paper Q47","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 9, Financial Instruments, identify which of the following journal entries Helix should record in their accounts to recognise the interest expense for the six months to 31 December 20X5?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":317359,"el":"x"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Premium on bonds payable","amount":42641,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":360000,"el":"a"}],"ok":true,"why":"Six months to 31 Dec 20X5 is the **second** half-year. {l|Opening} = 6,387,793 + {x|interest} 319,390 − {a|cash} 360,000 = 6,347,183; × 5% = 317,359 {x|expense}; 360,000 {a|cash}; 42,641 {l|premium amortised}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Premium on bonds payable","amount":319390,"el":"l"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":40610,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Financial liabilities – bonds","amount":360000,"el":"l"}],"pit":"wrong-period","why":"These are the first half-year’s figures (319,390 {x|interest}, 40,610 {l|premium amortisation}) with the labels swapped, and the {a|coupon} is credited to the {l|liability} instead of {a|cash}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":636749,"el":"x"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Premium on bonds payable","amount":83251,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":720000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"annual-periods","why":"A full year’s {x|interest} (319,390 + 317,359 = 636,749). The question asks for six months only."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Premium on bonds payable","amount":630657,"el":"l"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":89343,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Financial liabilities – bonds","amount":720000,"el":"l"}],"pit":"premium-direction","why":"{l|Premium} and {x|expense} swapped round, annual amounts, and the {a|coupon} credited to the {l|liability} rather than paid in {a|cash}."}]},{"id":"q3","case":"helix","lo":36,"type":"premium-treatment","source":"Official practice paper Q48","q":"With reference to SFRS (I) 9, Financial Instruments, identify how Helix should account for the premium payable on the bond?","options":[{"t":"The premium is added to the interest expense and increases the total interest cost over the term of the bond","pit":"premium-direction","why":"That describes a {l|discount}. A {l|premium} reduces the total {x|interest cost}."},{"t":"The premium is recorded in the Statement of profit or loss as income","pit":"premium-income","why":"The {l|premium} is not {i|income}: Helix pays it back through coupons above the market rate."},{"t":"The premium should be deducted from the bond liability and only recognised at maturity","pit":"premium-at-maturity","why":"It is part of the {l|liability} (added, not deducted) and is amortised every period, not released at maturity."},{"t":"The premium is amortised over the term of the bond and reduces the interest expense in each period","ok":true,"why":"{x|Interest expense} = {l|carrying amount} × 5%, below the 360,000 {a|coupon}. The difference amortises the {l|premium} each half-year."}]},{"id":"q4","case":"helix","lo":36,"type":"carrying-amount","q":"Using the effective interest method, what is the {l|carrying amount} of Helix’s bond at 31 December 20X5?","options":[{"t":"$6,000,000","pit":"face-not-carrying","why":"Face value is reached only at maturity in 20X8."},{"t":"$6,387,793","why":"The issue price. Two half-years of {l|premium} have been amortised since then."},{"t":"$6,304,542","ok":true,"why":"{l|Issue price} 6,387,793 − {l|premium amortised} 40,610 − 42,641 = {l|6,304,542}."},{"t":"$6,298,422","pit":"annual-periods","why":"Uses annual periods at 10% and 12%. Interest is paid half-yearly, so use 5% and 6% per half-year."}]},{"id":"q5","case":"helix","lo":35,"type":"issue-costs","q":"Suppose Helix had also paid bond issue costs of $120,000. At what amount should the {l|bond liability} be initially recognised under SFRS(I) 9?","options":[{"t":"$6,387,793, with the costs expensed","pit":"costs-expensed-ac","why":"Expensing them up front is the FVTPL rule. The bond is at amortised cost."},{"t":"$6,267,793","ok":true,"why":"Fair value ({a|cash received}) 6,387,793 − transaction costs 120,000. The costs then raise the effective rate above 5% per half-year."},{"t":"$6,507,793","pit":"costs-added","why":"Adding costs is the rule for a financial **{a|asset}**. For a {l|liability} they are deducted."},{"t":"$6,387,793, with the costs added to the {a|lab equipment}","pit":"capitalise-equipment","why":"Ready-to-use {a|equipment} is not a qualifying asset, and in any case issue costs belong in the bond’s effective interest under SFRS(I) 9."}]},{"id":"q6","case":"merlion","lo":35,"type":"issue-costs","q":"At what amount should Merlion initially recognise the {l|bank loan}?","options":[{"t":"$800,000","pit":"costs-expensed-ac","why":"Ignores the fee, which reduces the {l|liability} at amortised cost."},{"t":"$821,000","pit":"costs-added","why":"Adds the fee, as you would for a {a|financial asset}. For a {l|liability} it is deducted."},{"t":"$779,000","ok":true,"why":"Fair value 800,000 − transaction costs 21,000 = 779,000, the {a|cash actually received}."},{"t":"$800,000, with a $21,000 {a|prepayment asset}","why":"The fee is not a separate {a|asset}; it is netted against the {l|loan} and spread through the effective rate."}]},{"id":"q7","case":"merlion","lo":36,"type":"carrying-amount","q":"What {x|interest expense} should Merlion recognise for 20X1?","options":[{"t":"$48,000","pit":"coupon-as-expense","why":"The {a|cash interest}. The {x|expense} also unwinds part of the fee."},{"t":"$54,532","ok":true,"why":"{l|Opening carrying amount} 779,000 × 7.0003% = 54,532."},{"t":"$56,002","pit":"face-not-carrying","why":"Applies the effective rate to face value. Use the {l|carrying amount}."},{"t":"$69,000","pit":"costs-expensed-ac","why":"Expenses the whole fee in year 1. It is spread over the 3 years."}]},{"id":"q8","case":"merlion","lo":36,"type":"interest-je","q":"Which journal entry records the interest on 31 December 20X1?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":48000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":48000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"coupon-as-expense","why":"Only the {a|cash interest}. The fee also has to unwind into {x|expense}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":54532,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":48000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Bank loan","amount":6532,"el":"l"}],"ok":true,"why":"{x|Expense} 54,532; {a|cash} 48,000; the 6,532 difference is added to the {l|loan} as it climbs towards 800,000."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":54532,"el":"x"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Bank loan","amount":6532,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":61064,"el":"a"}],"pit":"premium-direction","why":"Premium logic, and the {a|cash paid} is overstated. Merlion pays only the 6% interest in {a|cash}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":48000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":6532,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":54532,"el":"a"}],"why":"Right {x|expense}, but Merlion only pays 48,000 in {a|cash}. The 6,532 unwinding is not paid until the {l|loan} is repaid at face."}]},{"id":"q9","case":"merlion","lo":36,"type":"carrying-amount","q":"What is the {l|carrying amount} of the loan at 31 December 20X2?","options":[{"t":"$785,532","why":"That is the {l|31 December 20X1 balance}. Another year of the fee unwinds in 20X2."},{"t":"$792,522","ok":true,"why":"{l|Opening} 785,532 + {x|interest} 54,990 − {a|cash} 48,000 = {l|closing} 792,522."},{"t":"$800,000","pit":"face-not-carrying","why":"The {l|loan} only reaches face value on 31 December 20X3."},{"t":"$779,000","why":"Leaves the {l|loan} at its {l|initial amount}. Under amortised cost the fee unwinds every year."}]},{"id":"q10","case":"merlion","lo":36,"type":"carrying-amount","q":"What is the {x|total interest expense} Merlion recognises over the life of the loan?","options":[{"t":"$144,000","pit":"coupon-as-expense","why":"{a|Cash interest} only. The fee is part of the cost of borrowing too."},{"t":"$165,000","ok":true,"why":"3 × 48,000 {a|cash interest} + 21,000 fee = {x|165,000}. The effective rate just spreads it."},{"t":"$123,000","pit":"premium-direction","why":"Subtracts the fee as if it were a premium received. It is a cost, so it adds to the {x|expense}."},{"t":"$168,007","pit":"face-not-carrying","why":"Applies the effective rate to face value every year. {x|Total expense} must equal the total cost of the {l|loan}."}]},{"id":"q11","lo":35,"type":"classify-liab","q":"An entity holds an interest rate swap that currently has a negative fair value. It is not a hedging instrument. How is it measured?","options":[{"t":"Amortised cost","why":"Derivatives have no fixed principal to amortise; they are held for trading."},{"t":"Fair value through profit or loss","ok":true,"why":"A {l|derivative liability} is held for trading, so FVTPL, with every change in P&L."},{"t":"Fair value through OCI","pit":"own-credit-pl","why":"There is no FVOCI category for {l|financial liabilities}."},{"t":"Not recognised until it is settled","why":"Derivatives are recognised when the entity becomes a party to the contract."}]},{"id":"q12","lo":35,"type":"classify-liab","q":"A company issues preference shares that it must redeem for cash at a fixed date. In the company’s own statement of financial position, they are:","options":[{"t":"Equity, because they are shares","pit":"redeemable-equity","why":"The obligation to pay cash on a fixed date makes them a {l|financial liability}, whatever they are called."},{"t":"A financial liability","ok":true,"why":"A contractual obligation to deliver cash that the company cannot avoid."},{"t":"A provision","why":"{l|Provisions} are uncertain in timing or amount and not from contracts to pay cash. This is a financial instrument."},{"t":"Shown in equity until the year of redemption","pit":"redeemable-equity","why":"Classification is decided on issue from the contract terms, not by how near redemption is."}]},{"id":"q13","lo":35,"type":"issue-costs","q":"An entity issues notes and irrevocably designates them at FVTPL on day one. It pays transaction costs on the issue. How are the costs treated?","options":[{"t":"Deducted from the liability and spread through the effective rate","pit":"costs-added","why":"That is the amortised cost treatment."},{"t":"Expensed in profit or loss immediately","ok":true,"why":"{l|FVTPL liabilities} start at fair value; {x|transaction costs} are expensed."},{"t":"Added to the liability","pit":"costs-added","why":"Costs are never added to a {l|liability}."},{"t":"Taken to OCI","why":"Only own-credit changes on designated liabilities go to OCI."}]},{"id":"q14","lo":36,"type":"premium-treatment","q":"A company issues a 4-year bond (face $1,000,000, coupon 5%) when the market rate is 7%. It receives $932,256. Which statement about year 1 is correct?","options":[{"t":"Interest expense is $50,000, the coupon","pit":"coupon-as-expense","why":"The coupon is the {a|cash}. The {x|expense} is {l|carrying amount} × effective rate."},{"t":"Interest expense is $65,258, and the liability rises","ok":true,"why":"932,256 × 7% = 65,258, above the 50,000 coupon. The 15,258 difference unwinds the {l|discount}, adding to the {l|liability}."},{"t":"Interest expense is $65,258, and the liability falls","pit":"premium-direction","why":"The {l|liability} falls only for a premium bond. Here {x|expense} exceeds {a|cash}, so it rises."},{"t":"The $67,744 discount is expensed in year 1","why":"The {l|discount} is spread over the term through the effective interest method."}]},{"id":"q15","lo":35,"type":"early-repay","q":"A bond with face value $1,000,000 is carried at amortised cost of $1,018,868. The issuer buys it back early for $1,010,000 cash. What is recognised?","options":[{"t":"A loss of $10,000 in profit or loss","pit":"repay-vs-face","why":"Compares {a|cash} with face value. Use the {l|carrying amount}."},{"t":"A gain of $8,868 in profit or loss","ok":true,"why":"{l|Carrying amount} 1,018,868 − {a|cash} 1,010,000 = {i|8,868}: the obligation was settled for less than its {l|carrying amount}."},{"t":"A gain of $8,868 in OCI","why":"Derecognition {i|gains} on amortised cost {l|liabilities} go to profit or loss."},{"t":"No gain or loss; the difference adjusts {e|share capital}","why":"The bondholders are creditors, not owners. The difference goes to P&L."}]},{"id":"q16","lo":35,"type":"classify-liab","q":"The board approves a bond issue in November 20X1. The bonds are issued and the cash received on 3 January 20X2. The year ends on 31 December. When is the liability recognised?","options":[{"t":"November 20X1, when the board approves the issue","why":"A board decision creates no obligation to anyone. No contract, no {l|liability}."},{"t":"3 January 20X2, when the bonds are issued","ok":true,"why":"The entity becomes a party to the contract when the bonds are issued."},{"t":"At the first coupon date","why":"The whole obligation exists from issue, not only when interest falls due."},{"t":"At maturity","why":"That is when it is derecognised."}]},{"id":"q17","lo":35,"type":"bond-price","q":"A 5-year bond has an 8% coupon paid half-yearly. The market rate is 10% a year. To price it, which rate and number of periods should be used?","options":[{"t":"10% for 5 periods, with a coupon of 8% of face","pit":"annual-periods","why":"Ignores the half-yearly payments."},{"t":"5% for 10 periods, with a coupon of 4% of face","ok":true,"why":"Halve both rates and double the periods."},{"t":"4% for 10 periods, with a coupon of 4% of face","pit":"coupon-rate-pricing","why":"Discounting at the coupon rate always gives face value. Use the market rate."},{"t":"5% for 5 periods, with a coupon of 4% of face","pit":"annual-periods","why":"Halved the rates but forgot to double the periods."}]},{"id":"q18","lo":36,"type":"fvtpl-own-credit","q":"A bank designated its own issued notes at FVTPL on day one. During the year its credit rating is downgraded and the notes’ fair value falls. Where does the part of the change caused by its own credit risk go?","options":[{"t":"Profit or loss, like the rest of the change","pit":"own-credit-pl","why":"For designated {l|liabilities}, the own-credit part is kept out of P&L so a downgrade does not show as {i|profit}."},{"t":"Other comprehensive income","ok":true,"why":"Designated {l|FVTPL liabilities}: own-credit changes to OCI, the rest to P&L."},{"t":"Nowhere: designated liabilities ignore credit risk","why":"The fair value includes the effect of own credit; the question is only where it is presented."},{"t":"Directly to {e|retained earnings}","why":"It is presented in OCI first."}]}]},"exam":{"cases":{"kallang":{"name":"Kallang Shipping","text":["Kallang Shipping Pte Ltd (Kallang) has a financial year ending 31 December. On 1 January 20X1 it issued 5-year bonds with a face value of $2,000,000 and a coupon of 4%, payable each 31 December. The market rate for similar bonds was 5%, and investors paid $1,913,410.","Kallang paid $40,000 of legal and underwriting costs directly attributable to the issue. Taking these costs into account, the effective interest rate is 5.4815%.","The bonds are not held for trading and were not designated at FVTPL.","On 31 December 20X2, immediately after paying that year’s coupon, Kallang bought back the entire issue in the market for $1,940,000."]},"tanjong":{"name":"Tanjong Capital","text":["Tanjong Capital Pte Ltd (Tanjong) has a financial year ending 31 December. On 1 January 20X1 it issued notes with a face value of $5,000,000 at par, paying 6% interest each 31 December and repayable on 31 December 20X3.","To eliminate an accounting mismatch with a portfolio of assets measured at FVTPL, Tanjong irrevocably designated the notes at fair value through profit or loss on initial recognition. It paid issue costs of $40,000.","At 31 December 20X1 the notes’ fair value was $4,880,000. Of the $120,000 decrease, $50,000 was caused by a deterioration in Tanjong’s own credit risk and the rest by a rise in market interest rates."]}},"questions":[{"id":"x1","case":"kallang","lo":35,"type":"issue-costs","q":"At what amount should Kallang initially recognise the {l|bond liability}?","options":[{"t":"$2,000,000","pit":"face-not-carrying","why":"Face value is what is repaid at maturity, not what was received."},{"t":"$1,913,410","pit":"costs-expensed-ac","why":"Ignores the issue costs, which reduce an amortised cost {l|liability}."},{"t":"$1,873,410","ok":true,"why":"1,913,410 {a|cash received} − 40,000 costs = {l|1,873,410}."},{"t":"$1,953,410","pit":"costs-added","why":"Adding costs is the {a|financial asset} rule."}]},{"id":"x2","case":"kallang","lo":36,"type":"carrying-amount","q":"What {x|interest expense} should Kallang recognise for 20X1?","options":[{"t":"$80,000","pit":"coupon-as-expense","why":"The {a|cash} coupon."},{"t":"$93,671","why":"Uses the 5% market rate. The issue costs push the effective rate up to 5.4815%."},{"t":"$102,691","ok":true,"why":"{l|Opening carrying amount} 1,873,410 × 5.4815% = 102,691."},{"t":"$104,884","pit":"costs-expensed-ac","why":"Applies the effective rate to the price before costs. The {l|carrying amount} is net of costs."}]},{"id":"x3","case":"kallang","lo":36,"type":"carrying-amount","q":"What is the {l|carrying amount} of the bonds at 31 December 20X2, immediately before the buy-back?","options":[{"t":"$1,896,101","why":"That is the {l|31 December 20X1 balance}."},{"t":"$1,920,036","ok":true,"why":"{l|Opening} 1,896,101 + {x|interest} 103,935 − {a|cash} 80,000 = {l|closing} 1,920,036."},{"t":"$1,945,535","pit":"costs-expensed-ac","why":"Runs the schedule from the price at the 5% market rate, leaving out the issue costs."},{"t":"$2,000,000","pit":"face-not-carrying","why":"The {l|bonds} would only reach face value at maturity in 20X5."}]},{"id":"x4","case":"kallang","lo":36,"type":"interest-je","q":"Which journal entry records the interest for 20X2 (net presentation)?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":103935,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":80000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Bonds payable","amount":23935,"el":"l"}],"ok":true,"why":"{x|Expense} = {l|opening} 1,896,101 × 5.4815% = 103,935; {a|cash} 80,000; 23,935 unwound into the {l|liability}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":103935,"el":"x"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Bonds payable","amount":23935,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":127870,"el":"a"}],"pit":"premium-direction","why":"Treats the bond as if issued at a {l|premium} and overstates the {a|cash}. A discount bond’s {l|liability} rises."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":80000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":80000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"coupon-as-expense","why":"{a|Coupon} only; the {l|discount} and costs must unwind."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":102691,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":80000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Bonds payable","amount":22691,"el":"l"}],"pit":"wrong-period","why":"Those are the 20X1 figures. In 20X2 the {l|carrying amount} is higher (1,896,101), so the {x|expense} is higher too."}]},{"id":"x5","case":"kallang","lo":35,"type":"early-repay","q":"What gain or loss should Kallang recognise on the buy-back?","options":[{"t":"A loss of $19,964 in profit or loss","ok":true,"why":"{a|Cash} 1,940,000 − {l|carrying amount} 1,920,036 = 19,964 {x|loss}."},{"t":"A gain of $60,000 in profit or loss","pit":"repay-vs-face","why":"Compares the {a|cash} with face value. The {l|carrying amount} still carries {l|unamortised discount} and costs."},{"t":"A loss of $26,590 in profit or loss","why":"Compares with the original issue price. The {l|carrying amount} has moved since then."},{"t":"A loss of $19,964 in OCI","why":"Derecognition {i|gains} and {x|losses} on amortised cost {l|liabilities} go to profit or loss."}]},{"id":"x6","case":"tanjong","lo":35,"type":"issue-costs","q":"Which journal entry records the issue of the notes on 1 January 20X1?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":4960000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Financial liability at FVTPL","amount":4960000,"el":"l"}],"pit":"costs-expensed-ac","why":"Deducting the costs is the amortised cost treatment. At FVTPL they are expensed."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":4960000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Transaction costs expense","amount":40000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Financial liability at FVTPL","amount":5000000,"el":"l"}],"ok":true,"why":"The {l|liability} starts at fair value 5,000,000 ({a|cash} 4,960,000 after costs); the 40,000 {x|costs} are expensed."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":4960000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Own credit reserve (OCI)","amount":40000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Financial liability at FVTPL","amount":5000000,"el":"l"}],"pit":"own-credit-pl","why":"Transaction costs are not an OCI item."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":5000000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Financial liability at FVTPL","amount":5000000,"el":"l"}],"why":"Ignores the 40,000 paid in {x|issue costs}."}]},{"id":"x7","case":"tanjong","lo":36,"type":"fvtpl-own-credit","q":"Which journal entry records the remeasurement of the notes at 31 December 20X1?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Financial liability at FVTPL","amount":120000,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Fair value gain (P&L)","amount":120000,"el":"i"}],"pit":"own-credit-pl","why":"Puts the own-credit part in profit. For designated {l|liabilities} it goes to OCI."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Financial liability at FVTPL","amount":120000,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Fair value gain (P&L)","amount":70000,"el":"i"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Own credit reserve (OCI)","amount":50000,"el":"e"}],"ok":true,"why":"{l|Liability} down 120,000: 70,000 from market rates to P&L, 50,000 from own credit to OCI."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Financial liability at FVTPL","amount":120000,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Fair value gain (P&L)","amount":50000,"el":"i"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Own credit reserve (OCI)","amount":70000,"el":"e"}],"why":"The split is the wrong way round: only the own-credit part goes to OCI."},{"t":"No entry: the notes are carried at amortised cost","why":"They were designated at FVTPL, so they are remeasured to fair value."}]},{"id":"x8","case":"tanjong","lo":36,"type":"fvtpl-own-credit","q":"What is the net effect of the notes on Tanjong’s profit or loss for 20X1?","options":[{"t":"$270,000 net expense","ok":true,"why":"{x|Interest} 300,000 + {x|issue costs} 40,000 − {i|market-rate gain} 70,000 = 270,000."},{"t":"$220,000 net expense","pit":"own-credit-pl","why":"Includes the {i|own-credit gain} in profit."},{"t":"$340,000 net expense","why":"Ignores the {i|fair value gain} from market rates, which goes to P&L."},{"t":"$230,000 net expense","pit":"costs-expensed-ac","why":"Leaves out the {x|issue costs}, which are expensed for an {l|FVTPL liability}."}]},{"id":"x9","case":"tanjong","lo":36,"type":"fvtpl-own-credit","q":"At what amount are the notes carried in Tanjong’s statement of financial position at 31 December 20X1?","options":[{"t":"$5,000,000","why":"Face value. {l|Designated FVTPL liabilities} are carried at fair value."},{"t":"$4,880,000","ok":true,"why":"{l|Fair value} at the reporting date. The own-credit split affects only where the change is presented, not the {l|carrying amount}."},{"t":"$4,930,000","pit":"own-credit-pl","why":"Strips out the own-credit change from the {l|carrying amount}. It is still part of fair value; only its presentation differs."},{"t":"$4,960,000","pit":"costs-expensed-ac","why":"Amortised cost thinking (net of costs). The notes are at fair value."}]},{"id":"x10","case":"tanjong","lo":36,"type":"fvtpl-own-credit","q":"The notes are repaid at maturity on 31 December 20X3. What happens to the cumulative balance in the {e|own credit reserve}?","options":[{"t":"It is reclassified to profit or loss on repayment","pit":"own-credit-pl","why":"Own-credit amounts in OCI are not recycled to profit or loss."},{"t":"It stays in equity; it may be transferred within equity (e.g. to retained earnings)","ok":true,"why":"The own-credit OCI amount is never recycled to P&L; a transfer within {e|equity} is allowed."},{"t":"It reduces the amount repaid to the noteholders","why":"The noteholders get the contractual amount. The {e|reserve} is an accounting balance in {e|equity}."},{"t":"It is released to profit or loss evenly over the remaining term","why":"There is no amortisation of the {e|own-credit reserve}."}]},{"id":"x11","lo":36,"type":"carrying-amount","q":"On 1 January 20X1 a company issues a 4-year bond, face $800,000, coupon 7% paid each 31 December, for $856,735. The effective rate is 5%. What interest expense is recognised for 20X2?","options":[{"t":"$42,837","pit":"wrong-period","why":"That is 20X1. {l|Premium} amortisation in 20X1 lowered the {l|carrying amount}, so 20X2 {x|expense} is lower."},{"t":"$42,179","ok":true,"why":"{l|20X1 closing} = 856,735 + {x|interest} 42,837 − {a|cash} 56,000 = {l|843,572}; × 5% = 42,179."},{"t":"$56,000","pit":"coupon-as-expense","why":"The {a|cash} coupon."},{"t":"$40,000","pit":"face-not-carrying","why":"Effective rate × face value. Use the {l|carrying amount}."}]},{"id":"x12","lo":36,"type":"carrying-amount","q":"On 1 January 20X1 a company issues a 3-year bond, face $3,000,000, with a 5% coupon paid on 30 June and 31 December. The market rate is 6% a year, and the bond is issued for $2,918,742. What is its {l|carrying amount} at 31 December 20X1?","options":[{"t":"$2,931,304","pit":"wrong-period","why":"That is the {l|30 June 20X1 balance}, after only one half-year."},{"t":"$2,944,243","ok":true,"why":"Two half-years at 3%: {l|issue price} 2,918,742 → 2,931,304 → {l|2,944,243}."},{"t":"$2,944,999","pit":"annual-periods","why":"Prices and rolls forward with annual periods. The coupons are half-yearly."},{"t":"$2,918,742","why":"The issue price. The {l|discount} has started to unwind."}]},{"id":"x13","lo":35,"type":"premium-treatment","q":"On 1 January 20X1 a company issues a 4-year bond with a face value of $800,000 and a 7% coupon for $856,735 cash. It keeps face value and the premium in separate accounts. Which entry records the issue?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":856735,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Bonds payable","amount":800000,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Premium on bonds payable","amount":56735,"el":"l"}],"ok":true,"why":"Face 800,000 in {l|Bonds payable}; the 56,735 {l|premium} in its own liability account. {l|Net liability} 856,735."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":856735,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Bonds payable","amount":800000,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Fair value gain (P&L)","amount":56735,"el":"i"}],"pit":"premium-income","why":"The {l|premium} is not a {i|gain}: it is part of what must be repaid through the higher coupons."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":856735,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Discount on bonds payable","amount":56735,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Bonds payable","amount":913470,"el":"l"}],"pit":"premium-direction","why":"Treats a {l|premium} as a {l|discount} and overstates face value."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":800000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Bonds payable","amount":800000,"el":"l"}],"why":"The company received {a|cash} of 856,735, not just face value."}]},{"id":"x14","lo":35,"type":"classify-liab","q":"Which of the following is a financial liability measured under SFRS(I) 9?","options":[{"t":"A provision for restoring a leased site at the end of the lease","why":"An obligation, but not a contract to deliver cash to a counterparty in the SFRS(I) 9 sense. It is a {l|provision} under SFRS(I) 1-37."},{"t":"Cash received in advance for goods to be delivered next month","why":"The entity owes goods, not cash: a {l|contract liability} under SFRS(I) 15."},{"t":"Preference shares that the issuer must redeem for cash in 20X8","ok":true,"why":"A contractual obligation to deliver cash: a {l|financial liability}, whatever the label."},{"t":"Ordinary shares issued to the public","pit":"redeemable-equity","why":"No obligation to pay: {e|equity}."}]},{"id":"x15","lo":36,"type":"fvtpl-own-credit","q":"An entity holds a derivative liability (held for trading). Its fair value rises during the year, partly because the entity’s own credit risk improved. How is the change presented?","options":[{"t":"All of it in profit or loss","ok":true,"why":"The own-credit split to OCI applies only to {l|liabilities} **designated** at FVTPL. {l|Held-for-trading liabilities} put every change through P&L."},{"t":"The own-credit part in OCI, the rest in profit or loss","pit":"own-credit-pl","why":"That is the rule for **designated** liabilities, not for derivatives held for trading."},{"t":"All of it in OCI","why":"There is no FVOCI category for {l|liabilities}."},{"t":"No change: derivatives are carried at cost","why":"{l|Derivatives} are always at fair value."}]}]},"prepared":true},{"id":"impairment","code":"B3","title":"Impairment of Assets","subtitle":"SFRS(I) 1-36 · indicators, recoverable amount, losses and reversals","los":{"37":"When an impairment test is needed","38":"Indicators of impairment","39":"Recoverable amount and impairment losses"},"accounts":[{"name":"Machine","el":"a"},{"name":"Building","el":"a"},{"name":"Accumulated depreciation and impairment","el":"a"},{"name":"Goodwill","el":"a"},{"name":"Cash","el":"a"},{"name":"Impairment loss","el":"x"},{"name":"Depreciation expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Reversal of impairment loss","el":"i"},{"name":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","el":"e"},{"name":"Retained earnings","el":"e"},{"name":"Provision for impairment","el":"l"}],"map":{"classify":{"label":"In scope? Test alone or as a CGU"},"recognise":{"label":"Indicators; annual test for G-I-N","star":"Test only on an indication, except goodwill and two kinds of intangible"},"initial":{"label":"Recoverable amount: higher of FVLCOD and VIU","star":"A second value is built: sell vs use, take the higher"},"subsequent":{"label":"Loss to P&L (revalued: surplus first); CGU allocation","star":"Revalued assets use their own {e|surplus} first; {a|goodwill} absorbs a CGU loss first"},"derecognise":{"label":"{i|Reversal}, capped; never for {a|goodwill}"},"present":{"label":"Losses and reversals by class; cause and basis"}},"preamble":["Welcome to impairment, the chapter that keeps the balance sheet honest. The whole idea fits on a sticky note: **no asset may be carried at more than the cash it can bring in.** If it is, write it down.","This chapter is different from the others: impairment isn’t an asset, it is a **health check** run on other assets ({a|PPE}, {a|intangibles}, {a|goodwill}, {a|investment property} at cost, {a|right-of-use assets}). So our six-stop map gets bent a little. Here is the route, with ★ on the bits that matter most:","1. **Classify:** which assets are in scope, and is each tested alone or as part of a **cash-generating unit**?","2. **Recognise:** *when* do we test? Only when there is an **indication**, except for {a|goodwill} and two kinds of intangible, which are tested every year. ★ special","3. **Measure:** build the **recoverable amount**, the higher of selling it (FVLCOD) and using it (value in use). ★ special","4. **The loss:** write down to recoverable amount, to P&L, or first against the {e|revaluation surplus} for a revalued asset; in a CGU, {a|goodwill} takes the first hit. Then depreciate the {a|new amount}. ★ special","5. **The way back:** a **{i|reversal}** when things improve, capped, and never for {a|goodwill}.","6. **Present:** where losses show up, and what to explain in the notes.","+ The one-liner to carry into the exam: **if C > R, write it down. R is the higher of Sell (FVLCOD) and Use (VIU).**"],"intros":{"classify":"First stop: what does this standard actually police? Impairment is a security guard, not an asset, and it only patrols assets that nobody else is watching. {a|Inventory}, {a|financial assets} and fair-value {a|investment property} have their own guards. Then a practical twist: some assets can’t be valued on their own, so we test them in a group, the **cash-generating unit**.","recognise":"Now that we know *what* gets tested, the question is *when*. ★ This is the first special bit: we don’t test everything every year (thank goodness). We look for **signs of trouble** at each reporting date and only test when we find one. The exception is the **G-I-N** club ({a|goodwill}, indefinite-life intangibles, intangibles not yet in use), which gets checked every year, no excuses.","initial":"We have a reason to test, so time to put a number on it. ★ Here is the heart of the chapter: we build a second value, the **recoverable amount**, from two routes, selling it (FVLCOD) and using it (value in use), and we take the **higher**. Value in use is a present value of the asset as it stands today: no wishful upgrades, no financing, no tax.","subsequent":"We have recoverable amount; if the {a|carrying amount} is above it, the asset has to come down. ★ Special again: where the loss lands depends on the asset’s history. Cost model → straight to P&L. Revalued → its own {e|surplus} in OCI takes the first bite. In a CGU, {a|goodwill} is first in line. Then {x|depreciation} restarts from the {a|new, lower base}. This is where most exam marks live.","derecognise":"Nothing gets thrown out here; our “derecognise” stop is really the **way back**. When things improve, an impairment can be **reversed**, but only up to where the asset would have been without it. Fairly vanilla once you see the ceiling, with one hard rule: {a|goodwill}, once impaired, stays impaired.","present":"Last stop: showing it off. Mostly routine: losses in P&L (or OCI for revalued assets), by class of asset, and for big ones a short story of what happened and how recoverable amount was measured. Not much in your notes here, so treat this as a quick look."},"pitfalls":{"out-of-scope":"Applying SFRS(I) 1-36 to {a|inventory}, {a|financial assets} or fair-value {a|investment property}.","test-all":"Testing every asset every year, instead of only on an indication (G-I-N excepted).","miss-gin":"Forgetting that {a|goodwill}, indefinite-life intangibles and intangibles not yet in use are tested every year.","normal-decline":"Treating an expected decline from age and normal use as an indicator.","indicator-is-loss":"Writing an asset down because of an indicator, without testing.","lower-of":"Taking the lower of FVLCOD and value in use instead of the higher.","gross-fv":"Forgetting to deduct costs of disposal from fair value.","undiscounted":"Using undiscounted cash flows as value in use.","viu-finance":"Including financing or {x|income tax} cash flows (or deducting them as costs of disposal).","viu-future":"Including uncommitted restructurings or upgrades in value in use.","reval-all-pl":"Revalued asset: taking the whole {x|loss} to P&L, ignoring its {e|revaluation surplus}.","reval-all-oci":"Taking more of the {x|loss} to OCI than the asset’s own {e|revaluation surplus} (or using OCI for a cost-model asset).","old-dep":"Depreciating from the {a|pre-impairment carrying amount}.","cgu-prorata-gw":"Spreading a CGU {x|loss} pro rata over all assets instead of {a|goodwill} first.","reverse-cap":"Reversing above the {a|carrying amount} the asset would have had with no impairment.","reverse-gw":"Reversing a {a|goodwill} impairment, or saying no impairment can ever be reversed."},"examTypes":[{"id":"scope","title":"Which assets are within SFRS(I) 1-36? What is a CGU?","pitfall":"Including {a|inventory}, {a|financial assets} or fair-value {a|investment property}.","see":"classify"},{"id":"annual-test","title":"Which assets must be tested every year?","pitfall":"Testing all {a|PPE} every year, or forgetting intangibles not yet in use.","see":"recognise"},{"id":"indicator","title":"Which of these is an indicator of impairment?","pitfall":"Picking an expected decline from normal use, or writing down on an indicator alone.","see":"recognise"},{"id":"recoverable","title":"What is the recoverable amount / impairment loss?","pitfall":"Taking the lower of FVLCOD and VIU, or forgetting costs of disposal.","see":"initial"},{"id":"viu-cashflows","title":"Which cash flows go into value in use?","pitfall":"Including financing, tax, upgrades or an uncommitted restructuring.","see":"initial"},{"id":"loss-entry","title":"Which entry records the impairment loss (cost model)?","pitfall":"Crediting a {l|provision} or debiting OCI for a cost-model asset.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"revalued-loss","title":"How is a loss on a revalued asset recognised?","pitfall":"All to P&L, or all to OCI beyond the {e|surplus}.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"dep-after","title":"What is depreciation after an impairment?","pitfall":"Using the {a|old carrying amount}.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"cgu","title":"How is a CGU’s loss allocated?","pitfall":"Pro rata over all assets instead of {a|goodwill} first; ignoring the floor.","see":"subsequent"},{"id":"reversal","title":"Can the loss be reversed, and by how much?","pitfall":"Reversing above the {a|no-impairment carrying amount}, or reversing {a|goodwill}.","see":"derecognise"},{"id":"disclosure","title":"What must be disclosed about an impairment?","pitfall":"Thinking recognition in P&L is enough.","see":"present"}],"pages":[{"type":"map","id":"map-overview","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"intro","lo":37,"title":"Impairment, the Socratic way","body":["One rule runs this whole chapter: **an asset must not sit on the balance sheet at more than the cash it can bring in.** If it does, write it down.","Each idea starts with a question. Think it through, then reveal the answer. The rule comes after, once you have half-worked it out.","Account names are coloured by element. Tap **Legend** at the top any time. At the end: a chapter quiz in exam style, then a harder exam."],"id":"intro-16erwg","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"map","id":"map-1-classify","bucket":"classify","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"id":"think-13fh0t","type":"think","bucket":"classify","lo":37,"scenario":"Raffles Foods Pte Ltd owns: a factory ({a|PPE}), a warehouse of frozen dumplings ({a|inventory}), a corporate bond it holds to collect interest, a famous brand bought in an acquisition, and a shophouse it rents out, carried under the fair value model as {a|investment property}.","prompt":"Impairment asks “is this asset carried above what it can bring in?”. For which of these does another standard already make sure that can’t happen?","answer":["The **dumplings**: {a|inventory} is already held at the lower of cost and **NRV** (SFRS(I) 1-2), which has its own write-down.","The **bond**: {a|financial assets} have their own expected credit loss model (SFRS(I) 9).","The **shophouse at fair value**: it is remeasured to fair value every year end, and any fall already goes to P&L.","That leaves the **{a|factory}** and the **{a|brand}**: carried at cost (or a revaluation that may be stale), with nothing else watching them. That is SFRS(I) 1-36’s job. It is the security guard for assets nobody else is guarding."],"sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"classify","lo":37,"title":"Which assets does SFRS(I) 1-36 cover?","body":["First question of the chapter: which assets does this test even apply to? The previous page showed that some assets already have their own write-down, so this standard only guards the rest.","SFRS(I) 1-36 applies to:","- {a|Property, plant and equipment} (cost or revaluation model)","- **{a|intangible assets}**, including {a|Goodwill}","- **{a|investment property} under the cost model**","- **{a|right-of-use assets}** (leases)","It does **not** apply to assets with their own measurement rule:","1. **{a|inventory}**: lower of cost and NRV (SFRS(I) 1-2)","2. **{a|financial assets}**: SFRS(I) 9","3. **{a|investment property} at fair value**: SFRS(I) 1-40","+ Impairment is a **process applied to other assets**, not an asset class. It plugs into the subsequent measurement of whatever asset is being tested."],"id":"rule-1eyx6e","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"classify","lo":37,"title":"In scope or not?","intro":"Five kinds of asset feed into the impairment test; three have their own rules and stay out.","notes":{"ppe":"Both models. A revalued asset can still be impaired: its last valuation may be out of date.","intang":"Bought or internally generated. Some are tested **every year** (indefinite life, not yet in use).","gw":"Never generates cash on its own, so it is always tested as part of a group of assets. Tested **every year**.","ipc":"Under the cost model nothing else catches a fall in value, so impairment applies.","rou":"A lessee’s {a|right-of-use asset} is tested like {a|PPE}.","ias":"Compare {a|carrying amount} with recoverable amount; write down any excess.","inv":"Already written down to NRV under SFRS(I) 1-2.","fa":"Expected credit losses or fair value under SFRS(I) 9.","ipfv":"Already at fair value, with every fall in P&L.","own":"These assets have their own write-down mechanism, so SFRS(I) 1-36 stays out."},"walk":[{"nodes":["ppe","intang","gw","ipc","rou","ias"],"text":"Long-lived assets carried at cost (or a revaluation) go through the **impairment test**."},{"nodes":["inv","fa","ipfv","own"],"text":"{a|Inventory}, {a|financial assets} and fair-value {a|investment property} have their **own** rules, so they stay out."},{"nodes":["ipc","ipfv"],"text":"Same building, two answers: {a|investment property} is in scope under the **cost** model, out under the **fair value** model."}],"caption":"Rule of thumb: if another standard already caps the {a|carrying amount}, SFRS(I) 1-36 stays out.","id":"diagram-sne5jw","sect":1,"part":"Classify","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:298px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 271.00 408.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 396.8)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-396.8 260.61,-396.8 260.61,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>ppe\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M95.65,-236C95.65,-236 27.37,-236 27.37,-236 21.37,-236 15.37,-230 15.37,-224 15.37,-224 15.37,-212 15.37,-212 15.37,-206 21.37,-200 27.37,-200 27.37,-200 95.65,-200 95.65,-200 101.65,-200 107.65,-206 107.65,-212 107.65,-212 107.65,-224 107.65,-224 107.65,-230 101.65,-236 95.65,-236\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"50.26\" y=\"-218.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=