6 85.15,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"31.51\" y=\"-14.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Income tax\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge7\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>tax&#45;&gt;viu\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,2\" d=\"M97.61,-30.56C105.35,-34.01 113.23,-38.16 120.03,-43 159.84,-71.37 195.74,-116.06 215.82,-143.61\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"214.01,-144.69 219.22,-148.33 217.42,-142.23 214.01,-144.69\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"147.03\" y=\"-75.91\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> ✗ \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"calc","bucket":"initial","lo":39,"q":"Bedok Bakery’s oven line: net operating inflows of $90,000 at the end of each of the next 4 years, plus $20,000 from its disposal at the end of year 4. Discount rate 8%. What is its value in use (to the nearest dollar)?","answer":312792,"unit":"$","working":["Operating inflows: 90,000 × 3.3121 (4-year annuity factor at 8%) = 298,091.","Disposal: 20,000 ÷ 1.08^4 = 14,701.","Value in use = 298,091 + 14,701 = **$312,792**. Not the undiscounted 380,000: VIU is a present value."],"id":"calc-1m80p4","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"initial","lo":39,"q":"Which cash flow is included when estimating an asset’s value in use?","options":[{"t":"Interest payments on the {l|loan} used to buy the asset","why":"Financing cash flows are excluded."},{"t":"Savings from a restructuring the board is still discussing","why":"Restructurings the entity is not yet committed to are excluded."},{"t":"Net proceeds expected from disposing of the asset at the end of its life","ok":true,"why":"VIU covers cash flows from use **and** from final disposal."},{"t":"Income tax payable on the profits the asset generates","why":"Income tax cash flows are excluded."}],"id":"mcq-yd0j0g","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"mcq-lvr570","type":"mcq","bucket":"initial","lo":39,"q":"A machine has a {a|carrying amount} of $750,000. Its fair value less costs of disposal is $790,000. Its value in use has not been calculated. What follows?","options":[{"t":"Value in use must be calculated before any conclusion can be reached","why":"Not needed: one measure above {a|carrying amount} already proves recoverable amount is above it."},{"t":"There is no impairment; value in use need not be calculated","ok":true,"why":"Recoverable amount is at least 790,000, which is above 750,000. If either measure exceeds {a|carrying amount}, there is no impairment."},{"t":"The machine is revalued up to $790,000","why":"Impairment only writes down; it never writes up to recoverable amount."},{"t":"The machine is impaired if value in use turns out to be lower","why":"Recoverable amount is the higher of the two, so a low VIU doesn’t matter here."}],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"initial","lo":39,"special":"Impairment builds a second number from scratch: **recoverable amount = higher of FVLCOD and value in use**, compared with the {a|carrying amount} the asset’s own standard produced.","id":"compare-1kumyt-2","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"map","id":"map-4-subsequent","bucket":"subsequent","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"id":"think-4c53fu","type":"think","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"scenario":"A machine is under the cost model and has never been revalued. Its {a|carrying amount} is $400,000 and its recoverable amount $350,000, so it has an {x|impairment loss} of $50,000.","prompt":"Where does the loss go, and which account takes the credit: the machine’s cost, or something else?","answer":["To **profit or loss**, straight away, as an {x|Impairment loss} of 50,000.","The credit usually goes to {a|Accumulated depreciation and impairment}, so the {a|original cost} stays visible in the records. The {a|net carrying amount} falls to the recoverable amount either way."],"sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"title":"Recognising the loss: cost model","body":["The test is done and the {a|carrying amount} is above recoverable amount. Time to book the {x|loss}. The simple case first: an asset under the cost model, never revalued.","> Dr {x|Impairment loss} (P&L) / Cr {a|Accumulated depreciation and impairment}","- Recognise it **immediately** in profit or loss.","- The asset’s {a|carrying amount} comes down to its **recoverable amount**."],"id":"rule-ms2of0","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"id":"je-d7m9q0","type":"je","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"q":"Record the impairment of a machine ({a|carrying amount} $400,000, FVLCOD $330,000, value in use $350,000). Cost model.","accounts":["Machine","Building","Accumulated depreciation and impairment","Goodwill","Cash","Impairment loss","Depreciation expense","Reversal of impairment loss","Revaluation surplus (OCI)","Retained earnings","Provision for impairment"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Impairment loss","amount":50000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation and impairment","amount":50000}],"why":"Recoverable amount = higher of 330,000 and 350,000 = 350,000. {x|Loss} 400,000 − 350,000 = 50,000, to profit or loss.","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"id":"think-vlbsk0","type":"think","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"scenario":"A building is carried under the **revaluation model**. {a|Carrying amount} $900,000; an earlier revaluation of this building left a {e|Revaluation surplus (OCI)} of $60,000. Recoverable amount is $820,000.","prompt":"The {x|loss} is $80,000. Part of the building’s value was only ever recorded through OCI. Should the whole loss still hit profit or loss?","answer":["No. Treat it like a **revaluation decrease**: first give back this building’s own {e|surplus}. 60,000 goes against the {e|Revaluation surplus (OCI)}.","Only the excess, 80,000 − 60,000 = **20,000**, goes to profit or loss.","The {i|gain} came in through OCI, so it leaves the same way it came in. Only when the {e|surplus} runs dry does P&L feel it."],"sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"title":"Recognising the loss: revalued asset","body":["Same {x|loss}, harder case: an asset whose earlier gains went to OCI. The previous page argued the {x|loss} should give those gains back first.","For an asset carried at a **revalued amount**, the {x|impairment loss} is treated as a **revaluation decrease**:","1. first, against **that asset’s** {e|Revaluation surplus (OCI)}, up to the {e|surplus} on that asset","2. any excess → {x|Impairment loss} in **profit or loss**","> Dr {e|Revaluation surplus (OCI)}, Dr {x|Impairment loss} / Cr {a|Accumulated depreciation and impairment}","! Two classic wrong answers: the whole {x|loss} to P&L (ignoring the {e|surplus}), or the whole {x|loss} to OCI (beyond the {e|surplus}).","- Only the {e|surplus} on **the same asset** can absorb it. Another building’s {e|surplus} doesn’t help."],"id":"rule-18pwfi","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"title":"Where the loss goes","intro":"Same loss, two homes, depending on how the asset was measured. The notes’ two examples side by side.","notes":{"loss":"{a|Carrying amount} minus recoverable amount, if positive.","cost":"Never revalued: there is no {e|surplus} to absorb anything.","reval":"Carried at a revalued amount: treat the loss as a revaluation decrease.","surplus":"Use up **this asset’s** {e|surplus} first: 60,000 of the 80,000.","pl1":"The whole 50,000 goes to profit or loss immediately.","pl2":"Only the excess over the {e|surplus}: 80,000 − 60,000 = 20,000.","after":"{x|Depreciation} is recalculated on the {a|new, lower carrying amount} from now on."},"walk":[{"nodes":["loss","cost","pl1"],"text":"**Cost model:** the whole {x|loss}, 50,000, to P&L."},{"nodes":["reval","surplus"],"text":"**Revalued:** first use up this asset’s {e|surplus}, 60,000, in OCI."},{"nodes":["pl2"],"text":"Then only the excess, 20,000, to P&L."},{"nodes":["after"],"text":"Either way, {x|depreciation} restarts on the {a|new carrying amount}."}],"caption":"Gains that came in through OCI leave through OCI first.","id":"diagram-10p5zz","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:410px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 373.00 266.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 255.33)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-255.33 362.33,-255.33 362.33,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>loss\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M187.16,-244.53C187.16,-244.53 36.38,-244.53 36.38,-244.53 30.37,-244.53 24.38,-238.53 24.38,-232.53 24.38,-232.53 24.38,-220.53 24.38,-220.53 24.38,-214.53 30.38,-208.53 36.38,-208.53 36.38,-208.53 187.16,-208.53 187.16,-208.53 193.16,-208.53 199.16,-214.53 199.16,-220.53 199.16,-220.53 199.16,-232.53 199.16,-232.53 199.16,-238.53 193.16,-244.53 187.16,-244.53\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"69.77\" y=\"-227.08\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Impairment loss\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"33.02\" y=\"-217.98\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">carrying amount − recoverable amount\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>cost\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M83.53,-181.53C83.53,-181.53