preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"174.76\" y=\"-56.38\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">the whole loss \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node7\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>split\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M269.65,-36C269.65,-36 167.62,-36 167.62,-36 161.62,-36 155.62,-30 155.62,-24 155.62,-24 155.62,-12 155.62,-12 155.62,-6 161.62,0 167.62,0 167.62,0 269.65,0 269.65,0 275.65,0 281.65,-6 281.65,-12 281.65,-12 281.65,-24 281.65,-24 281.65,-30 275.65,-36 269.65,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"218.64\" y=\"-19.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Split: surplus first, then\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"218.64\" y=\"-7.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">profit or loss\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge7\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>l3&#45;&gt;split\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M233.01,-95.31C230.63,-91.34 228.42,-87.03 226.89,-82.75 222.48,-70.42 220.38,-56.01 219.4,-44.01\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"221.49,-43.87 219,-38.03 217.3,-44.15 221.49,-43.87\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"261.76\" y=\"-73.25\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> Yes, but it is\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"261.76\" y=\"-62\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">smaller than the\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"261.76\" y=\"-50.75\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">loss \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"think-7r69bd","type":"think","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"scenario":"A machine carried at $400,000 was being depreciated at $100,000 a year, with 4 years of useful life left and no residual value. It has just been impaired to its recoverable amount and is now carried at $350,000; the 4 remaining years are unchanged.","prompt":"Next year, do you keep charging the {x|old depreciation}?","answer":["No. The {x|old depreciation charge}, 100,000 × 4 years, would write off 400,000, which is more than the 350,000 the machine is now carried at: it would sail past zero. {x|Depreciation} must spread the **{a|new carrying amount}** over the **remaining useful life**.","Here: 350,000 ÷ 4 = **87,500** a year (assuming no residual value)."],"sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"title":"After the loss: new depreciation","body":["The asset is now carried at a lower amount, so the old {x|depreciation} charge no longer fits. This rule resets it, as the notes’ machine showed.","> {x|New depreciation} = ({a|new carrying amount} − residual value) ÷ remaining useful life","- Revise the {x|charge} from the period after the impairment.","- Review the useful life, method and residual value at the same time: whatever caused the impairment may have changed them too.","! Using the **{a|old carrying amount}** (or the {x|old charge}) after an impairment is the classic slip."],"id":"rule-16j8dp","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"id":"worked-14vdqz","type":"worked","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"title":"Worked example: Tanjong Printing’s press","q":"Tanjong Printing bought a {a|press} on 1 Jan 20X1 for $1,200,000: 10-year life, no residual value, straight-line, cost model. At 31 Dec 20X3, demand for print falls sharply. The press could be sold for $610,000, with $15,000 of dismantling and selling costs. Using it will bring in net $150,000 a year for its remaining 7 years. Discount rate 10%.","intro":["A typical exam question: you get the asset’s history, a selling price and a cash-flow forecast, and must give the {x|impairment loss}, the entry or next year’s {x|depreciation}. Remember three things:","- Recoverable amount is the **higher** of FVLCOD and value in use.","- Under the cost model the whole {x|loss} goes to **P&L**.","- {x|Depreciation} then restarts from the {a|new carrying amount} over the **remaining** life.","Then follow these steps: indication → {a|carrying amount} → FVLCOD → value in use → recoverable amount and {x|loss} → entry → new {x|depreciation}."],"steps":[{"h":"Check for an indication.","t":"Yes: demand falling sharply is a significant adverse change in the market. So estimate the recoverable amount."},{"h":"Work out the carrying amount today.","t":["{x|Depreciation} = 1,200,000 ÷ 10 = **{x|120,000}** a year.","{a|Carrying amount at 31 Dec 20X3} = {a|cost 1,200,000} − 3 × {x|120,000} = **{a|840,000}**."]},{"h":"Calculate fair value less costs of disposal.","t":"FVLCOD = 610,000 − 15,000 = **595,000**."},{"h":"Calculate value in use.","t":"VIU = 150,000 × 4.8684 (7-year annuity factor at 10%) = **730,263**."},{"h":"Take the higher as recoverable amount, and find the loss.","t":["Recoverable amount = higher of 595,000 and 730,263 = **730,263** (value in use wins).","{x|Impairment loss} = {a|840,000} − 730,263 = **{x|109,737}**."]},{"h":"Record the impairment.","t":"Cost model, so the whole {x|loss} goes to P&L.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Impairment loss","amount":109737,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation and impairment","amount":109737,"el":"a"}]},{"h":"Recalculate depreciation from 20X4.","t":"{x|New depreciation} = {a|730,263} ÷ 7 remaining years = **{x|104,323}** a year (down from {x|120,000}).","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Depreciation expense","amount":104323,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation and impairment","amount":104323,"el":"a"}],"after":"The press is worth more kept than sold, so value in use sets the recoverable amount. Print may be dying, but not that fast."}],"sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"id":"calc-1hqifz","type":"calc","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"q":"Your turn, with the first two lines done. Jurong Bottling’s filling line has a {a|carrying amount} of $520,000. Fair value $470,000, costs of disposal $20,000. Net cash inflows of $120,000 a year for 5 years, discount rate 9%. What is the {x|impairment loss}?","answer":53242,"unit":"$","given":2,"working":["FVLCOD = 470,000 − 20,000 = 450,000.","Value in use = 120,000 × 3.8897 = 466,758.","Recoverable amount = higher = 466,758.","{x|Loss} = 520,000 − 466,758 = **$53,242**, to P&L."],"sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"id":"calc-gr7l7l","type":"calc","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"q":"Now a blank one. Woodlands Tools has a lathe with a {a|carrying amount} of $300,000. Fair value $275,000, costs of disposal $15,000. Net cash inflows from using it: $70,000 a year for 4 years, discount rate 6%. What is the {x|impairment loss}?","answer":40000,"unit":"$","working":["FVLCOD = 275,000 − 15,000 = 260,000.","Value in use = 70,000 × 3.4651 = 242,557.","This time **selling** wins: recoverable amount = 260,000.","{x|Loss} = 300,000 − 260,000 = **$40,000**. (Using VIU by habit would give 57,443.)"],"sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"id":"think-1yvy39","type":"think","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"scenario":"Changi Café is a CGU. Its assets: {a|goodwill} $120,000 (from buying the café), {a|PPE} $600,000, and a {a|food licence} $200,000. The café’s recoverable amount is $680,000, so the CGU has an {x|impairment loss} of $240,000.","prompt":"The loss belongs to the café as a whole. Which asset should take the first hit?","answer":["**{a|Goodwill} first.** It is the softest number on the list: it only exists because the café was expected to earn more than its separate assets. If the café disappoints, that premium is the first thing to go. {a|Goodwill} takes 120,000.","The remaining 120,000 is spread over the other assets **pro rata to their {a|carrying amounts}**: {a|PPE} 90,000, {a|licence} 30,000."],"sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"title":"Allocating a CGU’s loss","body":["For a CGU the test gives one {x|loss} for the whole group, but entries are made asset by asset. This is the order for sharing it out, which Changi Café just previewed.","1. Reduce any **{a|goodwill}** allocated to the CGU first.","2. Spread the rest over the CGU’s other assets **pro rata** to their {a|carrying amounts}.","3. **Floor:** no asset goes below the **highest** of its own FVLCOD, its value in use (if measurable) and **zero**. Any amount blocked by the floor is re-spread over the other assets.","- Each reduction is treated as an {x|impairment loss} on that asset (P&L, or OCI first for a revalued asset).","! Spreading the loss pro rata over **all** assets, {a|goodwill} included, is wrong. {a|Goodwill} goes first."],"id":"rule-1q6q7p","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"subsequent","lo":39,"title":"Changi Café: allocating the CGU loss","intro":"The loss flows in a fixed order: {a|goodwill} soaks up what it can, and the rest is shared by size.","notes":{"cgu":"{a|Carrying amount} of the CGU 120,000 + 600,000 + 200,000 = 920,000, less recoverable amount 680,000.","gw":"{a|Goodwill} absorbs the loss first, up to its full 120,000.","rest":"240,000 − 120,000 = 120,000 left to allocate.","ppe":"120,000 × 600,000 ÷ 800,000 = 90,000.","lic":"120,000 × 200,000 ÷ 800,000 = 30,000.","floor":"If a pro rata cut would push an asset below this floor, cap it and re-spread the excess over the other assets."},"walk":[{"nodes":["cgu"],"text":"Work out the CGU’s {x|loss}: 240,000."},{"nodes":["gw"],"text":"**{a|Goodwill} first.** It is written down before anything else."},{"nodes":["rest","ppe","lic"],"text":"**Then pro rata** over the other assets, by {a|carrying amount}."},{"nodes":["floor"],"text":"Check the **floor**: no asset below the highest of its FVLCOD, VIU and zero."}],"caption":"{a|Goodwill} first, then pro rata, never below the floor.","id":"diagram-mdmgji","sect":4,"part":"Subsequent measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:253px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 230.00 327.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 316.33)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-316.33 219.33,-316.33 219.33,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>cgu\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M171.66,-305.53C171.66,-305.53 35.87,-305.53 35.87,-305.53 29.87,-305.53 23.88,-299.53 23.88,-293.53 23.88,-293.53 23.88,-281.53 23.88,-281.53 23.88,-275.53 29.88,-269.53 35.88,-269.53 35.88,-269.53 171.66,-269.53 171.66,-269.53 177.66,-269.53 183.66,-275.53 183.66,-281.53 183.66,-281.53 183.66,-293.53 183.66,-293.53 183.66,-299.53 177.66,-305.53 171.66,-305.53\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"79.02\" y=\"-289.08\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">CGU loss\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"32.52\" y=\"-278.08\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">920,000 − 680,000 = 240,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>gw\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#ffe4e6\" stroke=\"#be123c\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M129.28,-242.53C129.28,-242.53 78.25,-242.53 78.25,-242.53 72.25,-242.53 66.25,-236.53 66.25,-230.53 66.25,-230.53 66.25,-214.89 66.25,-214.89 66.25,-208.89 72.25,-202.89 78.25,-202.89 78.25,-202.89 129.28,-202.89 129.28,-202.89 135.28,-202.89 141.28,-208.89 141.28,-214.89 141.28,-214.89 141.28,-230.53 141.28,-230.53 141.28,-236.53 135.28,-242.53 129.28,-242.53\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"pr