chor=\"start\" x=\"394.73\" y=\"-47.09\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">4 Allocate\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"399.23\" y=\"-37.99\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">by relative\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"397.35\" y=\"-28.99\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">standalone\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"392.1\" y=\"-19.99\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">selling prices;\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"385.35\" y=\"-10.99\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">spread discounts\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge3\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>s3&#45;&gt;s4\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M356.85,-33.04C359.71,-33.04 362.59,-33.04 365.45,-33.04\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"365.44,-35.49 372.44,-33.04 365.44,-30.59 365.44,-35.49\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node5\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>s5\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M579,-61.58C579,-61.58 495.9,-61.58 495.9,-61.58 489.9,-61.58 483.9,-55.58 483.9,-49.58 483.9,-49.58 483.9,-16.5 483.9,-16.5 483.9,-10.5 489.9,-4.5 495.9,-4.5 495.9,-4.5 579,-4.5 579,-4.5 585,-4.5 591,-10.5 591,-16.5 591,-16.5 591,-49.58 591,-49.58 591,-55.58 585,-61.58 579,-61.58\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"505.2\" y=\"-47.09\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">5 Recognise\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"494.7\" y=\"-37.99\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">over time if a para 35\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"506.7\" y=\"-28.99\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">criterion is met;\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"505.2\" y=\"-19.99\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">otherwise at the\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"496.58\" y=\"-10.99\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">point control passes\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge4\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>s4&#45;&gt;s5\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M466.43,-33.04C469.21,-33.04 472.02,-33.04 474.85,-33.04\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"474.83,-35.49 481.83,-33.04 474.83,-30.59 474.83,-35.49\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"think","bucket":"contract","lo":40,"scenario":"Three deals at Tanjong Tools. (1) A long-standing customer orders by phone, as it always does, and Tanjong ships on its usual 30-day terms. (2) A new customer signs a 3-year contract, but Tanjong already knows it is insolvent and will almost certainly never pay. (3) Two companies swap identical stock of the same value just to boost both their sales figures.","prompt":"Which of these is a contract that SFRS(I) 15 will account for?","answer":["**(1) Yes.** A contract can be approved **orally or by customary business practice**: it doesn’t have to be in writing. Rights and payment terms are clear.","**(2) No.** Collection of the consideration is **not probable**, so it fails step 1 however long and shiny the contract looks.","**(3) No.** The swap has **no commercial substance**: nothing about either company’s cash flows changes. It is just window dressing."],"id":"think-krvcbm","sect":1,"part":"Identify the contract"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"contract","lo":40,"title":"Step 1: the five contract criteria","body":["Step 1 is the gatekeeper: no {i|revenue} until there is a contract the standard recognises. Tanjong’s deals showed a contract can be oral, but it can’t be hopeless or hollow.","A contract is accounted for under SFRS(I) 15 only when **all five** are met:","1. The parties have **approved** the contract (in writing, orally or by customary practice) and are **committed** to perform.","2. Each party’s **rights** to the goods or services can be identified.","3. The **payment terms** can be identified.","4. The contract has **commercial substance**.","5. **Collection is probable** of the consideration the entity will be entitled to.","! Not on the list: the contract’s **length**, finishing it **within 12 months**, or having it **in writing**. Exam options love dressing these up as criteria."],"id":"rule-4ykey8","sect":1,"part":"Identify the contract"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"contract","lo":40,"q":"Which of the following is a criterion for identifying a contract with a customer under SFRS(I) 15?","options":[{"t":"The contract must be in writing and signed by both parties","why":"Approval can also be oral or by customary business practice. Writing is common, but not required."},{"t":"It is probable that the entity will collect the consideration it is entitled to","ok":true,"why":"Collection being probable is one of the five criteria."},{"t":"The entity must be able to complete its obligations within one year","why":"The length of the contract is not a criterion. Construction contracts run for years."},{"t":"The customer must have paid a deposit","why":"No payment is needed up front; the payment terms only have to be identifiable."}],"id":"mcq-1loqg8","sect":1,"part":"Identify the contract"},{"type":"map","id":"map-2-pos","bucket":"pos","sect":2,"part":"Performance obligations"},{"type":"think","bucket":"pos","lo":40,"scenario":"Marina Mobile sells a phone with a 2-year airtime plan for one price. Separately, Jurong Systems sells a software licence plus standard installation that any IT firm could do; and Bedok Builders contracts to build a house, supplying bricks, labour and plumbing.","prompt":"How many separate promises are there in each contract? Is the house really three promises: bricks, labour and plumbing?","answer":["**Marina:** two. The phone works with other networks and the plan works with other phones; each is useful on its own and they are not blended into something else.","**Jurong:** two. The installation is standard, others could do it, and it doesn’t modify the software. Licence and installation are separate.","**Bedok:** one. The bricks and labour are **inputs** into a single combined output, the house. The customer is buying a house, not a pile of bricks with a plumber attached."],"id":"think-13nazy","sect":2,"part":"Performance obligations"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"pos","lo":40,"title":"Step 2: what makes a promise distinct","body":["With a valid contract in hand, step 2 asks what exactly was promised. Each separate promise later gets its own slice of the price and its own timing, so the split matters.","Each **distinct** good or service is a separate **performance obligation**. It is distinct if **both** are true:","1. **Capable of being distinct:** the customer can benefit from it on its own or with readily available resources.","2. **Separately identifiable:** it is not, for example, an input into a combined output, it doesn’t significantly modify another promise, and it isn’t highly dependent on the others.","If not distinct, combine it with other promises until you have a distinct bundle.","- A **series** of substantially the same services (monthly cleaning, daily security) is **one** performance obligation satisfied over time."],"id":"rule-ozo2v8","sect":2,"part":"Performance obligations"},{"type":"tree","bucket":"pos","lo":40,"title":"Is this promise a separate performance obligation?","scenario":"Jurong Systems sells a software licence plus installation. The installation is routine set-up that several other IT firms offer; it does not modify or customise the software. Tap your way to the answer for the installation.","expect":"sep","start":"n1","nodes":{"n1":{"q":"Can the customer benefit from the installation on its own, or with resources readily available to it?","why":"If the promise is useless on its own and nothing in the market makes it useful, it cannot be distinct.","opts":[{"t":"Yes: the software is delivered, and installation services are sold by others too","to":"n2"},{"t":"No","to":"comb"}]},"n2":{"q":"Is it separately identifiable from the other promises in the contract?","why":"Even a useful promise is not separate if the entity is really selling one combined output.","opts":[{"t":"Yes: no significant integration, no modification, not highly dependent","to":"sep"},{"t":"No: it significantly customises the software or is an input into one combined item","to":"comb"}]},"sep":{"result":"Separate performance obligation","body":["Both tests are met, so licence and installation are **two** performance obligations.","- Each gets its own slice of the transaction price at step 4.","- Each is recognised when (or as) it is satisfied at step 5."]},"comb":{"result":"Combine it with other promises","body":["Not distinct on its own: combine it with the promises it is integrated with, until the bundle is distinct.","! A house is one obligation, not bricks + labour + plumbing."]}},"id":"tree-1tk5ct","sect":2,"part":"Performance obligations","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:339px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 308.00 335.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 323.83)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-323.83 297.08,-323.83 297.08,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>n1\u003c/title>\n\u0