#374151\"> Yes, including a\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"117.26\" y=\"-50.75\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">reasonable margin \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge8\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>r4&#45;&gt;pit\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M158.74,-83.96C161.35,-72.52 166.01,-58.88 174.26,-49 177.25,-45.43 180.77,-42.21 184.58,-39.33\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"185.74,-41.08 189.49,-35.95 183.36,-37.62 185.74,-41.08\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"214.39\" y=\"-62\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> No, payment only\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"214.39\" y=\"-50.75\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">on delivery \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"recognise","lo":40,"q":"Which of these performance obligations is satisfied at a point in time?","options":[{"t":"A 12-month software subscription","why":"The customer receives and consumes the benefit throughout the year: over time."},{"t":"Standard laptops sold by an electronics retailer","ok":true,"why":"Standard goods with an alternative use: {i|revenue} when control passes to the customer."},{"t":"Renovating the customer’s own office","why":"The work enhances an asset the customer controls: over time."},{"t":"Monthly payroll processing for a client","why":"Consumed as performed: over time."}],"id":"mcq-9vcy8h","sect":5,"part":"Recognise revenue"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"recognise","lo":42,"title":"Measuring progress over time","body":["Once an obligation is satisfied over time, the next question is how much of it is done so far. That needs a measure of progress, and the exam’s favourite is cost-to-cost.","For an obligation satisfied over time, recognise {i|revenue} by measuring **progress** towards completion:","- **Output methods:** surveys of work done, milestones reached, units delivered.","- **Input methods:** costs incurred, labour hours, machine hours.","Cost-to-cost is the usual exam method:","> Progress = {x|costs incurred to date} ÷ estimated total costs","> {i|Revenue this year} = contract price × progress to date − {i|revenue already recognised}","- If progress can’t be measured reasonably but the costs will be recovered, recognise {i|revenue} **only up to the {x|costs incurred}**."],"id":"rule-nj98ig","sect":5,"part":"Recognise revenue"},{"type":"map","id":"map-6-recognise","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"construction","sect":6,"part":"Recognise revenue · construction"},{"id":"think-138rjt","type":"think","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"construction","lo":42,"scenario":"Bedok Builders signs a 3-year contract to build a warehouse on the customer’s land for $10,000,000. In year 1 it spends $2,000,000 and expects to spend $6,000,000 more.","prompt":"How much {i|revenue} for year 1? Think: how far through the job is Bedok?","answer":["Total expected cost = 2,000,000 + 6,000,000 = 8,000,000. Progress = 2,000,000 ÷ 8,000,000 = **25%**.","{i|Revenue} = 10,000,000 × 25% = **$2,500,000**; profit = {i|2,500,000} − {x|2,000,000} = 500,000.","{i|Revenue} follows progress, not billing. If Bedok invoiced only $2m, that changes the balance sheet line, not the {i|revenue}."],"sect":6,"part":"Recognise revenue · construction"},{"id":"worked-2wu8y4","type":"worked","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"construction","lo":42,"title":"Worked example: year 2 goes wrong","q":"Bedok Builders has a 3-year contract to build a warehouse on the customer’s land for $10,000,000, with progress measured cost-to-cost. Year 1: costs $2,000,000, estimate to complete $6,000,000. Year 2: costs $4,300,000, and the estimate to complete is now $4,200,000. Year 1 billing was $2,000,000. Find revenue and profit for each year.","intro":["A typical exam question: a contract price, {x|costs to date} and an estimate to complete for two years; find each year’s {i|revenue} and profit. Remember two things:","- Progress uses the **latest** estimate of total cost, and each year’s {i|revenue} is {i|cumulative revenue to date} less {i|revenue already recognised}.","- If the latest total cost is above the price, the **whole** {x|expected loss} is recognised now, not spread.","Then follow these steps each year: total cost → progress → revenue → onerous check → result."],"steps":[{"h":"Measure year 1 progress.","t":"Total cost = {x|2,000,000} + 6,000,000 = 8,000,000. Progress = {x|2,000,000} ÷ 8,000,000 = **25%**."},{"h":"Recognise year 1 revenue and profit.","t":"{i|Revenue} = 10,000,000 × 25% = **{i|2,500,000}**; {x|costs} 2,000,000 expensed; profit {i|2,500,000} − {x|2,000,000} = **500,000**.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Contract asset","amount":2500000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revenue","amount":2500000,"el":"i"}]},{"h":"Record the year 1 billing.","t":"Invoice 2,000,000: the right to that amount is now unconditional, a {a|receivable}.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Accounts receivable","amount":2000000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Contract asset","amount":2000000,"el":"a"}],"after":"{a|2,500,000} − 2,000,000 = **{a|500,000}** stays as a {a|contract asset} (earned, not yet billed)."},{"h":"Re-estimate the total cost in year 2.","t":"{x|Costs to date} 6,300,000; total now 6,300,000 + 4,200,000 = **10,500,000**, above the 10,000,000 price. Use this new total, not the old 8,000,000."},{"h":"Recognise year 2 revenue.","t":["Progress = {x|6,300,000} ÷ 10,500,000 = **60%**; {i|revenue to date} = 10,000,000 × 60% = {i|6,000,000}.","Year 2 {i|revenue} = {i|6,000,000} − {i|2,500,000 already recognised} = **{i|3,500,000}**."]},{"h":"Provide for the onerous contract.","t":["Whole-contract {x|loss} = 10,500,000 − 10,000,000 = {x|500,000}.","{i|Revenue to date} − {x|costs to date} already show {i|6,000,000} − {x|6,300,000} = (300,000), so provide the rest now: **{l|200,000}**."],"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Loss on onerous contract","amount":200000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for onerous contract","amount":200000,"el":"l"}]},{"h":"Work out the year 2 result.","t":"{i|3,500,000} − {x|4,300,000} − {x|200,000} = **(1,000,000)**. Check: year 1 500,000 + year 2 (1,000,000) = (500,000), the whole {x|loss}. The good news from year 1 gets clawed back, with interest."}],"sect":6,"part":"Recognise revenue · construction"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"recognise","blabel":"construction","lo":42,"title":"The three-year contract at a glance","intro":"Notes example 4 as a timeline. Watch year 2: the cost estimate jumps, the contract turns loss-making, and the whole remaining {x|loss} lands at once.","notes":{"y1":"{x|Costs} 2,000,000 ÷ estimated total 8,000,000 = 25%. {i|Revenue} 10,000,000 × 25% = 2,500,000; profit {i|2,500,000} − {x|2,000,000} = 500,000.","est":"Costs to complete are re-estimated: total cost is now 10,500,000, above the price of 10,000,000. The contract is **onerous**.","y2":"Progress 6,300,000 ÷ 10,500,000 = 60%. {i|Revenue to date} 6,000,000 − 2,500,000 = 3,500,000. Result {i|3,500,000} − {x|costs} 4,300,000 − {x|provision charge} 200,000 = (1,000,000).","prov":"The {l|provision} is the part of the 500,000 {x|loss} not already caught in {i|revenue} − {x|costs to date}: 500,000 − (6,300,000 − 6,000,000) = 200,000.","y3":"{i|Revenue} 4,000,000 − {x|costs} 4,200,000 + {l|provision} released 200,000 = 0. The {x|loss} was already booked.","tot":"500,000 + (1,000,000) + 0 = (500,000): price 10,000,000 − {x|total cost} 10,500,000."},"walk":[{"nodes":["y1"],"text":"Year 1 looks healthy: 25% done, 500,000 profit."},{"nodes":["est"],"text":"Then costs balloon. The estimated total is now **above the price**: an onerous contract."},{"nodes":["y2","prov"],"text":"Year 2 takes the hit for the **whole** remaining {x|loss}: {i|revenue} on progress, plus a 200,000 {l|provision} for the part still to come."},{"nodes":["y3"],"text":"Year 3 releases the {l|provision} against the {x|final costs}, so it shows nil."},{"nodes":["tot"],"text":"Add the three years: the contract’s {x|total loss}, and none of it was spread over later years."}],"caption":"Profit is spread with progress; a {x|loss} is recognised as soon as it is expected.","id":"diagram-hscf8f","sect":6,"part":"Recognise revenue · construction","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:640px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 884.00 158.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 147.26)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-147.26 873.48,-147.26 873.48,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>y1\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#166534\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M82.53,-132.14C82.53,-132.14 12,-132.14 12,-132.14 6,-132.14 0,-126.14 0,-120.14 0,-120.14 0,-95.5 0,-95.5 0,-89.5 6,-83.5 12,-83.5 12,-83.5 82.53,-83.5 82.53,-83.5 88.53,-83.5 94.53,-89.5 94.53,-95.5 94.53,-95.5 94.53,-120.14 94.53,-120.14 94.53,-126.14 88.53,-132.14 82.53,-132.14\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"31.14\" y=\"-118.37\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Year 1\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"13.89\" y=\"-107.37\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">progress 25%\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"8.64\" y=\"-98.27\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">revenue 2,500,000\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"19.14\" y=\"-89.27\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill