3cg id=\"edge7\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>rep&#45;&gt;yes\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M159.08,-78.65C167.45,-67.57 177.18,-54.7 185.39,-43.83\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"186.92,-45.28 188.87,-39.23 183.57,-42.75 186.92,-45.28\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"187.39\" y=\"-52.39\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> no \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"think-1fq2xc","type":"think","bucket":"classify","lo":43,"scenario":"Jurong Steel’s finance team lists its costs of borrowing this year: interest on a {l|bank loan}, worked out with the effective interest method; interest on its {l|lease liabilities}; a {x|foreign-exchange loss} on a US$ {l|loan} that partly reflects a lower US interest rate; and {e|dividends} paid to its ordinary shareholders.","prompt":"Which of these count as \"borrowing costs\"?","answer":["The first three. **Borrowing costs** are interest and other costs of borrowing funds:","- interest using the **effective interest method** (so issue costs and premiums or discounts are in it too)","- **interest on {l|lease liabilities}**","- **exchange differences** on foreign-currency {l|loans}, to the extent they adjust the interest cost","{e|Dividends} on ordinary shares are a return to owners, not a cost of **borrowing**. {e|Equity} isn’t a {l|loan}, however much shareholders might like it to be."],"sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"classify","lo":43,"title":"The core principle: capitalise, no choice","body":["Now that we know which assets qualify and what counts as a borrowing cost, the core rule says what to do with that interest. It exists so that an asset funded by debt shows its full cost, just as one paid for in cash would.","Borrowing costs **directly attributable** to the acquisition, construction or production of a qualifying asset **must be capitalised** as part of its cost. **All other borrowing costs are expensed.**","- **Directly attributable** = borrowing costs that would have been **avoided** if the money had not been spent on the asset.","- Capitalised interest becomes part of the {a|asset’s cost}, so it is depreciated along with the rest of it once the asset is in use.","! Capitalisation is **required**, not an accounting-policy choice. Practice Q9’s wrong options include \"only if in line with the company’s accounting policies\" and \"all borrowing costs should be capitalised\"."],"id":"rule-1312x0","sect":1,"part":"Classify"},{"id":"tree-1rlcix","type":"tree","bucket":"classify","lo":43,"title":"Capitalise or expense?","scenario":"Kallang Cold Stores takes out a $3,000,000 {l|bank loan} to build a cold-storage warehouse that will take 14 months. It is now month 6: the money is being spent, interest is running, and the builders are busy on site. Tap your way to what happens to this month’s interest.","expect":"cap","start":"t1","nodes":{"t1":{"q":"Is it a borrowing cost?","why":"Only interest and other costs of borrowing funds are in scope. A return to shareholders is not.","opts":[{"t":"Yes: interest (effective interest method), lease interest, or an FX difference that adjusts interest","to":"t2"},{"t":"No: a {e|dividend} on ordinary shares","to":"notbc"}]},"t2":{"q":"Does the asset necessarily take a substantial period to get ready for use or sale?","why":"That is the definition of a qualifying asset. No \"getting ready\" period, nothing to capitalise.","opts":[{"t":"Yes","to":"t3"},{"t":"No: it is ready when bought, or it is a {a|financial asset}","to":"notq"}]},"t3":{"q":"Is it {a|inventory} made in large quantities on a repetitive basis?","why":"The standard lets the entity leave mass-produced inventory out.","opts":[{"t":"Yes","to":"rep"},{"t":"No","to":"t4"}]},"t4":{"q":"Would the cost have been avoided if the money had not been spent on this asset?","why":"\"Directly attributable\" means avoidable: the interest exists because of this asset.","opts":[{"t":"Yes","to":"t5"},{"t":"No: it relates to something else","to":"other"}]},"t5":{"q":"Where are we on the asset’s timeline?","why":"Even for a qualifying asset, only the borrowing costs inside the capitalisation window go into cost.","opts":[{"t":"Before spending, borrowing costs and work have all begun","to":"before"},{"t":"Active development under way","to":"cap"},{"t":"An extended period with no active development","to":"susp"},{"t":"Substantially all the work is complete","to":"ceased"}]},"notbc":{"result":"Not a borrowing cost","body":["{e|Dividends} on ordinary shares are distributions to owners, recognised in {e|equity}. SFRS(I) 1-23 does not apply."]},"notq":{"result":"Expense: not a qualifying asset","body":["Ready-to-use assets and {a|financial assets} have no substantial \"getting ready\" period. Expense the borrowing costs as incurred.","! Classic exam trap: ready-to-use equipment bought with a bond (practice Q49)."]},"rep":{"result":"Need not capitalise","body":["{a|Inventory} made in large quantities on a repetitive basis: the entity need not apply the standard, so the interest is normally expensed."]},"other":{"result":"Expense","body":["Borrowing costs that are not directly attributable to a qualifying asset are expensed as incurred."]},"before":{"result":"Expense: capitalisation has not commenced","body":["Capitalisation starts only when **all three** have happened: expenditure on the asset, borrowing costs incurred, and activities to get it ready begun."]},"cap":{"result":"Capitalise into the {a|warehouse’s cost}","body":["A qualifying asset, under active development, with directly attributable interest: it **must** be capitalised.","> Dr {a|Building under construction} / Cr {a|Cash} (or {l|Interest payable})"]},"susp":{"result":"Suspend: expense for now","body":["During an **extended** halt in active development, borrowing costs are expensed. Normal or necessary delays do not suspend."]},"ceased":{"result":"Cease: expense from here","body":["Once substantially all the work is done, capitalisation stops, even if minor fixes remain. Later interest goes to profit or loss as {x|interest expense}."]}},"sect":1,"part":"Classify","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:640px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 730.00 596.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 585.22)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-585.22 719.26,-585.22 719.26,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>t1\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M602.53,-574.42C602.53,-574.42 504.25,-574.42 504.25,-574.42 498.25,-574.42 492.25,-568.42 492.25,-562.42 492.25,-562.42 492.25,-550.42 492.25,-550.42 492.25,-544.42 498.25,-538.42 504.25,-538.42 504.25,-538.42 602.53,-538.42 602.53,-538.42 608.53,-538.42 614.53,-544.42 614.53,-550.42 614.53,-550.42 614.53,-562.42 614.53,-562.42 614.53,-568.42 608.53,-574.42 602.53,-574.42\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"553.39\" y=\"-551.97\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Is it a borrowing cost?\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>t2\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M540.15,-433.67C540.15,-433.67 418.62,-433.67 418.62,-433.67 412.62,-433.67 406.62,-427.67 406.62,-421.67 406.62,-421.67 406.62,-401.03 406.62,-401.03 406.62,-395.03 412.62,-389.03 418.62,-389.03 418.62,-389.03 540.15,-389.03 540.15,-389.03 546.15,-389.03 552.15,-395.03 552.15,-401.03 552.15,-401.03 552.15,-421.67 552.15,-421.67 552.15,-427.67 546.15,-433.67 540.15,-433.67\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"479.39\" y=\"-418.9\" 