">Dec\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"349.26\" y=\"-81.05\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">complete\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"354.51\" y=\"-71.95\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">1 month\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge4\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>act2&#45;&gt;post\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M316.43,-86C321.8,-86 327.4,-86 332.88,-86\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"332.71,-88.1 338.71,-86 332.71,-83.9 332.71,-88.1\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge6\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>act2&#45;&gt;cap\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" d=\"M313.5,-106.3C319.77,-110.59 326.45,-115.17 332.94,-119.62\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#0f766e\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" points=\"331.45,-121.14 337.59,-122.8 333.82,-117.67 331.45,-121.14\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge9\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>post&#45;&gt;pl\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b45309\" d=\"M401.79,-72.37C408.56,-69.28 415.96,-65.89 423.38,-62.49\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#b45309\" stroke=\"#b45309\" points=\"424.23,-64.41 428.81,-60.01 422.48,-60.6 424.23,-64.41\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"visual","bucket":"recognise","lo":44,"title":"Tampines Depot, step by step","html":"\u003cp>The same year as a running total of \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">interest in the depot’s cost\u003c/span>. Only months inside the window add to it. The dashed line is the whole year’s interest: what the classic slip of capitalising from the day the \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">loan\u003c/span> is drawn would put in.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wg\" data-wg=\"roll\" data-key=\"td-c2\" data-cfg=\"{&quot;target&quot;:{&quot;v&quot;:180000,&quot;label&quot;:&quot;whole year’s interest 180,000&quot;},&quot;panels&quot;:[{&quot;title&quot;:&quot;The window applied&quot;,&quot;sub&quot;:&quot;only months inside the window count&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;a&quot;,&quot;bal&quot;:&quot;Interest in depot cost&quot;,&quot;start&quot;:0,&quot;startCap&quot;:&quot;{a|Interest in the depot’s cost} starts at nil. Interest runs at 15,000 a month.&quot;,&quot;years&quot;:[{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;Jan–Feb&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;interest&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:0,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;Jan–Feb: loan drawn, but no spending or work yet: not commenced. Its {x|30,000} goes to {x|Interest expense}, so nothing joins the {a|depot}.&quot;}]},{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;Mar–May&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;interest&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:45000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;Mar–May: spending, interest and building all under way: capitalise. 3 × 15,000 = {x|45,000} joins the {a|depot}: {a|45,000}.&quot;}]},{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;Jun–Jul&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;interest&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:0,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;Jun–Jul: site idle in a dispute, an extended halt: suspend. Its {x|30,000} goes to {x|Interest expense}, so nothing joins the {a|depot}.&quot;}]},{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;Aug–Nov&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;interest&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:60000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;Aug–Nov: building again until substantially complete: capitalise. 4 × 15,000 = {x|60,000} joins the {a|depot}: {a|105,000}.&quot;}]},{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;Dec&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;interest&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:0,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;Dec: depot substantially complete: ceased. Its {x|15,000} goes to {x|Interest expense}, so nothing joins the {a|depot}.&quot;}]}]}],&quot;notes&quot;:{&quot;start&quot;:&quot;Tampines Depot: $3,600,000 at 5% for the year, so {x|180,000} of interest. Press Play or Next and watch which stretches of months make it into the {a|depot}.&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[&quot;Each stretch: months × the monthly interest. Inside the window it is added to the {a|depot’s cost}; outside it, it stays in {x|Interest expense}.&quot;],&quot;end&quot;:&quot;**7 months in, 5 out.** The window puts {a|105,000} into the depot and leaves {x|75,000} in P&amp;L. Capitalising all year would overstate the {a|depot} (and understate {x|interest expense}) by 75,000.&quot;}}\">\u003c/div>","id":"visual-skfyp8","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"id":"calc-ptxvt7","type":"calc","bucket":"recognise","lo":44,"q":"Tampines Depot Pte Ltd draws a $3,600,000 {l|loan} at 5% on 1 January, specifically to build a logistics depot. Spending and building start on 1 March. Work stops in June and July because of a payment dispute with the contractor, with nobody on site, and the depot is substantially complete on 30 November. How much interest is capitalised for the year?","answer":105000,"unit":"$","working":["Interest for the year = 3,600,000 × 5% = 180,000.","Capitalisation months: March to November is 9 months, less 2 suspended = 7.","Capitalised = 180,000 × 7/12 = **$105,000**. The other {x|75,000} is {x|Interest expense}."],"sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"recognise","lo":44,"q":"A company is building a bridge with borrowed funds. Which event requires it to suspend capitalising borrowing costs?","options":[{"t":"Work pauses for three weeks during seasonal high water that is normal for the area at that time of year","why":"A delay that is a necessary, expected part of the process does not suspend capitalisation."},{"t":"Engineers spend a month on design approvals while no physical work takes place","why":"Substantial technical and administrative work is still active development: carry on capitalising."},{"t":"Work stops for five months because the company has run out of {a|cash} to pay the contractor","ok":true,"why":"An extended period with no active development, caused by a problem rather than the process: suspend, and expense the interest."},{"t":"A concrete pour is left to cure for ten days as planned","why":"Curing is a necessary part of construction: no suspension."}],"id":"mcq-wq1d4b","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"recognise","lo":44,"special":"Here \"recognise\" means **when interest may enter the {a|asset’s cost}**: commence when spending, borrowing costs and work are all under way; suspend for extended halts only.","id":"compare-1kumyt-2","sect":2,"part":"Recognise"},{"type":"map","id":"map-3-initial","bucket":"initial","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"think-1rch9e","type":"think","bucket":"initial","lo":44,"scenario":"A company borrows $5,000,000 at 6% on 1 January, specifically to build a warehouse. Work runs all year. It spends the loan gradually, and parks the unspent part in a {a|fixed deposit}, which earns $40,000 over the year.","prompt":"The loan costs $300,000 in interest. Is that the right amount to add to the {a|warehouse}?","answer":["Not quite. The {i|deposit income} came from the **same** borrowed money while it was waiting to be spent. The real net cost of financing the warehouse is 300,000 − 40,000 = **260,000**.","So for a **specific** loan: capitalise the actual borrowing costs, **less** {i|investment income} on any temporarily invested funds.","Think of it as interest with a small rebate: you only capitalise what the money really cost you."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","lo":44,"title":"Specific borrowings","body":["We have the window; now the amount. The easy case first: a loan taken out just for this asset, where the only twist is the interest earned on money not yet spent.","When funds are borrowed **specifically** for a qualifying asset:","> {a|Capitalised} = actual borrowing costs in the period − {i|investment income} on temporary investment of those funds","- Count only the borrowing costs inside the capitalisation window (commence to cease, less suspensions).","- Use the **effective interest**: issue costs and any discount or premium are part of the borrowing cost.","! Forgetting to deduct the {i|investment income} is the classic slip."],"id":"rule-1n4y10","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"initial","lo":44,"title":"Specific loan: what reaches the asset","intro":"The notes’ warehouse example as a build-up. The interest flows in, the {i|deposit income} flows back out, and the net lands in the {a|asset}.","notes":{"loan":"Borrowed specifically for this warehouse, so its own interest is used (no pooled rate).","int":"5,000,000 × 6% = 300,000 for the year, all inside the capitalisation window.","inc":"{i|Income} earned by investing the borrowed money until it is spent. It reduces the amount capitalised.","net":"300,000 − {i|40,000} = {a|260,000}.","asset":"Added to the {a|cost of the warehouse}, and depreciated with it once it is in use."},"walk":[{"nodes":["loan","int"],"text":"The loan’s actual interest for the period: 300,000."},{"nodes":["inc","net"],"text":"Deduct what the idle borrowed {a|cash} earned: {i|40,000}."},{"nodes":["net","asset"],"text":"The net 260,000 is capitalised into the {a|warehouse}."}],"caption":"Specific loan: actual cost less investment income.","id":"diagram-bqcnjw","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:640px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 588.00 117.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 106.62)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-106.62 576.92,-106.62 576.92,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>loan\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M84.03,-95.82C84.03,-95.82 12,-95.82 12,-95