l\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M323.78,-36C323.78,-36 244.25,-36 244.25,-36 238.25,-36 232.25,-30 232.25,-24 232.25,-24 232.25,-12 232.25,-12 232.25,-6 238.25,0 244.25,0 244.25,0 323.78,0 323.78,0 329.78,0 335.78,-6 335.78,-12 335.78,-12 335.78,-24 335.78,-24 335.78,-30 329.78,-36 323.78,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"240.89\" y=\"-19.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Interest expense\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"264.51\" y=\"-8.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">216,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge9\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>capchk&#45;&gt;pl\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b45309\" d=\"M245.52,-77.72C252.82,-66.59 261.36,-53.56 268.53,-42.61\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#b45309\" stroke=\"#b45309\" points=\"270.22,-43.87 271.75,-37.7 266.71,-41.57 270.22,-43.87\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"282.56\" y=\"-50.75\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> the rest \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"worked-29tdty","type":"worked","bucket":"initial","lo":44,"title":"Worked example: general borrowings","q":"General {l|loans}: $2,000,000 at 6% and $3,000,000 at 8%, outstanding all year. Spend on a qualifying plant: $1,000,000 on 1 January and $2,000,000 on 1 July. Work runs all year. Interest was debited to {x|Interest expense} as it was paid.","intro":["A typical exam question: a pool of general {l|loans}, spending on a qualifying asset on different dates, and \"how much interest is capitalised?\". Remember two things:","- The **capitalisation rate** is the pool’s total {x|borrowing costs} ÷ the {l|general borrowings} outstanding, so bigger {l|loans} weigh more. Never a simple average of the rates.","- Spending counts only for the months it is tied up in the {a|asset}: weight each amount by time.","Then follow these steps: rate → weighted spend → multiply → cap check → entry."],"steps":[{"h":"Calculate the capitalisation rate.","t":["{x|Borrowing costs} = 2,000,000 × 6% + 3,000,000 × 8% = 120,000 + 240,000 = **{x|360,000}**","Rate = {x|360,000} ÷ {l|5,000,000} = **7.2%**"]},{"h":"Weight the spending by time.","t":"{a|Weighted spend} = 1,000,000 × 12/12 + 2,000,000 × 6/12 = **{a|2,000,000}**"},{"h":"Multiply the rate by the weighted spend.","t":"{a|2,000,000} × 7.2% = **{a|144,000}**"},{"h":"Check the cap.","t":"{a|144,000} is below the {x|360,000} actually incurred, so all of it is capitalised."},{"h":"Move it out of P&L into the asset.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Asset under construction","amount":144000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Interest expense","amount":144000,"el":"x"}],"after":"The other {x|216,000} stays in profit or loss as {x|Interest expense}. The plant didn’t cause it, so the plant doesn’t carry it."}],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"calc-kfjt0s","type":"calc","bucket":"initial","lo":44,"given":2,"q":"Your turn, with the first two steps done. General {l|loans}: $4,000,000 at 5% and $6,000,000 at 7%, all year. Spend on a qualifying asset: $3,000,000 on 1 January and $4,000,000 on 1 April. How much is capitalised?","answer":372000,"unit":"$","working":["Rate = (200,000 + 420,000) ÷ 10,000,000 = 6.2%.","Weighted spend = 3,000,000 × 12/12 + 4,000,000 × 9/12 = 6,000,000.","Capitalised = 6,000,000 × 6.2% = **$372,000** (below the actual 620,000, so no cap)."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"calc-re6hgl","type":"calc","bucket":"initial","lo":44,"q":"Now on your own, with a twist. General {l|loans}: $6,000,000 at 6% outstanding all year, and $4,000,000 at 9% drawn on 1 July. Spend on a qualifying asset: $2,000,000 on 1 January and $2,400,000 on 1 October. How much is capitalised for the year?","answer":175500,"unit":"$","working":["Borrowing costs: 6,000,000 × 6% = 360,000; 4,000,000 × 9% × 6/12 = 180,000. Total 540,000.","Average loans outstanding: 6,000,000 + 4,000,000 × 6/12 = 8,000,000. Rate = 540,000 ÷ 8,000,000 = 6.75%.","Weighted spend: 2,000,000 + 2,400,000 × 3/12 = 2,600,000.","Capitalised = 2,600,000 × 6.75% = **$175,500** (below 540,000, so no cap).","The second loan was only around for half the year, so both its cost and its {l|balance} count for half."],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"id":"mcq-yg480r","type":"mcq","bucket":"initial","lo":44,"q":"General {l|loans}: $2,000,000 at 5% and $1,000,000 at 8%, all year. Part of a qualifying asset is funded with {e|equity}, and the time-weighted expenditure on it is $3,500,000. How much can be capitalised?","options":[{"t":"$210,000","why":"6% × 3,500,000 = 210,000, but that is more than the 180,000 actually incurred. Apply the cap."},{"t":"$180,000","ok":true,"why":"Rate 6% × 3,500,000 = 210,000, which exceeds the actual borrowing costs of 180,000. You can’t capitalise interest you never paid: 180,000."},{"t":"$227,500","why":"A simple average of the two rates, and no cap either."},{"t":"Nil, because part of the asset is funded with {e|equity}","why":"{e|Equity} funding doesn’t stop the borrowed part being capitalised. {e|Equity} just has no \"borrowing cost\" of its own."}],"sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"initial","lo":44,"title":"Specific vs general, side by side","body":["Two methods, easy to mix up in the exam. Here they are next to each other, plus what happens when a project uses both.","- **Specific {l|loan}:** its own actual cost in the window, **minus** {i|investment income} on the unspent funds. No rate to compute.","- **General pool:** **weighted** capitalisation rate × **time-weighted** spending, capped at the pool’s actual cost. No deduction for {i|investment income}.","- Spending more than the specific {l|loan}? The excess is funded from the {l|general pool}, so that part gets the general-borrowings treatment.","- If a specific {l|loan} is still outstanding after its asset is ready, it joins the {l|general pool} from then on."],"id":"rule-1rpzc3","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"visual","bucket":"initial","lo":44,"title":"Where the interest goes","html":"\u003cp>Three bars, three situations from this section. Each splits the interest into the layer that joins the \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">asset\u003c/span> and the layer that doesn’t: \u003cspan class=\"el el-i\">deposit income\u003c/span> netted off a specific loan, the part of a general pool the asset didn’t use, and anything above the cap.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"cbars\">\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-key\">\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg a\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">into the asset\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg out-i\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-i\">netted off: deposit income\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg x\">\u003c/i>\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">stays in P&amp;L\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan>\u003ci class=\"seg out\">\u003c/i>over the cap\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">\u003cstrong>Specific loan:\u003c/strong> \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">interest\u003c/span> 300,000 − \u003cspan class=\"el el-i\">deposit income\u003c/span> 40,000 = \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">260,000\u003c/span>\u003c/strong>\u003c/div>\n      \u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003ci class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:86.67%\" title=\"Capitalised 260,000\">\u003c/i>\u003ci class=\"seg out-i\" style=\"width:13.33%\" title=\"Deposit income 40,000\">\u003c/i>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">\u003cstrong>General pool:\u003c/strong> \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">pool cost\u003c/span> 360,000 = \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">144,000\u003c/span>\u003c/strong> (rate × weighted spend) + \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">216,000\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\n      \u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003ci class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:40.00%\" title=\"Capitalised 144,000\">\u003c/i>\u003ci class=\"seg x\" style=\"width:60.00%\" title=\"Interest expense 216,000\">\u003c/i>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"cb-year\">\u003cdiv class=\"cb-cap\">\u003cstrong>Cap hit:\u003c/strong> rate × spend 210,000, but only \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">180,000\u003c/span> was incurred → \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">180,000\u003c/span>\u003c/strong>\u003c/div>\n      \u003cdiv class=\"cb-track\">\u003ci class=\"seg a\" style=\"width:85.71%\" title=\"Capitalised 180,000\">\u003c/i>\u003ci class=\"seg out\" style=\"width:14.29%\" title=\"Over the cap 30,000\">\u003c/i>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n  \u003c/div>\n  \u003cp class=\"cap\">Each bar is scaled to its own total. Specific: the whole loan’s interest is the asset’s, less what the idle cash earned. General: only the asset’s share of the pool. Cap: never more than was actually paid.\u003c/p>","id":"visual-13vdyh","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"compare","bucket":"initial","lo":44,"special":"This chapter decides **how much interest** joins a qualifying {a|asset’s cost}: specific {l|loan} = actual cost less {i|investment income}; {l|general pool} = weighted rate × time-weighted spend, capped at actual cost.","id":"compare-1kumyt-3","sect":3,"part":"Initial measurement"},{"type":"map","id":"map-4-derecognise","bucket":"derecognise","sect":4,"part":"Derecognise / transfer"},{"id":"think-aufnox","type":"think","bucket":"derecognise","lo":44,"scenario":"Harbourfront Mall Pte Ltd builds a shopping mall with {l|general borrowings}. Construction is substantially complete on 30 September 20X3; a few snag-list items (touch-up paint, a sticky door) are fixed in October, tenants do their own fit-outs in November, and the mall opens on 1 December.","prompt":"When should Harbourfront stop capitalising interest: 30 September, when the snags are fixed, or the grand opening?","answer":["**30 September.** Capitalisation ceases when **substantially all** the activities needed to get the asset ready for its intended use or sale are complete.","Minor fixes and tenants’ fit-outs don’t keep the window open, and nor does waiting for the ribbon-cutting. The mall is ready; the party is optional."],"sect":4,"part":"Derecognise / transfer"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"derecognise","lo":44,"title":"Cessation: substantially complete","body":["Commencement opened the window and suspension paused it. This rule closes it, so that interest after the asset is ready is treated like any other finance cost.","Capitalisation **ceases** when substantially all the activities necessary to prepare the asset for its intended use or sale are complete.","- Minor modifications or routine administrative work still to do don’t delay it.","- It is about **ready**, not about **in use**: an asset waiting to be used has still ceased.","- If the asset is built in **parts** and each part can be used while the others are still being built, cease for **each part** as it is completed.","- If the parts only work together (a plant with linked processes), cease when the **whole** is complete.","After cessation:","1. new borrowing costs go to {x|Interest expense}","2. the interest already capitalised stays in the {a|asset’s cost} and is **depreciated with it**","! Capitalising until the asset is first used (or the {l|loan} is repaid) is the classic over-run."],"id":"rule-jm9ano","sect":4,"part":"Derecognise / transfer"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"derecognise","lo":44,"title":"One switch-off, or one per part?","intro":"When an asset is built in stages, ask whether each finished part can earn its keep on its own.","notes":{"done":"Substantially complete means ready for its intended use or sale. Snags and touch-ups don’t count.","keep":"Still inside the window: capitalise, unless there is an extended halt.","parts":"Can a finished part be used while construction continues on the others?","each":"Each office block in a business park can be let as soon as it is finished, so its capitalisation stops then.","whole":"A steel mill’s furnaces and rolling lines only work together, so capitalisation runs until the whole mill is ready.","after":"Interest from now on is an {x|expense}. The capitalised interest is part of {a|cost}, depreciated over the asset’s useful life."},"walk":[{"nodes":["done","keep"],"text":"Not yet substantially complete? Keep capitalising."},{"nodes":["parts","each"],"text":"Parts usable one at a time: switch off capitalisation for each part as it is finished."},{"nodes":["parts","whole"],"text":"Parts that only work together: switch off once, when the whole is finished."},{"nodes":["after"],"text":"Afterwards: interest is expensed, and the capitalised interest is depreciated with the {a|asset}."}],"caption":"Ready, not in use, ends the window.","id":"diagram-m3yyjy","sect":4,"part":"Derecognise / transfer","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:472px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 429.00 352.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 341.14)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-341.14 418.22,-341.14 418.22,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>done\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" points=\"129.03,-330.34 0,-299.7 129.03,-269.06 258.06,-299.7 129.03,-330.34\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"73.15\" y=\"-301.25\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Substantially all work\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"104.28\" y=\"-290.25\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\