receives $7,500 {a|cash}. It pays $300 to lawyers and the share registrar to do it.","prompt":"Is the $7,500 {i|income}? And is the $300 an {x|expense}?","answer":["Neither. {a|Cash} from owners buying shares is a **contribution**, not something the company earned. It goes straight to {e|equity}.","The 300 is a cost of that equity transaction, so it is **deducted from {e|equity}** too (net of any tax benefit), not expensed.","The company doesn’t get richer by selling bits of itself to its owners, and it doesn’t get poorer in P&L by paying the lawyers for it."],"sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"owners","lo":45,"title":"Share issues and issue costs","body":["First owner transaction: selling new shares. The think page said the {a|cash} is not {i|income} and the fees are not an {x|expense}; this rule says where each one goes instead.","- **Singapore shares have no par value** (Companies Act). All the {a|money received} goes to {e|Share capital}. There is no {e|share premium} account.","- Exam questions may still give a **par value** (practice Q13 uses $0.50). Then par goes to {e|Share capital} and the excess to {e|Share premium}.","- Either way, **{e|total equity} rises by the full {a|cash} received**.","- **Share issue costs** (legal, underwriting, registration) are **deducted from {e|equity}**, net of tax. Not an {x|expense}.","> Dr {a|Cash} / Cr {e|Share capital} (and {e|Share premium} if there is a par value)","! Don’t record the issue at par value only. {e|Total equity} goes up by the **{a|cash} received**, not by the par value."],"id":"rule-11wrkh","sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"owners","lo":45,"title":"One share issue, two jurisdictions","intro":"The notes’ example as a picture. The {a|cash} is the same; only the labels inside {e|equity} change.","notes":{"cash":"10,000 shares at $0.75: {e|total equity} rises by 7,500 whatever the labels.","sg":"Singapore companies have no par value, so there is nothing to split.","par":"Some exam questions give a par value anyway. Then split the {a|cash}.","sc1":"All of the {a|proceeds} go to {e|share capital}.","sc2":"Only the par value goes to {e|share capital}…","sp":"…and the excess, 7,500 − 5,000 = 2,500, goes to {e|share premium}.","costs":"Legal, underwriting and registration costs of issuing the shares.","less":"A cost of an equity transaction reduces {e|equity} directly (net of tax).","pl":"Not an {x|expense}: the company is dealing with its owners, not trading."},"walk":[{"nodes":["cash"],"text":"Start with the {a|cash}: 7,500. {e|Total equity} rises by this amount, full stop."},{"nodes":["sg","sc1"],"text":"**Singapore:** no par value, so it all goes to {e|share capital}."},{"nodes":["par","sc2","sp"],"text":"**Par value in the question:** par to {e|share capital} (5,000), the rest to {e|share premium} (2,500)."},{"nodes":["costs","less","pl"],"text":"The 300 issue costs come **off {e|equity}**, never through P&L."}],"caption":"Same {a|cash}, same {e|total equity}; par value only changes how it is labelled.","id":"diagram-17018c","sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:516px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 469.00 297.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 285.8)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-285.8 458.14,-285.8 458.14,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>cash\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M79.53,-149.82C79.53,-149.82 12,-149.82 12,-149.82 6,-149.82 0,-143.82 0,-137.82 0,-137.82 0,-120.18 0,-120.18 0,-114.18 6,-108.18 12,-108.18 12,-108.18 79.53,-108.18 79.53,-108.18 85.53,-108.18 91.53,-114.18 91.53,-120.18 91.53,-120.18 91.53,-137.82 91.53,-137.82 91