equity}** (net of tax), never through P&L.","Then follow these steps: cash in → record the issue → deduct the costs → net effect."],"steps":[{"h":"Work out the cash received.","t":"{a|Cash received} = 200,000 × $1.5 = **{a|300,000}**. No par value, so all of it is {e|share capital}."},{"h":"Record the issue.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":300000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Share capital","amount":300000,"el":"e"}]},{"h":"Deduct the issue costs from equity.","t":"The {a|6,000 fees paid} are a cost of an equity transaction, so they come off {e|share capital}, not P&L.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Share capital","amount":6000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":6000,"el":"a"}]},{"h":"Find the net effect on equity.","t":"{e|Share capital} and {e|total equity} rise by {a|300,000} − {a|6,000} = **{e|294,000}**. Profit is untouched."}],"sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"id":"je-1mmnyy","type":"je","bucket":"owners","lo":45,"q":"In a jurisdiction with par values, a company issues 40,000 shares with a par value of $1 at $2.5 each, for {a|cash}. The cash line is filled in. Complete the entry.","given":[0],"accounts":["Cash","Share capital","Share premium","Treasury shares","Capital reserve","Retained earnings","Revaluation surplus (OCI)","FVOCI reserve","Dividend payable","Building (PPE)","Accumulated depreciation: building","Share issue expense","Gain on treasury shares","Revaluation gain (P&L)","Dividend expense","Gain on disposal of investment"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":100000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Share capital","amount":40000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Share premium","amount":60000}],"why":"40,000 × $2.5 = 100,000 {a|cash}. Par 40,000 × $1 = 40,000 to {e|share capital}; the excess 60,000 to {e|share premium}.","sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"id":"je-170k7b","type":"je","bucket":"owners","lo":45,"q":"A blank one. A company issues 80,000 shares with a par value of $0.5 at $1.25 each. It pays issue costs of $3,000 in {a|cash}, charged against {e|share premium}. Ignore tax. Record it as one entry, with the {a|cash} net of costs.","accounts":["Cash","Share capital","Share premium","Treasury shares","Capital reserve","Retained earnings","Revaluation surplus (OCI)","FVOCI reserve","Dividend payable","Building (PPE)","Accumulated depreciation: building","Share issue expense","Gain on treasury shares","Revaluation gain (P&L)","Dividend expense","Gain on disposal of investment"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":97000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Share capital","amount":40000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Share premium","amount":57000}],"why":"{a|Gross proceeds} 80,000 × $1.25 = 100,000. {e|Share capital} 80,000 × $0.5 = 40,000. {e|Share premium} 60,000 − costs 3,000 = 57,000. Net {a|cash} 97,000.","alt":"Two entries are equally right: Dr {a|Cash} 100,000 / Cr {e|Share capital} 40,000, Cr {e|Share premium} 60,000; then Dr {e|Share premium} 3,000 / Cr {a|Cash} 3,000.","sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"id":"think-38n3m0","type":"think","bucket":"owners","lo":45,"scenario":"A company buys back 5,000 of its own shares at $3 (paying $15,000) and holds them. Six months later it sells them again at $3.6, receiving $18,000.","prompt":"Is the $3,000 difference a trading {i|gain}? It certainly feels like one.","answer":["No. The company was dealing in **its own shares** with its owners. You can’t make a profit by trading with yourself: that’s a contribution from the new shareholders, not performance.","The 3,000 stays **inside {e|equity}** (e.g. a {e|Capital reserve}). Nothing goes to profit or loss.","While held, the bought-back shares are **{e|treasury shares}**: a deduction from {e|equity}, not an {a|asset}."],"sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"owners","lo":45,"title":"Treasury shares","body":["Now the reverse: the company buys its own shares back, and may sell them again later. This rule stops anyone booking a “profit” on trading with its own owners.","- {e|Treasury shares} = the entity’s own shares, bought back and held. Shown as a **deduction from {e|equity}**.","- **No {i|gain} or {x|loss}** goes to profit or loss on buying, selling, issuing or cancelling them.","1. Buy back: Dr {e|Treasury shares} / Cr {a|Cash}","2. Reissue above cost: Dr {a|Cash} / Cr {e|Treasury shares} (at cost), Cr {e|Capital reserve} (the excess)","Notes example: buy 1,000 at $2 (2,000), reissue at $2.5 (2,500) → 500 to {e|Capital reserve}. Not {i|income}.","! A “{i|gain on treasury shares}” in P&L is a classic wrong option. Dealings with owners never create {i|income} or {x|expenses}."],"id":"rule-2kcats","sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"id":"je-1ucjve","type":"je","bucket":"owners","lo":45,"q":"A company bought back 5,000 of its own shares at $3 each and holds them as {e|treasury shares}. It now reissues all 5,000 for $3.6 each in {a|cash}. Record the reissue.","accounts":["Cash","Share capital","Share premium","Treasury shares","Capital reserve","Retained earnings","Revaluation surplus (OCI)","FVOCI reserve","Dividend payable","Building (PPE)","Accumulated depreciation: building","Share issue expense","Gain on treasury shares","Revaluation gain (P&L)","Dividend expense","Gain on disposal of investment"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":18000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Treasury shares","amount":15000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Capital reserve","amount":3000}],"why":"{e|Treasury shares} come out at their cost, 15,000. The 3,000 excess stays in {e|equity}, not in P&L.","sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"id":"think-1t2ypn","type":"think","bucket":"owners","lo":45,"scenario":"Two dividends from the same company (year end 31 December), each $20,000 (400,000 shares × $0.05). Dividend A is declared on 15 December and paid on 20 January. Dividend B is declared on 20 February, before the financial statements are authorised.","prompt":"Which one, if either, is a {l|liability} at 31 December?","answer":["**Dividend A**: declared before the year end, so the company has an obligation. Dr {e|Retained earnings} / Cr {l|Dividend payable}.","**Dividend B**: declared after the reporting date. At 31 December there was no obligation, so **no {l|liability}**. It is disclosed in the notes (a non-adjusting event).","And neither is an **{x|expense}**: dividends are distributions to owners, so they come out of {e|retained earnings}, never P&L."],"sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"owners","lo":45,"title":"Dividends and bonus issues","body":["Next, value going back to the owners. Two questions matter: when does a dividend become a {l|liability}, and what does a free (bonus) share issue actually change?","- **Cash dividends** reduce {e|Retained earnings} when **declared** (appropriately authorised): Dr {e|Retained earnings} / Cr {l|Dividend payable}; then Dr {l|Dividend payable} / Cr {a|Cash} when paid.","- A dividend declared **after** the reporting date is **not a {l|liability}** at year end. Disclose the amount (and amount per share) in the notes.","- **Bonus issue** (free shares from reserves): moves an amount from {e|Retained earnings} or other {e|reserves} into {e|Share capital}. **No {a|cash}, no change in {e|total equity}.**","Example: a 1-for-4 bonus issue on 400,000 shares, capitalising $1 a share → 100,000 new shares, Dr {e|Retained earnings} 100,000 / Cr {e|Share capital} 100,000.","! A dividend is **never** an {x|expense} in P&L, and a dividend declared after year end is **never** a year-end {l|liability}."],"id":"rule-tuabmr","sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"id":"mcq-15z4a6","type":"mcq","bucket":"owners","lo":45,"q":"A company with 400,000 shares makes a 1-for-4 bonus issue, capitalising $1 a share from {e|retained earnings}. No {a|cash} changes hands. After the 100,000 new shares are issued, what happens to {e|total equity}?","options":[{"t":"It rises by $100,000","why":"No {a|cash} comes in. {e|Share capital} rises, but {e|retained earnings} fall by the same amount."},{"t":"It is unchanged","ok":true,"why":"Dr {e|Retained earnings} 100,000 / Cr {e|Share capital} 100,000: a move inside {e|equity}."},{"t":"It falls by $100,000","why":"Nothing leaves the company; this isn’t a dividend in cash."},{"t":"It rises by $100,000, with the same amount shown as an {x|expense}","why":"Bonus issues never go through P&L."}],"sect":2,"part":"Transactions with owners"},{"type":"map","id":"map-3-oci","bucket":"oci","sect":3,"part":"OCI: what goes there"},{"id":"think-wv9jll","type":"think","bucket":"oci","lo":45,"scenario":"A company revalues its head-office building, using the revaluation model for PPE. The building’s {a|carrying amount} was $640,000 and it is now valued at $880,000. The company has no plan to sell it; it will work in it for another 16 years.","prompt":"Is the $240,000 increase {i|income}? Would it be fair to report it in this year’s profit next to {i|sales} and {x|wages}?","answer":["It is a real increase in value, so it is {i|income} in a broad sense. But it is **unrealised** and has nothing to do with this year’s trading. Putting it in profit would make profit swing with the property market.","So the standard sends it to **other comprehensive income** and builds it up in a {e|Revaluation surplus (OCI)} in equity. {i|Income}, yes, but kept in the waiting room, away from profit."],"sect":3,"part":"OCI: what goes there"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"oci","lo":45,"title":"Other comprehensive income","body":["That is the owners done; now performance. The head-office gain showed that some {i|income} is kept out of profit. This rule names that holding area and lists what goes into it.","**OCI** = {i|income} and {x|expenses} that standards say go **outside** profit or loss.","> Profit or loss + OCI = **total comprehensive income**","OCI items in this syllabus:","- **{e|Revaluation surplus}** on {a|PPE} and {a|intangibles} (SFRS(I) 1-16)","- Fair value changes on **{a|equity investments} elected at FVOCI**","- Fair value changes on **{a|debt instruments} at FVOCI**","- Change in **own credit risk** on {l|liabilities} designated at FVTPL","+ **Profit or loss is the default.** An item only goes to OCI when a standard says so."],"id":"rule-qqtq64","sect":3,"part":"OCI: what goes there"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"oci","lo":45,"title":"Same $10,000 rise, five different homes","intro":"The same {i|gain} lands in a different place depending on what the {a|asset} is and which standard covers it. This is the picture to hold in your head.","notes":{"g":"One number, five possible homes. The asset and its standard decide.","ip":"{a|Investment property} under the fair value model: {i|gains} go to **P&L**, no {x|depreciation}.","tpl":"{a|Shares held for trading} are at fair value through profit or loss: **P&L**.","ppe":"{a|PPE} (or an {a|intangible}) under the revaluation model: an increase goes to the **{e|revaluation surplus}** in OCI.","fe":"An irrevocable election at initial recognition. {i|Gains} and {x|losses} stay in OCI for good.","fd":"A {a|debt instrument} at FVOCI: fair value changes in OCI, then **recycled** to P&L when sold.","pl":"The default home for {i|income} and {x|expenses}.","on":"Never reclassified to profit or loss. May be transferred to {e|retained earnings} within equity.","oy":"Reclassified (recycled) to profit or loss on derecognition."},"walk":[{"nodes":["g"],"text":"Same $10,000 increase in value. Where does it go?"},{"nodes":["ip","tpl","pl"],"text":"{a|Investment property} (fair value model) and {a|trading shares} → **profit or loss**."},{"nodes":["ppe","fe","on"],"text":"Revalued {a|PPE} and FVOCI {a|equity investments} → **OCI, never recycled**."},{"nodes":["fd","oy"],"text":"FVOCI {a|debt} → **OCI, recycled** to P&L when the {a|bond} is sold."}],"caption":"{a|Investment property} is the odd one out among property: its {i|gains} go to P&L, not OCI.","id":"diagram-hl351n","sect":3,"part":"OCI: what goes there","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:516px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 469.00 266.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 254.8)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-254.8 458.64,-254.8 458.64,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>g\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M78.78,-140C78.78,-140 12,-140 12,-140 6,-140 0,-134 0,-128 0,-128 0,-116 0,-116 0,-110 6,-104 12,-104 12,-104 78.78,-104 78.78,-104 84.78,-104 90.78,-110 90.78,-116 90.78,-116 90.78,-128 90.78,-128 90.78,-134 84.78,-140 78.78,-140\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"8.64\" y=\"-123.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Value goes up\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"18.76\" y=\"-112.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">by $10,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>ip\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M237.81,-244C237.81,-244 138.78,-244 138.78,-244 132.78,-244 126.78,-238 126.78,-232 126.78,-232 126.78,-220 126.78,-220 126.78,-214 132.78,-208 138.78,-208 138.78,-208 237.81,-208 237.81,-208 243.81,-208 249.81,-214 249.81,-220 249.81,-220 249.81,-232 249.81,-232 249.81,-238 243.81,-244 237.81,-244\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"135.42\" y=\"-226.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Investment property\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"155.67\" y=\"-217.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-si