\n\n\u003cg id=\"node5\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>close\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M60.41,-36C60.41,-36 26.62,-36 26.62,-36 20.62,-36 14.62,-30 14.62,-24 14.62,-24 14.62,-12 14.62,-12 14.62,-6 20.62,0 26.62,0 26.62,0 60.41,0 60.41,0 66.41,0 72.41,-6 72.41,-12 72.41,-12 72.41,-24 72.41,-24 72.41,-30 66.41,-36 60.41,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"23.27\" y=\"-19.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Closing\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"34.52\" y=\"-8.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">620\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge4\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>xf&#45;&gt;close\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M43.52,-57.58C43.52,-53.15 43.52,-48.41 43.52,-43.83\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"45.62,-43.85 43.52,-37.85 41.42,-43.85 45.62,-43.85\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"calc-167fd1","type":"calc","bucket":"soce","lo":45,"q":"Tiong Bahru Bakeries Pte Ltd (no par value) had {e|total equity} of $1,250,000 at 1 January. For the year: profit $210,000; revaluation gain in OCI $60,000; issued 50,000 shares at $1.8, paying issue costs of $4,000; paid dividends of $75,000; transferred $12,000 excess depreciation from the {e|revaluation surplus} to {e|retained earnings}; and on 20 February next year declared a dividend of $40,000. What is {e|total equity} at 31 December? (Ignore tax.)","answer":1531000,"unit":"$","working":["TCI = 210,000 + 60,000 = 270,000.","Share issue net of costs = 50,000 × 1.8 − 4,000 = 86,000.","{e|Closing} = 1,250,000 + 270,000 + 86,000 − 75,000 = **$1,531,000**.","Ignore the 12,000 transfer (between reserves) and the 40,000 dividend declared after year end (not a {l|liability})."],"sect":5,"part":"Statement of changes in equity"},{"id":"calc-18nnn7","type":"calc","bucket":"soce","lo":45,"q":"Tiong Bahru Bakeries Pte Ltd had {e|retained earnings} of $600,000 at 1 January. For the year: profit $210,000; revaluation gain in OCI $60,000; issued shares for $90,000; paid dividends of $75,000; transferred $12,000 excess depreciation from the {e|revaluation surplus} to {e|retained earnings}; and on 20 February next year declared a dividend of $40,000. What are {e|retained earnings} at 31 December?","answer":747000,"unit":"$","working":["{e|Opening} 600,000 + profit 210,000 − dividends paid 75,000 + transfer in 12,000 = **$747,000**.","This time the transfer **does** count: it changes the {e|retained earnings} column, even though it doesn’t change the total.","Ignore the OCI gain (it sits in the {e|revaluation surplus}), the share issue ({e|share capital}) and the 40,000 dividend declared after year end."],"sect":5,"part":"Statement of changes in equity"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"soce","lo":45,"title":"Disclosures","body":["Beyond the SOCE numbers, the notes must explain the {e|share capital} and {e|reserves}. Plus one classic trap: shares that are really debt.","**For each class of share capital:**","- number of shares issued and fully paid; par value or no par","- a reconciliation of shares outstanding at the start and end of the year","- rights, preferences and restrictions; {e|treasury shares} held","**Also:**","- a **description of each {e|reserve}** in equity and what it is for","- **dividends** proposed or declared before the FS are authorised but **not recognised**: the amount and the amount per share","! **Preference shares** that must be redeemed, or are redeemable at the holder’s option, are usually **{l|liabilities}**, not {e|equity}. Check the terms, not the name."],"id":"rule-1iw2q2","sect":5,"part":"Statement of changes in equity"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"soce","lo":45,"q":"A company issues preference shares that the holders can require it to redeem for cash in five years. How are they presented?","options":[{"t":"In {e|equity}, as {e|share capital}, because they are called shares","why":"The name doesn’t decide it. An obligation to pay cash makes them a {l|liability}."},{"t":"As a {l|financial liability}","ok":true,"why":"The company cannot avoid paying cash if the holders demand it, so they are a {l|liability}."},{"t":"As {e|treasury shares}, deducted from {e|equity}","why":"{e|Treasury shares} are the company’s own shares bought back, not shares issued to others."},{"t":"In OCI until they are redeemed","why":"OCI is for certain income and expenses, not for instruments."}],"id":"mcq-17k34e","sect":5,"part":"Statement of changes in equity"},{"type":"examtypes","id":"examtypes","bucket":"wrap","sect":6,"part":"Wrap-up"},{"type":"summary","lo":45,"title":"Equity and OCI on one page","rows":[["What changes {e|total equity}?","Total comprehensive income and transactions with owners only. Reserve transfers change no totals."],["Share issue in Singapore?","No par value: all {a|proceeds} to {e|Share capital}. Par value in a question: excess to {e|Share premium}."],["Share issue costs?","Deducted from {e|equity} (net of tax), not expensed."],["{e|Treasury shares}?","Deduction from {e|equity}; no {i|gain} or {x|loss} in P&L."],["Dividend declared after year end?","Not a {l|liability}; disclose in the notes."],["Bonus issue?","{e|Reserves} → {e|share capital}. {e|Total equity} unchanged."],["{e|Revaluation surplus}?","OCI, accumulated in {e|equity}, never recycled; may be transferred to {e|retained earnings}."],["Recycled OCI in this syllabus?","FVOCI {a|debt instruments}, on derecognition."],["Excess depreciation transfer?","{e|Revaluation surplus} → {e|retained earnings}, directly within equity."],["SOCE shows?","Per component: profit, each OCI item, TCI, owner transactions, transfers, restatements."]],"id":"summary-rly0ke","bucket":"wrap","sect":6,"part":"Wrap-up"}],"quiz":{"cases":{"sigma":{"name":"Sigma Intelligence","source":"Official practice paper (Q26–30 case, revaluation paragraphs only)","text":["Sigma Intelligence Pte Ltd (Sigma) is a fast-growing Singapore based AI software company that uses machine learning and natural language processing to develop software solutions that help logistics companies to optimise their supply chain operations.","Sigma completed the construction of the new headquarters, and it was fully operational by the financial year ending 31 December 20X3. Sigma uses the revaluation model to value Property Plant and Equipment and depreciation is applied on a straight-line basis.","There was no significant change in the fair values of the headquarters until end of 20X6. On 31 December 20X6, Sigma had the headquarters revalued at a fair value of $24 million."]},"kembangan":{"name":"Kembangan Holdings","text":["Kembangan Holdings Pte Ltd (Kembangan) is a Singapore company (no par value shares) with a 31 December 20X5 year end. Its financial statements are authorised for issue on 15 March 20X6. Ignore tax.","(a) 1 March 20X5: issued 100,000 ordinary shares at $2.4 each for {a|cash}, paying $6,000 of legal and underwriting fees.","(b) 1 June 20X5: bought back 20,000 of its own shares at $2.5 each and held them as {e|treasury shares}.","(c) 1 October 20X5: reissued 8,000 of the {e|treasury shares} at $2.9 each.","(d) 15 December 20X5: declared a final dividend of $0.04 per share on 500,000 shares, payable on 15 January 20X6.","(e) 20 February 20X6: declared a special dividend of $30,000."]}},"questions":[{"id":"q1","case":"sigma","lo":45,"type":"reval-where","source":"Official practice paper Q29","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-16, identify how should Sigma account for the revaluation surplus arising from the revaluation of the headquarters on 31 December 20X6?","options":[{"t":"The revaluation surplus should be recognised as income in the Statement of profit or loss","pit":"reval-to-pl","why":"A revaluation increase goes to OCI, not P&L (unless it reverses an earlier decrease that went to P&L, which isn’t the case here)."},{"t":"The revaluation surplus should be credited to retained earnings","pit":"reval-to-re","why":"It goes through OCI into the {e|revaluation surplus} first. Only later may it be transferred to {e|retained earnings}."},{"t":"The revaluation surplus should be treated as a deferred income until the asset is sold","why":"There is no {l|deferred income}: the {e|surplus} is {e|equity}, recognised now through OCI."},{"t":"The revaluation surplus should be credited to other comprehensive income and accumulated in equity","ok":true,"why":"A revaluation increase on PPE is recognised in OCI and built up in {e|equity} under the {e|revaluation surplus}."}]},{"id":"q2","case":"sigma","lo":45,"type":"recycle","q":"In which part of Sigma’s statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income for 20X6 does the {i|revaluation gain} appear?","options":[{"t":"In profit or loss, as {i|other income}","pit":"reval-to-pl","why":"Revaluation increases go to OCI."},{"t":"In OCI, under items that will not be reclassified to profit or loss","ok":true,"why":"The {e|revaluation surplus} is never recycled, so it sits in the “will not be reclassified” group."},{"t":"In OCI, under items that may be reclassified to profit or loss","pit":"recycle-reval","why":"Only items such as FVOCI {a|debt} can be recycled. The {e|revaluation surplus} never is."},{"t":"It does not appear; it goes directly to the statement of changes in equity","why":"It is an {i|income} item in OCI first; the SOCE then shows it within total comprehensive income."}]},{"id":"q3","case":"sigma","lo":45,"type":"reserve-transfer","q":"From 20X7 Sigma’s {x|depreciation} charge is higher because it is based on the revalued amount. How may Sigma deal with the {e|revaluation surplus} as the headquarters is used?","options":[{"t":"Release the excess depreciation from the {e|surplus} to profit or loss each year, to offset the higher charge","pit":"excess-dep-pl","why":"The transfer never passes through P&L."},{"t":"Transfer the excess depreciation each year from the {e|revaluation surplus} directly to {e|retained earnings}","ok":true,"why":"A transfer within equity, not through P&L or OCI. It is allowed, not required."},{"t":"Show the excess depreciation as a negative item in OCI each year","pit":"excess-dep-pl","why":"The transfer is not an OCI item; it moves between reserves."},{"t":"Nothing: the {e|surplus} must stay unchanged until the next revaluation","why":"A yearly transfer of the excess depreciation to {e|retained earnings} is allowed."}]},{"id":"q4","case":"sigma","lo":45,"type":"closing-equity","q":"What is the effect of the 31 December 20X6 revaluation on Sigma’s {e|total equity} and total comprehensive income for 20X6?","options":[{"t":"Both increase by the amount of the surplus","ok":true,"why":"The gain is OCI, so it is part of total comprehensive income, and TCI flows into {e|equity}."},{"t":"{e|Total equity} increases; total comprehensive income is unchanged","why":"OCI is part of total comprehensive income."},{"t":"Neither changes until the headquarters is sold","why":"The {e|surplus} is recognised at the revaluation date, not on sale."},{"t":"Total comprehensive income increases; {e|total equity} is unchanged because it is only a reserve","pit":"reserve-transfer-total","why":"A new {e|surplus} is not a transfer between {e|reserves}: it is new {i|income} (in OCI), so {e|equity} rises."}]},{"id":"q5","case":"sigma","lo":45,"type":"recycle","q":"If Sigma sold the headquarters in 20X9, what would happen to the balance remaining in the {e|revaluation surplus}?","options":[{"t":"It would be reclassified to profit or loss as part of the {i|gain on disposal}","pit":"recycle-reval","why":"The {e|revaluation surplus} is never recycled through P&L."},{"t":"It may be transferred directly to {e|retained earnings}","ok":true,"why":"Once the {a|asset} is derecognised the {e|surplus} is realised, and it may move to {e|retained earnings} within equity."},{"t":"It would be reversed against the {a|carrying amount} of the headquarters","why":"The {e|surplus} is {e|equity}; it is not netted off the {a|asset} on sale."},{"t":"It would be shown as a {x|loss} in OCI","why":"A transfer within equity is not an OCI item."}]},{"id":"q6","case":"kembangan","lo":45,"type":"owner-txn","q":"By how much does {e|share capital} increase as a result of transaction (a)?","options":[{"t":"$240,000, with the $6,000 fees expensed in profit or loss","pit":"expense-issue-costs","why":"Issue costs of an equity transaction are deducted from {e|equity}, not expensed."},{"t":"$234,000","ok":true,"why":"100,000 × $2.4 = 240,000, less costs 6,000 deducted from {e|equity} = 234,000."},{"t":"$100,000, with $140,000 to {e|share premium}","pit":"par-not-cash","why":"Singapore shares have no par value; there is no {e|share premium} account."},{"t":"$246,000","why":"Costs reduce {e|equity}; they are not added to it."}]},{"id":"q7","case":"kembangan","lo":45,"type":"owner-txn","q":"Which journal entry records transaction (c)?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":23200,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Treasury shares","amount":20000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Gain on treasury shares","amount":3200,"el":"i"}],"pit":"treasury-gain-pl","why":"The excess is not {i|income}. Dealings with owners never go through P&L."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":23200,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Treasury shares","amount":20000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Capital reserve","amount":3200,"el":"e"}],"ok":true,"why":"{e|Treasury shares} out at cost: 8,000 × $2.5 = 20,000. The 3,200 excess stays in {e|equity}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":23200,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Treasury shares","amount":23200,"el":"e"}],"why":"{e|Treasury shares} are removed at their cost, not at the reissue price."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":23200,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Share capital","amount":23200,"el":"e"}],"why":"The reissued shares come out of {e|treasury shares} at cost; the excess goes to a {e|reserve}."}]},{"id":"q8","case":"kembangan","lo":45,"type":"dividends","q":"Which journal entry records the dividend in (d) on 15 December 20X5?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Dividend expense","amount":20000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Dividend payable","amount":20000,"el":"l"}],"pit":"div-expense","why":"A dividend is a distribution to owners, not an {x|expense}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":20000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Dividend payable","amount":20000,"el":"l"}],"ok":true,"why":"500,000 × $0.04 = 20,000. Declared before year end, so it is a {l|liability} now and reduces {e|retained earnings}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":20000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":20000,"el":"a"}],"why":"It isn’t paid until 15 January 20X6; at 31 December it is a {l|payable}."},{"t":"No entry until the dividend is paid in January 20X6","why":"The obligation arises when the dividend is declared, on 15 December."}]},{"id":"q9","case":"kembangan","lo":45,"type":"dividends","q":"How should Kembangan treat the special dividend in (e) in its 20X5 financial statements?","options":[{"t":"Recognise a {l|liability} of $30,000 at 31 December 20X5","pit":"div-after-ye-liability","why":"There was no obligation at the reporting date."},{"t":"Recognise a {x|dividend expense} of $30,000 in 20X5 profit or loss","pit":"div-expense","why":"Dividends are never {x|expenses}, and this one wasn’t declared in 20X5."},{"t":"Do not recognise it; disclose the amount and the amount per share in the notes","ok":true,"why":"Declared after the reporting date: a non-adjusting event. Disclose, don’t recognise."},{"t":"Ignore it completely","why":"It must be disclosed, because the FS were not yet authorised when it was declared."}]},{"id":"q10","case":"kembangan","lo":45,"type":"closing-equity","q":"What is the net effect of (a) to (e) on Kembangan’s {e|total equity} at 31 December 20X5?","options":[{"t":"Increase of $157,200","pit":"div-after-ye-liability","why":"Deducts the special dividend, which isn’t recognised in 20X5."},{"t":"Increase of $187,200","ok":true,"why":"234,000 − 50,000 + 23,200 − 20,000 = 187,200."},{"t":"Increase of $217,200","pit":"treasury-asset","why":"Treats the buy-back as an {a|asset} purchase (no effect on equity) and the reissue excess as a {i|gain}. {e|Treasury shares} are a deduction from {e|equity}."},{"t":"Increase of $193,200","pit":"expense-issue-costs","why":"Ignores the issue costs, which reduce {e|equity} whether deducted directly or (wrongly) expensed."}]},{"id":"q11","lo":45,"type":"closing-equity","source":"Official practice paper Q13","q":"You are preparing the Statement of Changes in Equity for the year and must account for the following transactions for the year ended 31 July 20X7: • Profit after tax for the year was $192,500. • A dividend of $42,575 was paid. • A transfer of $45,000 was made from the revaluation surplus to retained earnings. • 10,000 ordinary shares of par value $0.50 each were issued at $0.75 for every share. Total equity balance on 1 August 20X6 was $356,850. Identify which of the following is the closing equity balance at 31 July 20X7?","options":[{"t":"$559,275","pit":"reserve-transfer-total","why":"Adds the 45,000 reserve transfer, which moves between two {e|reserves} and doesn’t change {e|total equity}."},{"t":"$514,275","ok":true,"why":"356,850 + 192,500 − 42,575 + 7,500 (10,000 × $0.75) = 514,275."},{"t":"$511,775","pit":"par-not-cash","why":"Records the share issue at par (5,000) instead of the {a|cash received} (7,500). The {e|premium} is {e|equity} too."},{"t":"$469,275","pit":"reserve-transfer-total","why":"Subtracts the 45,000 transfer. It leaves the {e|revaluation surplus} but arrives in {e|retained earnings}: net nil."}]},{"id":"q12","lo":45,"type":"owner-txn","q":"A company with 400,000 shares makes a 1-for-4 bonus issue, capitalising $100,000 of {e|retained earnings}. What is the effect on {e|total equity}?","options":[{"t":"Increase of $100,000","pit":"bonus-raises-equity","why":"No {a|cash} comes in; {e|retained earnings} fall as {e|share capital} rises."},{"t":"No change","ok":true,"why":"Dr {e|Retained earnings} 100,000 / Cr {e|Share capital} 100,000: a move within {e|equity}."},{"t":"Decrease of $100,000","why":"Nothing is paid out to shareholders."},{"t":"Decrease of $100,000 through profit or loss","why":"Bonus issues never touch P&L."}]},{"id":"q13","lo":45,"type":"recycle","q":"Which of these OCI items may be reclassified to profit or loss in a later period?","options":[{"t":"A {e|revaluation surplus} on {a|land and buildings}","pit":"recycle-reval","why":"Never recycled; it may only be transferred to {e|retained earnings}."},{"t":"Fair value gains on {a|equity investments} elected at FVOCI","pit":"recycle-reval","why":"The FVOCI election for equity means gains never reach P&L."},{"t":"Fair value gains on {a|debt instruments} measured at FVOCI","ok":true,"why":"Recycled to P&L when the instrument is derecognised."},{"t":"Changes in own credit risk on {l|liabilities} designated at FVTPL","why":"These stay in OCI and are never recycled."}]},{"id":"q14","lo":45,"type":"reserve-transfer","q":"A building with a {a|carrying amount} of $450,000 is revalued to $600,000. Its remaining useful life is 15 years; {x|depreciation} before the revaluation was $30,000 a year. The company transfers excess depreciation annually. What is the annual transfer and where does it go?","options":[{"t":"$10,000, from the {e|revaluation surplus} to profit or loss","pit":"excess-dep-pl","why":"The transfer never goes through P&L."},{"t":"$10,000, from the {e|revaluation surplus} directly to {e|retained earnings}","ok":true,"why":"New {x|depreciation} 600,000 ÷ 15 = 40,000; excess 40,000 − 30,000 = 10,000 (= 150,000 ÷ 15)."},{"t":"$40,000, from the {e|revaluation surplus} to {e|retained earnings}","why":"Only the excess over the old {x|depreciation} is transferred, not the whole charge."},{"t":"$10,000, as a reclassification adjustment in OCI","pit":"recycle-reval","why":"A transfer within equity is not a reclassification and doesn’t appear in OCI."}]},{"id":"q15","lo":45,"type":"disclosure","q":"Which of these must be disclosed for each class of share capital?","options":[{"t":"The market price of the shares at the reporting date","why":"Not required by the presentation standard."},{"t":"A reconciliation of the number of shares outstanding at the start and end of the year","ok":true,"why":"Required, together with shares issued, par value or no par, rights and restrictions, and treasury shares held."},{"t":"The names of all shareholders","why":"Not required in the financial statements."},{"t":"The dividend the directors expect to pay next year","why":"Only dividends proposed or declared before the FS are authorised are disclosed, not expectations."}]}]},"exam":{"cases":{"changi":{"name":"Changi Precision","text":["Changi Precision Ltd (Changi) is a Singapore company (no par value shares) with a 31 December 20X6 year end. Its tax rate is 17%. Its financial statements are authorised on 10 March 20X7.","At 1 January 20X6: {e|share capital} $2,000,000, {e|revaluation surplus} $300,000, {e|FVOCI reserve} (equity investments) $50,000, {e|retained earnings} $1,450,000. No {e|treasury shares}.","1. Profit after tax for 20X6 was $520,000.","2. On 31 December 20X6 Changi revalued its factory ({a|carrying amount} $1,150,000) to $1,400,000. {l|Deferred tax} at 17% is provided on the increase.","3. Changi transferred $20,000 of excess depreciation on an earlier revaluation from the {e|revaluation surplus} to {e|retained earnings}.","4. The fair value of its {a|equity investments} elected at FVOCI fell by $30,000 (ignore tax).","5. On 1 April 20X6 Changi issued 150,000 shares at $3.2 for {a|cash}, paying issue costs of $12,000 (ignore tax on these).","6. On 1 September 20X6 it bought back 40,000 of its own shares at $3.5 each and holds them as {e|treasury shares}.","7. It paid an interim dividend of $90,000 in August 20X6, and declared a final dividend of $120,000 on 25 February 20X7."]}},"questions":[{"id":"x1","case":"changi","lo":45,"type":"reval-where","q":"What is Changi’s total comprehensive income for 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$520,000","why":"That is profit only. TCI adds the OCI items: the revaluation (net of tax) and the FVOCI {x|loss}."},{"t":"$740,000","why":"Uses the gross revaluation of 250,000. The {l|deferred tax} of 42,500 on it is also in OCI."},{"t":"$697,500","ok":true,"why":"520,000 + (250,000 − tax 42,500 = 207,500) − 30,000 = 697,500."},{"t":"$717,500","pit":"reserve-transfer-total","why":"Adds the 20,000 reserve transfer, which is not {i|income} at all."}]},{"id":"x2","case":"changi","lo":45,"type":"reserve-transfer","q":"What is the balance on Changi’s {e|revaluation surplus} at 31 December 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$487,500","ok":true,"why":"{e|Opening} 300,000 + 207,500 (net of tax) − 20,000 transferred out = 487,500."},{"t":"$507,500","why":"Forgets the 20,000 transferred to {e|retained earnings}."},{"t":"$530,000","why":"Uses the gross increase; the 42,500 {l|deferred tax} reduces the {e|surplus}."},{"t":"$550,000","why":"Gross of tax and without the transfer out: two slips at once."}]},{"id":"x3","case":"changi","lo":45,"type":"dividends","q":"What is the balance on Changi’s {e|retained earnings} at 31 December 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$1,780,000","pit":"div-after-ye-liability","why":"Deducts the final dividend declared in February 20X7. It is not recognised at 31 December 20X6."},{"t":"$1,880,000","why":"Leaves out the 20,000 transfer in from the {e|revaluation surplus}. It doesn’t change {e|total equity}, but it does change this column."},{"t":"$1,760,000","why":"The buy-back goes to a separate {e|treasury shares} line in {e|equity}, not against {e|retained earnings}."},{"t":"$1,900,000","ok":true,"why":"{e|Opening} 1,450,000 + 520,000 − interim 90,000 + transfer 20,000 = 1,900,000."}]},{"id":"x4","case":"changi","lo":45,"type":"closing-equity","q":"What is Changi’s {e|total equity} at 31 December 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$4,755,500","pit":"reserve-transfer-total","why":"Counts the 20,000 reserve transfer, which nets to nil."},{"t":"$4,735,500","ok":true,"why":"{e|Opening} 3,800,000 + TCI 697,500 + share issue 468,000 (480,000 − 12,000) − buy-back 140,000 − interim dividend 90,000 = 4,735,500."},{"t":"$4,615,500","pit":"div-after-ye-liability","why":"Deducts the final dividend declared after year end."},{"t":"$4,875,500","pit":"treasury-asset","why":"Ignores the 140,000 buy-back, as if {e|treasury shares} were an {a|asset}. They are a deduction from {e|equity}."}]},{"id":"x5","case":"changi","lo":45,"type":"reval-where","q":"Which journal entry records the factory revaluation on 31 December 20X6 (in one entry, with the asset increased directly)?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Factory (PPE)","amount":250000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation gain (P&L)","amount":207500,"el":"i"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":42500,"el":"l"}],"pit":"reval-to-pl","why":"The revaluation increase goes to OCI, not P&L."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Factory (PPE)","amount":250000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Income tax expense","amount":42500,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":250000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":42500,"el":"l"}],"why":"Tax on an OCI item follows the item into OCI; it doesn’t go to the {x|tax expense} in P&L."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Factory (PPE)","amount":250000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","amount":207500,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":42500,"el":"l"}],"ok":true,"why":"Increase 250,000; {l|deferred tax} 17% = 42,500, charged in OCI. Net 207,500 to the {e|revaluation surplus}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Factory (PPE)","amount":250000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":250000,"el":"e"}],"pit":"reval-to-re","why":"Goes through OCI into the {e|revaluation surplus}, not straight into {e|retained earnings}, and ignores the {l|deferred tax}."}]},{"id":"x6","lo":45,"type":"owner-txn","q":"A company bought back 10,000 of its own shares at $4 each and held them as {e|treasury shares}. It later reissues all of them at $3.4 each. It has no {e|capital reserve} from earlier treasury share deals. Which entry records the reissue?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":34000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Loss on treasury shares","amount":6000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Treasury shares","amount":40000,"el":"e"}],"pit":"treasury-gain-pl","why":"A shortfall on {e|treasury shares} is not a {x|loss} in P&L, just as an excess is not a {i|gain}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":34000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Treasury shares","amount":34000,"el":"e"}],"why":"Leaves 6,000 stuck in {e|treasury shares}. They must come out at their full cost."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cash","amount":34000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":6000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Treasury shares","amount":40000,"el":"e"}],"ok":true,"why":"{e|Treasury shares} out at cost 40,000; {a|cash} 34,000; the 6,000 shortfall is absorbed within {e|equity}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Treasury shares","amount":34000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":34000,"el":"a"}],"why":"That is a buy-back entry, the wrong way round for a reissue."}]},{"id":"x7","lo":45,"type":"recycle","q":"A company sells a {a|bond} it measured at FVOCI. Cumulative fair value gains of $18,000 sit in its {e|FVOCI reserve}. Which entry reclassifies them?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"FVOCI reserve","amount":18000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":18000,"el":"e"}],"pit":"no-recycle-debt","why":"That is the treatment for FVOCI {a|equity investments}. {a|Debt} at FVOCI is recycled through P&L."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"FVOCI reserve","amount":18000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Gain on disposal of investment","amount":18000,"el":"i"}],"ok":true,"why":"On derecognition of a FVOCI debt instrument, the cumulative gain is reclassified from OCI to profit or loss."},{"t":"No entry: amounts in OCI stay there permanently","pit":"no-recycle-debt","why":"True for FVOCI equity and the {e|revaluation surplus}, but not for {a|debt}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Gain on disposal of investment","amount":18000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"FVOCI reserve","amount":18000,"el":"e"}],"why":"The wrong way round: the {e|reserve} is a credit balance and must be debited to release it."}]},{"id":"x8","lo":45,"type":"reserve-transfer","q":"On 1 January 20X1 a building with a {a|carrying amount} of $1,200,000 is revalued to $1,500,000. Remaining useful life 25 years, no residual value. The company transfers the excess depreciation each year. What is the {e|revaluation surplus} at 31 December 20X2? (Ignore tax.)","options":[{"t":"$300,000","why":"Ignores the yearly transfers."},{"t":"$288,000","why":"Only one year’s transfer. Two years have passed."},{"t":"$276,000","ok":true,"why":"{e|Surplus} 300,000; excess depreciation 300,000 ÷ 25 = 12,000 a year; 300,000 − 2 × 12,000 = 276,000."},{"t":"$180,000","pit":"excess-dep-pl","why":"Transfers the whole {x|depreciation charge} (60,000) each year instead of the excess."}]},{"id":"x9","lo":45,"type":"recycle","q":"Which statement about the presentation of other comprehensive income is correct?","options":[{"t":"OCI items are presented as one total, with no grouping","why":"They must be grouped: will not be reclassified, and may be reclassified."},{"t":"The yearly transfer of excess depreciation from the {e|revaluation surplus} is shown as a negative OCI item","pit":"excess-dep-pl","why":"It is a transfer within {e|equity}, shown in the SOCE, not in OCI."},{"t":"OCI items may be shown net of tax, or before tax with a separate tax amount for each group","ok":true,"why":"Either presentation is allowed; the tax must follow the OCI groups."},{"t":"Tax on OCI items is included in the {x|income tax expense} in profit or loss","why":"Tax on OCI items is presented in OCI with the items."}]},{"id":"x10","lo":45,"type":"owner-txn","q":"A company in a jurisdiction with par values issues 60,000 shares of par value $0.5 at $1.75 each. Issue costs of $2,500 are charged against {e|share premium}. Ignore tax. By how much does {e|share premium} increase?","options":[{"t":"$75,000","pit":"expense-issue-costs","why":"Forgets to charge the issue costs against {e|share premium}."},{"t":"$72,500","ok":true,"why":"60,000 × ($1.75 − $0.5) = 75,000, less costs 2,500 = 72,500."},{"t":"$102,500","why":"That is the increase in {e|total equity}, not in {e|share premium}."},{"t":"$30,000","pit":"par-not-cash","why":"That is the par value going to {e|share capital}."}]}]},"prepared":true},{"id":"fs-preparation","code":"D1","title":"Preparation of Financial Statements","subtitle":"SFRS(I) 1-1 · SFRS(I) 1-7 · the statements and cash flows","los":{"46":"Prepare a complete set of financial statements","47":"Three classifications of cash flows","48":"Statement of cash flows: direct and indirect methods"},"accounts":[{"name":"Salary expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Salary payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Rent expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Prepaid rent","el":"a"},{"name":"Deferred revenue","el":"l"},{"name":"Revenue","el":"i"},{"name":"Impairment loss on receivables","el":"x"},{"name":"Allowance for uncollectible accounts","el":"a"},{"name":"Interest expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Interest payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Share capital","el":"e"},{"name":"Tax payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Inventory","el":"a"},{"name":"Cost of sales","el":"x"},{"name":"Investment property","el":"a"},{"name":"Fair value gain on investment property","el":"i"},{"name":"Depreciation expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Accumulated depreciation","el":"a"},{"name":"Retained earnings","el":"e"},{"name":"Cash","el":"a"}],"steps":[{"id":"set","name":"The complete set","short":"The set"},{"id":"adjust","name":"Trial balance to adjusted figures","short":"Adjust TB"},{"id":"pl","name":"Profit or loss, OCI and equity","short":"P&L & equity"},{"id":"sfp","name":"Financial position: current or not","short":"SFP"},{"id":"cfclass","name":"Cash flows: which activity?","short":"Which flow?"},{"id":"cfop","name":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct","short":"Operating CF"},{"id":"cfinvfin","name":"Investing, financing and the whole statement","short":"Inv & fin"}],"map":{"set":{"label":"Five statements, comparatives, general features"},"adjust":{"label":"Post every adjustment before you answer","star":"The exam hands you a trial balance and a list of traps"},"pl":{"label":"By function or nature; OCI split; SOCE"},"sfp":{"label":"Current if any test says yes"},"cfclass":{"label":"Operating, investing or financing"},"cfop":{"label":"Profit → cash: indirect method","star":"Core LO at cognitive level 3: profit is not cash"},"cfinvfin":{"label":"Gross flows; non-cash items disclosed"}},"preamble":["Welcome to the chapter where everything you’ve learned so far gets put on the page. You take a trial balance, post the year-end adjustments using the rules from the earlier chapters, and lay the numbers out the way SFRS(I) 1-1 wants. Then you do it all again in cash.","1. First, the **complete set**: five statements, comparatives, and a few general features such as going concern and no offsetting.","2. Then the **trial balance**: accruals, prepayments, write-downs, {x|depreciation}, fair values. ★ The exam loves this: one case, five questions, one trap per line of additional information.","3. **Profit or loss, OCI and equity**: by function or by nature, the manufacturer’s {x|cost of sales}, the two OCI groups, and the statement of changes in equity.","4. **The statement of financial position**: current or non-current. {l|Bonds} due next March and breached covenants are the favourite traps.","5. **Cash flows**: which activity, then the operating section by the indirect method (★ the cognitive-level-3 skill), then investing, financing and the whole statement.","★ What is special: this chapter isn’t about one asset or one liability, so it has its own route instead of the six-step backbone. It is the most heavily examined chapter in the practice paper (12 questions out of 50), so take it slowly."],"intros":{"set":"First stop: what “the financial statements” actually means. There are five of them, each with last year’s figures beside it, plus a handful of ground rules (going concern, accruals, no offsetting) that apply to all of them. Mostly vocabulary, quick to learn, and it turns up as easy marks.","adjust":"Now we roll up our sleeves. ★ This is the special part of the first half: the exam hands you an unadjusted trial balance and a list of “additional information”, and every line hides one adjustment. We go through LCV’s list one entry at a time, and none of them involves cash. Fair warning: the {x|depreciation} is left for you in the quiz.","pl":"The adjusted numbers need a home. This step lays out profit or loss and OCI (by function or by nature), builds a manufacturer’s cost of sales in two layers, and sorts OCI into its two groups. Then the statement of changes in equity ties profit, OCI and the owners’ transactions together. Familiar territory, with a manufacturing twist.","sfp":"From performance to position. Every balance has to be called current or non-current, and the rule is simple: any one test says current, it’s current. The traps are all about timing: the right to defer settlement must exist *at the reporting date*, and nothing the bank agrees in January can rescue December’s balance sheet.","cfclass":"Second half: cash. Profit is an opinion, cash is a fact, and this statement shows where the cash came from and went. First we sort each flow into operating, investing or financing. Mostly common sense, with two classic traps: the direction of a loan, and the policy choices for interest and dividends.","cfop":"Now the heavy lifting. ★ The indirect method is the core skill of this chapter (cognitive level 3): start from profit, strip out what isn’t cash, fix the working capital, and deduct what was actually paid. Get the signs right ({a|assets} up, {a|cash} down) and the rest is arithmetic. The direct method gets a cameo, since it lands on the same number.","cfinvfin":"Last stop. Investing and financing are shown gross, cash only, which sounds easy until a number has to be backed out of a balance (how much {a|PPE} did they buy?) or a non-cash lease tries to sneak in. Then we put the whole statement together and make it agree with {a|cash} on the balance sheet."},"pitfalls":{"wd-out-cos":"Leaving the inventory write-down out of {x|cost of sales} (or out of {a|closing inventory}).","def-rev-wrong":"Moving {l|deferred revenue} the wrong way, or ignoring the part earned this year.","no-residual":"Ignoring residual value, or depreciating the {a|carrying amount} instead of cost less residual value.","stale-ca":"Using {a|carrying amounts} before this year’s {x|depreciation} or fair value remeasurement.","gross-receivables":"Using {a|receivables} gross of the {a|allowance}, or {a|financial assets} at cost instead of fair value.","missed-accrual":"Missing an accrual or correction ({x|bond interest}, {x|salaries}, a tax posting error, {l|deferred revenue}).","debt-noncurrent":"Leaving {l|debt} due within 12 months (or repayable on demand after a breach) in {l|non-current liabilities}.","post-ye-waiver":"Using a waiver or refinancing agreed after the reporting date to keep a {l|liability} non-current.","liquidation-basis":"Switching to a liquidation basis (or saying nothing) when there is only a material uncertainty.","cogm-as-cos":"Using cost of goods manufactured as {x|cost of sales}, or mishandling the {a|WIP} and {a|finished goods} adjustments.","oci-wrong":"Mixing up P&L, OCI that will be reclassified, and OCI that won’t; or the sign of an OCI loss.","oci-in-re":"Putting OCI into {e|retained earnings}, or running {e|dividends} through profit or loss.","wc-sign":"Getting working-capital signs the wrong way round ({a|assets} up = {a|cash} down; {l|liabilities} up = {a|cash} up).","gain-in-op":"Leaving a {i|gain} or {x|loss} on disposal in operating cash flow, or putting the {i|gain} or {a|carrying amount} in investing instead of the {a|proceeds}.","expense-not-paid":"Deducting the {x|interest or tax expense} instead of the {a|cash} actually paid.","dep-cash":"Treating {x|depreciation} as a cash flow.","loan-direction":"Classifying a {a|loan made to others} as financing, or a {l|loan received} as investing.","premium-missed":"Missing a redemption premium, or using par value instead of the issue price.","interest-policy":"Ignoring the entity’s stated policy for interest or dividends.","noncash-in-cf":"Putting a non-cash transaction (a new lease, {e|shares} issued for an {a|asset}) in the cash flow statement.","purpose-profit":"Saying the cash flow statement is for assessing profitability or gearing.","ce-equity":"Counting {a|equity investments} or {a|deposits} over 3 months as {a|cash equivalents}, or not deducting an {l|overdraft}."},"examTypes":[{"id":"complete-set","title":"Which is (not) part of a complete set? Which general feature applies?","pitfall":"Forgetting comparatives or the notes; adding a directors’ or management report","see":"set"},{"id":"going-concern","title":"Doubt about going concern: how is it reported?","pitfall":"Switching to a liquidation basis, or saying nothing","see":"set"},{"id":"tb-adjust","title":"From a trial balance: what gross profit or depreciation after adjustments?","pitfall":"Leaving the inventory write-down out of {x|cost of sales}; ignoring residual value","see":"adjust"},{"id":"adjust-je","title":"Which journal entry records this year-end adjustment?","pitfall":"Accruing the wrong number of months, or crediting {a|cash} for an {l|accrual}","see":"adjust"},{"id":"cogs-manuf","title":"Cost of goods manufactured and cost of sales for a manufacturer","pitfall":"Using COGM as {x|cost of sales}; putting {x|factory depreciation} in {x|admin}","see":"pl"},{"id":"oci-soce","title":"Total comprehensive income, OCI groups and equity movements","pitfall":"Putting OCI into {e|retained earnings} or {e|dividends} through P&L","see":"pl"},{"id":"cur-noncur","title":"Total current or non-current assets or liabilities?","pitfall":"Leaving a {l|bond} due within 12 months in {l|non-current liabilities}; using gross {a|receivables}","see":"sfp"},{"id":"cf-classify","title":"Which activity is this cash flow? What counts as cash?","pitfall":"{a|Loans made} are investing, {l|loans received} are financing; {x|depreciation} isn’t a cash flow","see":"cfclass"},{"id":"cf-purpose","title":"What is the statement of cash flows for?","pitfall":"Choosing profitability or gearing instead of liquidity and solvency","see":"cfclass"},{"id":"noncash","title":"Which is a non-cash transaction, and how is it shown?","pitfall":"Putting a new lease or a share-for-asset deal in investing","see":"cfinvfin"},{"id":"cf-indirect","title":"Net cash from operating activities (indirect method)?","pitfall":"Wrong working-capital signs; leaving in a {i|gain} or {x|loss} on disposal","see":"cfop"},{"id":"cf-direct","title":"Cash received from customers or paid to suppliers?","pitfall":"Adjusting for {a|receivables} or {a|inventory} the wrong way","see":"cfop"},{"id":"cf-invfin","title":"Net cash from investing or financing activities?","pitfall":"Missing a redemption premium; using par value; the wrong home for a loan","see":"cfinvfin"}],"pages":[{"type":"map","id":"map-overview","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"intro","lo":46,"title":"Preparing financial statements, the Socratic way","body":["Each idea starts with a question for you. Think it through, then reveal the answer. The rule comes after.","Examples use S$. Some practice-paper cases are in $’000; the question says so. Account names are coloured by element. Tap **Legend** any time.","This chapter was 12 of the 50 practice-paper questions: two whole case sets plus two standalones. They are all in the quizzes at the end."],"id":"intro-1xf64p","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"map","id":"map-1-set","bucket":"set","sect":1,"part":"The complete set"},{"type":"think","bucket":"set","lo":46,"scenario":"Sembawang Studio sends its bank a profit or loss statement and a balance sheet for 20X5, and nothing else. “That’s the accounts,” says the founder.","prompt":"What is missing from a complete set of financial statements? Think about what a lender would want to see.","answer":["Three statements and a habit are missing: the **statement of changes in equity**, the **statement of cash flows**, the **notes**, and **comparatives** for 20X4 for every number.","A bank especially wants the cash flow statement: profit can be opinion, cash is fact."],"id":"think-1r9pr1","sect":1,"part":"The complete set"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"set","lo":46,"title":"The complete set (SFRS(I) 1-1)","body":["So what counts as “the accounts”? SFRS(I) 1-1 lists the full kit, so no reader is left with half the story.","1. **Statement of financial position** at the end of the period.","2. **Statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income** for the period (one statement, or P&L followed by a statement starting from profit).","3. **Statement of changes in equity** for the period.","4. **Statement of cash flows** for the period (SFRS(I) 1-7).","5. **Notes**: significant accounting policies and other explanatory information.","+ Each with **comparatives** for the preceding period, including narrative where it helps.","- A **third** statement of financial position (at the start of the earlier period) is needed only after a retrospective policy change, restatement or reclassification."],"id":"rule-a3xp8r","sect":1,"part":"The complete set"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"set","lo":46,"title":"How the statements link","intro":"The chart from your notes (master.pdf). Profit flows into equity; cash flows explain the change in cash; the notes explain everything.","notes":{"pl":"Profit or loss for the year, then other comprehensive income. Together: total comprehensive income.","scf":"Operating, investing and financing cash flows. Their total is the change in {a|cash and cash equivalents} for the year.","soce":"{e|Opening equity} + profit + OCI + {e|share issues} − {e|dividends} = {e|closing equity}, for each component.","sfp":"{a|Assets} = {l|liabilities} + {e|equity} at the reporting date. {e|Closing equity} comes from the SOCE, {a|closing cash} from the cash flow statement.","notes":"Accounting policies, judgements, sources of estimation uncertainty, and the detail behind each line."},"walk":[{"nodes":["pl","soce"],"text":"Profit and OCI for the year feed the statement of changes in equity."},{"nodes":["soce","sfp"],"text":"{e|Closing equity} in the SOCE is the {e|equity} section of the statement of financial position."},{"nodes":["scf","sfp"],"text":"The cash flow statement explains how {a|opening cash} became {a|closing cash}, the number in the SFP."},{"nodes":["notes"],"text":"The notes back up every statement. They are part of the set, not an optional extra."}],"caption":"Three flows (P&L, SOCE, cash) all land in one position statement.","id":"diagram-1qqyk3","sect":1,"part":"The complete set","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:593px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 539.00 248.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 233.46)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c