 on strategy and market outlook","ok":true,"why":"Useful, and sometimes required elsewhere, but not part of the financial statements under SFRS(I) 1-1."},{"t":"Comparative information for the preceding period","why":"Comparatives are required for every amount."}],"id":"mcq-o78k9x","sect":1,"part":"The complete set"},{"id":"think-lp3u5r","type":"think","bucket":"set","lo":46,"scenario":"Tuas Tooling has lost its biggest customer. Its $5m {l|loan} falls due in 14 months and it has not yet found refinancing. Management fully intends to keep trading and is negotiating with two banks.","prompt":"Should Tuas still prepare its statements as a going concern? And should it say anything about the doubt?","answer":["**Yes, going concern**: management neither intends nor is forced to liquidate. A liquidation basis is only for when there is no realistic alternative.","But the doubt is a **material uncertainty**, so it must be **disclosed** in the notes. Silence is not an option, and neither is panic."],"sect":1,"part":"The complete set"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"set","lo":46,"title":"Eight general features","body":["The going concern answer was one of eight ground rules that apply to every set of statements, whatever the business. They decide the basis, the level of detail and what may be netted.","1. **Fair presentation:** faithfully represent transactions. Applying SFRS(I), with extra disclosure where needed, is presumed to achieve it.","2. **Going concern:** the default basis, unless management intends to liquidate or has no realistic alternative. Disclose **material uncertainties**.","3. **Accrual basis:** everything except the cash flow statement.","4. **Materiality and aggregation:** each material class separately; similar immaterial items may be combined.","5. **Offsetting:** no netting of {a|assets} against {l|liabilities}, or {i|income} against {x|expenses}, unless a standard requires or allows it.","6. **Frequency:** at least annually.","7. **Comparatives:** for every amount, and for narrative where relevant.","8. **Consistency:** same presentation and classification each year, unless a change is clearly better or required.","! Doubt about going concern means **disclose**, not switch to a liquidation basis."],"id":"rule-1d0egh","sect":1,"part":"The complete set"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"set","lo":46,"q":"Kallang Kitchens owes a supplier $40,000 and the same supplier owes Kallang $15,000 for returned goods. There is no legal right or standard that allows netting. How should they be presented?","options":[{"t":"{l|Trade payables} of $25,000 net","why":"Offsetting is not allowed unless a standard requires or permits it."},{"t":"{l|Trade payables} of $40,000 and {a|Trade receivables} of $15,000","ok":true,"why":"Present {a|assets} and {l|liabilities} gross. Netting would hide the size of both."},{"t":"Either, as long as the policy is disclosed","why":"There is no free choice here. The default is no offsetting."},{"t":"Only the $15,000 {a|receivable}; the {l|payable} goes in the notes","why":"Both are recognised in the statement of financial position."}],"id":"mcq-fsgivr","sect":1,"part":"The complete set"},{"type":"map","id":"map-2-adjust","bucket":"adjust","sect":2,"part":"Trial balance to adjusted figures"},{"id":"think-1axoeh","type":"think","bucket":"adjust","lo":46,"scenario":"Lion City Venture is a company in one of the practice-paper cases. Its trial balance at 31 December 20X5 is “prepared prior to adjustments”. The notes below it say, among other things: $500k of December salaries hasn’t been recorded, {a|prepaid rent} is now only $2.75m, and the {a|allowance for uncollectible accounts} should be $6.2m.","prompt":"Why can’t you just copy the trial balance into the statements? What do these notes have in common?","answer":["Each note is an amount that belongs to **this** period (or the next) but isn’t in the books yet. Copy the trial balance and every one of them lands in the wrong year.","What they have in common: **none of them involves cash**. They are accruals, deferrals and estimates, the accrual basis at work."],"sect":2,"part":"Trial balance to adjusted figures"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"adjust","lo":46,"title":"The usual year-end adjustments","body":["The trial balance is the raw material, not the finished product. Here is the checklist of adjustments that turn it into accrual-basis figures; most cases plant several of them.","1. **Accrued expense** (work done, not yet billed or recorded): Dr {x|expense}, Cr {l|Salary payable} or {l|accrual}.","2. **Prepayment used up:** Dr {x|expense}, Cr {a|Prepaid rent}.","3. **Deferred revenue earned:** Dr {l|Deferred revenue}, Cr {i|Revenue}.","4. **Allowance for doubtful debts increased:** Dr {x|Impairment loss on receivables}, Cr {a|Allowance for uncollectible accounts}.","5. **Inventory to the lower of cost and NRV:** under the periodic method, record {a|closing inventory} net of the write-down, so the write-down sits inside {x|Cost of sales}.","6. **{x|Depreciation and amortisation}** for the year, with residual values.","7. **Fair value remeasurements:** {a|investment property} (fair value model) and {a|FVTPL securities}, {i|gains} to P&L.","8. **{x|Interest} accrued** on {l|borrowings} since the last payment date.","9. **Errors** found before the statements are issued: correct them.","+ Tick each line of “additional information” as you use it. Examiners plant one trap per line."],"id":"rule-1dybid","sect":2,"part":"Trial balance to adjusted figures"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"adjust","lo":46,"title":"From trial balance to statements","intro":"The route every “prepare the statements” question follows. Tap a box for what happens there.","notes":{"tb":"The unadjusted trial balance. In Lion City Venture, debits and credits both total $347,250k.","adj":"Post each piece of additional information as a journal entry. None of them involves cash.","err":"An error found after year end but before the statements are issued is corrected. LCV posted $550k of {l|tax payable} to {e|share capital} by mistake.","pl":"{i|Income} and {x|expense} accounts become P&L lines: {i|revenue}, {x|cost of sales}, {x|operating expenses}, {x|finance costs}, {x|tax}.","bal":"{a|Asset}, {l|liability} and {e|equity} accounts become closing balances, after every adjustment.","cls":"Each balance is then sorted: current or non-current (step 4 of this chapter).","fs":"Lay it all out in the SFRS(I) 1-1 formats, with comparatives and notes."},"walk":[{"nodes":["tb"],"text":"Start from the trial balance. It balances, but it isn’t finished."},{"nodes":["adj","err"],"text":"Post every adjustment, and fix any error found before issue."},{"nodes":["pl","bal"],"text":"The adjusted accounts split into P&L lines and closing balances."},{"nodes":["cls","fs"],"text":"Classify the balances, then present the statements."}],"caption":"TB → adjust → classify → present.","id":"diagram-v9ya6r","sect":2,"part":"Trial balance to adjusted figures","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:370px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 336.00 321.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 306.48)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-306.48 321.75,-306.48 321.75,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>tb\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M118.98,-292.08C118.98,-292.08 43.38,-292.08 43.38,-292.08 37.38,-292.08 31.38,-286.08 31.38,-280.08 31.38,-280.08 31.38,-268.08 31.38,-268.08 31.38,-262.08 37.38,-256.08 43.38,-256.08 43.38,-256.08 118.98,-256.08 118.98,-256.08 124.98,-256.08 130.98,-262.08 130.98,-268.08 130.98,-268.08 130.98,-280.08 130.98,-280.08 130.98,-286.08 124.98,-292.08 118.98,-292.08\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"48.18\" y=\"-2