 On 10 February 20X6, before the 31 December 20X5 statements are authorised, the lender agrees to extend it to 20X9. How is it classified at 31 December 20X5?","options":[{"t":"Non-current, because the extension was agreed before the statements were authorised","why":"The right to defer must exist at the reporting date. The extension came later."},{"t":"Current","ok":true,"why":"At 31 December 20X5 it was due within 12 months and there was no right to defer. The refinancing is disclosed as a non-adjusting event."},{"t":"Split: half current, half non-current","why":"The whole {l|loan} was due within 12 months at the reporting date."},{"t":"Non-current, with a note on the original due date","why":"Classification follows the position at the reporting date."}],"sect":4,"part":"Financial position: current or not"},{"id":"visual-hk3y9d","type":"visual","bucket":"sfp","lo":46,"title":"Model statement of financial position","html":"\u003cp>The tests applied to a whole balance sheet. Each side is split into non-current and current, with \u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">equity\u003c/span> at the top of the right-hand side.\u003c/p>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl reveal-rows\">\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Assets\u003c/th>\u003cth>Equity and liabilities\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Non-current assets\u003c/span>\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"row\">\u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">Equity\u003c/span>\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Property, plant and equipment\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">Share capital\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Investment property\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">Retained earnings\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Intangible assets\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">Other reserves\u003c/span> (e.g. \u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">revaluation surplus\u003c/span>)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Financial assets\u003c/span> (long-term)\u003c/td>\u003cth scope=\"row\">\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Non-current liabilities\u003c/span>\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Deferred tax asset\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Long-term borrowings\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Lease liabilities\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Deferred tax liability\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">long-term provisions\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Current assets\u003c/span>\u003c/th>\u003cth scope=\"row\">\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Current liabilities\u003c/span>\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Inventories\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Trade and other payables\u003c/span> (incl. \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">accruals\u003c/span>)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Trade receivables\u003c/span> (net of \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">allowance\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">prepayments\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Short-term borrowings\u003c/span> and the \u003cstrong>current portion\u003c/strong> of \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">long-term debt\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Financial assets at FVTPL\u003c/span> held for trading\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Current tax payable\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">short-term provisions\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash and cash equivalents\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Contract liabilities\u003c/span> (\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">deferred revenue\u003c/span>)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>","sect":4,"part":"Financial position: current or not"},{"id":"calc-36nqh3","type":"calc","bucket":"sfp","lo":46,"q":"Orchard Retail’s {l|liabilities} at year end: {l|trade payables} $80,000; {l|accrued expenses} $12,000; a {l|bank loan} of $200,000 repayable in instalments, of which $50,000 falls due in the next 12 months; a {l|deferred tax liability} of $15,000; {l|current tax payable} $18,000; {l|contract liabilities} (customer deposits) of $9,000. What are total {l|current liabilities}?","answer":169000,"unit":"$","working":["{l|Current}: {l|trade payables} 80,000 + {l|accrued expenses} 12,000 + {l|loan instalment} 50,000 + {l|current tax payable} 18,000 + {l|contract liabilities} 9,000 = $169,000.","{l|Non-current}: the rest of the {l|loan} (150,000) and the {l|deferred tax liability} (15,000): $165,000. {l|Deferred tax} is always non-current."],"sect":4,"part":"Financial position: current or not"},{"id":"mcq-1oi1bj","type":"mcq","bucket":"sfp","lo":46,"q":"Lion City Venture (LCV) holds {a|marketable equity securities} at FVTPL (cost $10,000k; fair value at 31 December 20X5 $11,250k) and plans to sell them in 20X6. Where do they go in the 31 December 20X5 statement of financial position, and at what amount?","options":[{"t":"{a|Non-current assets}, at cost of $10,000k","why":"They are to be realised within 12 months, and FVTPL assets are carried at fair value."},{"t":"{a|Current assets}, at fair value of $11,250k","ok":true,"why":"To be sold within 12 months, so current; FVTPL, so remeasured to fair value with the 1,250k {i|gain} in P&L."},{"t":"{a|Current assets}, at cost of $10,000k","why":"Right class, wrong amount: FVTPL means fair value at each reporting date."},{"t":"{a|Cash equivalents}","why":"{a|Equity investments} are not {a|cash equivalents}: their value can change significantly."}],"sect":4,"part":"Financial position: current or not"},{"type":"map","id":"map-5-cfclass","bucket":"cfclass","sect":5,"part":"Cash flows: which activity?"},{"id":"think-15s29g","type":"think","bucket":"cfclass","lo":47,"scenario":"Geylang Gadgets reported a record profit of $2m this year. Three months later it couldn’t pay its suppliers. Its customers were slow to pay and it had piled up {a|stock}.","prompt":"How can a profitable company run out of cash? Which statement would have warned you?","answer":["Profit is earned on the accrual basis: sales count when made, not when paid. Geylang’s profit was sitting in {a|receivables} and {a|inventory}, not in the {a|bank}.","The **statement of cash flows** shows that, so users can judge **liquidity and solvency** and the **quality of earnings** (how much profit turns into cash). Profit is vanity, cash is sanity."],"sect":5,"part":"Cash flows: which activity?"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"cfclass","lo":47,"title":"What the statement of cash flows is for","body":["Accrual accounting is great for measuring performance, but it can hide a cash crunch. The cash flow statement fills that gap.","- Shows where cash came from and went, so users can assess the entity’s ability to **generate {a|cash}**, **pay its {l|debts}** (liquidity, solvency) and **pay {e|dividends}**.","- Compares profit with cash: the **quality of earnings**.","- **{a|Cash equivalents}:** short-term, highly liquid investments, readily convertible to known amounts of cash, with insignificant risk of value change. Normally **3 months or less from acquisition**. {a|Equity investments} are usually **not** cash equivalents.","- **{l|Bank overdrafts}** repayable on demand that form part of cash management are **deducted** within {a|cash and cash equivalents}.","! The cash flow statement is about **liquidity and solvency**, not profitability or gearing."],"id":"rule-1f9u86","sect":5,"part":"Cash flows: which activity?"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"cfclass","lo":47,"q":"Which of these is a **cash equivalent**?","options":[{"t":"{a|Listed shares} that could be sold on the exchange tomorrow","why":"Readily sold, but their value can change significantly. {a|Equity investments} are usually not {a|cash equivalents}."},{"t":"A {a|treasury bill} bought two months before it matures","ok":true,"why":"Short-term (3 months or less from acquisition), highly liquid, known amount, insignificant risk."},{"t":"A {a|fixed deposit} placed for 9 months","why":"More than 3 months from acquisition, so it is an investment, not a cash equivalent."},{"t":"A one-year {a|government bond} bought 11 months before maturity","why":"The 3-month test is measured from acquisition. 11 months is too long."}],"id":"mcq-1lr3mq","sect":5,"part":"Cash flows: which activity?"},{"type":"think","bucket":"cfclass","lo":47,"scenario":"In one week a food manufacturer: buys flour for production; buys a new oven; borrows $500,000 from a bank; lends $65,000 to a start-up supplier; pays its staff.","prompt":"Sort these into three piles. What question decides each pile?","answer":["**Running the business** (operating): {a|flour}, {x|staff wages}.","**Long-term assets and lending to others** (investing): the {a|oven}, and the {a|loan} **to** the start-up.","**Owners and lenders funding you** (financing): the {l|bank loan} **from** the bank.","The trick is direction: lending money **out** is investing; borrowing money **in** is financing."],"id":"think-bvlbp2","sect":5,"part":"Cash flows: which activity?"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"cfclass","lo":47,"title":"Which activity?","intro":"The chart from your notes (master.pdf). Work down the questions in order.","notes":{"start":"Any cash receipt or payment. (No cash moved? Then it isn’t in the statement at all: {x|depreciation}, a new lease, shares issued for an asset.)","q1":"Principal revenue-producing activities: {i|sales}, buying {a|inventory}, {x|wages}, {x|rent}, and {x|tax} paid.","q2":"Buying or selling long-term assets ({a|PPE}, {a|intangibles}, {a|land}), investments in other entities’ shares or bonds, and {a|loans made to others}.","q3":"Changes in the entity’s own {e|equity} and {l|borrowings}: issue or buy back {e|shares}, borrow, repay {l|principal}, {l|lease principal}.","op":"Operating activities: the default for anything that isn’t investing or financing.","inv":"Investing activities: spending now for returns later.","fin":"Financing activities: the people who fund the entity, owners and lenders.","note":"Interest, dividends and tax have policy choices. Pick one and apply it consistently."},"walk":[{"nodes":["start","q1","op"],"text":"Part of the main revenue-producing activities? Operating."},{"nodes":["q2","inv"],"text":"{a|Long-term assets}, investments, or lending to others? Investing."},{"nodes":["q3","fin"],"text":"The entity’s own {e|shares} or {l|borrowings}? Financing."},{"nodes":["note"],"text":"Interest, dividends and tax: a consistent policy choice."}],"caption":"Operating = running it · Investing = {a|long-term assets} and lending · Financing = owners and lenders.","id":"diagram-440wr9","sect":5,"part":"Cash flows: which activity?","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:508px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 462.00 346.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 332.06)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-332.06 447.25,-332.06 447.25,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>start\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M178.73,-317.66C178.73,-317.66 121.13,-317.66 121.13,-317.66 115.13,-317.66 109.13,-311.66 109.13,-305.66 109.13,-305.66 109.13,-293.66 109.13,-293.66 109.13,-287.66 115.13,-281.66 121.13,-281.66 121.13,-281.66 178.73,-281.66 178.73,-281.66 184.73,-281.66 190.73,-287.66 190.73,-293.66 1