 d=\"M536.15,-36C536.15,-36 452.88,-36 452.88,-36 446.88,-36 440.88,-30 440.88,-24 440.88,-24 440.88,-12 440.88,-12 440.88,-6 446.88,0 452.88,0 452.88,0 536.15,0 536.15,0 542.15,0 548.15,-6 548.15,-12 548.15,-12 548.15,-24 548.15,-24 548.15,-30 542.15,-36 536.15,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"494.51\" y=\"-13.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Financing activities\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge9\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>n4&#45;&gt;fin\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M494.51,-73C494.51,-64.29 494.51,-53.57 494.51,-43.97\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"496.62,-44.1 494.52,-38.1 492.42,-44.1 496.62,-44.1\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"505.76\" y=\"-50.75\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> Yes \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"cfclass","lo":47,"title":"Policy choices and non-cash items","body":["The three-question test sorts most flows. Two leftovers need their own rules: interest, dividends and tax, where you get a choice, and transactions with no cash at all.","- **{x|Interest} paid:** operating **or** financing.","- **{e|Dividends} paid:** financing **or** operating.","- **{i|Interest and dividends} received:** operating **or** investing.","- **{x|Income tax} paid:** operating, unless specifically identified with investing or financing.","+ Pick one and apply it **consistently**. In a case, read the entity’s stated policy first.","- **Non-cash transactions** are left out and **disclosed**: a new lease ({a|ROU asset}), an {a|asset} bought by issuing {e|shares}, {l|debt} converted to {e|equity}.","! {x|Depreciation} is not a cash flow at all. It appears only as an add-back in the indirect method."],"id":"rule-e6ymgz","sect":5,"part":"Cash flows: which activity?"},{"id":"visual-1h8279","type":"visual","bucket":"cfclass","lo":47,"title":"The three activities","html":"\u003cp>The chart and the tree in one table, with the examples the exam likes to use.\u003c/p>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl reveal-rows\">\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Activity\u003c/th>\u003cth>What it is\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical examples\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Operating\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Principal revenue-producing activities, and anything not investing or financing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash\u003c/span> from customers; paid to suppliers and employees; \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">rent\u003c/span>; \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">tax\u003c/span> paid; \u003cstrong>purchase of \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">inventory\u003c/span>\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Investing\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Buying and disposing of \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">long-term assets\u003c/span> and other investments not in \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash equivalents\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Buy or sell \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">PPE\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">intangibles\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">land\u003c/span>; buy shares or bonds of others; \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">loans made to others\u003c/span>\u003c/strong> and their repayment\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Financing\u003c/th>\u003ctd>Changes in the size and composition of contributed \u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">equity\u003c/span> and \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">borrowings\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Issue or buy back \u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">shares\u003c/span>; borrow; \u003cstrong>repay \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">loan principal\u003c/span>\u003c/strong>; redeem \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">debentures\u003c/span>; \u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">lease principal\u003c/span>\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cp class=\"key\">A \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">loan\u003c/span> \u003cstrong>to\u003c/strong> someone: investing. A \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">loan\u003c/span> \u003cstrong>from\u003c/strong> someone: financing.\u003c/p>","sect":5,"part":"Cash flows: which activity?"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"cfclass","lo":47,"q":"Which cash flow is classified as **investing**?","options":[{"t":"Repayment of the principal on a {l|bank loan}","why":"Repaying your own {l|borrowing} is financing."},{"t":"Collection of a {a|loan} previously made to an associate","ok":true,"why":"{a|Loans made to others}, and their repayment, are investing."},{"t":"Payment for {a|inventory}","why":"{a|Inventory} is part of the revenue-producing activities: operating."},{"t":"Proceeds from issuing {e|preference shares}","why":"Issuing your own {e|shares} is financing."}],"id":"mcq-1k484y","sect":5,"part":"Cash flows: which activity?"},{"id":"mcq-5qvahx","type":"mcq","bucket":"cfclass","lo":47,"q":"During the year a company starts a 5-year lease of a warehouse and recognises a {a|right-of-use asset} and {l|lease liability} of $400,000. It also pays $70,000 of {l|lease principal}. How is this shown in the statement of cash flows?","options":[{"t":"Investing outflow $400,000 and financing inflow $400,000","why":"No cash moved when the lease started. That part is a non-cash transaction."},{"t":"Financing outflow $70,000; the new lease is disclosed as a non-cash transaction","ok":true,"why":"Only the cash principal payment is a cash flow (financing). The $400,000 recognition is disclosed, not shown."},{"t":"Investing outflow $70,000","why":"{l|Lease principal} payments are financing: they reduce a {l|borrowing}."},{"t":"Investing outflow $400,000; financing outflow $70,000","why":"Shows a $400,000 payment that never happened."}],"sect":5,"part":"Cash flows: which activity?"},{"type":"map","id":"map-6-cfop","bucket":"cfop","sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct"},{"id":"think-9jtqhk","type":"think","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"scenario":"A company’s profit before tax is $400,000. That figure is after $120,000 of {x|depreciation} and includes a $15,000 {i|gain} on selling equipment. {a|Receivables} went up by $50,000 during the year.","prompt":"Starting from profit, which way would you adjust for each of these to get to cash?","answer":["**{x|Depreciation}: add back.** It reduced profit, but no cash left the building.","**{i|Gain}: deduct.** It isn’t operating cash; the full {a|sale proceeds} go to investing.","**{a|Receivables} up: deduct.** Some of this year’s sales are still owed, so {a|cash} is less than {i|revenue}."],"sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"title":"Direct vs indirect","body":["Now for the operating section, the hardest part. There are two ways to present it, and the exam mostly wants the one that starts from profit.","- The two methods differ **only in the operating section**. Investing and financing are always shown gross, line by line. Both give the **same** net operating cash flow.","- **Direct:** list actual receipts and payments (from customers, to suppliers, to employees). SFRS(I) 1-7 **encourages** it.","- **Indirect:** start from profit and adjust it to cash. More common in practice and in exam questions.","**The indirect method in four moves:**","1. Start from **profit before tax**.","2. **Add** non-cash {x|expenses} ({x|depreciation}, {x|impairment}, {x|loss on disposal}); **deduct** non-cash {i|gains} ({i|gain on disposal}).","3. **Add back** {x|interest expense} (and remove {i|investment income}) if those cash flows are shown elsewhere.","4. **Adjust for working capital**, then deduct {x|interest} and {x|tax} **paid** if they are operating."],"id":"rule-afv6nb","sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"title":"The indirect method bridge","intro":"The chart from your notes (master.pdf): from profit before tax to net cash from operating activities.","notes":{"pbt":"Start from profit before tax, the accrual-basis number.","nc":"Add back {x|expenses} with no cash outflow ({x|depreciation}, {x|impairment}, {x|losses on disposal}); take off {i|gains} that aren’t operating cash ({i|gain on disposal}).","if":"{x|Interest expense} is added back so the actual {x|interest} paid can be shown separately. {i|Investment income} is removed if its receipts go in investing.","wc":"{a|Current asset} up: {a|cash} tied up, deduct. {l|Current liability} up: {x|expense} not yet paid, add.","cgo":"Cash generated from operations: the subtotal before interest and tax payments.","pd":"Deduct the {x|interest} and {x|tax} actually **paid** (not the {x|expense}), if the policy classifies them as operating.","net":"Net cash from operating activities. The same answer as the direct method."},"walk":[{"nodes":["pbt"],"text":"Start from profit before tax."},{"nodes":["nc","if"],"text":"Strip out the non-cash items and the items shown elsewhere."},{"nodes":["wc","cgo"],"text":"Adjust for working capital: cash generated from operations."},{"nodes":["pd","net"],"text":"Deduct {x|interest} and {x|tax} paid: net cash from operating activities."}],"caption":"Profit → strip non-cash → working capital → cash.","id":"diagram-so4ljv","sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:640px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 592.00 171.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 157.06)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-157.06 577.4,-157.06 577.4,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>pbt\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2