t":"cfop","lo":48,"title":"Sign rules for the indirect method","html":"\u003cp>Step 4 of the indirect method is where most marks are lost. These sign rules turn the working capital changes into cash.\u003c/p>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl reveal-rows\">\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Change in the balance\u003c/th>\u003cth>Adjustment\u003c/th>\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Current asset\u003c/span>\u003c/strong> ↑ (\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">receivables\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">inventory\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">prepayments\u003c/span>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Deduct\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash\u003c/span> is tied up\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Current asset\u003c/span>\u003c/strong> ↓\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Add\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash\u003c/span> released\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Current liability\u003c/span>\u003c/strong> ↑ (\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">payables\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">accruals\u003c/span>)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Add\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Expense\u003c/span> recorded but not yet paid\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Current liability\u003c/span>\u003c/strong> ↓\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Deduct\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Paid more than the \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">expense\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-i\">Gain on disposal\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Deduct\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The full \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">proceeds\u003c/span> go in investing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Loss on disposal\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">depreciation\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">impairment\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Add\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Expenses\u003c/span> with no \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash\u003c/span> outflow\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cp class=\"key\">\u003cstrong>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Assets\u003c/span> up, \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash\u003c/span> down. \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">Liabilities\u003c/span> up, \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash\u003c/span> up.\u003c/strong>\u003c/p>","sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct"},{"id":"worked-um8t7r","type":"worked","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"title":"Worked example: net cash from operating activities","q":"{i|Revenue} $2,000,000; {x|cost of sales} $1,100,000; {x|depreciation} $120,000; {x|other cash operating expenses} $365,000; {i|gain on sale of equipment} $15,000; {x|interest expense} $30,000. {a|Receivables} rose $50,000, {a|inventory} fell $20,000, {l|payables} rose $25,000. {x|Interest} paid $30,000 and {x|tax} paid $80,000, both operating. Find net cash from operating activities (indirect method).","intro":["A typical exam question: you get a P&L, the movements in working capital and the {x|interest} and {x|tax} paid, and must find net cash from operating activities. Remember two things:","- Start from **profit before tax** and undo everything in it that isn’t operating cash: add back non-cash {x|expenses}, take off non-cash {i|gains}, add back {x|interest expense} so the actual payment can be shown.","- Working capital: **{a|assets} up, {a|cash} down; {l|liabilities} up, {a|cash} up.**","Then follow these steps: profit → non-cash items → interest → working capital → cash generated → interest and tax paid."],"steps":[{"h":"Calculate profit before tax.","t":"{i|Revenue} 2,000,000 − {x|cost of sales} 1,100,000 − {x|depreciation} 120,000 − {x|other operating expenses} 365,000 + {i|gain} 15,000 − {x|interest expense} 30,000 = **$400,000**"},{"h":"Strip out the non-cash items.","t":["Add back {x|depreciation} 120,000: it reduced profit, but no {a|cash} left.","Take off the {i|gain on sale} 15,000: the full {a|sale proceeds} 60,000 go to investing instead."]},{"h":"Add back the interest expense.","t":"Add back {x|interest expense} 30,000. The {x|interest} actually **paid** comes off at the end, in step 6."},{"h":"Adjust for working capital.","t":["{a|Receivables} up → − 50,000 (cash still owed by customers)","{a|Inventory} down → + 20,000 (stock sold that was paid for last year)","{l|Payables} up → + 25,000 (costs not yet paid)"]},{"h":"Total cash generated from operations.","t":"400,000 + 120,000 − 15,000 + 30,000 − 50,000 + 20,000 + 25,000 = **$530,000**"},{"h":"Deduct the interest and tax paid.","t":"530,000 − {x|interest} paid 30,000 − {x|tax} paid 80,000 = **net cash from operating activities $420,000**"},{"h":"Check with the direct method.","t":["From customers: {i|revenue} 2,000,000 − {a|receivables} increase 50,000 = 1,950,000","To suppliers: {x|cost of sales} 1,100,000 − {a|inventory} decrease 20,000 − {l|payables} increase 25,000 = 1,055,000","1,950,000 − 1,055,000 − {x|other operating expenses} 365,000 = **$530,000**. Same cash generated, same answer."]}],"sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"title":"Two roads, one destination","intro":"The worked example both ways. They differ only in how they reach cash generated from operations.","notes":{"ind":"Indirect starts from profit before tax: $400,000.","adj":"+ {x|depreciation} 120,000, − {i|gain} 15,000, + {x|interest expense} 30,000.","wc":"{a|Receivables} up 50,000 (deduct), {a|inventory} down 20,000 (add), {l|payables} up 25,000 (add).","dir":"Direct starts from cash received from customers: {i|revenue} 2,000,000 − increase in {a|receivables} 50,000 = $1,950,000.","sup":"Paid to suppliers = {x|cost of sales} 1,100,000 + increase in {a|inventory} (here a decrease of 20,000) − increase in {l|payables} 25,000 = $1,055,000.","emp":"{x|Other cash operating expenses}: $365,000.","cgo":"Both roads: $530,000.","pd":"{x|Interest} and {x|tax} paid come off after the subtotal under both methods.","net":"Net cash from operating activities: $420,000."},"walk":[{"nodes":["ind","adj","wc"],"text":"Indirect: profit, then strip out non-cash items, then working capital."},{"nodes":["dir","sup","emp"],"text":"Direct: actual receipts less actual payments."},{"nodes":["cgo"],"text":"They meet at cash generated from operations, $530,000."},{"nodes":["pd","net"],"text":"Then {x|interest} and {x|tax} paid: $420,000."}],"caption":"Direct and indirect: same operating cash flow, different story.","id":"diagram-zki9cl","sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:432px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 393.00 370.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 355.4)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-355.4 378.75,-355.4 378.75,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>ind\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M153.23,-341C153.23,-341 56.62,-341 56.62,-341 50.62,-341 44.63,-335 44.63,-329 44.63,-329 44.63,-317 44.63,-317 44.63,-311 50.63,-305 56.63,-305 56.63,-305 153.23,-305 153.23,-305 159.23,-305 165.23,-311 165.23,-317 165.23,-317 165.23,-329 165.23,-329 165.23,-335 159.23,-341 153.23,-341\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"85.05\" y=\"-323.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Indirect\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"s