\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#374151\">30,000 + 80,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge7\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>cgo&#45;&gt;pd\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M197.93,-121.62C197.93,-116.62 197.93,-111.06 197.93,-105.68\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"200.38,-105.98 197.93,-98.98 195.48,-105.98 200.38,-105.98\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node9\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>net\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M284.85,-36C284.85,-36 111,-36 111,-36 105,-36 99,-30 99,-24 99,-24 99,-12 99,-12 99,-6 105,0 111,0 111,0 284.85,0 284.85,0 290.85,0 296.85,-6 296.85,-12 296.85,-12 296.85,-24 296.85,-24 296.85,-30 290.85,-36 284.85,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"109.8\" y=\"-18.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Net cash from operating activities\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"181.05\" y=\"-9.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">420,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge8\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>pd&#45;&gt;net\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M197.93,-60.62C197.93,-55.62 197.93,-50.06 197.93,-44.68\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"200.38,-44.98 197.93,-37.98 195.48,-44.98 200.38,-44.98\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"calc-12trh3","type":"calc","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"given":3,"q":"Tiong Bahru Bakery: profit before tax $250,000, after {x|depreciation} of $40,000, a {x|loss on disposal} of $8,000 and {x|interest expense} of $12,000. {a|Receivables} rose $18,000, {a|inventory} rose $6,000, {l|payables} fell $9,000. {x|Interest} paid $12,000 and {x|tax} paid $35,000 are operating. The first three lines are done. What is net cash from operating activities?","answer":230000,"unit":"$","working":["Start: profit before tax $250,000.","Non-cash: + {x|depreciation} 40,000, + {x|loss on disposal} 8,000.","Shown elsewhere: + {x|interest expense} 12,000.","Working capital: {a|receivables} up → − 18,000; {a|inventory} up → − 6,000; {l|payables} down → − 9,000.","Cash generated from operations = $277,000.","Less {x|interest} paid 12,000 and {x|tax} paid 35,000 → $230,000."],"sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct"},{"id":"calc-1sijk0","type":"calc","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"q":"Jurong Joinery: profit before tax $180,000. It includes {x|depreciation} $55,000, an {x|impairment loss} $10,000, a {i|gain on sale of a van} $7,000 and {x|interest expense} $9,000. {a|Receivables} fell $12,000, {a|inventory} rose $20,000, {l|trade payables} rose $14,000 and {l|accruals} fell $3,000. {x|Interest} paid $9,000 and {x|tax} paid $28,000 are both operating. What is net cash from operating activities (indirect method)?","answer":213000,"unit":"$","working":["180,000 + 55,000 + 10,000 − 7,000 + 9,000 = 247,000.","Working capital: + 12,000 ({a|receivables} down) − 20,000 ({a|inventory} up) + 14,000 ({l|payables} up) − 3,000 ({l|accruals} down).","Cash generated from operations = $250,000.","Less 9,000 {x|interest} and 28,000 {x|tax} paid → $213,000."],"sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct"},{"id":"think-19bofv","type":"think","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"scenario":"Pasir Ris Logistics sells a {a|forklift} with a {a|carrying amount} of $30,000 for $42,000 {a|cash}.","prompt":"The P&L shows a {i|gain}. Where does the {i|gain} go in the cash flow statement, and where does the cash go?","answer":["The **{i|gain}** (12,000) is **deducted** in the operating section (indirect method), because it is inside profit but isn’t operating cash.","The **full {a|proceeds}** (42,000) are an **investing** inflow. Not the {i|gain}, not the {a|carrying amount}."],"sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"title":"One sale, two places","intro":"How a disposal shows up in the cash flow statement.","notes":{"sale":"{a|Carrying amount} 30,000 ({a|cost} 100,000 less {a|accumulated depreciation} 70,000), sold for 42,000.","gain":"{i|Gain} in P&L = 42,000 − 30,000 = 12,000. It is part of profit before tax.","op":"Indirect method: deduct the 12,000 {i|gain}, so profit is stripped back to operating cash. (A {x|loss} would be added back.)","inv":"Investing: the full 42,000 received.","ppe":"In the PPE roll-forward, the {a|carrying amount} (30,000) leaves. Useful when you have to back out the year’s {a|purchases}."},"walk":[{"nodes":["sale","inv"],"text":"The {a|cash} received, 42,000, is investing."},{"nodes":["gain","op"],"text":"The {i|gain}, 12,000, is taken out of operating so it isn’t counted twice."},{"nodes":["ppe"],"text":"The {a|carrying amount} only matters for the PPE working."}],"caption":"{a|Proceeds} in investing; {i|gain} out of operating.","id":"diagram-821bnn","sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:474px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 431.00 187.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 172.4)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-172.4 416.38,-172.4 416.38,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>sale\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M281.23,-158C281.23,-158 113.37,-158 113.37,-158 107.38,-158 101.38,-152 101.38,-146 101.38,-146 101.38,-134 101.38,-134 101.38,-128 107.38,-122 113.38,-122 113.38,-122 281.23,-122 281.23,-122 287.23,-122 293.23,-128 293.23,-134 293.23,-134 293.23,-146 293.23,-146 293.23,-152 287.23,-158 281.23,-158\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"158.3\" y=\"-140.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Sell equipment\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"112.18\" y=\"-131.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">carrying amount 30,000, proceeds 42,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>gain\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M114.6,-97C114.6,-97 12,-97 12,-97 6,-97 0,-91 0,-85 0,-85 0,-73 0,-73 0,-67 6,-61 12,-61 12,-61 114.6,-61 114.6,-61 120.6,-61 126.6,-67 126.6,-73 126.6,-73 126.6,-85 126.6,-85 126.6,-91 120.6,-97 114.6,-97\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"32.17\" y=\"-79.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Gain in P&amp;L\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"10.8\" y=\"-70.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">42,000 − 30,000 = 12,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge1\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>sale&#45;&gt;gain\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M157.46,-121.46C142.74,-114.98 125.92,-107.57 110.6,-100.83\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"112,-98.76 104.6,-98.19 110.02,-103.25 112,-98.76\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node4\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>inv\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M234.35,-97C234.35,-97 160.25,-97 160.25,-97 154.25,-97 148.25,-91 148.25,-85 148.25,-85 148.25,-73 148.25,-73 148.25,-67 154.25,-61 160.25,-61 160.25,-61 234.35,-61 234.35,-61 240.35,-61 246.35,-67 246.35,-73 246.35,-73 246.35,-85 246.35,-85 246.35,-91 240.35,-97 234.35,-97\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"173.3\" y=\"-79.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Investing\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"159.05\" y=\"-70.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">proceeds + 42,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge3\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>sale&#45;&gt;inv\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M197.3,-121.62C197.3,-116.62 197.3,-111.06 197.3,-105.68\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"199.75,-105.98 197.3,-98.98 194.85,-105.98 199.75,-105.98\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node5\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>ppe\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#f3f4f6\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,2\" d=\"M389.98,-97C389.98,-97 280.62,-97 280.62,-97 274.62,-97 268.62,-91 268.62,-85 268.62,-85 268.62,-73 268.62,-73 268.62,-67 274.62,-61 280.62,-61 280.62,-61 389.98,-61 389.98,-61 395.98,-61 401.98,-67 401.98,-73 401.98,-73 401.98,-85 401.98,-85 401.98,-91 395.98,-97 389.98,-97\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"291.8\" y=\"-79.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#374151\">PPE roll&#45;forward\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"279.43\" y=\"-70.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#374151\">carrying amount out 30,000\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge4\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>sale&#45;&gt;ppe\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,2\" d=\"M238.33,-121.46C253.49,-114.98 270.81,-107.57 286.59,-100.83\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"287.32,-103.18 292.8,-98.17 285.4,-98.67 287.32,-103.18\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node3\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>op\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M113.1,-36C113.1,-36 13.5,-36 13.5,-36 7.5,-36 1.5,-30 1.5,-24 1.5,-24 1.5,-12 1.5,-12 1.5,-6 7.5,0 13.5,0 13.5,0 113.1,0 113.1,0 119.1,0 125.1,-6 125.1,-12 125.1,-12 125.1,-24 125.1,-24 125.1,-30 119.1,-36 113.1,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"12.3\" y=\"-18.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Operating (indirect)\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"13.42\" y=\"-9.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">deduct the gain (12,000)\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge2\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>gain&#45;&gt;op\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M63.3,-60.62C63.3,-55.62 63.3,-50.06 63.3,-44.68\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"65.75,-44.98 63.3,-37.98 60.85,-44.98 65.75,-44.98\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"calc-xeibay","type":"calc","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"q":"Seletar Supplies (direct method): {i|revenue} $900,000; {a|trade receivables} rose by $30,000. {x|Cost of sales} $540,000; {a|inventory} rose by $15,000; {l|trade payables} rose by $20,000. How much cash was **paid to suppliers**?","answer":535000,"unit":"$","working":["> Paid to suppliers = {x|cost of sales} + increase in {a|inventory} − increase in {l|payables}","540,000 + 15,000 − 20,000 = $535,000.","For comparison, cash from customers = 900,000 − 30,000 = 870,000."],"sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct"},{"id":"mcq-ctw9yw","type":"mcq","bucket":"cfop","lo":48,"q":"Using the indirect method, how is a **decrease in {l|trade payables}** of $20,000 treated?","options":[{"t":"Added to profit, because {l|payables} are a {l|liability}","why":"A {l|liability} going **down** means more was paid than expensed: {a|cash} is lower.","pit":"wc-sign"},{"t":"Deducted from profit","ok":true,"why":"Paying off suppliers uses {a|cash} beyond the {x|expense}: deduct."},{"t":"Shown as a financing outflow","why":"{l|Trade payables} are operating working capital, not {l|borrowings}."},{"t":"Ignored, because it doesn’t affect profit","why":"That is exactly why it must be adjusted: cash moved without profit moving."}],"sect":6,"part":"Operating cash flow: indirect and direct"},{"type":"map","id":"map-7-cfinvfin","bucket":"cfinvfin","sect":7,"part":"Investing, financing and the whole statement"},{"id":"think-1afiv8","type":"think","bucket":"cfinvfin","lo":48,"scenario":"Pasir Ris Logistics’ {a|PPE} was $800,000 at the start of the year and $950,000 at the end. {x|Depreciation} for the year was $120,000, and it sold a forklift ({a|carrying amount} $30,000). Nobody told you what it spent on new {a|PPE}.","prompt":"Can you work out the cash spent on {a|PPE} from these numbers?","answer":["Yes: roll the balance forward and the {a|purchases} are the **balancing figure**.","{a|Opening 800,000} − {x|depreciation} 120,000 − {a|disposal} 30,000 + {a|purchases} = {a|closing 950,000}, so {a|purchases} = **$300,000** (assuming all paid in cash, with no revaluations or leases)."],"sect":7,"part":"Investing, financing and the whole statement"},{"id":"diagram-12vklp","type":"visual","bucket":"cfinvfin","lo":48,"title":"PPE roll-forward: find the purchases","html":"\u003cp>The T-account as a moving picture. Everything is known except the \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">purchases\u003c/span>: step through and watch them fall out as the balancing figure.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wg\" data-wg=\"roll\" data-key=\"ppe-d1\" data-cfg=\"{&quot;panels&quot;:[{&quot;title&quot;:&quot;Pasir Ris Logistics: {a|PPE}&quot;,&quot;sub&quot;:&quot;one year&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;a&quot;,&quot;bal&quot;:&quot;PPE carrying amount&quot;,&quot;start&quot;:800000,&quot;startCap&quot;:&quot;{a|Opening PPE}: {a|800,000}.&quot;,&quot;years&quot;:[{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;The year&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;depreciation&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;x&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:-120000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;{x|Depreciation} 120,000 comes off: {a|800,000} − 120,000 = {a|680,000}. No {a|cash} moves.&quot;},{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;disposal&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;a&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:-30000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;The forklift leaves at its {a|carrying amount} 30,000 ({a|cost} 100,000 − {a|accumulated depreciation} 70,000), not at the 42,000 {a|proceeds}: {a|650,000}.&quot;},{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;purchases&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;a&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:300000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;To land on {a|closing PPE} 950,000, {a|purchases} must be 950,000 − 650,000 = **{a|300,000}**.&quot;}]}]}],&quot;notes&quot;:{&quot;start&quot;:&quot;You know {a|opening} and {a|closing PPE}, the {x|depreciation} and the disposal. Nobody told you the spending. Press Play or Next.&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[&quot;Step 1: {x|depreciation} reduces the {a|carrying amount}. It is not a cash flow.&quot;,&quot;Step 2: a disposal leaves at its {a|carrying amount}. The {a|proceeds} are a separate investing inflow.&quot;,&quot;Step 3: {a|purchases} are whatever closes the gap.&quot;],&quot;end&quot;:&quot;**Purchases are the plug:** 950,000 − 800,000 + 120,000 + 30,000 = {a|300,000}, an investing outflow if all paid in cash. Take out additions from new leases or bought on credit, and any revaluation, first: they move the balance without {a|cash}.&quot;}}\">\u003c/div>","sect":7,"part":"Investing, financing and the whole statement"},{"id":"calc-1eru11","type":"calc","bucket":"cfinvfin","lo":48,"q":"{e|Retained earnings} were $400,000 at the start of the year and $480,000 at the end. Profit for the year was $150,000. There were no other movements and no {l|dividends payable} at either date. How much was paid in {e|dividends}?","answer":70000,"unit":"$","working":["{e|Opening 400,000} + profit 150,000 − {e|dividends} = {e|closing 480,000}.","{e|Dividends} paid = $70,000: a financing outflow (or operating, if that is the policy)."],"sect":7,"part":"Investing, financing and the whole statement"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"cfinvfin","lo":48,"title":"Investing and financing: gross, cash only","body":["Operating is done. The other two sections are simpler: list the actual cash in and out, line by line, and find missing figures from roll-forwards like the one you just did.","- Show investing and financing flows **gross**: purchases and sales of {a|PPE} as separate lines, new {l|loans} and repayments as separate lines.","- **Investing:** buy or sell {a|PPE}, {a|intangibles}, {a|land}; buy or sell other entities’ shares or bonds; {a|loans made to others} and their repayment.","- **Financing:** {e|share issues} (at the **issue price**, not par); {e|share buy-backs}; new {l|borrowings}; repayments of {l|principal}; redemptions **including any premium**; {l|lease principal}.","- **Changes in {l|liabilities} from financing activities:** reconcile {l|opening} to {l|closing} {l|loans}, {l|bonds} and {l|lease liabilities}, split into **cash** changes and **non-cash** changes (new leases, FX, fair value changes).","- Disclose the components of **{a|cash and cash equivalents}** and reconcile them to the statement of financial position.","! Redeeming $500,000 of {l|debentures} at a 5% premium costs $525,000 of {a|cash}. The premium is part of the financing outflow."],"id":"rule-adk1l5","sect":7,"part":"Investing, financing and the whole statement"},{"id":"visual-y6qxs1","type":"visual","bucket":"cfinvfin","lo":48,"title":"The whole statement (worked example)","html":"\u003cp>Put the three sections together and the statement explains how \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">opening cash\u003c/span> became \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">closing cash\u003c/span>. Here is the worked example in full.\u003c/p>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl num reveal-rows\">\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Net cash from operating activities\u003c/th>\u003ctd>\u003cb>420,000\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Purchase of \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">PPE\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(300,000)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Proceeds from sale of \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">equipment\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>60,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Net cash used in investing activities\u003c/th>\u003ctd>\u003cb>(240,000)\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Proceeds from \u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">share issue\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>100,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>Repayment of \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">loan\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(150,000)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">Dividends\u003c/span> paid\u003c/td>\u003ctd>(40,000)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Net cash used in financing activities\u003c/th>\u003ctd>\u003cb>(90,000)\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">Net increase in \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash and cash equivalents\u003c/span>\u003c/th>\u003ctd>\u003cb>90,000\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash and cash equivalents\u003c/span> at the start of the year\u003c/td>\u003ctd>60,000\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003cth scope=\"row\">\u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">Cash and cash equivalents\u003c/span> at the end of the year\u003c/th>\u003ctd>\u003cb>150,000\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cp class=\"cap\">Brackets are outflows. The last line must agree with \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash\u003c/span> in the statement of financial position.\u003c/p>","sect":7,"part":"Investing, financing and the whole statement"},{"type":"visual","bucket":"cfinvfin","lo":48,"title":"Opening cash to closing cash","html":"\u003cp>The same statement as a roll-forward of \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">cash\u003c/span>: each activity’s net total moves the balance once.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wg\" data-wg=\"roll\" data-key=\"cash-d1\" data-cfg=\"{&quot;panels&quot;:[{&quot;title&quot;:&quot;{a|Cash and cash equivalents}&quot;,&quot;sub&quot;:&quot;the worked example&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;a&quot;,&quot;bal&quot;:&quot;Cash&quot;,&quot;start&quot;:60000,&quot;startCap&quot;:&quot;{a|Opening cash}: {a|60,000}.&quot;,&quot;years&quot;:[{&quot;label&quot;:&quot;The year&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;operating&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;a&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:420000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;Operating: {a|cash generated} 530,000 − {x|interest} 30,000 − {x|tax} 80,000 paid = +420,000. {a|Cash}: {a|480,000}.&quot;},{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;investing&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;a&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:-240000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;Investing: {a|PPE} bought (300,000) + {a|sale proceeds} 60,000 = (240,000). {a|Cash}: {a|240,000}.&quot;},{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;financing&quot;,&quot;el&quot;:&quot;a&quot;,&quot;v&quot;:-90000,&quot;cap&quot;:&quot;Financing: {e|shares} 100,000 − {l|loan repaid} 150,000 − {e|dividends} 40,000 = (90,000). {a|Closing cash}: **{a|150,000}**.&quot;}]}]}],&quot;notes&quot;:{&quot;start&quot;:&quot;Start from {a|opening cash and cash equivalents}. Press Play or Next.&quot;,&quot;parts&quot;:[&quot;Operating: the net total from the indirect (or direct) method.&quot;,&quot;Investing: gross lines, netted only in the subtotal.&quot;,&quot;Financing: owners and lenders.&quot;],&quot;end&quot;:&quot;**Operating cash pays for the rest.** 420,000 came in from running the business; 240,000 went into {a|long-term assets} and 90,000 back to lenders and owners, leaving {a|cash} up 90,000 to {a|150,000}, the figure in the statement of financial position.&quot;}}\">\u003c/div>","id":"visual-17tnsg","sect":7,"part":"Investing, financing and the whole statement"},{"id":"mcq-lpeehn","type":"mcq","bucket":"cfinvfin","lo":48,"q":"During the year a company issued 200,000 {e|shares} of $1 par value at $3 each, borrowed $400,000 from a bank, and redeemed $300,000 of {l|bonds} at a 4% premium. {x|Interest} paid is classified as operating. What is net cash from financing activities?","options":[{"t":"$300,000 inflow","pit":"premium-missed","why":"Uses par value for the shares and ignores the premium."},{"t":"$700,000 inflow","pit":"premium-missed","why":"Ignores the redemption premium: the bonds cost more than their face value to redeem."},{"t":"$688,000 inflow","ok":true,"why":"{e|Shares} 600,000 + {l|loan} 400,000 − redemption 312,000 (including the 12,000 premium)."},{"t":"$288,000 inflow","pit":"premium-missed","why":"Shares are recorded at the issue price received ($3), not the $1 par value."}],"sect":7,"part":"Investing, financing and the whole statement"},{"type":"examtypes","id":"examtypes","bucket":"wrap","sect":8,"part":"Wrap-up"},{"type":"summary","lo":48,"title":"Preparing financial statements on one page","rows":[["Complete set?","SFP, P&L and OCI, SOCE, statement of cash flows, notes, all with comparatives."],["Going concern doubt?","Still a going concern unless liquidation is intended or unavoidable; disclose material uncertainties."],["Trial balance case?","Post every adjustment into one table first, then classify, then answer."],["Periodic inventory write-down?","{a|Closing inventory} net of the write-down, so it sits inside {x|cost of sales}."],["Manufacturing cost of sales?","RM used + labour + overheads ± {a|WIP} = COGM; ± {a|finished goods} = {x|cost of sales}."],["OCI?","Split: will not be reclassified ({e|revaluation surplus}, FVOCI equity) vs may be (FVOCI debt, hedges)."],["Current vs non-current?","Current if any test says yes. A {l|liability} is current unless, at the reporting date, there is a right to defer 12+ months."],["Three activities?","Operating (running it), investing (long-term assets and lending), financing (owners and lenders)."],["Interest, dividends, tax?","Policy choice for interest and dividends, applied consistently; tax usually operating."],["Indirect method?","PBT + non-cash {x|expenses} − non-cash {i|gains} ± working capital = cash generated; − {x|interest} and {x|tax} paid."],["Disposal?","{i|Gain} deducted in operating; full {a|proceeds} in investing."],["Non-cash transactions?","Left out of the statement; disclosed."]],"id":"summary-114nhl","bucket":"wrap","sect":8,"part":"Wrap-up"}],"quiz":{"cases":{"lcv":{"name":"Lion City Venture","source":"Official practice paper (Q21–25 case)","text":["The following trial balance has been extracted from the accounting system of Lion City Venture (LCV) on 31 December 20X5, the date of the financial year end. The trial balance has been prepared prior to adjustments based on additional information below.","**Debits ($’000):** Cash 2,125 · Accounts receivable 21,250 · Prepaid rent 3,000 · Inventory 31,250 · Marketable securities at FVPL 10,000 · Patent 50,000 · Investment property 35,000 · Building 63,500 · Machinery 13,750 · Purchases 100,000 · Salary expenses 11,000 · Other operating expenses 3,000 · Interest expense 1,500 · Tax expense 625 · Dividend declared 1,250.","**Credits ($’000):** Accumulated amortisation to 31 Dec 20X5 – patent 20,000 · Accumulated depreciation to 31 Dec 20X4 – building 20,000 · Accumulated depreciation to 31 Dec 20X4 – machinery 2,500 · Accounts payable 13,250 · Allowance for uncollectible accounts 5,750 · Salary payable 4,000 · Bond payable 17,500 · Tax payable 2,250 · Deferred revenue 3,500 · Share capital 75,000 · Retained earnings 57,750 · Sales 125,000 · Other income 750.","**Totals:** 347,250 each side.","**Additional information:**","• Inventory on 31 December 20X5 was $39,850 before write-down. Inventory is accounted for using the periodic method. Write-down was $6,800.","• Deferred revenue on 31 December 20X5 is $2,750.","• LCV applies the cost model on building and machinery held for business. The building was depreciated over 50 years on a straight-line basis with an expected residual value of $13,500. Machinery was depreciated over 5 years on a straight-line basis with an expected residual value of $1,250. LCV charges all depreciation to other operating expenses.","• LCV applies the fair value model on investment property and appraised the investment property’s fair value on 31 December 20X5 at $37.5 million.","• LCV plans to sell marketable equity securities in 20X6. Fair values of the marketable equity securities on 31 December 20X5 amounted to $11.25 million.","• Prepaid rent on 31 December 20X5 was $2.75 million.","• Allowance for uncollectible accounts was estimated to be $6.2 million on 31 December 20X5.","• Salary payable has not included $0.5 million owing to employees for work done in December 20X5.","• Bond payables with a coupon rate of 8% payable annually on 31 March are due on 31 March 20X6.","• An error in the trial balance was discovered on 15 January 20X6. A tax payable liability of $550 was posted erroneously to the share capital account."]},"tampines":{"name":"Tampines Traders","text":["Tampines Traders Pte Ltd (Tampines) is preparing its statement of cash flows for the year ended 31 December 20X6. Profit before tax was $320,000, after {i|revenue} of $1,400,000, {x|cost of sales} of $820,000, {x|depreciation} of $60,000, {x|other operating expenses} of $195,000 (all paid in cash), {x|interest expense} of $14,000, and a {i|gain} on selling equipment.","The {a|equipment} had a {a|carrying amount} of $21,000 and was sold for $30,000 {a|cash}.","{a|Trade receivables} rose from $90,000 to $110,000; {a|inventory} fell from $75,000 to $68,000; {l|trade payables} rose from $50,000 to $61,000.","{x|Interest} paid was $14,000 and {x|tax} paid $52,000; Tampines classifies both as operating. {e|Dividends} paid ($30,000) are classified as financing.","Tampines bought new {a|PPE} for $150,000 {a|cash}, issued {e|shares} for $80,000 and repaid $40,000 of a {l|bank loan}. {a|Cash} at 1 January 20X6 was $45,000."]}},"questions":[{"id":"q1","case":"lcv","lo":46,"type":"tb-adjust","source":"Official practice paper Q21","q":"Identify which of the following is the correct gross profit amount to be shown in the Statement of profit or loss for LCV on 31 December 20X5? All figures are in $’000.","options":[{"t":"$23,950","why":"Too low. Gross profit is {i|revenue} less {x|cost of sales} only; operating costs such as {x|depreciation} and {x|salaries} come below it."},{"t":"$26,050","pit":"def-rev-wrong","why":"Takes the 750 of {l|deferred revenue} earned off {i|sales} instead of adding it."},{"t":"$27,550","ok":true,"why":"{i|Revenue} 125,000 + 750 earned = 125,750. {x|Cost of sales} = {a|opening inventory} 31,250 + {x|purchases} 100,000 − {a|closing inventory} (39,850 − 6,800) = 98,200. Gross profit 27,550."},{"t":"$34,350","pit":"wd-out-cos","why":"Uses {a|closing inventory} at cost (39,850), leaving the 6,800 write-down out of {x|cost of sales}."}]},{"id":"q2","case":"lcv","lo":46,"type":"tb-adjust","source":"Official practice paper Q22","q":"Identify which of the following is the correct depreciation amount to be shown in the Statement of profit or loss for LCV on 31 December 20X5? All figures are in $'000.","options":[{"t":"$3,120","pit":"no-residual","why":"Depreciates the opening {a|carrying amounts} with no residual value. Straight-line is (cost − residual) ÷ life."},{"t":"$3,500","ok":true,"why":"{a|Building} (63,500 − 13,500) ÷ 50 = 1,000; {a|machinery} (13,750 − 1,250) ÷ 5 = 2,500. Total 3,500. The {a|patent} is already amortised to 31 Dec 20X5."},{"t":"$3,750","pit":"no-residual","why":"Ignores the {a|machinery}’s 1,250 residual value."},{"t":"$4,020","pit":"no-residual","why":"Ignores both residual values."}]},{"id":"q3","case":"lcv","lo":46,"type":"cur-noncur","source":"Official practice paper Q23","q":"Identify which of the following is the total non-current asset balance to be shown in the Statement of financial position for LCV on 31 December 20X5? All figures are in $’000.","options":[{"t":"$116,250","pit":"stale-ca","why":"Leaves {a|investment property} at 35,000. Under the fair value model it goes to 37,500."},{"t":"$118,750","ok":true,"why":"{a|Patent} 30,000 + {a|investment property} 37,500 + {a|building} 42,500 (63,500 − 21,000) + {a|machinery} 8,750 (13,750 − 5,000) = 118,750."},{"t":"$122,250","pit":"stale-ca","why":"Forgets this year’s {x|depreciation} (3,500): the {a|accumulated depreciation} in the trial balance is only to 31 Dec 20X4."},{"t":"$134,750","why":"Too high. It includes items that aren’t non-current; the {a|marketable securities}, for one, are to be sold in 20X6 and so are current."}]},{"id":"q4","case":"lcv","lo":46,"type":"cur-noncur","source":"Official practice paper Q24","q":"Identify which of the following is the total current asset balance to be shown in the Statement of financial position for LCV on 31 December 20X5? All figures are in $’000.","options":[{"t":"$64,225","ok":true,"why":"{a|Cash} 2,125 + {a|receivables} 15,050 (21,250 − 6,200) + {a|prepaid rent} 2,750 + {a|inventory} 33,050 + {a|securities} 11,250 = 64,225."},{"t":"$69,175","pit":"gross-receivables","why":"Uses gross {a|receivables} (no {a|allowance}) and the {a|securities} at cost instead of fair value."},{"t":"$70,425","pit":"gross-receivables","why":"Uses {a|receivables} gross of the 6,200 {a|allowance}."},{"t":"$71,025","pit":"wd-out-cos","why":"{a|Inventory} at cost (39,850). It must be written down to 33,050."}]},{"id":"q5","case":"lcv","lo":46,"type":"cur-noncur","source":"Official practice paper Q25","q":"Identify which of the following is the total current liabilities balance to be shown in the Statement of financial position for LCV on 31 December 20X5? All figures are in $’000.","options":[{"t":"$39,100","pit":"missed-accrual","why":"Leaves out {l|deferred revenue} (2,750), a {l|contract liability} that is current."},{"t":"$40,800","pit":"missed-accrual","why":"Forgets the accrued {l|bond interest}: 17,500 × 8% × 9/12 = 1,050."},{"t":"$41,300","pit":"missed-accrual","why":"Forgets the 550 {l|tax payable} posted to {e|share capital} in error."},{"t":"$41,850","ok":true,"why":"{l|Payables} 13,250 + {l|salaries} 4,500 + {l|bond} 17,500 (due 31 Mar 20X6) + {l|interest} 1,050 + {l|tax} 2,800 + {l|deferred revenue} 2,750 = 41,850."}]},{"id":"q6","case":"tampines","lo":48,"type":"cf-indirect","q":"What is Tampines’s net cash from operating activities for 20X6 (indirect method)?","options":[{"t":"$317,000","ok":true,"why":"320,000 + {x|dep} 60,000 − {i|gain} 9,000 + {x|interest} 14,000 − {a|rec} 20,000 + {a|inv} 7,000 + {l|pay} 11,000 = 383,000; − {x|interest} paid 14,000 − {x|tax} paid 52,000 = 317,000."},{"t":"$383,000","pit":"expense-not-paid","why":"That is cash generated from operations. {x|Interest} and {x|tax} paid are operating under Tampines’s policy, so deduct them."},{"t":"$335,000","pit":"gain-in-op","why":"Adds the {i|gain} on disposal instead of deducting it."},{"t":"$321,000","pit":"wc-sign","why":"Working-capital signs flipped: {a|receivables} up is a deduction, {a|inventory} down and {l|payables} up are additions."}]},{"id":"q7","case":"tampines","lo":48,"type":"cf-direct","q":"Under the direct method, how much cash did Tampines receive from customers?","options":[{"t":"$1,400,000","why":"{i|Revenue}, not {a|cash}. {a|Receivables} went up, so not all of it was collected."},{"t":"$1,420,000","pit":"wc-sign","why":"Adds the increase in {a|receivables}. {a|Receivables} up means less {a|cash}."},{"t":"$1,380,000","ok":true,"why":"1,400,000 − increase in {a|receivables} 20,000 = 1,380,000."},{"t":"$1,290,000","why":"Deducts the whole {a|closing balance}. Only the change in {a|receivables} matters."}]},{"id":"q8","case":"tampines","lo":48,"type":"cf-direct","q":"Under the direct method, how much cash did Tampines pay to suppliers?","options":[{"t":"$809,000","why":"Adjusts for {l|payables} but forgets that {a|inventory} fell by 7,000."},{"t":"$813,000","why":"Adjusts for {a|inventory} but forgets that {l|payables} rose by 11,000."},{"t":"$838,000","pit":"wc-sign","why":"Both adjustments the wrong way round."},{"t":"$802,000","ok":true,"why":"{x|Cost of sales} 820,000 + change in {a|inventory} ((7,000)) − increase in {l|payables} 11,000 = 802,000."}]},{"id":"q9","case":"tampines","lo":48,"type":"cf-invfin","q":"What is Tampines’s net cash used in investing activities?","options":[{"t":"$120,000 outflow","ok":true,"why":"{a|PPE} bought (150,000) + {a|proceeds} from {a|equipment} 30,000 = (120,000)."},{"t":"$129,000 outflow","pit":"gain-in-op","why":"Uses the {a|carrying amount} of the {a|equipment} instead of the {a|cash proceeds}."},{"t":"$141,000 outflow","pit":"gain-in-op","why":"Uses the {i|gain} instead of the full {a|proceeds}."},{"t":"$150,000 outflow","why":"Leaves out the {a|sale proceeds}, which are an investing inflow."}]},{"id":"q10","case":"tampines","lo":48,"type":"cf-invfin","q":"What is the net increase in Tampines’s {a|cash and cash equivalents} for 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$273,000","pit":"expense-not-paid","why":"Starts from cash generated from operations, missing the {x|interest} and {x|tax} paid."},{"t":"$207,000","ok":true,"why":"Operating 317,000 + investing (120,000) + financing 10,000 ({e|shares} 80,000 − {l|loan} 40,000 − {e|dividends} 30,000) = 207,000."},{"t":"$252,000","why":"That is {a|closing cash} (45,000 + 207,000), not the increase."},{"t":"$47,000","why":"Treats the {e|share issue} as an outflow. Issuing {e|shares} brings {a|cash} in."}]},{"id":"q11","lo":47,"type":"cf-classify","source":"Official practice paper Q11","q":"To enhance the informational value of the presentation of the Statement of Cash Flows (SCF), the cash flows are classified according to the nature of the activities that bring about the cash flows. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-7, Statement of Cash Flows, identify which type of activity should the purchase of inventory be classified as?","options":[{"t":"Investing activities","why":"Investing is for long-term assets and investments. {a|Inventory} is bought to sell."},{"t":"Operating activities","ok":true,"why":"Buying {a|inventory} is part of the principal revenue-producing activities."},{"t":"Financing activities","why":"Financing is about the entity’s own {e|equity} and {l|borrowings}."},{"t":"Equity activities","why":"There is no such category. SFRS(I) 1-7 has three: operating, investing and financing."}]},{"id":"q12","lo":48,"type":"cf-indirect","source":"Official practice paper Q12","q":"You are an accountant preparing the Statement of Cash Flows for inclusion in the year-end financial statements. The profit before tax for the year is $175k. You also have a note of the following: Depreciation expense 38; Loss on the sale of land 15; Gain on the sale of property 12; Increase in inventories 9; Decrease in trade receivables 6; Decrease in trade payables 5 (all $’000). With reference to SFRS(I) 1-7, Statement of Cash Flows, and using the details above, identify which of the following is the net cash flow from operating activities using the indirect method?","options":[{"t":"$90,000","pit":"dep-cash","why":"Deducts every item, as if {x|depreciation} and the {x|loss} were cash payments and every working-capital change used cash."},{"t":"$142,000","pit":"wc-sign","why":"Every adjustment with the wrong sign."},{"t":"$208,000","ok":true,"why":"175 + {x|depreciation} 38 + {x|loss} 15 − {i|gain} 12 − {a|inventory} up 9 + {a|receivables} down 6 − {l|payables} down 5 = 208 ($’000). No {x|interest} or {x|tax} paid is given, so nothing more is deducted."},{"t":"$224,000","pit":"wc-sign","why":"Non-cash items right, but all three working-capital signs flipped."}]},{"id":"q13","lo":46,"type":"complete-set","q":"Which of the following is **not** a component of a complete set of financial statements under SFRS(I) 1-1?","options":[{"t":"Statement of cash flows","why":"It is a component (prepared under SFRS(I) 1-7)."},{"t":"Comparative information for the preceding period","why":"Required for every amount."},{"t":"A directors’ report on the year’s performance","ok":true,"why":"Not part of the financial statements under SFRS(I) 1-1, even if other rules require it alongside them."},{"t":"Notes comprising significant accounting policies and other explanatory information","why":"The notes are a component."}]},{"id":"q14","lo":46,"type":"cur-noncur","q":"Sim Logistics has a $3m {l|bank loan} repayable in 20X9. On 18 December 20X5 it breached a covenant, making the {l|loan} repayable on demand. On 12 January 20X6, before the 20X5 statements are authorised, the bank agrees not to demand repayment. How is the {l|loan} classified at 31 December 20X5?","options":[{"t":"Non-current, because the bank agreed not to demand repayment","pit":"post-ye-waiver","why":"The agreement came after the reporting date. At 31 December there was no right to defer."},{"t":"Current","ok":true,"why":"Repayable on demand at the reporting date with no right to defer settlement. The January waiver is disclosed as a non-adjusting event."},{"t":"Non-current, with the breach disclosed","pit":"post-ye-waiver","why":"Disclosure doesn’t fix the classification. Judge it at the reporting date."},{"t":"Split: the amount repayable in 20X6 current, the rest non-current","why":"After the breach the whole {l|loan} was repayable on demand."}]},{"id":"q15","lo":46,"type":"going-concern","q":"Woodlands Wood Pte Ltd’s main {l|loan} falls due in 13 months. Refinancing talks with two banks are ongoing but nothing is agreed, and management intends to keep trading. How should the uncertainty be reported?","options":[{"t":"Prepare the statements on a liquidation basis","pit":"liquidation-basis","why":"Only if management intends to liquidate or has no realistic alternative. Neither is true here."},{"t":"Prepare on a going concern basis and disclose the material uncertainty","ok":true,"why":"Going concern is still appropriate, but the doubt must be disclosed."},{"t":"Say nothing until the {l|loan} actually falls due","pit":"liquidation-basis","why":"Material uncertainties about going concern are disclosed now."},{"t":"Recognise a {l|provision} for the loan repayment","why":"The {l|loan} is already a {l|liability}. The issue is disclosure of the uncertainty, not a new {l|provision}."}]},{"id":"q16","lo":46,"type":"oci-soce","q":"Which item is presented in OCI under “items that will **not** be reclassified to profit or loss”?","options":[{"t":"A {x|fair value loss} on an {a|FVOCI debt investment}","pit":"oci-wrong","why":"FVOCI debt is recycled to P&L on derecognition: it “may” be reclassified."},{"t":"A {i|gain} on remeasuring {a|FVTPL securities}","pit":"oci-wrong","why":"{i|FVTPL gains} go to profit or loss."},{"t":"A {e|revaluation surplus} on {a|land and buildings}","ok":true,"why":"{e|Revaluation surplus} stays in OCI and equity. It is never recycled."},{"t":"A {i|fair value gain} on {a|investment property}","pit":"oci-wrong","why":"Under the fair value model, {i|IP gains} go to profit or loss."}]},{"id":"q17","lo":46,"type":"cogs-manuf","q":"Woodlands Widgets: {a|raw materials} {a|opening} $12,000, {x|purchases} $88,000, {a|closing} $16,000; direct labour $50,000; production overheads $30,000; {a|work in progress} {a|opening} $8,000, {a|closing} $14,000. What is the cost of goods manufactured?","options":[{"t":"$158,000","ok":true,"why":"Raw materials used 12,000 + 88,000 − 16,000 = 84,000; + 50,000 + 30,000 + 8,000 − 14,000 = 158,000."},{"t":"$162,000","pit":"cogm-as-cos","why":"Uses {x|purchases} instead of raw materials used."},{"t":"$164,000","pit":"cogm-as-cos","why":"Leaves out the {a|WIP} adjustment."},{"t":"$170,000","pit":"cogm-as-cos","why":"{a|WIP} the wrong way round: add {a|opening}, deduct {a|closing}."}]},{"id":"q18","lo":47,"type":"cf-classify","q":"Which of these would normally be included in **{a|cash and cash equivalents}**?","options":[{"t":"{a|Shares in a listed bank}, held as a short-term investment","pit":"ce-equity","why":"{a|Equity investments} are not {a|cash equivalents}: their value isn’t a known amount."},{"t":"A 6-month {a|fixed deposit} placed during the year","pit":"ce-equity","why":"More than 3 months from acquisition."},{"t":"A {l|bank overdraft} repayable on demand that forms part of cash management, deducted","ok":true,"why":"Such {l|overdrafts} are a component of {a|cash and cash equivalents}, as a deduction."},{"t":"{a|Trade receivables} due within 30 days","why":"{a|Receivables} are not {a|cash} or {a|cash equivalents}."}]},{"id":"q19","lo":48,"type":"noncash","q":"Which transaction is **excluded** from the statement of cash flows and disclosed instead?","options":[{"t":"Paying $50,000 of {l|lease principal}","why":"A cash payment: financing."},{"t":"Acquiring a {a|building} by issuing {e|shares} to the seller","ok":true,"why":"No cash moves: a non-cash investing and financing transaction, disclosed in the notes."},{"t":"Receiving a {i|dividend} from an investment","why":"A cash receipt: operating or investing by policy."},{"t":"Buying {a|equipment} for cash","why":"A cash payment: investing."}]},{"id":"q20","lo":47,"type":"cf-classify","q":"How may **{x|interest} paid** be classified under SFRS(I) 1-7?","options":[{"t":"Always as financing, because it relates to {l|borrowings}","pit":"interest-policy","why":"Financing is allowed, but so is operating. It is a policy choice."},{"t":"Always as operating, because it is an {x|expense} in profit or loss","pit":"interest-policy","why":"Operating is allowed, but so is financing."},{"t":"As operating or financing, applied consistently from period to period","ok":true,"why":"A policy choice, applied consistently."},{"t":"As investing, if the {l|borrowing} funded {a|PPE}","why":"{x|Interest} paid is operating or financing. (Capitalised borrowing costs are a separate question.)"}]}]},"exam":{"cases":{"harvest":{"name":"Harvest","source":"Official practice paper (Q41–45 case)","text":["Harvest Foods Pte Ltd (Harvest) is a food manufacturing company and is preparing its Statement of cash flows for the financial year ended 30 September 20X6.","During the financial year, Harvest made the following transactions:","• Purchased a new industrial food sorting conveyor for $225,000","• Paid employees $1,250,000","• Issued 300,000 preference shares with a par value of $1 and issue price of $5 to fund the building of a new warehouse","• Paid a tax bill of $565,000","• Made loan repayments of $25,000 of which $19,000 was principal","• Incurred a depreciation charge for the year of $650,000","• Lent $65,000 to a plant-based protein startup company","• Earned cash sales revenue of $2,875,000","• Sold a piece of land to a developer for $625,000","• Purchased raw food stuffs costing $1,950,000","• Redeemed $500,000 of debentures at a 5% premium","In their accounting policies, Harvest have chosen to classify interest paid as an operating activity in accordance with SFRS(I) 1-7, Financial Instruments: Disclosures."]},"bukit":{"name":"Bukit Batok Manufacturing","text":["Bukit Batok Manufacturing Pte Ltd (BBM) makes kitchen cabinets and prepares financial statements for the year ended 31 December 20X6, presenting expenses by function.","{a|Raw materials}: {a|opening} $30,000, {x|purchases} $260,000, {a|closing} $34,000. Direct labour $140,000. {x|Factory rent} $48,000, {x|factory depreciation} $22,000, {x|indirect factory labour} $30,000. {a|Work in progress}: {a|opening} $25,000, {a|closing} $31,000.","{a|Finished goods}: {a|opening} $60,000; {a|closing} at cost $72,000, but one discontinued range must be written down by $5,000 to net realisable value.","{i|Revenue} $820,000. {x|Administrative expenses} $150,000 (including {x|office depreciation} of $8,000); {x|distribution costs} $70,000; {x|finance costs} $18,000. {x|Income tax expense} is 17% of profit before tax, all unpaid at year end.","During 20X6 BBM revalued its {a|land} upward by $40,000, and its {a|FVOCI debt investments} fell in fair value by $6,000. It paid {e|dividends} of $25,000. {e|Retained earnings} at 1 January 20X6 were $210,000.","{l|Liabilities} at 31 December 20X6 include: {l|trade payables} $64,000; {l|accrued wages} $9,000; a {l|bank loan} of $300,000 repayable in 20X9, on which BBM breached a covenant on 15 December 20X6 making it repayable on demand (on 20 January 20X7 the bank agreed not to demand repayment); a $100,000 {l|bond} due 31 March 20X7; a {l|deferred tax liability} of $21,000; and {l|lease liabilities} of $80,000, of which $18,000 is payable within 12 months."]},"changi":{"name":"Changi Gourmet","text":["Changi Gourmet Pte Ltd (Changi) is preparing its statement of cash flows for the year ended 31 December 20X8 using the indirect method. Its policy classifies {x|interest} paid and {x|tax} paid as operating, and {e|dividends} paid as financing.","Extracts from the statements of financial position (20X7 → 20X8): {a|PPE} $1,200,000 → $1,380,000; {a|inventory} $210,000 → $260,000; {a|trade receivables} $180,000 → $165,000; {l|trade payables} $140,000 → $158,000; {l|current tax payable} $40,000 → $46,000; {e|share capital} $400,000 → $520,000; {l|bank loan} $300,000 → $220,000; {l|lease liability} $0 → $48,000; {e|retained earnings} $780,000 → $948,000.","Profit before tax for 20X8 was $300,000, after {x|depreciation} of $160,000, {x|interest expense} of $24,000 (all paid, including {x|lease interest}) and a {x|loss} on the sale of equipment. {x|Income tax expense} was $52,000.","The {a|equipment} sold had a {a|carrying amount} of $50,000 and fetched $35,000 in {a|cash}.","On 1 January 20X8 Changi leased a delivery van and recognised a {a|right-of-use asset} (included in {a|PPE}) and a {l|lease liability} of $60,000. {e|Shares} were issued for {a|cash}. There were no revaluations."]}},"questions":[{"id":"x1","case":"harvest","lo":47,"type":"cf-classify","source":"Official practice paper Q41","q":"Identify which of the following should be reported in cash flows used in or from financing activities in Harvest’s statement of cash flows?","options":[{"t":"Loan of $65,000 to a plant-based protein startup company","pit":"loan-direction","why":"A {a|loan} made **to** another company is investing."},{"t":"A depreciation charge for the year of $650,000","pit":"dep-cash","why":"{x|Depreciation} is not a cash flow. It appears only as an add-back in the indirect method."},{"t":"Sale of a piece of land to a developer for $625,000","why":"Selling {a|land} is investing."},{"t":"Redemption of $500,000 of debentures at a 5% premium","ok":true,"why":"Redeeming its own {l|debentures} changes Harvest’s {l|borrowings}: financing (an outflow of 525,000 including the premium)."}]},{"id":"x2","case":"harvest","lo":47,"type":"cf-classify","source":"Official practice paper Q42","q":"Identify which of the following should be reported in cash flows from or used in investing activities in Harvest’s statement of cash flows?","options":[{"t":"Purchase of a new industrial food sorting conveyor for $225,000","ok":true,"why":"Buying a {a|long-term asset} ({a|PPE}): investing."},{"t":"Issue of 300,000 preference shares with a par value of $1 and issue price of $5 to fund the building of a new warehouse","why":"Issuing {e|shares} is financing, whatever the cash is later spent on."},{"t":"Purchase of raw food stuffs costing $1,950,000","why":"Raw food stuffs are {a|inventory}: operating."},{"t":"Made loan repayments of $25,000 of which $19,000 was principal","pit":"interest-policy","why":"The {l|principal} (19,000) is financing and the {x|interest} (6,000) is operating under Harvest’s policy. Neither is investing."}]},{"id":"x3","case":"harvest","lo":48,"type":"cf-invfin","source":"Official practice paper Q43","q":"Identify which of the following is the correct amount to be reported in Harvest’s statement of cash flows as the net inflow or outflow from financing activities?","options":[{"t":"$819,000 outflow","why":"Wrong direction: the {e|share issue} raised 1,500,000, so financing is a net inflow."},{"t":"$956,000 inflow","ok":true,"why":"{e|Shares} 300,000 × $5 = 1,500,000; {l|loan principal} (19,000) (the 6,000 {x|interest} is operating under Harvest’s policy); {l|debentures} 500,000 × 1.05 = (525,000). Net 956,000 inflow."},{"t":"$975,000 inflow","pit":"interest-policy","why":"Leaves out the 19,000 of {l|loan principal}, which is financing whatever the interest policy."},{"t":"$981,000 inflow","pit":"premium-missed","why":"Redeems the {l|debentures} at 500,000, missing the 5% premium (25,000)."}]},{"id":"x4","case":"harvest","lo":48,"type":"cf-invfin","source":"Official practice paper Q44","q":"Identify which of the following is the correct amount to be reported in Harvest’s Statement of cash flows as the net inflow or outflow from investing activities?","options":[{"t":"$225,000 outflow","why":"Only the {a|conveyor}. The {a|land} sale and the {a|loan} to the start-up are investing too."},{"t":"$335,000 inflow","ok":true,"why":"{a|Conveyor} (225,000) + {a|loan to start-up} (65,000) + {a|land} sale 625,000 = 335,000 inflow."},{"t":"$400,000 inflow","pit":"loan-direction","why":"Leaves out the 65,000 {a|loan} to the start-up. Lending to others is investing."},{"t":"$465,000 inflow","pit":"loan-direction","why":"Treats the {a|loan} made as an inflow. Harvest paid the {a|cash} out."}]},{"id":"x5","case":"harvest","lo":47,"type":"cf-purpose","source":"Official practice paper Q45","q":"Harvest’s board of directors are reviewing the financial statements prior to publication. Identify which of the following best describes the role of Harvest’s statement of cash flows in financial decision-making?","options":[{"t":"Allows decision-makers to assess the level of Harvest’s gearing","pit":"purpose-profit","why":"Gearing comes from the statement of financial position ({l|debt} vs {e|equity})."},{"t":"Allows decision-makers to assess liquidity and solvency of Harvest","ok":true,"why":"The cash flow statement shows the ability to generate {a|cash} and meet obligations: liquidity and solvency."},{"t":"Allows decision-makers to assess the profitability of Harvest","pit":"purpose-profit","why":"Profitability is the job of the statement of profit or loss. The cash flow statement shows how profit turns into cash."},{"t":"Allows decision-makers to assess the quality of Harvest’s equity","why":"Not a standard use of the cash flow statement."}]},{"id":"x6","case":"bukit","lo":46,"type":"cogs-manuf","q":"What is BBM’s cost of goods manufactured for 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$468,000","why":"Leaves {x|factory depreciation} out of production overheads. It belongs in {x|cost of sales}, not {x|admin}."},{"t":"$490,000","ok":true,"why":"Raw materials used 30,000 + 260,000 − 34,000 = 256,000; + labour 140,000 + overheads 100,000 + {a|opening WIP} 25,000 − {a|closing WIP} 31,000 = 490,000."},{"t":"$494,000","pit":"cogm-as-cos","why":"Uses {x|purchases} instead of raw materials used."},{"t":"$496,000","pit":"cogm-as-cos","why":"Leaves out the {a|WIP} adjustment."}]},{"id":"x7","case":"bukit","lo":46,"type":"cogs-manuf","q":"What is BBM’s cost of sales for 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$490,000","pit":"cogm-as-cos","why":"That is cost of goods manufactured. {a|Finished goods} still need adjusting."},{"t":"$478,000","pit":"wd-out-cos","why":"{a|Closing finished goods} at cost; the 5,000 write-down to NRV belongs in {x|cost of sales}."},{"t":"$483,000","ok":true,"why":"{a|Opening FG} 60,000 + COGM 490,000 − {a|closing FG} 67,000 (72,000 − 5,000) = 483,000."},{"t":"$497,000","pit":"cogm-as-cos","why":"{a|Finished goods} the wrong way round: add {a|opening}, deduct {a|closing}."}]},{"id":"x8","case":"bukit","lo":46,"type":"oci-soce","q":"What is BBM’s total comprehensive income for 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$82,170","why":"That is profit for the year. OCI items are added to reach total comprehensive income."},{"t":"$128,170","pit":"oci-wrong","why":"Treats the FVOCI fall in value as a {i|gain}."},{"t":"$116,170","ok":true,"why":"Profit 82,170 (PBT 99,000 − {x|tax} 16,830) + {i|revaluation} 40,000 − {x|FVOCI debt loss} 6,000 = 116,170."},{"t":"$91,170","pit":"oci-in-re","why":"Deducts {e|dividends}. They are transactions with owners, shown in the SOCE, not in comprehensive income."}]},{"id":"x9","case":"bukit","lo":46,"type":"cur-noncur","q":"What are BBM’s total {l|current liabilities} at 31 December 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$207,830","pit":"post-ye-waiver","why":"Keeps the {l|loan} non-current because of the 20 January waiver. At 31 December it was repayable on demand."},{"t":"$507,830","ok":true,"why":"{l|Payables} 64,000 + {l|wages} 9,000 + {l|loan} 300,000 (on demand at year end) + {l|bond} 100,000 + {l|tax} 16,830 + {l|current lease} 18,000 = 507,830."},{"t":"$590,830","pit":"debt-noncurrent","why":"Includes the whole {l|lease liability} and the {l|deferred tax liability}. Only the {l|lease} payable within 12 months is current, and {l|deferred tax} is always non-current."},{"t":"$107,830","pit":"debt-noncurrent","why":"Leaves both the {l|bond} (due within 12 months) and the {l|loan} (on demand) in non-current."}]},{"id":"x10","case":"bukit","lo":46,"type":"oci-soce","q":"What are BBM’s {e|retained earnings} at 31 December 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$301,170","pit":"oci-in-re","why":"Adds OCI to {e|retained earnings}. The revaluation and FVOCI movements go to their own {e|reserves}."},{"t":"$292,170","why":"Forgets the {e|dividends} paid."},{"t":"$267,170","ok":true,"why":"210,000 + profit 82,170 − {e|dividends} 25,000 = 267,170."},{"t":"$284,000","why":"Uses profit before tax. {e|Retained earnings} take profit after tax."}]},{"id":"x11","case":"changi","lo":48,"type":"cf-indirect","q":"What is Changi’s net cash from operating activities for 20X8?","options":[{"t":"$412,000","ok":true,"why":"300,000 + {x|dep} 160,000 + {x|loss} 15,000 + {x|interest} 24,000 − {a|inventory} 50,000 + {a|receivables} 15,000 + {l|payables} 18,000 = 482,000; − {x|interest} paid 24,000 − {x|tax} paid 46,000 ({l|opening} 40,000 + {x|expense} 52,000 − {l|closing} 46,000) = 412,000."},{"t":"$382,000","pit":"gain-in-op","why":"Deducts the {x|loss} on disposal. A {x|loss} is a non-cash {x|expense}: add it back."},{"t":"$406,000","pit":"expense-not-paid","why":"Deducts the {x|tax expense} (52,000) instead of the {x|tax} paid (46,000)."},{"t":"$482,000","pit":"expense-not-paid","why":"Stops at cash generated from operations. {x|Interest} and {x|tax} paid are operating under Changi’s policy."}]},{"id":"x12","case":"changi","lo":48,"type":"cf-invfin","q":"How much cash did Changi pay for {a|PPE} in 20X8?","options":[{"t":"$390,000","pit":"noncash-in-cf","why":"Includes the 60,000 {a|right-of-use asset}. A new lease involves no cash at the start."},{"t":"$180,000","why":"Just the change in the balance. {x|Depreciation} and {a|disposals} reduced {a|PPE} too."},{"t":"$330,000","ok":true,"why":"{a|Closing} 1,380,000 − {a|opening} 1,200,000 + {x|depreciation} 160,000 + {a|disposal} 50,000 = {a|additions} 390,000; less the 60,000 {a|ROU asset} (non-cash) = 330,000."},{"t":"$280,000","why":"Forgets that the {a|equipment} sold left the {a|PPE} balance."}]},{"id":"x13","case":"changi","lo":48,"type":"cf-invfin","q":"What is Changi’s net cash used in investing activities?","options":[{"t":"$295,000 outflow","ok":true,"why":"{a|PPE} (330,000) + {a|proceeds} 35,000 = (295,000)."},{"t":"$355,000 outflow","pit":"noncash-in-cf","why":"Includes the new lease as if it were a cash purchase."},{"t":"$280,000 outflow","pit":"gain-in-op","why":"Uses the {a|carrying amount} of the {a|equipment} sold instead of the {a|cash proceeds}."},{"t":"$310,000 outflow","pit":"gain-in-op","why":"Deducts the {x|loss} on disposal in investing. The {x|loss} belongs in the operating adjustments; investing takes the {a|proceeds}."}]},{"id":"x14","case":"changi","lo":48,"type":"cf-invfin","q":"What is Changi’s net cash from (used in) financing activities?","options":[{"t":"$52,000 outflow","ok":true,"why":"{e|Shares} 120,000 − {l|loan} repaid 80,000 − {l|lease principal} 12,000 (new 60,000 − {l|closing} 48,000) − {e|dividends} 80,000 ({e|opening} 780,000 + profit 248,000 − {e|closing} 948,000) = (52,000)."},{"t":"$40,000 outflow","why":"Leaves out the {l|lease principal} repaid, a financing outflow."},{"t":"$8,000 inflow","pit":"noncash-in-cf","why":"Treats the new lease as a cash inflow. No {a|cash} was received."},{"t":"$140,000 outflow","why":"Takes the change in {e|retained earnings} as the {e|dividend}. Profit for the year also moved it."}]},{"id":"x15","case":"changi","lo":48,"type":"noncash","q":"How should the new van lease be reflected in Changi’s 20X8 statement of cash flows and notes?","options":[{"t":"Investing outflow of $60,000 and financing inflow of $60,000","pit":"noncash-in-cf","why":"Grossing up a non-cash transaction shows cash that never moved."},{"t":"Excluded at inception and disclosed as a non-cash transaction; the $12,000 {l|principal} repaid is a financing outflow, and the lease appears as a non-cash change in the reconciliation of {l|financing liabilities}","ok":true,"why":"Only the cash payments are cash flows. The new lease is disclosed."},{"t":"An investing outflow of $60,000 only","pit":"noncash-in-cf","why":"No cash was paid to start the lease."},{"t":"Ignored completely, because leases are not {l|financing liabilities}","why":"{l|Lease liabilities} are {l|financing liabilities}: {l|principal} repayments are financing, and the new lease is a non-cash change to disclose."}]},{"id":"x16","lo":46,"type":"adjust-je","q":"Ang Mo Kio Holdings has a $2,000,000 {l|bond} with a 6% coupon paid annually on 30 September. Its year end is 31 December. No entry has been made since the last {x|coupon} was paid. Which year-end entry is needed?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":90000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Interest payable","amount":90000}],"pit":"missed-accrual","why":"Accrues January to September, which was covered by the 30 September payment. Only October to December is outstanding."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":30000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Interest payable","amount":30000}],"ok":true,"why":"2,000,000 × 6% × 3/12 = 30,000 for October to December, owed until next September: a {l|current liability}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest expense","amount":30000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":30000}],"why":"Nothing has been paid yet. An accrual credits a {l|liability}, not {a|cash}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Interest payable","amount":30000},{"side":"Cr","account":"Interest expense","amount":30000}],"why":"The entry reversed: it would reduce the {x|expense}."}]},{"id":"x17","lo":47,"type":"cf-classify","q":"At year end a company has: {a|cash at bank} $50,000; a {a|fixed deposit} of $30,000 placed one month ago for a 2-month term; {a|listed shares} of $20,000 held for short-term gains; and a {l|bank overdraft} of $12,000, repayable on demand and used as part of day-to-day cash management. What are {a|cash and cash equivalents}?","options":[{"t":"$88,000","pit":"ce-equity","why":"Includes the {a|listed shares}. {a|Equity investments} are not {a|cash equivalents}."},{"t":"$80,000","pit":"ce-equity","why":"Doesn’t deduct the {l|overdraft}, which is part of cash management."},{"t":"$68,000","ok":true,"why":"{a|Cash} 50,000 + 2-month {a|deposit} 30,000 − {l|overdraft} 12,000 = 68,000."},{"t":"$38,000","pit":"ce-equity","why":"Leaves out the {a|deposit}, which was placed for 2 months (3 months or less from acquisition)."}]}]},"prepared":true},{"id":"policies-estimates-errors","code":"E1","title":"Accounting Policies, Estimates and Errors","subtitle":"SFRS(I) 1-8 · back in time or forward only","los":{"49":"Selecting and changing accounting policies","50":"Accounting for policy changes, estimate changes and prior period errors","51":"Disclosures for policies, estimates and errors"},"accounts":[{"name":"Inventory","el":"a"},{"name":"Plant and equipment","el":"a"},{"name":"Accumulated depreciation","el":"a"},{"name":"Cash","el":"a"},{"name":"Retained earnings","el":"e"},{"name":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","el":"e"},{"name":"Deferred tax liability","el":"l"},{"name":"Current tax payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Cost of sales","el":"x"},{"name":"Depreciation expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Repairs expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Tax expense","el":"x"}],"steps":[{"id":"which","name":"Policy, estimate or error?","short":"Which is it"},{"id":"policy","name":"Policy change: retrospective","short":"Policy"},{"id":"estimate","name":"Estimate change: prospective","short":"Estimate"},{"id":"error","name":"Prior period error: restate","short":"Error"},{"id":"disclose","name":"Disclosure","short":"Disclose"}],"map":{"which":{"label":"Rule, input or mistake?","star":"The label decides whether the past is touched"},"policy":{"label":"Select, change, apply as if always applied"},"estimate":{"label":"Forward only, through P&L","star":"Depreciation method is an estimate, not a policy"},"error":{"label":"Restate comparatives, net of tax"},"disclose":{"label":"What, why, how much"}},"preamble":["Welcome to the chapter about **changing your mind**, and owning up to mistakes. Every year something in the accounts gets done differently: a new formula, a new guess, or a fix for a slip-up. SFRS(I) 1-8 tells you whether to rewrite history or just move on.","This one isn’t an asset or a liability, so it has its own five-stop route:","1. **Which is it?** Policy, estimate or error. The label decides everything. ★ special","2. **Policy change**: when you may change one, and applying it **retrospectively**, as if it had always applied.","3. **Estimate change**: new information about an uncertain number, applied **prospectively**. ★ special: the depreciation method trap lives here.","4. **Prior period error**: restate the past, net of tax, through {e|opening retained earnings}.","5. **Disclosure**: what happened, why (sometimes), and how much.","+ The big idea: **policies and errors go back in time; estimates only go forward.** And this year’s profit never carries the baggage of earlier years."],"intros":{"which":"First stop, and the one that decides the rest. Before any numbers we ask: is this a change of **rule**, a new **input** for an uncertain amount, or a **mistake**? ★ This is the special part: get the label right and the treatment follows automatically. The famous trap is waiting here too: “depreciation method” sounds like a rule but is an estimate.","policy":"We know what a policy change looks like, so now the details. First, where policies come from (a short ladder: standard, similar standards, Framework), and when you’re allowed to change one (a standard says so, or it’s more relevant; a nicer profit doesn’t count). Then the mechanics: apply it as if it had always applied, with earlier years landing in {e|opening retained earnings}. Expect a bit of arithmetic and a 17% tax bite.","estimate":"From time travel to the easy one. Estimate changes only look **forward**: no restatement, no {e|opening retained earnings}, just a new number from now on. ★ Special, because it’s where the exam sets its traps: start from the **{a|carrying amount}**, divide by the **remaining** life, and no catch-up for the years already gone.","error":"Now the embarrassing one. A prior period error is a mistake in statements already issued, and it gets the same back-in-time treatment as a policy change: restate the comparatives, and the opening balances too if the error is older. Mostly familiar mechanics after the policy stop, with two twists: errors caught before this year’s statements are out aren’t “prior period” at all, and older errors can call for a third balance sheet.","disclose":"Last stop: telling the readers. Routine stuff with a simple pattern: **what** happened and **how much**, line by line, plus **why** for voluntary policy changes. Say it once; it isn’t repeated in later years."},"pitfalls":{"method-as-policy":"Calling a change in depreciation method a change in accounting policy (it is an estimate).","estimate-retro":"Restating comparatives or adjusting {e|opening retained earnings} for a change in estimate.","catch-up":"Recomputing {x|depreciation} from {a|original cost}, or adding a catch-up for past years, after an estimate change.","remaining-life":"Dividing the {a|carrying amount} by the total useful life instead of the remaining life.","change-to-pl":"Putting the effect of earlier years (policy change or prior period error) through this year’s profit or loss.","ignore-tax":"Forgetting the tax effect when adjusting {e|retained earnings} or restating profit.","wrong-period":"Mixing up the {e|opening retained earnings} adjustment (years before the earliest period presented) with the cumulative effect to the end of the comparative year.","no-restate":"Leaving comparatives as originally reported after a policy change or prior period error.","profit-reason":"Accepting higher profit or management preference as a reason to change accounting policy.","new-txn-policy":"Treating a policy for new or substantively different transactions as a change in accounting policy.","reval-retro":"Applying the first adoption of the revaluation model retrospectively under SFRS(I) 1-8.","repeat-disclosure":"Thinking the change disclosures must be repeated in later years."},"examTypes":[{"id":"classify-change","title":"Is it a policy change, an estimate change or an error?","pitfall":"Calling a depreciation method change a policy change, or a current-period error a prior period error.","see":"which"},{"id":"when-change","title":"When may an entity select or change an accounting policy?","pitfall":"Accepting “higher profit” as a reason, or calling a policy for a new transaction a change.","see":"policy"},{"id":"reval-first","title":"First adoption of the revaluation model: how is it applied?","pitfall":"Restating prior years retrospectively under 1-8.","see":"policy"},{"id":"policy-amount","title":"Policy change: opening retained earnings, restated profit, or the entry?","pitfall":"Forgetting tax, or mixing up opening and cumulative amounts.","see":"policy"},{"id":"estimate-calc","title":"The estimate was revised: what is this year’s charge?","pitfall":"Spreading the {a|original cost}, dividing by the total life, or adding a catch-up.","see":"estimate"},{"id":"error-restate","title":"An error from last year is found: what is the adjustment?","pitfall":"Putting it through this year’s profit, or forgetting the tax effect.","see":"error"},{"id":"third-sfp","title":"Is a third statement of financial position needed?","pitfall":"Thinking every restatement needs one, or dating it wrongly.","see":"error"},{"id":"disclosure","title":"Which disclosure is required?","pitfall":"Expecting restated comparatives or a “why” for an estimate change.","see":"disclose"}],"pages":[{"type":"map","id":"map-overview","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"intro","lo":50,"title":"Policies, estimates and errors, the Socratic way","body":["This chapter is about **changing your mind** in the accounts, and fixing your mistakes. It turns on one question: what kind of change is this?","Each idea starts with a question. Think it through, then reveal the answer. The rule comes after, once you have half-worked it out.","Account names are coloured by element. Tap **Legend** at the top any time. At the end: a chapter quiz in exam style, then a harder exam."],"id":"intro-82r6x4","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"map","id":"map-1-which","bucket":"which","sect":1,"part":"Policy, estimate or error?"},{"id":"think-s68jd4","type":"think","bucket":"which","lo":50,"scenario":"Kranji Manufacturing is preparing its 20X5 statements (comparatives: 20X4). Three things happened this year: (1) it switched its {a|inventory} cost formula from FIFO to weighted average; (2) it revised a {a|machine}’s remaining useful life from 7 to 4 years after an engineering review; (3) it found that 20X4’s {x|depreciation} on a {a|building} was never recorded.","prompt":"Are these the same kind of change? For which of them, if any, should last year’s figures be touched?","answer":["(1) is a change in **rule**: how {a|inventory} cost is measured. That is a **change in accounting policy**. To keep the years comparable, the new rule is applied as if it had always been used, so 20X4 is restated.","(2) is **new information** about an uncertain number. The old estimate was the best one at the time; nothing was wrong. That is a **change in accounting estimate**, applied from now on only. 20X4 is left alone.","(3) is a **mistake**: the information was there and was ignored. That is a **prior period error**, and 20X4 is restated so it shows what should have been reported.","So: policies and errors go **back in time**; estimates only go **forward**. Like a time machine that only two of the three are allowed to ride."],"sect":1,"part":"Policy, estimate or error?"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"which","lo":50,"title":"Three kinds of change, three treatments","body":["Kranji’s three changes looked alike, but they weren’t. SFRS(I) 1-8 sorts every change into one of three boxes, and the box decides whether last year’s figures get touched.","1. **Accounting policies** are the specific principles, bases, conventions, rules and practices used to prepare and present the statements. A change → **retrospective application**.","2. **Accounting estimates** are monetary amounts in the statements that are **subject to measurement uncertainty**. A change in an **input** or a **measurement technique** used to make one is a change in estimate (unless it corrects an error) → **prospective**.","3. A **prior period error** is a mistake in earlier statements: reliable information was available but was ignored or misused → **retrospective restatement**.","+ Policy changes and errors restate the past. Estimate changes don’t.","- Your IA textbook may use the pre-2021 wording of “estimate”. Follow the current definition above."],"id":"rule-11gsvz","sect":1,"part":"Policy, estimate or error?"},{"id":"visual-1i3d1o","type":"visual","bucket":"which","lo":50,"title":"The three side by side","html":"\u003cp>The same three boxes as a table. Read down a column for one kind of change, or across a row to compare all three.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl reveal-rows\">\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Policy change\u003c/th>\u003cth>Estimate change\u003c/th>\u003cth>Prior period error\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>What it is\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A different principle, basis or rule\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A new input or technique for an \u003cstrong>uncertain\u003c/strong> amount\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A mistake: the information was there\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>How applied\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Retrospective application\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Prospective\u003c/strong>: this period and future ones\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Retrospective restatement\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Restate comparatives?\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Adjust \u003cspan class=\"el el-e\">opening retained earnings\u003c/span>?\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, of the \u003cstrong>earliest\u003c/strong> period presented\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes, if it began \u003cstrong>before\u003c/strong> the earliest period presented\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Examples\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>FIFO → weighted average; how a class of items is presented\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Useful life, residual value, \u003cstrong>depreciation method\u003c/strong>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">bad debt allowance\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"el el-l\">warranty provision\u003c/span>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">Depreciation\u003c/span> omitted; \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">inventory\u003c/span> miscounted; fraud; misapplying a standard\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"cap\">Read across a row: the two retrospective columns always agree with each other, and the estimate column is the odd one out.\u003c/p>","sect":1,"part":"Policy, estimate or error?"},{"id":"think-d3pvqi","type":"think","bucket":"which","lo":50,"scenario":"Tuas Shipping has always depreciated its {a|cranes} straight-line. An engineering study shows the cranes deliver most of their benefit in the early years, so from 1 January 20X5 it switches to the reducing-balance method.","prompt":"A “method” sounds like a rule. Is this a change in accounting policy?","answer":["No. The **policy** is “depreciate {a|PPE} over its useful life to reflect how its benefits are consumed”. The depreciation **method** is the technique used to estimate that pattern of consumption, which is uncertain.","So a change in depreciation method is a **change in accounting estimate** (SFRS(I) 1-16 says so too). It is applied **prospectively** from 20X5.","This is the single most popular trap in this chapter. The word “method” is wearing a policy costume."],"sect":1,"part":"Policy, estimate or error?"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"which","lo":50,"title":"The tricky border: policy or estimate?","body":["Tuas Shipping showed the hard part: some estimates *sound* like rules. Here is the border, item by item, so you can sort the usual suspects on sight.","Changes in **estimate** (inputs and techniques):","- useful life, residual value","- **depreciation method**","- {a|allowance for bad debts}, {l|warranty provision}, {a|inventory} obsolescence","- the technique used to measure a fair value","Changes in **policy** (the rule itself):","- {a|inventory} **cost formula** (FIFO ↔ weighted average)","- the measurement basis (e.g. cost model ↔ fair value model for {a|investment property})","- how a class of items is presented","+ If you **can’t tell** whether it is a policy or an estimate change, treat it as an **estimate** change.","! A change in depreciation method is an **estimate**, not a policy. A change in cost formula **is** a policy. Mix these up and you restate when you shouldn’t, or vice versa."],"id":"rule-14echy","sect":1,"part":"Policy, estimate or error?"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"which","lo":50,"title":"Policy, estimate or error?","intro":"This is the chart from your notes. Three questions, asked in this order, sort every change.","notes":{"start":"Anything in the accounts that is done differently from last year.","q1":"Ask about **mistakes first**. An error is not a change of mind; the right answer was available at the time.","err":"Restate the past as it should have been: comparatives, and opening balances if the error is older than that.","q2":"Is the **rule** changing (recognition, measurement basis, presentation, cost formula), or just a number the rule needs?","est":"A new input or technique for an uncertain amount: from now on only. And the tie-breaker: **if unsure, it is an estimate**.","q3":"The one policy change with its own lane.","reval":"First move to the revaluation model: a policy change, but just revalue from now on under 1-16 / 1-38.","pol":"Every other policy change: transitional provisions, otherwise retrospective, as if the new policy had always applied."},"walk":[{"nodes":["start","q1"],"text":"Start with: is this **fixing a mistake** in earlier statements?"},{"nodes":["err"],"text":"Yes → **prior period error**. Restate retrospectively."},{"nodes":["q2"],"text":"No → is the **principle, basis or rule** changing?"},{"nodes":["est"],"text":"No, only an input or technique → **estimate**, prospective. Depreciation method lives here."},{"nodes":["q3","reval"],"text":"Yes, and it is the first use of the **revaluation model** → treat it as a revaluation."},{"nodes":["pol"],"text":"Any other rule change → **policy change**, applied retrospectively."}],"caption":"Mistake? Rule? Revaluation? Three questions, four lanes.","id":"diagram-1xnt4h","sect":1,"part":"Policy, estimate or error?","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:574px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 522.00 376.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 361.47)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-361.47 507.12,-361.47 507.12,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>start\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M271.98,-347.07C271.98,-347.07 86.88,-347.07 86.88,-347.07 80.88,-347.07 74.88,-341.07 74.88,-335.07 74.88,-335.07 74.88,-323.07 74.88,-323.07 74.88,-317.07 80.88,-311.07 86.88,-311.07 86.88,-311.07 271.98,-311.07 271.98,-311.07 277.98,-311.07 283.98,-317.07 283.98,-323.07 283.98,-323.07 283.98,-335.07 283.98,-335.07 283.98,-341.07 277.98,-347.07 271.98,-347.07\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"85.68\" y=\"-325.62\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Something in the accounts changes\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>q1\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M244.6,-286.07C244.6,-286.07 114.25,-286.07 114.25,-286.07 108.25,-286.07 102.25,-280.07 102.25,-274.07 102.25,-274.07 102.25,-247.99 102.25,-247.99 102.25,-241.99 108.25,-235.99 114.25,-235.99 114.25,-235.99 244.6,-235.99 244.6,-235.99 250.6,-235.99 256.6,-241.99 256.6,-247.99 256.6,-247.99 256.6,-274.07 256.6,-274.07 256.6,-280.07 250.6,-286.07 244.6,-286.07\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"131.05\" y=\"-271.58\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Is it fixing a \u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start