>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,2\" d=\"M175.39,-62.61C175.39,-56.67 175.39,-49.97 175.39,-43.64\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"177.49,-43.96 175.39,-37.96 173.29,-43.96 177.49,-43.96\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"think-fvz1po","type":"think","bucket":"policy","lo":49,"scenario":"Three proposals land on the CFO’s desk: (a) switch {a|inventory} to FIFO because, with rising prices, it gives a higher profit and the bonus pool is looking thin; (b) adopt a new SFRS(I) that becomes effective this year; (c) write a policy for leasing out property, which the company has never done before.","prompt":"Which of these is a valid change in accounting policy?","answer":["(a) Not valid on that reason. A policy may change only if a standard **requires** it or the change gives **reliable and more relevant** information. A fatter bonus pool is neither.","(b) Valid: **required by an SFRS(I)**. Follow its transitional provisions.","(c) Not a change in policy at all. A policy for transactions that **didn’t happen before** (or differ in substance, or were immaterial) is just a new policy. There is nothing to restate."],"sect":2,"part":"Policy change: retrospective"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"policy","lo":49,"title":"When may a policy change?","body":["Once a policy is chosen, **consistency** is the default, so users can compare one year with the next. This rule says when you may break it, and what doesn’t even count as a change.","Only if the change:","1. is **required by an SFRS(I)**, or","2. results in **reliable and more relevant** information.","**Not** a change in policy:","- a policy for transactions that **differ in substance** from before","- a policy for transactions that **didn’t happen before**, or were immaterial","**Exception:** first switching to the **revaluation model** for {a|PPE} or {a|intangibles} **is** a policy change, but it is accounted for as a revaluation under SFRS(I) 1-16 / 1-38, **not** retrospectively under 1-8.","! “The new policy gives a higher profit” is never a valid reason. The test is reliable and more relevant information, or a standard requiring it."],"id":"rule-8391nu","sect":2,"part":"Policy change: retrospective"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"policy","lo":49,"q":"In which situation may an entity voluntarily change an accounting policy?","options":[{"t":"When the new policy results in a higher reported profit","why":"Profit is not a test. The change must give reliable and more relevant information."},{"t":"When the new policy results in reliable and more relevant information","ok":true,"why":"That is the test for a voluntary change (the other allowed reason is that a standard requires it)."},{"t":"Whenever the directors prefer the new policy","why":"Preference alone is not enough. Consistency is the default."},{"t":"When the entity starts a type of transaction it has never had before","why":"That is not a change in policy at all: it is adopting a policy for something new."}],"id":"mcq-2acg1b","sect":2,"part":"Policy change: retrospective"},{"id":"think-qdib81","type":"think","bucket":"policy","lo":50,"scenario":"In 20X5 a company switches from FIFO to weighted average (WA). Its 20X5 statements show 20X4 comparatives. {a|Inventory} under each method: 31 Dec 20X3 FIFO $42,000, WA $40,000; 31 Dec 20X4 FIFO $50,000, WA $46,000. Tax is 17%.","prompt":"“Apply it as if it had always applied.” What does that mean for the 20X4 comparatives, and for the years before 20X4?","answer":["**Before 20X4:** under WA, {a|closing inventory} at 31 Dec 20X3 would have been 2,000 lower, so cumulative profits to that date 2,000 lower before tax. Those years are not presented, so their effect goes to **{e|opening retained earnings} at 1 Jan 20X4**, the earliest period presented: 2,000 × 83% = **1,660**.","**20X4:** the comparative is restated. {x|Cost of sales} rises by 4,000 − 2,000 = 2,000 ({a|closing inventory} is 4,000 lower, {a|opening inventory} 2,000 lower), so 20X4 profit falls by 1,660 after tax.","**20X5:** nothing from the past touches this year’s profit. It just uses WA."],"sect":2,"part":"Policy change: retrospective"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"policy","lo":50,"title":"Retrospective application","body":["So a valid policy change must make every year shown look as if the new rule had always applied. Here is how that is done, and where the {e|opening retained earnings} adjustment comes from.","1. If the new policy comes from a standard with **transitional provisions**, follow them.","2. Otherwise, apply it **retrospectively**: adjust the **{e|opening balance} of each affected {e|component of equity}** for the **earliest prior period presented**, and restate the comparatives **as if the new policy had always applied**.","- **Impracticable?** Apply it from the earliest period for which it is practicable. Impracticable means you can’t do it after every reasonable effort.","- **Third statement of financial position:** present one as at the **start of the preceding period** when a retrospective change has a **material** effect on it.","+ The current year’s profit never carries the effect of earlier years. That goes to {e|opening retained earnings}.","! The {e|opening retained earnings} adjustment is for years **before** the earliest period presented, and it is **after tax**."],"id":"rule-5crxus","sect":2,"part":"Policy change: retrospective"},{"id":"worked-xki74s","type":"worked","bucket":"policy","lo":50,"title":"Worked example: FIFO → weighted average, with tax at 17%","q":"A company switches its {a|inventory} cost formula from FIFO to weighted average (WA) in 20X5; its statements show 20X4 comparatives. {a|Inventory} is lower under WA by 2,000 at 31 Dec 20X3 and by 4,000 at 31 Dec 20X4. Tax is 17%, booked to {l|deferred tax}. Restate, and record the adjustment at 1 Jan 20X5.","intro":["A typical exam question: {a|inventory} under the old and new formula at two year-ends, a tax rate, then “record the adjustment” or “by how much is the comparative restated?”. Remember two things:","- Years **before** the earliest period presented go to {e|opening retained earnings}, **net of tax**.","- The comparative year is **restated**; the current year’s profit takes **none** of the past.","Then follow these steps: opening retained earnings → restated comparative profit → cumulative effect → entry."],"steps":[{"h":"Adjust opening retained earnings at 1 Jan 20X4.","t":["Only the years before 20X4, the earliest period presented: the gap at 31 Dec 20X3, 2,000 before tax.","{l|Tax effect} 2,000 × 17% = {l|340}, so {e|opening retained earnings} fall by **{e|1,660}**."]},{"h":"Restate the 20X4 profit.","t":["{x|Cost of sales} rises by 4,000 − 2,000 = **{x|2,000}** ({a|closing inventory} 4,000 lower, {a|opening inventory} 2,000 lower).","{x|Tax expense} falls by 340, so 20X4 profit falls by **1,660**."]},{"h":"Add up the cumulative effect at 31 Dec 20X4.","t":["{a|Inventory} down **{a|4,000}**; {l|tax effect} 4,000 × 17% = **{l|680}**.","{e|Retained earnings} down {e|1,660} + 1,660 = **{e|3,320}**."]},{"h":"Record the adjustment at 1 Jan 20X5.","t":"This brings the books onto the new policy. The 20X4 comparatives in the statements are restated to match.","je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":3320,"el":"e"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":680,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Inventory","amount":4000,"el":"a"}],"after":"Notice what is missing: 20X5 {x|cost of sales}. The past goes through {e|retained earnings}, so this year’s profit is clean. (Your notes book the tax effect to {l|deferred tax}, as here.)"}],"sect":2,"part":"Policy change: retrospective"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"policy","lo":50,"title":"Where the 4,000 goes","intro":"The worked example as a flow. Two slices of the past, one destination: {e|retained earnings}, net of tax.","notes":{"d3":"Every year before the earliest period presented, rolled into one number: {a|closing inventory} at 31 Dec 20X3 is 2,000 lower under WA.","d4":"In 20X4 the gap widens from 2,000 to 4,000, so 20X4 {x|cost of sales} is 2,000 higher.","ore":"2,000 − tax 340 = 1,660 off {e|retained earnings} at 1 Jan 20X4.","cmp":"The restated 20X4 comparative shows profit 1,660 lower.","cum":"1,660 + 1,660 = 3,320: the {e|opening retained earnings} adjustment in the 20X5 books.","sfp":"The other side of the entry: {a|Inventory} down, and the {l|tax effect}.","cur":"None of this goes through 20X5 profit or loss."},"walk":[{"nodes":["d3","ore"],"text":"Years before 20X4 → **{e|opening retained earnings}** of the earliest period presented: −1,660."},{"nodes":["d4","cmp"],"text":"20X4 itself → the **restated comparative** profit: −1,660."},{"nodes":["cum","sfp"],"text":"Together: {e|retained earnings} at 31 Dec 20X4 down 3,320, matching {a|inventory} down 4,000 less {l|deferred tax} 680."},{"nodes":["cur"],"text":"And 20X5 profit takes **none** of it."}],"caption":"Before the earliest period → {e|opening RE}. Comparative year → restated profit. Current year → nothing.","id":"diagram-146gln","sect":2,"part":"Policy change: retrospective","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:640px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 644.00 123.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 112.44)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-112.44 633.17,-112.44 633.17,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>d3\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M114.78,-99.82C114.78,-99.82 12,-99.82 12,-99.82 6,-99.82 0,-93.82 0,-87.82 0,-87.82 0,-75.82 0,-75.82 0,-69.82 6,-63.82 12,-63.82 12,-63.82 114.78,-63.82 114.78,-63.82 120.78,-63.82 126.78,-69.82 126.78,-75.82 126.78,-75.82 126.78,-87.82 126.78,-87.82 126.78,-93.82 120.78,-9