 189.15,-6 189.15,-12 189.15,-12 189.15,-32.64 189.15,-32.64 189.15,-38.64 183.15,-44.64 177.15,-44.64\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"128.39\" y=\"-29.87\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Change in accounting\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"128.39\" y=\"-17.87\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">policy: retrospective\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"128.39\" y=\"-5.87\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">application\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge12\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>n5&#45;&gt;pol\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M183.33,-103.8C172.29,-87.63 158.44,-67.34 147.41,-51.18\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"149.15,-50 144.03,-46.23 145.68,-52.37 149.15,-50\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"185.44\" y=\"-70.64\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> Yes \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node13\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>bad\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M333.78,-40.32C333.78,-40.32 219,-40.32 219,-40.32 213,-40.32 207,-34.32 207,-28.32 207,-28.32 207,-16.32 207,-16.32 207,-10.32 213,-4.32 219,-4.32 219,-4.32 333.78,-4.32 333.78,-4.32 339.78,-4.32 345.78,-10.32 345.78,-16.32 345.78,-16.32 345.78,-28.32 345.78,-28.32 345.78,-34.32 339.78,-40.32 333.78,-40.32\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"276.39\" y=\"-23.87\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Not allowed: keep the old\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"276.39\" y=\"-11.87\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">policy\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge13\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>n5&#45;&gt;bad\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M221.45,-103.8C233.58,-86.03 249.1,-63.3 260.55,-46.52\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"262.07,-48.02 263.72,-41.89 258.6,-45.66 262.07,-48.02\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"293.33\" y=\"-81.89\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> No: it just gives\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"293.33\" y=\"-70.64\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">a nicer profit, or\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"293.33\" y=\"-59.39\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">the CFO prefers it \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"error","lo":50,"q":"When must an entity present a third statement of financial position?","options":[{"t":"Whenever it changes an accounting estimate","why":"Estimates are prospective. Nothing about past balances changes."},{"t":"When a retrospective policy change or restatement materially affects the balances at the start of the preceding period","ok":true,"why":"Then a statement of financial position as at the start of the preceding period is presented as well."},{"t":"Whenever any prior period error is corrected, however small","why":"Only if the start of the preceding period is **materially** affected. An error that began in the comparative year doesn’t touch that date."},{"t":"Only when a new SFRS(I) is adopted","why":"Voluntary policy changes and error restatements can trigger it too."}],"id":"mcq-wqca6h","sect":4,"part":"Prior period error: restate"},{"type":"map","id":"map-5-disclose","bucket":"disclose","sect":5,"part":"Disclosure"},{"id":"think-1fogn3","type":"think","bucket":"disclose","lo":51,"scenario":"You are an analyst. Last year’s profit in this year’s report is different from the profit you saw last year. In another company, {x|depreciation} jumped this year with no restatement at all.","prompt":"What would you want each company to tell you in the notes?","answer":["**What** happened (the nature of the change or error), and **how much** it moved each line item, for each period shown.","For a voluntary policy change, also **why** the new policy gives reliable and more relevant information.","For an estimate change, the nature and amount of the effect this year, and the expected effect on future years (or that it can’t be estimated)."],"sect":5,"part":"Disclosure"},{"id":"visual-fj3yrv","type":"visual","bucket":"disclose","lo":51,"title":"Disclosure checklist","html":"\u003cp>What the notes must tell that analyst, row by row. Skim it now; the next page boils it down to a pattern.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tbl-wrap\">\u003ctable class=\"tbl reveal-rows\">\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Change\u003c/th>\u003cth>Disclose\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>New standard applied\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Title of the standard; that its transitional provisions were followed; nature of the change; adjustment for \u003cstrong>each line item\u003c/strong> affected, current and each prior period presented (and EPS); adjustment for periods before those presented\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Voluntary policy change\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nature of the change; \u003cstrong>why\u003c/strong> the new policy gives reliable and more relevant information; adjustment for each line item, current and prior periods; adjustment for earlier periods; if impracticable, why and how applied\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Standard issued, not yet effective\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>That fact, and known or reasonably estimable information about its likely impact\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Change in estimate\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nature and \u003cstrong>amount\u003c/strong> of the effect on the current period and expected effect on future periods; if the future effect can’t be estimated, say so\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n      \u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003cb>Prior period error\u003c/b>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nature of the error; correction for \u003cstrong>each line item\u003c/strong> in each prior period presented (and EPS); amount at the start of the earliest period presented; if impracticable, why and how corrected\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\n    \u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"cap\">Every row answers \u003cstrong>what\u003c/strong> happened and \u003cstrong>how much\u003c/strong>. Only the voluntary policy change also has to explain \u003cstrong>why\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>","sect":5,"part":"Disclosure"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"disclose","lo":51,"title":"Disclosure: the pattern","body":["The checklist looks long, but every row follows one pattern. Learn the pattern and you can rebuild any row in the exam.","1. **What** happened: the nature of the change or error.","2. **Why** (voluntary policy changes only): why it gives reliable and more relevant information.","3. **How much**: line by line, for each period presented, plus the amount relating to earlier periods.","- These disclosures are **not repeated** in later periods’ statements.","- A standard issued but **not yet effective** is disclosed too, with its likely impact if known.","! An estimate change does **not** restate comparatives, and does not need a “why it is more relevant” justification. It needs the nature and the amount."],"id":"rule-1i6sgz","sect":5,"part":"Disclosure"},{"id":"mcq-6t672c","type":"mcq","bucket":"disclose","lo":51,"q":"An entity revises the useful life of its {a|fleet} this year. Which disclosure is required?","options":[{"t":"Restated comparatives showing the effect on the prior year","why":"An estimate change is prospective. Nothing is restated."},{"t":"Why the new useful life gives reliable and more relevant information","why":"That justification is for a voluntary change in accounting **policy**."},{"t":"The nature and amount of the change affecting the current period and expected to affect future periods","ok":true,"why":"That is the estimate-change disclosure (or a statement that the future effect can’t be estimated)."},{"t":"Nothing, because estimates are revised routinely","why":"The nature and amount of the change must be disclosed."}],"sect":5,"part":"Disclosure"},{"type":"examtypes","id":"examtypes","bucket":"wrap","sect":6,"part":"Wrap-up"},{"type":"summary","lo":51,"title":"Policies, estimates and errors on one page","rows":[["Selecting a policy?","SFRS(I) first; else judgement: similar standards → Framework → (optionally) others."],["When can a policy change?","Required by a standard, or gives reliable and more relevant information. Never “higher profit”."],["Policy change applied how?","Transitional provisions, otherwise retrospectively: {e|opening equity} of the earliest period presented, and comparatives restated."],["What is an accounting estimate?","A monetary amount subject to measurement uncertainty."],["Estimate change applied how?","Prospectively: current and future periods, through profit or loss. No restatement."],["{x|New depreciation} after a revision?","({a|Carrying amount} − new residual) ÷ remaining life. No catch-up."],["Depreciation method change?","Estimate (prospective)."],["Inventory cost formula change?","Policy (retrospective)."],["Can’t tell policy from estimate?","Treat it as an estimate."],["Prior period error?","Retrospective restatement, net of tax, through {e|opening retained earnings}. Third SFP if the start of the prior period is materially affected."],["Error found before this year’s statements are authorised?","Current-period error: just correct it."],["First revaluation of {a|PPE}?","Policy change, but applied as a revaluation under 1-16, not retrospectively."],["Disclose?","What, how much (line by line), and why for voluntary policy changes. Not repeated later."]],"id":"summary-iofhyu","bucket":"wrap","sect":6,"part":"Wrap-up"}],"quiz":{"cases":{"kranji":{"name":"Kranji Manufacturing","text":["Kranji Manufacturing Pte Ltd (Kranji) prepares financial statements for the year ending 31 December 20X5, with comparatives for 20X4. The tax rate is 17%, and tax effects are recognised in {l|deferred tax}.","(1) From 1 January 20X5, Kranji changed its {a|inventory} cost formula from FIFO to weighted average, because weighted average gives reliable and more relevant information. {a|Inventory} under each formula: 31 December 20X3: FIFO $180,000, weighted average $171,000; 31 December 20X4: FIFO $210,000, weighted average $198,000.","(2) A {a|machine} was bought on 1 January 20X2 for $360,000, with a useful life of 12 years and no residual value (straight-line). On 1 January 20X5, Kranji revised the remaining useful life to 6 years and the residual value to $18,000.","(3) From 1 January 20X5, Kranji changed the depreciation method for its {a|delivery vans} from straight-line to reducing balance, to reflect the pattern in which their benefits are consumed."]},"jurong":{"name":"Jurong Foods","text":["Jurong Foods Pte Ltd (Jurong) is preparing its financial statements for the year ending 31 December 20X5, with comparatives for 20X4. The statements will be authorised for issue in March 20X6. The tax rate is 17%, and tax effects are recognised in {l|deferred tax}.","(a) On 1 January 20X3, Jurong spent $50,000 on {x|repairs} to its production line. The amount was capitalised as {a|equipment} and depreciated straight-line over 5 years with no residual value. It should have been expensed. The 20X3 and 20X4 statements have been issued.","(b) On 10 January 20X6, Jurong found that a $15,000 {i|sales} invoice dated 20 December 20X5 had been posted twice."]}},"questions":[{"id":"q1","case":"kranji","lo":50,"type":"classify-change","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify how Kranji should account for the change in depreciation method in (3).","options":[{"t":"As a change in accounting policy, applied retrospectively with 20X4 restated","pit":"method-as-policy","why":"A depreciation method is a technique for estimating how benefits are consumed: an estimate, not a policy."},{"t":"As a change in accounting estimate, applied prospectively from 1 January 20X5","ok":true,"why":"Changes in depreciation method are changes in estimate, so they apply from the date of change with no restatement."},{"t":"As a prior period error, with {e|opening retained earnings} adjusted","why":"Nothing was wrong with the old method given what was known; this is new information, not an error."},{"t":"As a change in accounting policy, applied prospectively because restatement is impracticable","pit":"method-as-policy","why":"It is not a policy change in the first place, and nothing suggests restatement is impracticable."}]},{"id":"q2","case":"kranji","lo":50,"type":"estimate-calc","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8 and SFRS(I) 1-16, identify which of the following is the {x|depreciation charge} on the {a|machine} in (2) for the year ending 31 December 20X5.","options":[{"t":"$30,000","why":"That is the old {x|charge}. The revised life and residual value apply from 1 January 20X5."},{"t":"$38,000","pit":"catch-up","why":"That spreads the original cost (less residual) over the new total life of 9 years, as if the new estimate had always applied. Start from the {a|carrying amount} instead."},{"t":"$42,000","ok":true,"why":"{a|Carrying amount} 360,000 − 3 × 30,000 = 270,000. (270,000 − 18,000) ÷ 6 = 42,000."},{"t":"$66,000","pit":"catch-up","why":"That adds a catch-up for the years already gone. An estimate change has no catch-up."}]},{"id":"q3","case":"kranji","lo":50,"type":"policy-amount","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify by how much Kranji should reduce {e|retained earnings} at 1 January 20X4 in its restated financial statements.","options":[{"t":"$9,000","pit":"ignore-tax","why":"That is the pre-tax difference. {e|Retained earnings} takes the after-tax amount."},{"t":"$7,470","ok":true,"why":"Only the years before 20X4 go to {e|opening retained earnings} at 1 January 20X4: 9,000 × 83% = 7,470."},{"t":"$9,960","pit":"wrong-period","why":"That is the cumulative effect at 31 December 20X4. It includes 20X4 itself, which is shown in the restated 20X4 profit."},{"t":"$0, because the change is made in 20X5","pit":"no-restate","why":"A policy change is applied retrospectively, so the {e|opening balance} of the earliest period presented is adjusted."}]},{"id":"q4","case":"kranji","lo":50,"type":"policy-amount","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify which of the following journal entries Kranji should record on 1 January 20X5 to apply the new cost formula.","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Cost of sales","amount":12000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Inventory","amount":12000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"change-to-pl","why":"The effect of earlier years must not go through 20X5 profit. It goes to {e|opening retained earnings}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":12000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Inventory","amount":12000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"ignore-tax","why":"This ignores the tax effect of the lower {a|inventory}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":9960,"el":"e"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":2040,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Inventory","amount":12000,"el":"a"}],"ok":true,"why":"Cumulative difference at 31 December 20X4 is 12,000; tax 2,040; {e|retained earnings} down 9,960."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":7470,"el":"e"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":1530,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Inventory","amount":9000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"wrong-period","why":"This uses the 31 December 20X3 difference. By 1 January 20X5 the books must reflect the cumulative difference at 31 December 20X4."}]},{"id":"q5","case":"kranji","lo":50,"type":"policy-amount","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify how the change in cost formula affects Kranji’s restated profit for 20X4.","options":[{"t":"No effect: 20X4 profit is presented as originally reported","pit":"no-restate","why":"Comparatives are restated as if the new policy had always applied."},{"t":"Decrease of $3,000","pit":"ignore-tax","why":"That is the pre-tax effect on {x|cost of sales}. {x|Tax} falls too."},{"t":"Decrease of $2,490","ok":true,"why":"{x|Cost of sales} rises by 12,000 − 9,000 = 3,000; after tax, profit falls by 2,490."},{"t":"Decrease of $9,960","pit":"wrong-period","why":"That is the cumulative effect up to 31 December 20X4, including years before 20X4."}]},{"id":"q6","case":"jurong","lo":50,"type":"classify-change","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify how Jurong should treat the double-posted invoice in (b).","options":[{"t":"As a prior period error, restating the 20X4 comparatives","why":"The invoice is from 20X5, and 20X5’s statements are not yet issued. Nothing in 20X4 is wrong."},{"t":"Correct it in the 20X5 financial statements before they are authorised","ok":true,"why":"An error found in the current period, before the statements are authorised, is simply corrected. It is not a prior period error."},{"t":"Correct it in 20X6, when it was discovered","why":"It belongs to 20X5, and the 20X5 statements can still be corrected."},{"t":"As a change in accounting estimate, applied prospectively","why":"A double posting is a mistake, not new information about an uncertain amount."}]},{"id":"q7","case":"jurong","lo":50,"type":"error-restate","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify the adjustment to Jurong’s {e|retained earnings} at 1 January 20X4 for the error in (a).","options":[{"t":"Decrease of $40,000","pit":"ignore-tax","why":"That is the pre-tax overstatement of 20X3 profit. {e|Retained earnings} takes the after-tax amount."},{"t":"Decrease of $33,200","ok":true,"why":"20X3 profit was overstated by 50,000 − 10,000 = 40,000; after tax, 33,200."},{"t":"Decrease of $24,900","pit":"wrong-period","why":"That is the cumulative effect at 31 December 20X4. The 20X4 part is shown in the restated 20X4 profit."},{"t":"Decrease of $41,500","why":"That treats the whole repair, after tax, as the 20X3 overstatement, ignoring the 10,000 of {x|depreciation} already charged in 20X3."}]},{"id":"q8","case":"jurong","lo":50,"type":"error-restate","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify how correcting the error in (a) affects Jurong’s restated profit for 20X4.","options":[{"t":"Increase of $8,300","ok":true,"why":"20X4 included 10,000 of {x|depreciation} on an {a|asset} that should not exist. Removing it raises profit by 8,300 after tax."},{"t":"Decrease of $8,300","why":"Wrong direction. Removing a {x|depreciation} charge increases profit."},{"t":"No effect: the error happened in 20X3","pit":"no-restate","why":"The error also affected 20X4, through the {x|depreciation} charged on the capitalised {a|repair}."},{"t":"Decrease of $41,500","pit":"change-to-pl","why":"The {x|repair} belongs to 20X3, not 20X4. Its effect goes to {e|opening retained earnings} at 1 January 20X4."}]},{"id":"q9","case":"jurong","lo":50,"type":"error-restate","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify which journal entry Jurong should record in its 20X5 books to correct the error in (a).","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Repairs expense","amount":30000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":20000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Plant and equipment","amount":50000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"change-to-pl","why":"A prior period error is not corrected through 20X5 profit."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":30000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":20000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Plant and equipment","amount":50000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"ignore-tax","why":"This ignores the tax effect."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":24900,"el":"e"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":5100,"el":"l"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":20000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Plant and equipment","amount":50000,"el":"a"}],"ok":true,"why":"Remove the {a|equipment} (50,000) and its {a|accumulated depreciation} (20,000). The overstated {a|carrying amount} of 30,000 is split into {l|deferred tax} 5,100 and {e|retained earnings} 24,900."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":41500,"el":"e"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":8500,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Plant and equipment","amount":50000,"el":"a"}],"why":"This leaves the {a|accumulated depreciation} on an {a|asset} that no longer exists, and overstates the {e|retained earnings} effect."}]},{"id":"q10","case":"jurong","lo":50,"type":"third-sfp","q":"Assuming the error in (a) is material, identify which statements of financial position Jurong should present in its 20X5 financial statements.","options":[{"t":"At 31 December 20X5 and 31 December 20X4 only","why":"The error began before 20X4, so the balances at 1 January 20X4 are restated too, and a third statement is needed."},{"t":"At 31 December 20X5, 31 December 20X4 and 1 January 20X4","ok":true,"why":"A material restatement of the balances at the start of the preceding period requires a third statement of financial position at that date."},{"t":"At 31 December 20X5, 31 December 20X4 and 1 January 20X3","why":"The third statement is at the start of the **preceding** period, 1 January 20X4, not the date the error began."},{"t":"At 31 December 20X5 only, with the error explained in the notes","pit":"no-restate","why":"Comparatives are always presented, and here they are restated."}]},{"id":"q11","lo":49,"type":"when-change","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, which of the following is a valid reason for an entity to change an accounting policy voluntarily?","options":[{"t":"The new policy will increase reported profit for the year","pit":"profit-reason","why":"Profit is not a test. The change must give reliable and more relevant information."},{"t":"The new policy results in the financial statements providing reliable and more relevant information","ok":true,"why":"That is the test for a voluntary change."},{"t":"The new policy is preferred by the majority shareholder","pit":"profit-reason","why":"Preference is not a test."},{"t":"The new policy is used by the entity’s main competitor","why":"Industry practice can inform judgement where no standard applies, but it is not by itself a reason to change a policy."}]},{"id":"q12","lo":49,"type":"when-change","q":"A manufacturer begins leasing out a {a|building} it has never used before, and adopts the fair value model for this new {a|investment property}. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, how is the adoption treated?","options":[{"t":"As a change in accounting policy, applied retrospectively","pit":"new-txn-policy","why":"There was no previous policy for investment property. Adopting one for transactions that didn’t happen before is not a change in policy."},{"t":"It is not a change in accounting policy: the policy is applied to the new transactions from now on","ok":true,"why":"A policy for transactions that did not occur before (or differ in substance) is not a change in policy."},{"t":"As a change in accounting estimate, applied prospectively","why":"Choosing a measurement model is a policy choice, not an estimate. It just isn’t a *change* here."},{"t":"As a prior period error, because it should have been adopted earlier","why":"There were no such transactions earlier, so nothing was wrong."}]},{"id":"q13","lo":49,"type":"reval-first","q":"An entity that has always used the cost model for its {a|office buildings} adopts the revaluation model in 20X5. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8 and SFRS(I) 1-16, how should the change be accounted for?","options":[{"t":"Retrospectively, restating 20X4 and {e|opening retained earnings} at 1 January 20X4","pit":"reval-retro","why":"First adoption of the revaluation model is the exception: it is not applied retrospectively under 1-8."},{"t":"As a revaluation in 20X5 under SFRS(I) 1-16, with no restatement of earlier periods","ok":true,"why":"It is a change in policy, but accounted for as a revaluation under 1-16 from now on."},{"t":"As a change in accounting estimate","pit":"reval-retro","why":"It changes the measurement basis, so it is a policy change, with its own special treatment."},{"t":"It is not permitted once the cost model has been chosen","why":"It is permitted if it gives reliable and more relevant information."}]},{"id":"q14","lo":51,"type":"disclosure","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, which of the following must be disclosed for a change in accounting estimate?","options":[{"t":"Restated comparative figures for each line item affected","pit":"estimate-retro","why":"Estimates are not restated. That disclosure belongs to policy changes and errors."},{"t":"The nature and amount of the change that affects the current period and is expected to affect future periods","ok":true,"why":"Or, if the future effect can’t be estimated, that fact."},{"t":"The reasons why the new estimate provides reliable and more relevant information","why":"That reason is required for a voluntary change in accounting **policy**."},{"t":"The amount of the adjustment relating to periods before those presented","pit":"estimate-retro","why":"There is no such adjustment for an estimate change."}]},{"id":"q15","lo":50,"type":"classify-change","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, which of the following should be accounted for retrospectively?","options":[{"t":"Revising the residual value of a {a|fleet of trucks}","pit":"estimate-retro","why":"Residual value is an estimate: prospective."},{"t":"Increasing the {l|warranty provision} rate after a rise in claims","pit":"estimate-retro","why":"A {l|warranty provision} is an estimate: prospective."},{"t":"Correcting last year’s {a|inventory} count, which omitted a warehouse that the staff forgot to count","ok":true,"why":"An oversight in issued statements is a prior period error: retrospective restatement."},{"t":"Changing from straight-line to units-of-production depreciation","pit":"method-as-policy","why":"A depreciation method change is an estimate: prospective."}]},{"id":"q16","lo":51,"type":"disclosure","q":"An entity voluntarily changes an accounting policy. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, which disclosure is required in addition to the nature of the change and the amounts of the adjustment?","options":[{"t":"The reasons why applying the new policy provides reliable and more relevant information","ok":true,"why":"Voluntary changes must be justified on exactly that test."},{"t":"The title of the standard and its transitional provisions","why":"That applies when the change comes from initially applying a standard, not a voluntary change."},{"t":"A statement that the disclosure will be repeated in each later year","pit":"repeat-disclosure","why":"These disclosures are not repeated in later periods’ statements."},{"t":"The effect on future periods only, since the change is prospective","pit":"estimate-retro","why":"A policy change is retrospective. The future-effect disclosure belongs to estimates."}]}]},"exam":{"cases":{"woodlands":{"name":"Woodlands Logistics","text":["Woodlands Logistics Pte Ltd (Woodlands) prepares financial statements for the year ending 31 December 20X6, with comparatives for 20X5. The tax rate is 17%, and tax effects are recognised in {l|deferred tax}.","Woodlands bought a {a|fleet of trucks} on 1 January 20X3 for $1,200,000. It was depreciated straight-line over 10 years with a residual value of $200,000. On 1 January 20X6, after a review of maintenance records, management revised the total useful life to 8 years and the residual value to $120,000.","While preparing the 20X6 statements, the accountant found that {x|depreciation} of $60,000 on a {a|warehouse} for the year ended 31 December 20X5 had never been recorded. The 20X5 statements were issued in March 20X6 and reported a profit after tax of $850,000. The warehouse was bought on 1 January 20X5."]},"changi":{"name":"Changi Retail","text":["Changi Retail Pte Ltd (Changi) prepares financial statements for the year ending 31 December 20X6, with comparatives for 20X5. The tax rate is 17%, and tax effects are recognised in {l|deferred tax}.","From 1 January 20X6, Changi changed its {a|inventory} cost formula from weighted average to FIFO, because FIFO provides reliable and more relevant information. {a|Inventory} under each formula: 31 December 20X4: weighted average $500,000, FIFO $520,000; 31 December 20X5: weighted average $640,000, FIFO $675,000.","As originally reported, profit after tax for 20X5 was $400,000 and {e|retained earnings} at 1 January 20X5 were $2,000,000. The effect of the change is material."]}},"questions":[{"id":"x1","case":"woodlands","lo":50,"type":"estimate-calc","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8 and SFRS(I) 1-16, identify the {x|depreciation charge} on the {a|fleet} for the year ending 31 December 20X6.","options":[{"t":"$135,000","pit":"catch-up","why":"That spreads {a|original cost} less the new residual over the full 8 years, as if the new estimate had always applied."},{"t":"$156,000","ok":true,"why":"Old {x|depreciation} (1,200,000 − 200,000) ÷ 10 = 100,000; {a|carrying amount} at 1 January 20X6 = 900,000. Remaining life 8 − 3 = 5 years: (900,000 − 120,000) ÷ 5 = 156,000."},{"t":"$261,000","pit":"catch-up","why":"That adds a catch-up for 20X3–20X5. Estimate changes have none."},{"t":"$97,500","pit":"remaining-life","why":"That divides by the total life of 8 years. The {a|carrying amount} is spread over the **remaining** 5."}]},{"id":"x2","case":"woodlands","lo":50,"type":"estimate-calc","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8 and SFRS(I) 1-16, identify the {a|carrying amount} of the {a|fleet} at 31 December 20X6.","options":[{"t":"$800,000","why":"That uses the old estimate for 20X6 as well. The revision applies from 1 January 20X6."},{"t":"$660,000","pit":"estimate-retro","why":"That applies the new estimate to all four years from cost, i.e. retrospectively."},{"t":"$802,500","pit":"remaining-life","why":"That deducts a charge based on the total life rather than the remaining life."},{"t":"$744,000","ok":true,"why":"{a|Carrying amount} at 1 January 20X6 900,000 − 20X6 {x|depreciation} 156,000 = 744,000."}]},{"id":"x3","case":"woodlands","lo":50,"type":"error-restate","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify which journal entry Woodlands should record in its 20X6 books to correct the omitted {x|warehouse depreciation}.","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Depreciation expense","amount":60000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":60000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"change-to-pl","why":"That charges 20X5’s {x|depreciation} to 20X6 profit. A prior period error is corrected through {e|opening retained earnings}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":60000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":60000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"ignore-tax","why":"This ignores the tax effect."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":49800,"el":"e"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":10200,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":60000,"el":"a"}],"ok":true,"why":"60,000 less {l|deferred tax} 10,200 = 49,800 off {e|opening retained earnings}; the 20X5 comparatives are restated."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Depreciation expense","amount":49800,"el":"x"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":10200,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Accumulated depreciation","amount":60000,"el":"a"}],"pit":"change-to-pl","why":"The tax is right but the debit lands in 20X6 profit. It belongs in {e|opening retained earnings}."}]},{"id":"x4","case":"woodlands","lo":50,"type":"error-restate","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify the restated profit after tax for 20X5 shown as a comparative in Woodlands’ 20X6 financial statements.","options":[{"t":"$850,000","pit":"no-restate","why":"Prior period errors are corrected by restating the comparatives."},{"t":"$790,000","pit":"ignore-tax","why":"That deducts the {x|depreciation} without the {x|tax} saving."},{"t":"$800,200","ok":true,"why":"850,000 − (60,000 − 10,200) = 800,200."},{"t":"$899,800","why":"Wrong direction: missing {x|depreciation} means profit was **overstated**."}]},{"id":"x5","case":"woodlands","lo":50,"type":"third-sfp","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-1 and SFRS(I) 1-8, is a third statement of financial position required as a result of the warehouse error?","options":[{"t":"Yes, at 1 January 20X5, because any prior period error requires one","why":"Only if the balances at the start of the preceding period are materially affected."},{"t":"No, because the error arose in 20X5, so the balances at 1 January 20X5 are not affected","ok":true,"why":"The warehouse was bought on 1 January 20X5 and the error is 20X5’s {x|depreciation}. Nothing at 1 January 20X5 changes."},{"t":"Yes, at 1 January 20X6, the start of the current period","why":"The third statement, when needed, is at the start of the preceding period (1 January 20X5)."},{"t":"No, because prior period errors are corrected prospectively","pit":"change-to-pl","why":"Errors are corrected retrospectively. It is just that this one doesn’t touch 1 January 20X5."}]},{"id":"x6","case":"changi","lo":50,"type":"policy-amount","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify the restated {e|retained earnings} at 1 January 20X5 in Changi’s 20X6 financial statements.","options":[{"t":"$2,000,000","pit":"no-restate","why":"A policy change is applied retrospectively, so the {e|opening balance} of the earliest period presented is adjusted."},{"t":"$2,020,000","pit":"ignore-tax","why":"That adds the pre-tax difference. {e|Retained earnings} takes the after-tax amount."},{"t":"$2,016,600","ok":true,"why":"FIFO is higher by 20,000 at 31 December 20X4; after tax 16,600. 2,000,000 + 16,600 = 2,016,600."},{"t":"$2,029,050","pit":"wrong-period","why":"That uses the 31 December 20X5 difference, which includes 20X5 itself."}]},{"id":"x7","case":"changi","lo":50,"type":"policy-amount","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify Changi’s restated profit after tax for 20X5.","options":[{"t":"$400,000","pit":"no-restate","why":"Comparatives are restated under a retrospective policy change."},{"t":"$412,450","ok":true,"why":"20X5 effect: 35,000 − 20,000 = 15,000 lower {x|cost of sales}; after tax 12,450. 400,000 + 12,450 = 412,450."},{"t":"$415,000","pit":"ignore-tax","why":"That adds the pre-tax effect without the extra tax."},{"t":"$429,050","pit":"wrong-period","why":"That puts the whole cumulative effect into 20X5. The years before 20X5 go to {e|opening retained earnings}."}]},{"id":"x8","case":"changi","lo":50,"type":"policy-amount","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify which journal entry Changi should record on 1 January 20X6 to apply FIFO.","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Inventory","amount":35000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cost of sales","amount":35000,"el":"x"}],"pit":"change-to-pl","why":"That puts earlier years’ effect into 20X6 profit. It belongs in {e|opening retained earnings}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Inventory","amount":35000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":29050,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":5950,"el":"l"}],"ok":true,"why":"Cumulative difference at 31 December 20X5 is 35,000; tax 5,950; {e|retained earnings} up 29,050."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Inventory","amount":35000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":35000,"el":"e"}],"pit":"ignore-tax","why":"This ignores the tax effect of the higher {a|inventory}."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Inventory","amount":20000,"el":"a"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":16600,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Deferred tax liability","amount":3400,"el":"l"}],"pit":"wrong-period","why":"That uses the 31 December 20X4 difference. By 1 January 20X6 the books must carry the cumulative difference at 31 December 20X5."}]},{"id":"x9","case":"changi","lo":50,"type":"third-sfp","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-1, identify which statements of financial position Changi should present in its 20X6 financial statements.","options":[{"t":"At 31 December 20X6 and 31 December 20X5, with 20X5 as originally reported","pit":"no-restate","why":"The 20X5 comparatives must be restated."},{"t":"At 31 December 20X6 and 31 December 20X5 (restated) only","why":"The change materially affects the balances at 1 January 20X5, so a third statement is needed."},{"t":"At 31 December 20X6, 31 December 20X5 (restated) and 1 January 20X5 (restated)","ok":true,"why":"A material retrospective change to the start of the preceding period requires a third statement at that date."},{"t":"At 31 December 20X6, 31 December 20X5 (restated) and 1 January 20X6","why":"1 January 20X6 is the same as 31 December 20X5. The third statement is at the start of the preceding period."}]},{"id":"x10","case":"changi","lo":51,"type":"disclosure","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, which of the following is NOT a required disclosure for Changi’s change in cost formula?","options":[{"t":"The nature of the change in accounting policy","why":"This is required."},{"t":"The reasons why FIFO provides reliable and more relevant information","why":"This is required for a voluntary change."},{"t":"The amount of the adjustment for each financial statement line item affected, for 20X6 and 20X5","why":"This is required."},{"t":"The same disclosures again in the 20X7 financial statements","ok":true,"why":"Correct choice: these disclosures are not repeated in later periods’ statements."}]},{"id":"x11","lo":49,"type":"when-change","q":"No SFRS(I) specifically applies to a transaction. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, which source should management consider FIRST in developing an accounting policy?","options":[{"t":"The pronouncements of other standard-setting bodies","why":"These may be considered, but only after similar SFRS(I)s and the Conceptual Framework."},{"t":"Accepted industry practice","why":"Also optional, and only if consistent with the higher sources."},{"t":"SFRS(I)s dealing with similar and related issues","ok":true,"why":"First similar standards, then the Conceptual Framework, then (optionally) other sources."},{"t":"The policy that gives the highest profit","pit":"profit-reason","why":"The aim is relevant and reliable information, not profit."}]},{"id":"x12","lo":49,"type":"reval-first","q":"In 20X6 an entity adopts the revaluation model for its {a|land} for the first time; the {a|land}’s fair value exceeds its {a|carrying amount}. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8 and SFRS(I) 1-16, identify the correct treatment.","options":[{"t":"Restate the 20X5 comparatives and adjust {e|retained earnings} at 1 January 20X5","pit":"reval-retro","why":"First adoption of the revaluation model is not applied retrospectively under 1-8."},{"t":"Credit the increase to 20X6 profit or loss as a change in estimate","why":"It is a policy change, and a {i|revaluation increase} goes to OCI, not profit or loss."},{"t":"Recognise the increase in OCI and the {e|revaluation surplus} in 20X6, with no restatement","ok":true,"why":"It is accounted for as a revaluation under 1-16 from 20X6."},{"t":"Adjust {e|opening retained earnings} at 1 January 20X6 by the increase","pit":"reval-retro","why":"The increase is a {e|revaluation surplus} in OCI, not a {e|retained earnings} adjustment."}]},{"id":"x13","lo":50,"type":"classify-change","q":"An entity changes the valuation technique used to measure the fair value of its investment property, from the comparable-sales approach to an income approach, because better rental data is now available. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, how is this accounted for?","options":[{"t":"As a change in accounting policy, applied retrospectively","why":"The measurement basis (fair value) has not changed. Only the technique used to estimate it has."},{"t":"As a prior period error","why":"Nothing was wrong before. Better data is new information."},{"t":"As a change in accounting estimate, applied prospectively","ok":true,"why":"A change in a measurement technique used to make an estimate is a change in estimate (unless it corrects an error)."},{"t":"It is not permitted to change valuation technique","why":"A different technique may be used if it gives a better estimate."}]},{"id":"x14","lo":50,"type":"estimate-calc","q":"At 31 December 20X6, an entity’s {a|trade receivables} are $900,000 and its {a|allowance for doubtful debts} stands at $18,000 (2%). Following news of financial difficulty among its customers, it revises the {a|allowance} to 5% of {a|receivables}. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-8, identify the correct treatment.","options":[{"t":"Charge $27,000 to 20X6 profit or loss","ok":true,"why":"45,000 − 18,000 = 27,000. A change in estimate goes to profit or loss of the period of change."},{"t":"Debit $27,000 to {e|opening retained earnings} at 1 January 20X6","pit":"estimate-retro","why":"Estimate changes are prospective; {e|retained earnings} is not adjusted."},{"t":"Charge $45,000 to 20X6 profit or loss","why":"Only the increase is charged; 18,000 is already in the {a|allowance}."},{"t":"Restate 20X5 with the new percentage","pit":"estimate-retro","why":"The downturn is new information in 20X6. Nothing is restated."}]}]},"prepared":true},{"id":"events-after","code":"E2","title":"Events After the Reporting Period","subtitle":"SFRS(I) 1-10 · adjusting, non-adjusting and going concern","los":{"52":"Adjusting and non-adjusting events","53":"Disclosure of events after the reporting period","54":"Going concern issues after the reporting period"},"accounts":[{"name":"Trade receivables","el":"a"},{"name":"Allowance for doubtful debts","el":"a"},{"name":"Impairment loss on receivables","el":"x"},{"name":"Inventory","el":"a"},{"name":"Cost of sales","el":"x"},{"name":"Provision for legal claim","el":"l"},{"name":"Legal expense","el":"x"},{"name":"Dividend payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Retained earnings","el":"e"},{"name":"Share capital","el":"e"},{"name":"Tax payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Revenue","el":"i"},{"name":"Cash","el":"a"},{"name":"Loss on fire","el":"x"}],"steps":[{"id":"window","name":"The window: year end to authorisation","short":"Window"},{"id":"adjusting","name":"Adjusting events","short":"Adjusting"},{"id":"nonadj","name":"Non-adjusting events","short":"Non-adjust"},{"id":"dividends","name":"Dividends declared after year end","short":"Dividends"},{"id":"goingconcern","name":"Going concern","short":"Going conc."},{"id":"disclose","name":"Disclosure","short":"Disclosure"}],"map":{"window":{"label":"Reporting date → FS authorised by directors"},"adjusting":{"label":"Condition existed at year end → change the numbers","star":"The timing of the condition decides, not the timing of the event"},"nonadj":{"label":"New condition after year end → disclose if material"},"dividends":{"label":"Declared after year end: not a {l|liability}"},"goingconcern":{"label":"Liquidation decided → change the basis","star":"The one post-year-end event that changes the whole basis"},"disclose":{"label":"Authorisation date; nature + estimated effect"}},"preamble":["Welcome to the chapter about the awkward gap between the year end and the signatures. The books close on 31 December, but the directors don’t sign until March, and the world doesn’t stop for the auditors. Customers go bust, warehouses burn, lawsuits settle. Which of that news belongs in last year’s numbers?","This chapter isn’t about an {a|asset} or a {l|liability}, so it has its own route instead of the usual six stops:","1. **The window**: exactly which period are we talking about? (Directors’ signature, not the AGM.)","2. **Adjusting events**: news about something that was already true at year end. Change the numbers. ★ special","3. **Non-adjusting events**: genuinely new news. Leave the numbers; disclose if material.","4. **Dividends** declared after year end: the classic non-adjusting event, and an exam favourite.","5. **Going concern**: the one bit of new news that can tear up the whole basis of the FS. ★ special","6. **Disclosure**: what the notes must say.","+ The one question that answers most of the exam: **did the condition already exist at the reporting date?** Yes → adjust. No → don’t, and disclose if material. Never mind *when* the event happened."],"intros":{"window":"First stop: drawing the boundaries. Before we sort any events, we need to know which period SFRS(I) 1-10 covers at all. It is short: from the reporting date to the day the **directors** authorise the FS. Pretty vanilla, with one trap: the AGM date and a profit announcement don’t close anything.","adjusting":"Now that we know the window, what do we do with the news that arrives in it? ★ This is the special part, and the heart of the chapter: news that reveals a condition that **already existed** at year end changes the numbers. A customer going bust in February, stock sold below cost in January, a lawsuit settling: all of them rewrite December. The event can be late; the condition can’t.","nonadj":"The flip side. Some news is genuinely **new**: a fire, a market crash, a restructuring announced in February. However big, it doesn’t touch last year’s numbers; it goes in the notes if material. Mostly a list to learn, and the one trap is letting the size of an event bully you into adjusting.","dividends":"A non-adjusting event so popular it gets its own stop (practice Q35 and Q14 both use it). Declaring a dividend after year end creates no {l|liability} at year end, and a dividend is never an {x|expense}. Short, sweet and very examinable.","goingconcern":"We have sorted events one balance at a time. Now the big exception. ★ If, after year end, management decides to wind up, or has no realistic alternative, the whole going concern basis falls away: it is the one post-year-end event that changes **everything**. Below that bar, doubt just means disclosure. Knowing which side of the line you are on is practice Q50.","disclose":"Last stop: what the notes must say. Mostly common sense once you picture the reader: when were the FS signed and by whom, what happened afterwards, and roughly how much it will cost. The one to remember: “nature” alone isn’t enough; you need the estimated financial effect too."},"pitfalls":{"timing-of-event":"Deciding by when the event happened, instead of when the condition arose.","big-means-adjust":"Adjusting for a non-adjusting event (fire, market fall) because it is large.","disclose-only-adjusting":"Only disclosing an adjusting event instead of changing the numbers.","dividend-liability":"Recognising a dividend declared after year end as a {l|liability} at the reporting date.","dividend-expense":"Putting a dividend through profit or loss as an {x|expense}.","no-disclosure":"Saying a material non-adjusting event or a going concern doubt needs no disclosure (or can wait until next year).","after-authorisation":"Including events after the FS were authorised, or using the AGM or announcement date as the end of the window.","liquidation-basis":"Switching to a liquidation basis on mere uncertainty, without an intention to liquidate or no realistic alternative.","gc-contingent":"Treating going concern doubt as a contingent liability.","nrv-selling-costs":"Leaving selling costs out of NRV when {a|stock} is sold below cost after year end."},"examTypes":[{"id":"window","title":"Which period is covered? Is this event in or out?","pitfall":"Using the AGM or profit-announcement date as the end of the window.","see":"window"},{"id":"which-adjusting","title":"Which of these is an adjusting event? (practice Q14)","pitfall":"Picking a fire, a market fall or a dividend just because it is big.","see":"adjusting"},{"id":"adjust-amount","title":"What adjustment, and what is the adjusted profit?","pitfall":"Disclosing instead of adjusting; ignoring selling costs or {a|cash} already received.","see":"adjusting"},{"id":"non-adjusting","title":"How is a fire, market fall or refinancing after year end treated?","pitfall":"Booking it in this year’s numbers because it is material.","see":"nonadj"},{"id":"dividend","title":"A dividend declared after year end (practice Q35)","pitfall":"Recording a {l|liability} or an {x|expense}, or saying nothing until it is paid.","see":"dividends"},{"id":"going-concern","title":"Going concern doubts after year end (practice Q50)","pitfall":"Jumping to the liquidation basis, or calling it a contingent liability.","see":"goingconcern"},{"id":"disclosure","title":"What must be disclosed?","pitfall":"Forgetting the estimate of financial effect, or the authorisation date and who.","see":"disclose"}],"pages":[{"type":"map","id":"map-overview","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"intro","lo":52,"title":"Events after the reporting period, the Socratic way","body":["The year has ended, but the accounts aren’t signed yet. News keeps arriving. Which of it belongs in the numbers?","Each idea starts with a question. Think it through, then reveal the answer. The rule comes after, once you have half-worked it out.","Account names are coloured by element. Tap **Legend** at the top any time. At the end: a chapter quiz in exam style, then a harder exam."],"id":"intro-mo20uu","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"map","id":"map-1-window","bucket":"window","sect":1,"part":"The window: year end to authorisation"},{"type":"think","bucket":"window","lo":52,"scenario":"Merlion Trading’s year ends on 31 December 20X5. The accountants close the books in January, the auditors finish in early March, and the directors sign the FS on 15 March 20X6. Between New Year and 15 March, a customer goes bust, a warehouse floods and the board declares a dividend.","prompt":"The FS are “as at 31 December”. Should anything that happened in January to March show up in them at all?","answer":["Some of it, yes. The FS are not yet finished, and users want them to be **as right as possible** when they are published.","But not everything: the FS describe the position at 31 December, so news that only tells us about **next year** shouldn’t rewrite this year’s numbers.","SFRS(I) 1-10 draws that line. It covers the gap between the year end and the signing, and sorts every event into “adjust” or “don’t adjust”."],"id":"think-il5nu3","sect":1,"part":"The window: year end to authorisation"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"window","lo":52,"title":"What counts as an event after the reporting period?","body":["So some post-year-end news belongs in the FS and some doesn’t. Before sorting it, pin down exactly which stretch of time the standard covers.","**Events after the reporting period** are events, **favourable or unfavourable**, that occur between:","1. the **end of the reporting period** (the reporting date), and","2. the date the FS are **authorised for issue**.","They come in two kinds:","- **adjusting**: evidence of conditions that **existed at the reporting date** → change the amounts","- **non-adjusting**: conditions that **arose after** the reporting date → don’t change the amounts; disclose if material","+ Good news counts too. A lawsuit that settles for **less** than provided is an event after the reporting period just as much as one that settles for more."],"id":"rule-6qw6f7","sect":1,"part":"The window: year end to authorisation"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"window","lo":52,"title":"The window","intro":"The timeline from your notes. Only events inside the dashed box are covered by SFRS(I) 1-10.","notes":{"rd":"The **reporting date**: the date the FS describe. Anything that happened up to here is an ordinary transaction of the year.","ev":"Events in the window, **good or bad**. Each one is either adjusting or non-adjusting.","au":"The date the **directors** approve the FS for issue. The window shuts here.","af":"Too late for these FS. It is next year’s news."},"walk":[{"nodes":["rd"],"text":"The FS describe the position at the **reporting date**."},{"nodes":["ev"],"text":"From then until signing, news keeps arriving. These are the **events after the reporting period**, favourable or unfavourable."},{"nodes":["au"],"text":"The window closes when the FS are **authorised for issue**."},{"nodes":["af"],"text":"Anything later belongs to **next year’s** FS."}],"caption":"Year end → authorisation: the only stretch SFRS(I) 1-10 cares about.","id":"diagram-38bjov","sect":1,"part":"The window: year end to authorisation","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:584px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 531.00 133.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 118.4)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-118.4 516.3,-118.4 516.3,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\u003cg id=\"clust1\" class=\"cluster\">\n\u003ctitle>cluster_w\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"white\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,2\" d=\"M128.6,-8C128.6,-8 252.45,-8 252.45,-8 258.45,-8 264.45,-14 264.45,-20 264.45,-20 264.45,-84 264.45,-84 264.45,-90 258.45,-96 252.45,-96 252.45,-96 128.6,-96 128.6,-96 122.6,-96 116.6,-90 116.6,-84 116.6,-84 116.6,-20 116.6,-20 116.6,-14 122.6,-8 128.6,-8\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"136.53\" y=\"-24.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#0f766e\">SFRS(I) 1&#45;10 window\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"130.9\" y=\"-13.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#0f766e\">only events in here count\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>rd\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M87.6,-85C87.6,-85 12,-85 12,-85 6,-85 0,-79 0,-73 0,-73 0,-61 0,-61 0,-55 6,-49 12,-49 12,-49 87.6,-49 87.6,-49 93.6,-49 99.6,-55 99.6,-61 99.6,-61 99.6,-73 99.6,-73 99.6,-79 93.6,-85 87.6,-85\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"10.8\" y=\"-67.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">Reporting date\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"14.55\" y=\"-58.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">e.g. 31 Dec 20X5\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node2\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>ev\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M244.45,-87.54C244.45,-87.54 136.6,-87.54 136.6,-87.54 130.6,-87.54 124.6,-81.54 124.6,-75.54 124.6,-75.54 124.6,-58.46 124.6,-58.46 124.6,-52.46 130.6,-46.46 136.6,-46.46 136.6,-46.46 244.45,-46.46 244.45,-46.46 250.45,-46.46 256.45,-52.46 256.45,-58.46 256.45,-58.46 256.45,-75.54 256.45,-75.54 256.45,-81.54 250.45,-87.54 244.45,-87.54\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"149.28\" y=\"-73.05\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Events after the\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"147.78\" y=\"-62.05\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">reporting period\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"135.4\" y=\"-52.95\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">favourable or unfavourable\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge1\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>rd&#45;&gt;ev\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M100.1,-67C105.21,-67 110.48,-67 115.78,-67\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"115.62,-69.45 122.62,-67 115.62,-64.55 115.62,-69.45\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node3\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>au\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M364.55,-92.04C364.55,-92.04 293.45,-92.04 293.45,-92.04 287.45,-92.04 281.45,-86.04 281.45,-80.04 281.45,-80.04 281.45,-53.96 281.45,-53.96 281.45,-47.96 287.45,-41.96 293.45,-41.96 293.45,-41.96 364.55,-41.96 364.55,-41.96 370.55,-41.96 376.55,-47.96 376.55,-53.96 376.55,-53.96 376.55,-80.04 376.55,-80.04 376.55,-86.04 370.55,-92.04 364.55,-92.04\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"292.25\" y=\"-77.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">FS authorised\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"306.12\" y=\"-66.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"white\">for issue\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"292.25\" y=\"-57.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">directors approve,\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"294.12\" y=\"-48.45\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"white\">e.g. 15 Mar 20X6\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge2\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>ev&#45;&gt;au\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M256.96,-67C262.14,-67 267.33,-67 272.42,-67\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"272.37,-69.45 279.37,-67 272.37,-64.55 272.37,-69.45\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node4\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>af\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#f3f4f6\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M489.9,-86.54C489.9,-86.54 413.55,-86.54 413.55,-86.54 407.55,-86.54 401.55,-80.54 401.55,-74.54 401.55,-74.54 401.55,-59.46 401.55,-59.46 401.55,-53.46 407.55,-47.46 413.55,-47.46 413.55,-47.46 489.9,-47.46 489.9,-47.46 495.9,-47.46 501.9,-53.46 501.9,-59.46 501.9,-59.46 501.9,-74.54 501.9,-74.54 501.9,-80.54 495.9,-86.54 489.9,-86.54\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"419.48\" y=\"-72.05\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Later events\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"420.23\" y=\"-62.95\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">not in these FS;\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"412.35\" y=\"-53.95\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">dealt with next year\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge3\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>au&#45;&gt;af\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M377.02,-67C382.23,-67 387.59,-67 392.92,-67\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"392.73,-69.45 399.73,-67 392.73,-64.55 392.73,-69.45\"/>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"think-a3wbie","type":"think","bucket":"window","lo":52,"scenario":"Merlion Trading’s year ends on 31 December 20X5. It announces its preliminary 20X5 profit to the market on 25 February 20X6. The directors approve the FS on 15 March 20X6 and they are issued to shareholders soon after. The shareholders approve them at the AGM on 28 April 20X6.","prompt":"Which date closes the window: 25 February, 15 March or 28 April?","answer":["**15 March 20X6**, the date the directors authorised the FS for issue.","Not the AGM: even though shareholders approve the FS later, they were authorised when the directors approved them.","Not the profit announcement either: events after an announcement but before authorisation are still in the window. A press release is not a signature."],"sect":1,"part":"The window: year end to authorisation"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"window","lo":52,"title":"The authorisation date","body":["The window starts at the reporting date; that part is easy. Its end is where people slip, because several dates compete for it.","- In Singapore the **directors** approve the FS before they go to shareholders at the AGM.","- If shareholders approve them later, the FS were still **authorised** when the directors approved them, not on the AGM date.","- A public **profit announcement** before authorisation doesn’t close the window. Events after it, up to authorisation, are still covered.","- The FS must **disclose** the authorisation date and who gave it (more in the disclosure step).","! Using the AGM date as the end of the window is a classic slip. It is the **directors’ approval** that counts."],"id":"rule-xc3ltw","sect":1,"part":"The window: year end to authorisation"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"window","lo":52,"q":"Sentosa Ltd’s year ends 30 June 20X6. Directors approve the FS on 12 September 20X6; the AGM approves them on 25 October 20X6. A major customer goes into liquidation on 2 October 20X6. How is this dealt with in the 20X6 FS?","options":[{"t":"As an adjusting event, because the AGM hadn’t yet approved the FS","why":"The window closed on 12 September, when the directors authorised the FS. The AGM date doesn’t matter."},{"t":"Not at all: it happened after the FS were authorised for issue","ok":true,"why":"Authorisation on 12 September closed the window. The liquidation is dealt with in the FY20X7 FS."},{"t":"As a non-adjusting event disclosed in the notes","why":"Only events before authorisation are covered, adjusting or not."},{"t":"By restating the 20X6 FS after the AGM","why":"Nothing is restated. The event is after authorisation, so it is next year’s."}],"id":"mcq-r9d9rn","sect":1,"part":"The window: year end to authorisation"},{"type":"map","id":"map-2-adjusting","bucket":"adjusting","sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events"},{"id":"think-189sud","type":"think","bucket":"adjusting","lo":52,"scenario":"Two pieces of bad news arrive on the same day, 20 February 20X6, for a company with a 31 December 20X5 year end. (1) A customer that owed $40,000 at year end is declared bankrupt; it had been making losses and paying late for months. (2) A fire destroys one of the company’s {a|warehouses}.","prompt":"Same date, both bad. Should either of them change the 31 December 20X5 numbers?","answer":["**The bankruptcy, yes.** The customer was already in trouble at 31 December. The {a|receivable} was already impaired; we only found out how badly in February. Adjust it.","**The fire, no.** At 31 December the {a|warehouse} was fine. The fire is **new news** about 20X6. Don’t adjust; disclose it if material.","So the date of the **event** doesn’t decide it. What decides it is when the **condition** arose."],"sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"adjusting","lo":52,"title":"Adjusting vs non-adjusting: one test","body":["Once an event is inside the window, it has to be sorted. The bankruptcy and the fire showed that the event date can’t do the sorting, so the standard uses one question instead.","Ask: **did the condition already exist at the reporting date?**","- **Yes → adjusting event.** It gives evidence about that condition. Change the amounts recognised (or recognise items not recognised before).","- **No → non-adjusting event.** It shows a condition that arose after. Don’t change the amounts. Disclose it if material.","+ Adjusting = “already true at year end, we just found out later”. Non-adjusting = “new news”.","! “After the year end” doesn’t mean non-adjusting. A bankruptcy in February is adjusting; a fire in February is not. **The timing of the condition decides, not the timing of the event.**"],"id":"rule-1p3q1z","sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"adjusting","lo":52,"title":"Adjusting or not? The decision chart","intro":"The chart from your notes. Going concern is checked first, because it overrides everything; then the condition test.","notes":{"st":"Any event, good or bad, in the window between the reporting date and authorisation.","gc":"Does the event mean management will **liquidate** or **stop trading**, or has no realistic alternative?","gcy":"The whole basis changes. This beats every other answer on the chart.","q":"The key test: was the **condition** already there at the reporting date?","adj":"Change the amounts: impair, write down, adjust the {l|provision}.","non":"Leave the numbers. If material, disclose its **nature** and an **estimate of its effect**.","div":"A dividend declared after year end is the classic non-adjusting event: no {l|liability} at the reporting date."},"walk":[{"nodes":["st"],"text":"Start with an event in the window."},{"nodes":["gc","gcy"],"text":"First: is the business still a **going concern**? If not, stop: don’t use the going concern basis."},{"nodes":["q"],"text":"Otherwise ask the one question: did the **condition** exist at the reporting date?"},{"nodes":["adj"],"text":"Yes → **adjusting**. Change the numbers."},{"nodes":["non","div"],"text":"No → **non-adjusting**. Disclose if material. Dividends declared after year end live here."}],"caption":"Going concern first, then the condition test.","id":"diagram-10cdl5","sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:527px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 479.00 341.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 326.39)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-326.39 464.75,-326.39 464.75,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>st\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M306.85,-311.99C306.85,-311.99 1