he {a|debt} was bad in December."}],"sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events"},{"id":"calc-ejta2n","type":"calc","bucket":"adjusting","lo":52,"given":1,"q":"Your turn, with the first line done. At 31 Dec 20X5 Bukit Timah Traders owed $64,000, and an {a|allowance} of $6,400 (10%) was already made against it. On 3 Mar 20X6, before the FS are authorised, it goes into liquidation; the liquidator expects to pay 25 cents in the dollar. By how much must the {a|allowance} be **increased** in the 20X5 FS?","answer":41600,"unit":"$","working":["{a|Allowance} needed = 64,000 × 75% = 48,000.","Already provided: {a|6,400}. Increase = 48,000 − 6,400 = **$41,600**.","Dr {x|Impairment loss on receivables} 41,600 / Cr {a|Allowance for doubtful debts} 41,600. Adjusting: the {a|debt} was already doubtful at year end."],"sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events"},{"id":"je-qpb06d","type":"je","bucket":"adjusting","lo":52,"q":"Now a blank one. At 31 Dec 20X5 Jurong Movers owed $90,000. It paid $15,000 on 18 Jan 20X6, then went bankrupt on 10 Feb 20X6 after years of losses. The liquidator expects to pay 20 cents in the dollar on what is still owed. No {a|allowance} was made at year end. Record the adjustment in the 20X5 FS.","accounts":["Trade receivables","Allowance for doubtful debts","Impairment loss on receivables","Inventory","Cost of sales","Provision for legal claim","Legal expense","Dividend payable","Retained earnings","Share capital","Tax payable","Revenue","Cash","Loss on fire"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Impairment loss on receivables","amount":60000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Allowance for doubtful debts","amount":60000,"el":"a"}],"why":"Of the 90,000 owed at year end, 15,000 came in and 20% of the remaining 75,000 is expected, so 30,000 is recoverable. {x|Loss} = 90,000 − 30,000 = 60,000. The January payment is evidence too: it shows that part was collectable.","sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events"},{"id":"think-1rvzz8","type":"think","bucket":"adjusting","lo":52,"scenario":"At 31 Dec 20X5, {a|inventory} that cost $25,000 is in the warehouse. On 10 Jan 20X6 it is sold for $21,000, with $1,000 of selling costs.","prompt":"The sale happened in January. Why should it change the {a|inventory} figure at 31 December?","answer":["Because {a|inventory} is carried at the lower of cost and **NRV**, and the January sale is the best evidence of what that stock was really worth **at year end**.","NRV = 21,000 − 1,000 = 20,000, below its {a|cost} of 25,000. The {a|stock} was overvalued at 31 December; we only found out in January."],"sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events"},{"id":"calc-j3ps9f","type":"calc","bucket":"adjusting","lo":52,"q":"Year end 31 Dec 20X5; FS authorised 15 Mar 20X6. {a|Inventory} that cost $25,000 is on hand at year end. On 10 Jan 20X6 it is sold for $21,000, with $1,000 of selling costs. By how much must the {a|inventory} be written down in the 20X5 FS?","answer":5000,"unit":"$","working":["NRV = selling price 21,000 − selling costs 1,000 = 20,000.","{x|Write-down} = {a|cost} 25,000 − NRV 20,000 = **$5,000**.","Dr {x|Cost of sales} 5,000 / Cr {a|Inventory} 5,000."],"sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events"},{"id":"think-13g2of","type":"think","bucket":"adjusting","lo":52,"scenario":"Year end 31 Dec 20X5; FS authorised 15 Mar 20X6. A customer sued the company in 20X5 over a faulty product supplied in 20X5. At year end the lawyers said a loss was probable and a {l|provision} of $50,000 was made. On 1 Feb 20X6 the case settles for $80,000.","prompt":"Keep the old estimate and mention the settlement in a note, or change the {l|provision}?","answer":["Change it. The {l|obligation} **existed at year end** (the faulty product was supplied in 20X5); the settlement just tells us its true size.","Increase the {l|provision} to the settlement amount: 80,000 − 50,000 = 30,000.","Same logic if no {l|provision} had been made at all: the settlement would confirm a {l|present obligation} at year end, so recognise one."],"sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events"},{"id":"je-1rk0mu","type":"je","bucket":"adjusting","lo":52,"q":"Year end 31 Dec 20X5; FS authorised 15 Mar 20X6. A customer sued over a faulty product supplied in 20X5, and a {l|provision} of $50,000 was made at year end. On 1 Feb 20X6 the case settles for $80,000, to be paid in March. Record the adjustment to the {l|lawsuit provision} in the 20X5 FS.","accounts":["Trade receivables","Allowance for doubtful debts","Impairment loss on receivables","Inventory","Cost of sales","Provision for legal claim","Legal expense","Dividend payable","Retained earnings","Share capital","Tax payable","Revenue","Cash","Loss on fire"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Legal expense","amount":30000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for legal claim","amount":30000,"el":"l"}],"why":"Settlement 80,000 − {l|provision} 50,000 = 30,000 more {x|expense} in 20X5. The {a|cash} is paid in 20X6, so at 31 Dec 20X5 it is still a {l|provision}.","sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"adjusting","lo":52,"title":"Three adjusting events, one profit figure","intro":"The notes’ worked example in one picture: three pieces of February news, three year-end balances corrected, one hit to 20X5 profit.","notes":{"e1":"Reveals that the {a|receivable} was already impaired at year end.","e2":"The best evidence of the {a|stock}’s NRV at year end.","e3":"Confirms the size of an {l|obligation} that existed at year end.","b1":"40,000 × 90% = 36,000: Dr {x|Impairment loss on receivables} / Cr {a|Allowance for doubtful debts}.","b2":"NRV 21,000 − 1,000 = 20,000; {x|write-down} 5,000: Dr {x|Cost of sales} / Cr {a|Inventory}.","b3":"80,000 − 50,000 = 30,000: Dr {x|Legal expense} / Cr {l|Provision for legal claim}.","pl":"36,000 + 5,000 + 30,000 = 71,000 less profit for 20X5."},"walk":[{"nodes":["e1","e2","e3"],"text":"Three events, all in the window, all about conditions that existed at 31 December."},{"nodes":["b1","b2","b3"],"text":"Each one corrects a **year-end balance**: {a|receivable}, {a|inventory}, {l|provision}."},{"nodes":["pl"],"text":"Together they cut 20X5 profit by **71,000**. The news came in 20X6; the {x|loss} belongs to 20X5."}],"caption":"Adjusting events land in the year that has just ended.","id":"diagram-1b9da0","sect":2,"part":"Adjusting events","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:422px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 384.00 185.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 174.44)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-174.44 373.39,-174.44 373.39,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>e1\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M100.53,-161.82C100.53,-161.82 18,-161.82 18,-161.82 12,-161.82 6,-155.82 6,-149.82 6,-149.82 6,-137.82 6,-137.82 6,-131.82 12,-125.82 18,-125.82 18,-125.82 100.53,-125.82 100.53,-125.82 106.53,-125.82 112.53,-131.82 112.53,-137.82 112.53,-137.82 112.53,-149.82 112.53,-149.82 112.53,-155.82 106.53,-161.82 100.53,-161.82\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"42.01\" y=\"-145.37\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">20 Feb\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"14.64\" y=\"-134.37\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">customer bankrupt\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node4\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>b1\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e8eef6\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M228.56,-163.64C228.56,-163.64 178.28,-163.64 178.28,-163.64 172.28,-163.64 166.28,-157.64 166.28,-151.64 166.28,-151.64 166.28,-136 166.28,-136 166.28,-130 172.28,-124 178.28,-124 178.28,-124 228.56,-124 228.56,-124 234.56,-124 240.56,-130 240.56,-136 240.56,-136 240.56