.12,-12 149.12,-6 155.12,0 161.12,0 161.12,0 284.15,0 284.15,0 290.15,0 296.15,-6 296.15,-12 296.15,-12 296.15,-24 296.15,-24 296.15,-30 290.15,-36 284.15,-36\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"222.64\" y=\"-19.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">Non&#45;adjusting and\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"222.64\" y=\"-7.55\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">immaterial: nothing needed\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"edge11\" class=\"edge\">\n\u003ctitle>mat&#45;&gt;nonN\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" d=\"M162.76,-73C173.28,-63.52 186.45,-51.64 197.76,-41.44\"/>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"#6b7280\" stroke=\"#6b7280\" points=\"198.97,-43.18 202.02,-37.6 196.16,-40.06 198.97,-43.18\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"197.24\" y=\"-50.75\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\"> No \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"id":"je-150ewf","type":"je","bucket":"dividends","lo":52,"q":"Marina Ltd’s year ends 31 December 20X5. On 20 February 20X6 its board declares a final dividend of 6 cents a share on its 8,000,000 shares, payable on 10 May 20X6. Record the declaration in Marina’s 20X6 books.","accounts":["Trade receivables","Allowance for doubtful debts","Impairment loss on receivables","Inventory","Cost of sales","Provision for legal claim","Legal expense","Dividend payable","Retained earnings","Share capital","Tax payable","Revenue","Cash","Loss on fire"],"lines":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Retained earnings","amount":480000,"el":"e"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Dividend payable","amount":480000,"el":"l"}],"why":"In 20X6, the declaration creates the obligation. A dividend is a distribution to owners, so it reduces {e|retained earnings} directly, not through profit or loss.","sect":4,"part":"Dividends declared after year end"},{"type":"map","id":"map-5-goingconcern","bucket":"goingconcern","sect":5,"part":"Going concern"},{"type":"think","bucket":"goingconcern","lo":54,"scenario":"Year end 31 Dec 20X5. On 5 Feb 20X6 Changi Air Freight’s only bank withdraws its facilities. On 1 Mar 20X6, before the FS are authorised, the board resolves to stop trading and wind the company up.","prompt":"The board’s decision came after year end, so is it just another non-adjusting event to disclose? Or does it change something bigger?","answer":["Bigger. The FS have been prepared on the assumption that the company will **carry on**: {a|assets} at cost less {a|depreciation}, {l|debts} due in years classified as non-current, and so on.","If the business is going to be wound up, that assumption is false, and so is **every number built on it**.","So SFRS(I) 1-10 says: don’t prepare the FS on a going concern basis. It is the one post-year-end event that changes the **whole basis** of the FS."],"id":"think-puab8b","sect":5,"part":"Going concern"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"goingconcern","lo":54,"title":"Going concern: the override","body":["Every event so far changed one balance at most. Going concern is different: it is the assumption under **every** number, so if it fails, everything moves.","If, after the reporting date, management decides to **liquidate** the entity or **stop trading**, or has **no realistic alternative** but to do so → **don’t** prepare the FS on a going concern basis.","- This changes the **whole basis** of the FS, not just one balance.","- A big fall in results or financial position after year end is a warning sign: reassess whether going concern is still appropriate.","- Management assesses going concern over **at least 12 months from the end of the reporting period** (SFRS(I) 1-1)."],"id":"rule-a2ekfm","sect":5,"part":"Going concern"},{"id":"think-1wol97","type":"think","bucket":"goingconcern","lo":54,"scenario":"Seletar Labs’ year ends 31 Dec 20X5. At that date its large {l|loan} falls due in 11 months. It hasn’t yet found the money to repay it, but it is in talks with two banks and an investor, and nothing has been decided about closing down.","prompt":"Liquidation basis? Business as usual? Or something in between?","answer":["In between. There is **doubt**, but management neither intends to liquidate nor has run out of realistic options: it is actively seeking refinancing.","So keep the **going concern basis**, and **disclose the material uncertainty** and the events behind it, so users know the risk.","Liquidation basis needs an intention to liquidate or **no realistic alternative**. Worry is not the same as a decision."],"sect":5,"part":"Going concern"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"goingconcern","lo":54,"title":"Three outcomes","body":["Not every doubt leads to the liquidation basis. Seletar Labs showed a middle ground, so going concern cases sort into three outcomes.","1. **No significant doubts** → going concern basis; nothing special to disclose.","2. **Material uncertainty**, but management has realistic plans (e.g. seeking refinancing) → going concern basis, and **disclose the uncertainty** and the events behind it.","3. Management **intends to liquidate** or stop trading, or has **no realistic alternative** → **not** a going concern: say so, give the basis used instead (e.g. realisable values) and why.","! Uncertainty alone doesn’t mean the liquidation basis. And it isn’t a **contingent liability** either: the {l|bond or loan} is already a {l|liability}; the doubt is about the entity’s survival."],"id":"rule-16tqkx","sect":5,"part":"Going concern"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"goingconcern","lo":54,"title":"Going concern: which of the three?","intro":"Two questions sort every going concern case into one of three boxes.","notes":{"st":"Something makes you wonder whether the entity will survive the next 12 months or more.","q1":"The high bar: an **intention** to liquidate or stop trading, or **no realistic alternative**.","nogc":"The whole FS change basis. The one non-adjusting situation that changes the numbers.","q2":"Below that bar, is the doubt significant enough to be a **material uncertainty**?","unc":"Keep the basis but tell users about the risk: the uncertainty and the events behind it.","fine":"Business as usual."},"walk":[{"nodes":["st","q1"],"text":"First the high bar: has management decided to liquidate, or is there **no realistic alternative**?"},{"nodes":["nogc"],"text":"If yes, the FS are **not** on a going concern basis. Disclose that, the basis used and why."},{"nodes":["q2","unc"],"text":"If no, but there is a **material uncertainty**, keep the basis and **disclose the uncertainty**. This is practice Q50’s answer."},{"nodes":["fine"],"text":"No serious doubt: business as usual."}],"caption":"Worry → disclose. Decision (or no way out) → change the basis.","id":"diagram-190h4c","sect":5,"part":"Going concern","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:452px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 411.00 269.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(10.8 257.94)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-10.8,10.8 -10.8,-257.94 400.33,-257.94 400.33,10.8 -10.8,10.8\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>st\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke=\"#1f3a5f\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M287.15,-247.14C287.15,-247.14 152.12,-247.14 152.12,-247.14 146.12,-247.14 140.12,-241.14 140.12,-235.14 140.12,-235.14 140.12,-223.14 140.12,-223.14 140.12,-217.14 146.12,-211.14 152.12,-211.14 152.12,-211.14 287.15,-211.14 287.15,-211.14 293.15,-211.14 299.15,-217.14 299.15,-223.14 299.15,-223.14 299.15,-235.14 299.15,-235.14 299.15,-241.14 293.15,-247.14 287.15,-247.14\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"157.01\" y=\"-229.69\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bo