true,"why":"That is the minimum. Management takes into account all available information about the future, for at least that long."},{"t":"Only the period up to the date the FS are authorised","why":"Far too short: going concern looks forward at least 12 months from the reporting date."},{"t":"Until the maturity of the entity’s largest borrowing","why":"Not the rule; the period is at least 12 months from the end of the reporting period."}],"id":"mcq-17qtfp","sect":5,"part":"Going concern"},{"type":"map","id":"map-6-disclose","bucket":"disclose","sect":6,"part":"Disclosure"},{"id":"think-1m7hyb","type":"think","bucket":"disclose","lo":53,"scenario":"You are an investor reading Merlion Trading’s 20X5 annual report in April 20X6 (year end 31 December 20X5, FS authorised 15 March 20X6). You know one of its {a|warehouses} burned down in February 20X6, and the FS show no {x|loss} for it.","prompt":"What would you want the notes to tell you, so the 20X5 numbers don’t mislead you?","answer":["1. **When** the FS were signed off, and **by whom**, so you know how recent the information is.","2. **What happened** after year end that matters: the fire.","3. **How big** the hit is, or at least an estimate: otherwise you can’t adjust your view of next year.","That is almost exactly the standard’s disclosure list."],"sect":6,"part":"Disclosure"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"disclose","lo":53,"title":"Disclosure checklist","body":["Non-adjusting events leave the numbers alone, so the notes have to carry the news. The investor’s three questions map almost one to one onto the standard’s list.","1. The **date** the FS were authorised for issue and **who** gave that authorisation. If the owners or others can amend the FS after issue, say so.","2. **Update disclosures** about conditions that existed at year end when new information arrives (e.g. new evidence on a contingent liability).","3. For each **material non-adjusting** event: its **nature**, and an **estimate of its financial effect**, or a statement that no estimate can be made.","4. **Dividends** declared after year end: in the notes, not as a {l|liability}.","5. **Going concern**: a material uncertainty, or the fact that the FS are not on a going concern basis (with the basis used and why).","! For a non-adjusting event, the **nature alone** is not enough. The estimate of its financial effect (or a statement that it can’t be estimated) is required too."],"id":"rule-rmzbio","sect":6,"part":"Disclosure"},{"id":"visual-772e8t","type":"visual","bucket":"disclose","lo":53,"title":"What the note looks like","html":"\u003cp>The checklist in action: a note for the warehouse fire, a material non-adjusting event.\u003c/p>\n    \u003cdiv class=\"card\">\u003cp>\u003cb>Note 32 · Events after the reporting period\u003c/b>\u003c/p>\n      \u003cp>\u003ci>“On 3 February 20X6 a fire destroyed the Jurong warehouse and \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">inventory\u003c/span> with a \u003cspan class=\"el el-a\">carrying amount\u003c/span> of $1.2 million. The company is insured and expects to recover about $0.9 million. The \u003cspan class=\"el el-x\">loss\u003c/span> will be recognised in the year ending 31 December 20X6.”\u003c/i>\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\n    \u003cp class=\"cap\">From your notes. Spot the three parts: \u003cstrong>what happened\u003c/strong> (nature), \u003cstrong>how much\u003c/strong> (estimated effect, net of insurance) and \u003cstrong>when it will be booked\u003c/strong> (next year). No amount in the 20X5 statement of financial position changes.\u003c/p>","sect":6,"part":"Disclosure"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"disclose","lo":53,"q":"Which disclosure does SFRS(I) 1-10 require for a **material non-adjusting** event?","options":[{"t":"Only the nature of the event","why":"An estimate of its financial effect is required too, or a statement that no estimate can be made."},{"t":"The nature of the event and an estimate of its financial effect, or a statement that no estimate can be made","ok":true,"why":"Exactly the requirement."},{"t":"A restated statement of financial position showing the effect","why":"Non-adjusting events are not booked or restated; they are disclosed."},{"t":"Nothing until the event is recognised in the following year","why":"If material, it must be disclosed in this year’s FS."}],"id":"mcq-1f1dhy","sect":6,"part":"Disclosure"},{"type":"examtypes","id":"examtypes","bucket":"wrap","sect":7,"part":"Wrap-up"},{"type":"summary","lo":53,"title":"Events after the reporting period on one page","rows":[["Which period?","From the reporting date to the date the FS are **authorised for issue** (by the directors, not the AGM)."],["Adjusting event?","Evidence of a condition that **existed at the reporting date**: change the amounts."],["Non-adjusting event?","A condition that **arose after** the reporting date: don’t adjust; disclose if material."],["Typical adjusting","Lawsuit settles, customer bankrupt, {a|inventory} sold below cost, cost or proceeds finalised, bonus determined, fraud or error found."],["Typical non-adjusting","Fire or flood, market-value fall, acquisition, restructuring announced, share issue, tax rate change."],["Dividends declared after year end?","Not a {l|liability}; disclose in the notes. Recognised next year: Dr {e|Retained earnings} / Cr {l|Dividend payable}."],["Disclose what?","Authorisation date and who; nature + estimated effect of material non-adjusting events; updated disclosures."],["Liquidation decided after year end?","Change the basis: not a going concern. Disclose the fact, the basis and why."],["Only uncertainty?","Keep the going concern basis and disclose the material uncertainty."]],"id":"summary-v0k79k","bucket":"wrap","sect":7,"part":"Wrap-up"}],"quiz":{"cases":{"acorn":{"name":"Acorn","source":"Official practice paper (Q31–35 case)","text":["Acorn Pte Ltd (Acorn) specialises in carbon capture, utilisation and storage from thermal power generation. Acorn holds several equity investments, and no election was made to measure them at FVOCI. Acorn has a financial year ending 31 March.","Over the past 2 financial years, there has been the following activity in Acorn’s equity investment portfolio:","• 4 April 20X4: Acorn purchased 250,000 shares in Oak Ltd (Oak) at a cost of $1.25 per share. A brokerage fee of $2,350 was incurred on this transaction.","• Dividend income of $5,500 was received on 30 September 20X4.","• 31 March 20X5: Oak’s price per share was $1.35.","• 30 June 20X5: Acorn sold 100,000 Oak shares at a price per share of $1.20.","• Dividend income of $3,150 was received on 30 September 20X5.","• 31 March 20X6: Oak’s price per share was $1.05.","On 31 May 20X6, Acorn’s board declared a dividend of $2.75 million relating to profits for the financial year ended 31 March 20X6. Acorn’s financial statements were authorised for issue on 30 June 20X6."]},"tanjong":{"name":"Tanjong Fabrication","text":["Tanjong Fabrication Pte Ltd (Tanjong) has a financial year ending 31 December 20X5. Its draft profit before tax for 20X5 is $850,000. The directors authorised the FS for issue on 18 March 20X6. The following happened after the year end:","(a) 12 January 20X6: steel stock that cost $140,000 and was in {a|inventory} at year end was sold for $128,000, paying a sales commission of $3,000.","(b) 9 February 20X6: a flash flood destroyed a {a|warehouse} with a {a|carrying amount} of $600,000. Tanjong expects insurance to pay $450,000.","(c) 25 February 20X6: a lawsuit over a workplace accident in 20X5 was settled for $95,000. At year end Tanjong had provided $120,000 for it.","(d) 1 March 20X6: Tanjong issued 2,000,000 new {e|ordinary shares} for {a|cash}."]}},"questions":[{"id":"q1","case":"acorn","lo":53,"type":"dividend","source":"Official practice paper Q35","q":"With reference to SFRS(I) 1-10 Events after the Reporting Period, identify how Acorn should account for the dividend declaration on 31 May 20X6?","options":[{"t":"The dividend payable of $2.75 million should be recorded as a liability in the Statement of financial position as at 31 March 20X6","pit":"dividend-liability","why":"At 31 March 20X6 nothing had been declared, so Acorn had no obligation. A dividend declared after year end is not a {l|liability} at the reporting date."},{"t":"The dividend payable of $2.75 million should be recorded as an expense in the Statement of profit or loss for the financial year ended 31 March 20X6","pit":"dividend-expense","why":"A dividend is a distribution to owners, never an {x|expense} in profit or loss. And it isn’t recognised in FY20X6 at all."},{"t":"No adjustment or disclosure is required in the financial statements for the financial year ended 31 March 20X6 until the dividend is paid to Acorn’s shareholders","pit":"no-disclosure","why":"The declaration on 31 May falls inside the window (FS authorised 30 June), so it must be disclosed now, not when paid."},{"t":"The dividend of $2.75 million is disclosed in the notes to the financial statements as a non-adjusting event in the financial statements for the financial year ended 31 March 20X6","ok":true,"why":"Declared after the reporting date but before authorisation: a non-adjusting event. No liability, no expense; disclose the dividend in the notes."}]},{"id":"q2","case":"acorn","lo":52,"type":"window","q":"For Acorn’s financial year ended 31 March 20X6, which period does SFRS(I) 1-10 cover?","options":[{"t":"1 April 20X5 to 31 March 20X6","why":"That is the financial year itself. Events after the reporting period start the day after it ends."},{"t":"31 March 20X6 to 31 May 20X6, the date the dividend was declared","why":"The declaration date is just one event inside the window. It doesn’t close it."},{"t":"31 March 20X6 to 30 June 20X6, the date the FS were authorised for issue","ok":true,"why":"From the end of the reporting period to the date the FS are authorised for issue."},{"t":"31 March 20X6 to the date of Acorn’s AGM","pit":"after-authorisation","why":"The window closes when the FS are authorised for issue, not when shareholders meet."}]},{"id":"q3","case":"acorn","lo":52,"type":"non-adjusting","q":"After selling 100,000 of the 250,000 {a|Oak shares} it bought, Acorn holds 150,000, measured at FVTPL at $1.05 each at 31 March 20X6. On 20 April 20X6, after a market-wide sell-off that month, Oak’s price falls to $0.80. At what amount are the Oak shares carried at 31 March 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$120,000, reflecting the April price","pit":"big-means-adjust","why":"The April fall reflects conditions after year end: a non-adjusting event. Fair value at the reporting date is the 31 March price."},{"t":"$157,500, with the fall disclosed if material","ok":true,"why":"150,000 × $1.05 = 157,500. The 37,500 fall is a non-adjusting event; disclose it if material."},{"t":"$157,500, with a {l|provision} of $37,500 for the expected loss","why":"Nothing is recognised for a non-adjusting event, as a provision or otherwise."},{"t":"$138,750, splitting the fall between the two years","why":"There is no splitting. Year-end fair value is the price at the reporting date."}]},{"id":"q4","case":"acorn","lo":52,"type":"adjust-amount","q":"A customer owed Acorn $180,000 at 31 March 20X6. It had been in financial difficulty since late 20X5 and went into liquidation on 10 June 20X6. The liquidator expects to pay 30 cents in the dollar. Acorn’s FS are authorised for issue on 30 June 20X6. What should Acorn do in its FY20X6 FS?","options":[{"t":"Disclose the liquidation in the notes; no adjustment","pit":"disclose-only-adjusting","why":"The customer’s difficulties existed at year end, so the liquidation is evidence of an impaired {a|receivable}: adjust."},{"t":"Recognise an {x|impairment loss} of $126,000","ok":true,"why":"Adjusting event. 180,000 × 70% = 126,000."},{"t":"Recognise an {x|impairment loss} of $180,000","why":"The liquidator expects to pay 30%, so only 70% is lost."},{"t":"Nothing: the liquidation happened after the reporting date","pit":"timing-of-event","why":"The timing of the condition decides, not the timing of the event. The condition existed at year end."}]},{"id":"q5","case":"acorn","lo":52,"type":"window","q":"Acorn’s FY20X6 FS (year ended 31 March 20X6) were authorised for issue on 30 June 20X6. On 15 July 20X6 a court rules against Acorn in a dispute that was pending at 31 March 20X6. How is this dealt with in the FY20X6 FS?","options":[{"t":"As an adjusting event: the dispute existed at year end","pit":"after-authorisation","why":"The condition did exist at year end, but the ruling came after the FS were authorised on 30 June. The window had closed."},{"t":"As a non-adjusting event disclosed in the notes","why":"Only events before authorisation are covered at all."},{"t":"It is not dealt with in the FY20X6 FS; it is a matter for FY20X7","ok":true,"why":"The FS were authorised on 30 June 20X6. Anything after that is dealt with in the next year’s FS."},{"t":"The FY20X6 FS must be withdrawn and reissued","why":"Nothing in SFRS(I) 1-10 requires that: the ruling is after authorisation."}]},{"id":"q6","case":"tanjong","lo":52,"type":"which-adjusting","q":"Which of events (a) to (d) are adjusting events for Tanjong’s 20X5 FS?","options":[{"t":"(a) and (c)","ok":true,"why":"The stock sale is evidence of NRV at year end, and the settlement confirms an {l|obligation} from a 20X5 accident. The flood and the share issue are new conditions."},{"t":"(a), (b) and (c)","pit":"big-means-adjust","why":"The flood destroyed the warehouse after year end: a non-adjusting event, however large."},{"t":"(c) only","why":"The January sale below cost is evidence of the {a|stock}’s NRV at 31 December: adjusting."},{"t":"(b) and (d)","why":"Those are the two non-adjusting events: a new {x|loss} and a new {e|equity} transaction."}]},{"id":"q7","case":"tanjong","lo":52,"type":"adjust-amount","q":"By how much should Tanjong write down the {a|steel stock} in its 20X5 FS?","options":[{"t":"$12,000","pit":"nrv-selling-costs","why":"Ignores the sales commission. NRV is the selling price less the costs to sell."},{"t":"$15,000","ok":true,"why":"NRV = 128,000 − 3,000 = 125,000. {x|Write-down} = 140,000 − 125,000 = 15,000."},{"t":"Nil: the loss arose on the sale in 20X6","pit":"timing-of-event","why":"The sale is evidence that the {a|stock} was worth less than {a|cost} at 31 December. Adjust 20X5."},{"t":"$137,000","why":"That is cost less commission, not a {x|write-down}. Compare {a|cost} with NRV."}]},{"id":"q8","case":"tanjong","lo":52,"type":"adjust-amount","q":"Which journal entry records the effect of event (c) in Tanjong’s 20X5 FS?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Legal expense","amount":25000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for legal claim","amount":25000,"el":"l"}],"why":"Backwards: the case settled for **less** than provided, so the {l|provision} goes down and the {x|expense} is reduced."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Provision for legal claim","amount":25000,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Legal expense","amount":25000,"el":"x"}],"ok":true,"why":"{l|Provision} 120,000 − settlement 95,000 = 25,000 released. Good news is an adjusting event too."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Provision for legal claim","amount":120000,"el":"l"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":120000,"el":"a"}],"why":"The {a|cash} is paid in 20X6, and the amount is the settlement, not the old estimate. At 31 December the {l|provision} stays, at the settled amount."},{"t":"No entry: keep the {l|provision} at the year-end estimate and disclose the settlement","pit":"disclose-only-adjusting","why":"The settlement confirms the size of an obligation that existed at year end. Adjust the {l|provision}."}]},{"id":"q9","case":"tanjong","lo":53,"type":"non-adjusting","q":"How should Tanjong deal with the flood (b) in its 20X5 FS?","options":[{"t":"Write off the {a|warehouse}: recognise a $600,000 {x|loss} in 20X5","pit":"big-means-adjust","why":"The {a|warehouse} was intact at 31 December. The flood is a non-adjusting event."},{"t":"Recognise a $150,000 {x|loss} net of insurance in 20X5","pit":"big-means-adjust","why":"Netting the insurance doesn’t make it adjusting. Nothing is recognised in 20X5."},{"t":"No adjustment; disclose the nature of the flood and an estimate of its financial effect","ok":true,"why":"Non-adjusting and material. Disclose what happened and the estimated effect (a 600,000 {a|carrying amount} lost, about 450,000 expected from insurance)."},{"t":"No adjustment and no disclosure, because it will be recognised in 20X6","pit":"no-disclosure","why":"A material non-adjusting event must be disclosed in this year’s FS."}]},{"id":"q10","case":"tanjong","lo":52,"type":"adjust-amount","q":"What is Tanjong’s adjusted profit before tax for 20X5?","options":[{"t":"$835,000","pit":"disclose-only-adjusting","why":"Picks up the {x|stock write-down} but leaves the {l|lawsuit provision} at its old estimate."},{"t":"$860,000","ok":true,"why":"850,000 − 15,000 {x|write-down} + 25,000 {l|provision} released = 860,000. The flood and the share issue don’t change 20X5 profit."},{"t":"$260,000","pit":"big-means-adjust","why":"Also deducts the {x|flood loss}, which is non-adjusting."},{"t":"$810,000","why":"Treats the lawsuit as {x|extra cost}. It settled for less than provided, so profit goes up."}]},{"id":"q11","lo":52,"type":"which-adjusting","source":"Official practice paper Q14","q":"SFRS(I) 1-10, ‘Events after the Reporting Period’ sets out when an entity should adjust its financial statements for events after the reporting period. Identify which of the following constitutes an ‘adjusting event’ with reference to the standard?","options":[{"t":"A decline in the fair value of a company’s investment portfolio between the end of the reporting period and the date when the financial statements are authorised for issue","pit":"big-means-adjust","why":"The fall reflects market conditions after the reporting date: non-adjusting."},{"t":"Dividends declared after the reporting period but before the financial statements are authorised for issue","pit":"dividend-liability","why":"No obligation existed at the reporting date: non-adjusting, disclosed in the notes."},{"t":"A fire destroying a large percentage of inventory after the end of the reporting period","pit":"big-means-adjust","why":"The {a|inventory} was intact at year end; the fire is a new condition. Non-adjusting, disclose if material."},{"t":"The bankruptcy of a customer that occurs after the reporting period","ok":true,"why":"The bankruptcy usually shows the {a|receivable} was already impaired at the reporting date: adjusting."}]},{"id":"q12","lo":52,"type":"window","q":"Bedok Ltd (year end 31 December 20X5) announced its preliminary profit on 20 February 20X6. On 1 March 20X6 a lawsuit pending at year end settled for more than the amount provided. The directors authorised the FS on 15 March 20X6. What should Bedok do?","options":[{"t":"Nothing: the profit was already announced, so the FS are fixed","pit":"after-authorisation","why":"A profit announcement doesn’t close the window. Authorisation on 15 March does."},{"t":"Disclose the settlement only, since it came after the profit announcement","why":"It is still in the window, and it is adjusting: the obligation existed at year end."},{"t":"Increase the {l|provision} in the 20X5 FS to the settlement amount","ok":true,"why":"Events after a public profit announcement but before authorisation are still events after the reporting period. This one is adjusting."},{"t":"Recognise the extra amount in 20X6 when it is paid","pit":"timing-of-event","why":"The {l|obligation} existed at year end; the settlement is evidence of its size. Adjust 20X5."}]},{"id":"q13","lo":52,"type":"which-adjusting","q":"Which of the following is a **non-adjusting** event for a company with a 31 December 20X5 year end?","options":[{"t":"In January 20X6, the final price of a machine delivered in November 20X5 is agreed","why":"The purchase happened before year end; finalising its cost is adjusting."},{"t":"In February 20X6, the 20X5 staff bonus, which employees were entitled to at year end, is calculated","why":"The {l|obligation} existed at year end; determining the amount is adjusting."},{"t":"In February 20X6, the company agrees to buy a competitor","ok":true,"why":"A major acquisition after year end is a new transaction: non-adjusting, disclose if material."},{"t":"In March 20X6, auditors discover that 20X5 sales were overstated by fraud","why":"Discovering fraud or errors that show the FS are wrong is adjusting."}]},{"id":"q14","lo":54,"type":"going-concern","q":"Year end 31 December 20X5. In February 20X6 Kallang Ltd’s main customer cancels all contracts. The directors still hope to win new business but there is significant doubt that the company can continue. They have not decided to wind up, and refinancing talks are ongoing. How should the 20X5 FS be prepared?","options":[{"t":"On a liquidation basis, because of the significant doubt","pit":"liquidation-basis","why":"The bar for leaving the going concern basis is an intention to liquidate, or no realistic alternative. Doubt is not enough."},{"t":"On a going concern basis, disclosing the material uncertainty","ok":true,"why":"Management has realistic options and no intention to liquidate, so keep the basis and disclose the uncertainty."},{"t":"On a going concern basis with no disclosure, as the cancellation was after year end","pit":"no-disclosure","why":"A material uncertainty about going concern must be disclosed."},{"t":"On a going concern basis, recognising a {l|provision} for the {x|expected losses}","why":"{x|Future operating losses} are not provided for. The uncertainty is disclosed."}]},{"id":"q15","lo":53,"type":"disclosure","q":"Which of the following must every entity disclose under SFRS(I) 1-10?","options":[{"t":"The date of the AGM at which shareholders approved the FS","why":"The requirement is the date the FS were **authorised for issue** and who authorised them."},{"t":"The date the FS were authorised for issue and who gave that authorisation","ok":true,"why":"So users know that events after that date are not reflected."},{"t":"A list of all events after the reporting period, material or not","why":"Only material non-adjusting events need disclosing (adjusting ones are already in the numbers)."},{"t":"The adjusted and unadjusted figures for each adjusting event","why":"Adjusting events simply change the numbers; no before-and-after table is required."}]}]},"exam":{"cases":{"helix":{"name":"Helix Bio","source":"Official practice paper (Q46–50 case)","text":["Helix Bio Pte Ltd (Helix) is a biotechnology company that specialises in developing gene therapies for genetic blood disorders such as sickle cell anaemia and haemophilia. Helix has developed a gene therapy which is due to enter the final stage of clinical trials. The final stage is required before the therapy is approved for public use. To fund the purchase of specialised, ready-to-use lab equipment needed for the final stage of the trial, Helix issued a bond for S$6 million. The details of the bond issue are:","• $6,000,000 of 12% bonds","• Issued on 1 January 20X5","• Interest is payable twice a year on 30 June and 31 December","• Maturity date is 31 December 20X8","• Market yield for bonds of similar risk and maturity is 10%"]},"kranji":{"name":"Kranji Engineering","text":["Kranji Engineering Pte Ltd (Kranji) has a financial year ending 31 December 20X6. Its draft profit before tax is $2,400,000. The directors authorised the FS for issue on 24 March 20X7. No adjustments have yet been made for the following:","(1) Lim Bros owed Kranji $150,000 at 31 December 20X6. Lim Bros had been loss-making for two years. It paid $30,000 on 20 January 20X7 and was declared bankrupt on 14 February 20X7. The liquidator expects to pay 25 cents in the dollar on the balance still owed. No {a|allowance} was made at year end.","(2) Machine parts in {a|inventory} at 31 December 20X6 at a cost of $260,000 were sold in February 20X7 for $230,000, less an 8% agent’s commission.","(3) A customer sued Kranji in November 20X6 over a defective product supplied in 20X6. Kranji provided $200,000 at year end. On 3 March 20X7 the case settled for $260,000.","(4) On 2 February 20X7 a fire destroyed uninsured {a|inventory} that cost $180,000. It had been bought in January 20X7.","(5) On 10 March 20X7 the board declared a final dividend of 5 cents a share on Kranji’s 4,000,000 {e|ordinary shares}.","(6) On 5 March 20X7 Kranji discovered that a sales clerk had fraudulently recorded a fictitious 20X6 {i|credit sale} of $45,000."]}},"questions":[{"id":"x1","case":"helix","lo":54,"type":"going-concern","source":"Official practice paper Q50","q":"It is now November 20X7 and due to regulatory delays, the clinical trial is 12 months behind schedule and the gene therapy has not generated any revenue. The bond is due to mature on 31 December 20X8 and a review of Helix board minutes shows that management are unsure how they will repay the bond. Helix management have approached investors and banks but have yet to secure any refinancing options. With reference to SFRS(I) 1-10, Consolidated Financial Statements, identify how this uncertainty should be reported in the financial statements for the financial year ending 31 December 20X7?","options":[{"t":"Helix should continue to prepare the accounts on a going concern basis but the uncertainty over their ability to repay the bond should be disclosed in the notes to the financial statements","ok":true,"why":"Management is unsure but still seeking refinancing: a material uncertainty, not an intention to liquidate. Keep the going concern basis and disclose the uncertainty. (The stem’s “SFRS(I) 1-10, Consolidated Financial Statements” is the paper’s slip; and November 20X7 is before year end, so this is really the SFRS(I) 1-1 going concern assessment.)"},{"t":"Due to the inability to repay the bond, Helix should prepare the financial statements on a liquidation basis","pit":"liquidation-basis","why":"The liquidation basis needs an intention to liquidate or no realistic alternative. Helix is still talking to investors and banks."},{"t":"As the bond matures on 31 December 20X8, Helix should wait to make any disclosures in the 20X8 financial statements","pit":"no-disclosure","why":"The {l|bond} is due on 31 December 20X8, inside the 12-month going concern assessment period from 31 December 20X7. The uncertainty must be disclosed now."},{"t":"Helix should disclose a contingent liability in the notes to the financial statements","pit":"gc-contingent","why":"The {l|bond} is already a recognised {l|liability}. The doubt is about Helix’s ability to continue, which is a going concern disclosure, not a contingent liability."}]},{"id":"x2","case":"helix","lo":54,"type":"going-concern","q":"Helix’s year ends 31 December 20X7. Its $6 million {l|bond} matures on 31 December 20X8 and the clinical trial is 12 months behind schedule. Suppose that on 10 February 20X8, before the FY20X7 FS are authorised, the trial is abandoned and Helix’s board resolves to cease operations and wind the company up. How should the FY20X7 FS be prepared?","options":[{"t":"On a going concern basis, disclosing the board’s decision as a non-adjusting event","pit":"timing-of-event","why":"Going concern is the exception: a decision after year end to liquidate means the FS must not be prepared on a going concern basis."},{"t":"Not on a going concern basis","ok":true,"why":"Management intends to liquidate. The FS can’t be prepared on a going concern basis, even though the decision was after the reporting date."},{"t":"On a going concern basis for FY20X7, changing the basis from FY20X8","why":"The decision falls inside the window, so it already changes the FY20X7 basis."},{"t":"On a going concern basis, with a contingent liability for the {l|bond}","pit":"gc-contingent","why":"The {l|bond} is already a {l|liability}, and the basis itself must change."}]},{"id":"x3","case":"helix","lo":54,"type":"disclosure","q":"Helix’s year ends 31 December 20X7. On 10 February 20X8, before the FY20X7 FS are authorised, its clinical trial is abandoned and the board resolves to cease operations and wind the company up. What must the FY20X7 FS disclose about the basis of preparation?","options":[{"t":"Nothing: the change of basis speaks for itself","pit":"no-disclosure","why":"Not a going concern → disclose that fact, the basis used and the reason."},{"t":"Only that there is a material uncertainty about going concern","why":"That is the disclosure when the going concern basis is kept. Here the basis has changed."},{"t":"That the FS are not prepared on a going concern basis, the basis used instead, and why","ok":true,"why":"Exactly the three points required."},{"t":"Only the estimated loss on winding up","why":"The fact, the basis and the reason are required."}]},{"id":"x4","case":"helix","lo":54,"type":"going-concern","q":"Helix’s year ends 31 December 20X7 and its $6 million {l|bond} matures on 31 December 20X8. When assessing going concern for the FY20X7 FS, what is the minimum period Helix’s management must consider?","options":[{"t":"Up to the date the FS are authorised for issue","why":"Far too short. Going concern looks forward at least 12 months from the reporting date."},{"t":"At least 12 months from 31 December 20X7, so at least to 31 December 20X8, when the bond matures","ok":true,"why":"At least 12 months from the end of the reporting period. That captures the {l|bond} repayment."},{"t":"At least 12 months from the date the FS are authorised for issue","why":"SFRS(I) 1-1 measures from the end of the reporting period."},{"t":"Only until the clinical trial ends","why":"Not the rule. At least 12 months from the end of the reporting period."}]},{"id":"x5","case":"helix","lo":52,"type":"non-adjusting","q":"Helix’s year ends 31 December 20X7. Its $6 million {l|bond} matures on 31 December 20X8, and at year end Helix had not secured any refinancing. On 15 February 20X8, before the FY20X7 FS are authorised, Helix signs an agreement with a bank to refinance the {l|bond} into a {l|new 5-year loan}. How is the {l|bond} presented at 31 December 20X7?","options":[{"t":"As a {l|non-current liability}, because it was refinanced before the FS were authorised","pit":"timing-of-event","why":"At 31 December 20X7 the {l|bond} was due within 12 months and no refinancing right existed. The later agreement doesn’t change the classification."},{"t":"As a {l|current liability}; the refinancing is disclosed as a non-adjusting event","ok":true,"why":"Classification reflects the position at the reporting date. The refinancing is a new condition: disclose it."},{"t":"It is derecognised, because the {l|new loan} replaces it","why":"The refinancing happened in 20X8. At 31 December 20X7 the {l|bond} was still outstanding."},{"t":"As a contingent liability in the notes","pit":"gc-contingent","why":"The {l|bond} is an actual {l|liability}, recognised in the statement of financial position."}]},{"id":"x6","case":"kranji","lo":52,"type":"adjust-amount","q":"What is Kranji’s adjusted profit before tax for 20X6?","options":[{"t":"$1,776,600","pit":"dividend-expense","why":"Also deducts the fire (non-adjusting) and the dividend (never an {x|expense}, and not a 20X6 item)."},{"t":"$1,976,600","pit":"big-means-adjust","why":"Deducts the {x|fire loss}, but the {a|inventory} was bought in January 20X7 and destroyed in February: non-adjusting."},{"t":"$2,175,000","pit":"nrv-selling-costs","why":"Ignores the agent’s commission in NRV, so the {x|write-down} is too small."},{"t":"$2,156,600","ok":true,"why":"2,400,000 − {x|receivable impairment} 90,000 − {x|inventory write-down} 48,400 − {x|lawsuit} 60,000 − {i|fictitious sale} 45,000 = 2,156,600."}]},{"id":"x7","case":"kranji","lo":52,"type":"adjust-amount","q":"Which journal entry records the Lim Bros adjustment in the 20X6 FS?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Impairment loss on receivables","amount":112500,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Allowance for doubtful debts","amount":112500,"el":"a"}],"why":"Applies the {x|loss} to the full {a|150,000}, ignoring the 30,000 collected in January."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Impairment loss on receivables","amount":90000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Allowance for doubtful debts","amount":90000,"el":"a"}],"ok":true,"why":"{a|Remaining 120,000} × 75% = 90,000. The January payment shows that part was collectable."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Impairment loss on receivables","amount":120000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Allowance for doubtful debts","amount":120000,"el":"a"}],"why":"Writes off the whole 120,000 still owed, ignoring the liquidator’s expected 25% payment."},{"t":"No entry: disclose the bankruptcy as a non-adjusting event","pit":"disclose-only-adjusting","why":"Lim Bros had been loss-making for years: the {a|receivable} was already impaired at year end. Adjusting."}]},{"id":"x8","case":"kranji","lo":52,"type":"adjust-amount","q":"Which journal entry records the effect of the lawsuit (3) in the 20X6 FS?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Legal expense","amount":260000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for legal claim","amount":260000,"el":"l"}],"why":"Double counts: 200,000 is already provided. Only the 60,000 difference is needed."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Legal expense","amount":60000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Provision for legal claim","amount":60000,"el":"l"}],"ok":true,"why":"Settlement 260,000 − {l|provision} 200,000 = 60,000. The {l|obligation} existed at year end; the settlement fixes its size."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Provision for legal claim","amount":200000,"el":"l"},{"side":"Dr","account":"Legal expense","amount":60000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Cash","amount":260000,"el":"a"}],"why":"The settlement is paid in 20X7. At 31 December 20X6 it is still a {l|provision}, at the settled amount."},{"t":"No entry; disclose the settlement","pit":"disclose-only-adjusting","why":"Adjusting: it confirms the amount of an obligation that existed at year end."}]},{"id":"x9","case":"kranji","lo":53,"type":"disclosure","q":"How should Kranji deal with the fire (4) and the dividend (5) in its 20X6 FS?","options":[{"t":"Recognise a 180,000 {x|fire loss} and a 200,000 {l|dividend payable}","pit":"dividend-liability","why":"Both are non-adjusting: the fire destroyed 20X7 stock, and no dividend obligation existed at year end."},{"t":"Recognise the 200,000 {l|dividend payable} only; the fire is a 20X7 matter","pit":"dividend-liability","why":"A dividend declared after year end is not a {l|liability} at the reporting date."},{"t":"Disclose both in the notes: the fire’s nature and estimated effect (180,000), and the 200,000 dividend","ok":true,"why":"Both are non-adjusting. The dividend is 5 cents × 4,000,000 = 200,000."},{"t":"Neither needs any mention, as both are 20X7 transactions","pit":"no-disclosure","why":"Material non-adjusting events and dividends declared after year end must be disclosed."}]},{"id":"x10","case":"kranji","lo":52,"type":"adjust-amount","q":"How should Kranji deal with the fictitious sale (6)?","options":[{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Revenue","amount":45000,"el":"i"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Trade receivables","amount":45000,"el":"a"}],"ok":true,"why":"Fraud discovered before authorisation that shows the FS are wrong is adjusting. Reverse the {i|fictitious sale} in 20X6."},{"t":"Correct it in the 20X7 FS as a prior period error, restating the 20X6 comparatives","why":"The 20X6 FS haven’t been issued yet, so just fix them now. Prior period error restatement is for errors found after issue."},{"je":[{"side":"Dr","account":"Impairment loss on receivables","amount":45000,"el":"x"},{"side":"Cr","account":"Allowance for doubtful debts","amount":45000,"el":"a"}],"why":"The sale never happened, so {i|revenue} is overstated. It isn’t a {x|bad debt} on a real sale."},{"t":"Disclose the fraud as a non-adjusting event","pit":"disclose-only-adjusting","why":"The FS were wrong at year end: adjusting."}]},{"id":"x11","lo":52,"type":"which-adjusting","q":"At 31 December 20X5 Orchid Ltd was owed $300,000 by a customer that was trading profitably and paying on time. On 10 February 20X6 a flood destroyed the customer’s only factory, and the customer was declared bankrupt on 1 March 20X6. Orchid’s FS are authorised on 20 March 20X6. How is the bankruptcy treated?","options":[{"t":"As an adjusting event, because customer bankruptcies are always adjusting","pit":"timing-of-event","why":"Usually, but not always. The test is whether the condition existed at year end. Here the customer was healthy until the February flood."},{"t":"As a non-adjusting event, disclosed if material","ok":true,"why":"The bankruptcy was caused by a flood after year end: a new condition. The {a|receivable} was not impaired at 31 December."},{"t":"Ignored, because it happened after year end","why":"It is inside the window. As a material non-adjusting event it is disclosed."},{"t":"As an adjusting event, but only for the part not covered by the customer’s insurance","why":"It is non-adjusting, so nothing is adjusted in the 20X5 FS."}]},{"id":"x12","lo":52,"type":"which-adjusting","q":"For a 31 December 20X5 year end, with FS authorised on 18 March 20X6, how many of these are adjusting events? (i) 5 Jan: proceeds of a vehicle sold on 20 December 20X5 are finalised. (ii) 1 Feb: a large issue of new shares for cash. (iii) 12 Feb: a {l|provision} for a 20X5 warranty claim is confirmed by a court ruling. (iv) 25 Mar: a major customer goes into liquidation after long-standing problems.","options":[{"t":"One","why":"Count again: (i) and (iii) are both about conditions at year end."},{"t":"Two","ok":true,"why":"(i) the sale was before year end, (iii) the {l|obligation} existed at year end. (ii) is a new {e|equity} transaction; (iv) is after authorisation, so outside these FS."},{"t":"Three","pit":"after-authorisation","why":"(iv) happened after the FS were authorised on 18 March, so it is outside the window."},{"t":"Four","pit":"big-means-adjust","why":"The share issue is non-adjusting, and (iv) is after authorisation."}]}]},"prepared":true},{"id":"foreign-currency","title":"Foreign Currency Transactions","subtitle":"SFRS(I) 1-21 · single-entity transactions","code":"E3","los":{"55":"Determining an entity’s functional currency","56":"Recording and reporting foreign currency transactions (single entity, no hedging)"},"accounts":[{"name":"Inventory","el":"a"},{"name":"Trade payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Trade receivable","el":"a"},{"name":"Cash","el":"a"},{"name":"Loan payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Interest payable","el":"l"},{"name":"Revenue","el":"i"},{"name":"Foreign exchange gain","el":"i"},{"name":"Foreign exchange loss","el":"x"},{"name":"Revaluation surplus (OCI)","el":"e"},{"name":"Machinery","el":"a"},{"name":"Prepayment","el":"a"},{"name":"Contract liability","el":"l"},{"name":"Inventory write-down","el":"x"},{"name":"Cost of sales","el":"x"}],"steps":[{"id":"functional","name":"Functional currency","short":"Functional"},{"id":"initial","name":"Day 1: spot rate","short":"Spot rate"},{"id":"yearend","name":"Year end: monetary or not?","short":"Year end"},{"id":"settle","name":"Settlement differences","short":"Settlement"},{"id":"where","name":"Where differences go","short":"P&L or OCI"}],"map":{"functional":{"label":"Primary economic environment decides it","star":"Everything else is “foreign” only relative to this choice of currency"},"initial":{"label":"Spot rate on the transaction date"},"yearend":{"label":"Monetary → closing rate; non-monetary stays","star":"One question, “is it monetary?”, decides whether anything moves"},"settle":{"label":"Paid − recorded → P&L"},"where":{"label":"Monetary → P&L; non-monetary follows its gain"}},"preamble":["Welcome to foreign currency: the chapter where the numbers change even though nobody did anything. A Singapore company buys from a US supplier, the exchange rate wobbles, and suddenly the same {l|debt} is worth a different number of S$. Our job is to decide which amounts move, and where the difference lands.","1. **Functional currency:** first decide which currency the entity really lives in. Everything else is “foreign”.","2. **Day 1:** record each foreign transaction at the spot rate on the transaction date.","3. **Year end:** sort items into **monetary** (they move to the closing rate) and **non-monetary** (they stay put). This is the special part.","4. **Settlement:** pay or collect at that day’s rate, and book the difference.","5. **Where it goes:** monetary differences to P&L; non-monetary ones follow their own gain, to OCI or P&L.","Single-entity transactions only: no translating foreign subsidiaries, no hedging. It is a short chapter with a lot of tiny numbers, so keep a calculator handy and a sense of humour about the third decimal place."],"intros":{"functional":"First stop, before a single rate gets multiplied. “Foreign” only means something once we know what “home” is, so we pin down the **functional currency**: the currency of the entity’s primary economic environment. ★ This is the special part: a Singapore company can quite happily live in US$.","initial":"Now that we know home, a foreign transaction comes along. Day 1 is the easy bit: translate at the spot rate on the transaction date and book both sides at the same S$ amount. Mostly routine, with one shortcut (average rates) and one trap (using a later rate).","yearend":"The invoice is booked, and then the year ends before it is paid. Time to sort the balance sheet into two piles: items that are a fixed number of currency units (they move to the closing rate) and everything else (it stays at the historical rate). ★ This is where the chapter lives or dies: get “monetary or not?” right and the rest is arithmetic.","settle":"We’ve recorded it and maybe retranslated it; now the {a|cash} actually goes out (or comes in). Whatever the S$ difference is between what we paid and what the books say, it is a {i|gain} or {x|loss}. Same period or across a year end, same idea; the exam’s favourite version spans a year end.","where":"Last stop: where do all these differences land? Mostly P&L, which is why this step is short. The one twist: non-monetary items at fair value send their FX part wherever their own gain goes, so a {a|revalued building} drags its FX into OCI."},"pitfalls":{"presentation-functional":"Confusing the presentation currency (a choice) with the functional currency (a fact).","incorporation":"Choosing the currency of the country of incorporation, or the one shareholders prefer, as the functional currency.","secondary-first":"Letting secondary indicators (financing, retained receipts) override clear primary ones (sales prices, costs).","no-translation":"Recording the foreign currency number as if it were S$.","closing-initial":"Recording a transaction at the closing or payment rate instead of the spot rate on the transaction date.","mon-historical":"Leaving a monetary item ({l|payable}, {a|receivable}, {l|loan}, foreign {a|cash}, {l|accrued interest}) at the historical rate at the year end.","nonmon-retranslate":"Retranslating a non-monetary item at cost ({a|PPE}, {a|inventory}, {a|prepayments}) at the closing rate.","prepay-monetary":"Calling {a|prepayments}, {l|advances received} or {a|equity shares} monetary.","fx-oci":"Putting exchange differences on monetary items in OCI instead of P&L.","fx-pl-nonmon":"Sending the FX part of a revaluation to P&L when the {i|revaluation gain} itself goes to OCI.","fx-sign":"Getting the direction wrong: calling a {i|gain} a {x|loss}, or a {x|loss} a {i|gain}.","fx-to-asset":"Adjusting {a|inventory}, {a|PPE} or {i|revenue} for the exchange difference instead of booking a {i|gain} or {x|loss}.","no-entry-within-terms":"Thinking no exchange difference arises because the invoice was paid within the credit term or the same year.","ignore-ye":"Skipping the year-end retranslation, so the whole difference lands in the settlement year.","nrv-rate":"Using the wrong rates for LCNRV: cost goes at the historical rate, NRV at the rate when NRV is measured."},"examTypes":[{"id":"functional","title":"How is the functional currency determined? (practice Q15)","pitfall":"Choosing the presentation currency or the country of incorporation","see":"functional"},{"id":"initial-rate","title":"At what rate and amount is the transaction first recorded?","pitfall":"Using the closing or payment rate","see":"initial"},{"id":"monetary","title":"Which of these items is monetary?","pitfall":"Calling {a|prepayments}, {l|advances} or {a|shares} monetary","see":"yearend"},{"id":"closing","title":"What is the payable, receivable or loan at the year end?","pitfall":"Leaving it at the transaction-date rate","see":"yearend"},{"id":"nonmon","title":"At what amount is the machine or inventory carried at the year end?","pitfall":"Retranslating it at the closing rate","see":"yearend"},{"id":"settle","title":"Paid at a different rate: how is the effect recognised? (practice Q19)","pitfall":"Putting it in OCI, or saying no entry because it was paid on time","see":"settle"},{"id":"where","title":"Does the exchange difference go to P&L or OCI?","pitfall":"Sending monetary differences to OCI","see":"where"}],"pages":[{"type":"map","id":"map-overview","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"intro","lo":55,"title":"Foreign currency, the Socratic way","body":["Each idea starts with a question for you. Think it through, then reveal the answer. The rule comes after, once you have half-worked it out.","Account names are coloured by element. Tap **Legend** at the top any time. Rates are written as S$ per US$1 (or per RM1): a higher number means a **weaker** S$.","At the end there is an exam-style quiz and a harder exam, in the PFF format."],"id":"intro-3t852c","bucket":null,"sect":0,"part":"Overview"},{"type":"map","id":"map-1-functional","bucket":"functional","sect":1,"part":"Functional currency"},{"id":"think-rxfgnw","type":"think","bucket":"functional","lo":55,"scenario":"Merlion Bunkers Pte Ltd is incorporated and listed in Singapore. It sells marine fuel to ships worldwide at prices set in US$ on the global oil market. It buys its fuel in US$, and its {l|bank loans} are in US$. Its {x|office rent} and a few {x|salaries} are in S$.","prompt":"Which currency does Merlion Bunkers really “live in”? Does being Singaporean decide it?","answer":["**US$.** Its prices are set by a US$ market and most of its costs are in US$. That is its **primary economic environment**.","Being incorporated or listed in Singapore doesn’t decide it. Merlion can still **present** its financial statements in S$ if it wants to; that is a separate choice.","So for Merlion, an S$ rent invoice is a *foreign* currency transaction. Funny old world."],"sect":1,"part":"Functional currency"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"functional","lo":55,"title":"Five terms to get straight","body":["Merlion showed that where a company is incorporated doesn’t settle which currency it lives in. Before going further, pin down the vocabulary: everything later hangs on which currency is “functional”.","- **Functional currency:** the currency of the **primary economic environment** in which the entity operates.","- **Presentation currency:** the currency the financial statements are **presented** in. It can be chosen; the functional currency cannot.","- **Foreign currency:** any currency **other than** the functional currency.","- **Spot rate:** the exchange rate for immediate delivery.","- **Closing rate:** the spot rate at the end of the reporting period.","+ Functional = where you live economically. Presentation = what you print in."],"id":"rule-1fviav","sect":1,"part":"Functional currency"},{"id":"think-ax11fv","type":"think","bucket":"functional","lo":55,"scenario":"Tembusu Furniture Pte Ltd makes tables in Singapore and sells them to Singapore retailers at S$ prices set by local competition. Its wood and {x|wages} are paid in S$. Its parent in Hong Kong lent it HK$ to set up, and it parks spare {a|cash} in a HK$ account.","prompt":"The HK$ evidence is tempting. Which evidence should weigh most, and why?","answer":["The **sales prices and costs** weigh most. They show where the business actually earns and spends: S$.","Financing ({l|HK$ loan}) and where {a|cash} is parked (HK$ account) are only **secondary** indicators. They matter only when prices and costs are mixed. Here they aren’t, so the functional currency is **S$**."],"sect":1,"part":"Functional currency"},{"type":"rule","bucket":"functional","lo":55,"title":"Indicators: primary first","body":["Tembusu had evidence pointing two ways. The standard ranks the evidence, so you are not left weighing it by gut: some indicators decide, others only break ties.","**Primary indicators** (look here first):","1. **Sales prices:** which currency mainly influences the prices of goods and services (often the currency they are priced and settled in)?","2. **Market forces:** which country’s competitive forces and regulations mainly determine those prices?","3. **Costs:** which currency mainly influences {x|labour, material and other costs}?","**Secondary indicators** (only when the primary ones are mixed):","4. **Financing:** the currency funds are raised in ({l|debt} and {e|equity} issued).","5. **Retained receipts:** the currency {a|receipts} from operating activities are usually kept in.","The functional currency reflects the **underlying transactions and conditions**. It changes only when those conditions change, and then **prospectively** from the date of change.","! The country of incorporation and the currency shareholders prefer are **not** indicators. Examiners love them as distractors."],"id":"rule-1xoeqk","sect":1,"part":"Functional currency"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"functional","lo":55,"title":"Finding the functional currency","intro":"Primary indicators first. Only if they disagree do the secondary ones get a vote.","notes":{"price":"The heaviest indicator. Often the currency the goods and services are priced and settled in, and the country whose competition and regulation drive those prices.","cost":"Which currency mainly drives what it costs to make and sell: {x|wages, materials, overheads}.","noise":"Distractors. A Singapore-incorporated company can have US$ as its functional currency. Shareholders’ wishes don’t change economics.","q1":"If prices and costs agree on one currency, you are done.","sec":"Tie-breakers only: the currency of {l|debt} and {e|equity} raised, and the currency {a|operating receipts} are usually kept in.","judge":"Still mixed? Management judges which currency most faithfully represents the underlying transactions, giving priority to the primary indicators.","fc":"The currency of the primary economic environment in which the entity operates. Every other currency is a **foreign currency**.","pres":"What the statements are printed in. Can be chosen. The functional currency cannot."},"walk":[{"nodes":["price","cost"],"text":"Start with the primary indicators: what drives the selling prices, and what drives the costs."},{"nodes":["noise"],"text":"Ignore where the company is incorporated and what shareholders would like. Neither is an indicator."},{"nodes":["q1","fc"],"text":"If the primary indicators point to one currency, that is the functional currency."},{"nodes":["sec","judge"],"text":"If they are mixed, look at financing and retained receipts, then use judgment."},{"nodes":["pres"],"text":"Separately, the entity may choose to present its statements in another currency."}],"caption":"Functional = where you live economically. Presentation = what you print in.","id":"diagram-9fnpkg","sect":1,"part":"Functional currency","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:549px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 499.00 420.00\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">\n\u003cg id=\"graph0\" class=\"graph\" transform=\"scale(1 1) rotate(0) translate(14.4 405.39)\">\n\u003ctitle>G\u003c/title>\n\u003cpolygon fill=\"white\" stroke=\"none\" points=\"-14.4,14.4 -14.4,-405.39 484.25,-405.39 484.25,14.4 -14.4,14.4\"/>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node1\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>price\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#e6f4f1\" stroke=\"#0f766e\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M152.1,-390.99C152.1,-390.99 12,-390.99 12,-390.99 6,-390.99 0,-384.99 0,-378.99 0,-378.99 0,-363.91 0,-363.91 0,-357.91 6,-351.91 12,-351.91 12,-351.91 152.1,-351.91 152.1,-351.91 158.1,-351.91 164.1,-357.91 164.1,-363.91 164.1,-363.91 164.1,-378.99 164.1,-378.99 164.1,-384.99 158.1,-390.99 152.1,-390.99\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"42.68\" y=\"-376.5\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"11.00\" fill=\"#111827\">1 · Sales prices\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"14.93\" y=\"-367.4\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">which currency mainly sets them,\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"10.8\" y=\"-358.4\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"9.00\" fill=\"#111827\">and whose market forces and rules\u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\n\u003cg id=\"node4\" class=\"node\">\n\u003ctitle>q1\u003c/title>\n\u003cpath fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"1.2\" d=\"M295.85,-324.91C295.85,-324.91 214.25,-324.91 214.25,-324.91 208.25,-324.91 202.25,-318.91 202.25,-312.91 202.25,-312.91 202.25,-300.91 202.25,-300.91 202.25,-294.91 208.25,-288.91 214.25,-288.91 214.25,-288.91 295.85,-288.91 295.85,-288.91 301.85,-288.91 307.85,-294.91 307.85,-300.91 307.85,-300.91 307.85,-312.91 307.85,-312.91 307.85,-318.91 301.85,-324.91 295.85,-324.91\"/>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"start\" x=\"213.05\" y=\"-308.46\" font-fa