font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">property under the\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"263.71\" y=\"-70.64\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">fair value model,\u003c/text>\n\u003ctext xml:space=\"preserve\" text-anchor=\"middle\" x=\"263.71\" y=\"-59.39\" font-family=\"Helvetica,sans-Serif\" font-size=\"10.00\" fill=\"#374151\">shares at FVTPL \u003c/text>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/g>\n\u003c/svg>\n"},{"type":"mcq","bucket":"yearend","lo":56,"q":"Which of these is a **monetary** item?","options":[{"t":"A US$ {a|deposit} paid to a supplier for goods to be delivered next month","why":"A {a|prepayment}: it will be settled by receiving goods, not cash. Non-monetary."},{"t":"An {l|advance received} from a customer for goods to be shipped next month","why":"Settled by delivering goods, so non-monetary (a {l|contract liability})."},{"t":"{a|Shares} in a listed US company","why":"Equity shares are non-monetary, even though they are {a|financial assets}. No fixed number of dollars is receivable."},{"t":"{l|Interest accrued} on a US$ {l|bank loan}","ok":true,"why":"A fixed number of US$ to pay. Monetary, so it is retranslated at the closing rate like the {l|loan} itself."}],"id":"mcq-xl8j8l","sect":3,"part":"Year end: monetary or not?"},{"id":"calc-11ab0l","type":"calc","bucket":"yearend","lo":56,"unit":"$","q":"On 1 July 20X1 Orchid Trading (functional currency S$, year end 31 December) borrows US$100,000 from a US bank, when US$1 = S$1.34. At 31 December 20X1, US$1 = S$1.31. At what amount is the {l|Loan payable} carried at 31 December 20X1 (in S$)?","answer":131000,"working":["{l|Initial carrying amount}: US$100,000 × 1.34 = S$134,000.","A {l|loan} is monetary, so retranslate at the closing rate: US$100,000 × 1.31 = **S$131,000**.","The S$ strengthened, so the debt is worth less in S$: Dr {l|Loan payable} 3,000, Cr {i|Foreign exchange gain} 3,000."],"sect":3,"part":"Year end: monetary or not?"},{"id":"think-zrp8wv","type":"think","bucket":"yearend","lo":56,"scenario":"Orchid Trading (functional currency S$) bought a {a|packing machine} for US$200,000 cash when US$1 = S$1.33. It uses the cost model and depreciates the machine straight-line over 10 years, no residual value. At the year end, US$1 = S$1.30.","prompt":"Does the machine get retranslated? What is the annual {x|depreciation}?","answer":["**No retranslation.** It is non-monetary and carried at cost, so it stays at US$200,000 × 1.33 = **S$266,000** at every year end.","{x|Depreciation} is on that {a|S$ cost}: 266,000 ÷ 10 = **S$26,600** a year.","Retranslating at 1.30 (S$260,000) would be the classic slip: the machine doesn’t care what the dollar did after you bought it."],"sect":3,"part":"Year end: monetary or not?"},{"type":"diagram","bucket":"yearend","lo":56,"title":"Inventory: lower of cost and NRV, across currencies","intro":"The notes’ example. {a|Inventory} is non-monetary, but the NRV test still needs a rate, and it is a different one.","notes":{"cost":"{a|Cost} is translated once, at the transaction-date rate, and never retranslated.","nrv":"NRV is a current estimate, so it is translated at the rate on the date NRV is measured: here the closing rate.","cmp":"Compare in S$. In US$ terms the goods also lost value here, but it is the S$ comparison that counts.","ca":"The {a|inventory} is carried at the lower amount.","wd":"{x|Inventory write-down} in profit or loss. Part of it is a currency effect; it is not split out."},"walk":[{"nodes":["cost"],"text":"{a|Cost}: US$50,000 at the historical 1.35 = S$67,500."},{"nodes":["nrv"],"text":"NRV: US$48,000 at the year-end 1.38 = S$66,240."},{"nodes":["cmp","ca","wd"],"text":"Compare in S$: NRV is lower, so write down S$1,260 to profit or loss."}],"id":"diagram-1qel98","sect":3,"part":"Year end: monetary or not?","svg":"\u003csvg width=\"100%\" style=\"min-width:417px\" role=\"img\"\n viewBox=\"0.00 0.00 379.00 197.